aa47a4b9-ef6f-4a92-a2ec-ae0bd90e7024	\N	C:\\2026\\AI-Q\\Jul2026\\14July2026_bkp\\AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf	AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf	1	supports sales enablement by estimating the difficulty of closing a specific deal via a Sales Confidence\nScore (0–100, higher = stronger deal) that blends Customer Friction Risk (35%), Implementation Risk\n(35%), and Competitive Risk (30%), helping vendors anticipate blockers, prioritize mitigation, and decide\nwhere to invest sales effort\nMethodology Foundation\nThe scoring framework integrates established risk management standards with AI-specific evaluation\ncriteria:\n● FAIR Model (Factor Analysis of Information Risk): Loss Event Frequency × Loss Magnitude approach\nfor quantitative risk assessment\n● ISO 31000:2018: Risk = Likelihood × Consequence with systematic treatment framework\n● NIST AI Risk Management Framework (RMF): MAP, MEASURE, MANAGE functions across seven\ntrustworthiness characteristics\n● EU AI Act: Risk-based classification system (unacceptable, high, limited, minimal risk categories)\nAssessment Architecture\nThe platform employs four specialized assessment types:\nAssessment Type Primary User Decision Context Output Metric Scale\nVendor AI Vendors Trust Vendor Trust 0-100 (higher =\nSelf-Attestation establishment Score better)\nCOTS Vendor Side AI Vendors Sales opportunity Sales Confidence 0-100 (lower =\nevaluation Score Stronger Deal)\nCOTS Buyer Side Organizations Implementation Implementation 0-100 (lower =\nreadiness Risk Score safer)\nCustom AI Build Organizations Build vs. buy Build Risk Score 0-100 (lower =\ndecision viable)\nData Foundation\nAll assessments leverage:\n● Enhanced Risk Database: 1,248 curated AI risks across 7 domains with intent/timing classifications\n● Mitigation Database: 14,761 risk-mitigation mappings across 13 control categories\n● Assessment Field Schema: 143 structured data points across 8 assessment flows\nMathematical Framework Overview\nCore Calculation Pattern\nAll four assessment types follow a consistent three-layer architecture:\nLayer 1: Inherent Risk Calculation\n├─ Base Risk = Likelihood × Impact\n├─ Contextual Adjustment = Base Risk × Multipliers\n├─ Domain Weighting = Contextual Risk × Domain Weights\n└─ Sector Modification = Domain Risk + Sector Adjustments\nLayer 2: Mitigation Effectiveness\n├─ Control Coverage = Implemented Categories / Required Categories\n├─ Evidence Quality = Average(Relevance Scores)\n└─ Mitigation Effectiveness = (Coverage × 0.6) + (Quality × 0.4)\nLayer 3: Residual Risk & Final Scoring\n├─ Residual Risk = Inherent Risk × (1 - Mitigation) × Confidence\n└─ Final Score = Function(Residual Risks, Assessment-Specific Weights)\nStandardized Risk Scale\nAll intermediate risk calculations normalize to 0-100 scale:\n● 0-25: Low risk (acceptable with standard controls)\n● 26-50: Medium risk (tolerable with enhanced monitoring)\n● 51-75: High risk (requires mitigation before proceeding)\n● 76-100: Critical risk (unacceptable without major remediation)\nAssessment Type 1: Vendor Self-Attestation\n1.1 Assessment Overview\nObjective: Quantify vendor trustworthiness and risk profile for enterprise customer evaluation.\nOutput: Vendor Trust Score (VTS) = 0-100 (higher = more trustworthy)\nFormula Structure:\nVTS = 100 - [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) +\n(Operational_Risk × 0.30)]\nRationale for Weights:\n● Product Risk (40%): Primary evaluation criterion—the AI system itself\n● Governance Risk (30%): Indicates long-term reliability and compliance capability\n● Operational Risk (30%): Reflects day-to-day service delivery stability\n1.2 Product Risk Calculation\n1.2.1 Master Formula	[-0.00580885,0.03289391,0.053284585,0.0075950925,-0.033891067,0.013726702,0.053334884,-0.035985786,0.026171658,0.025987245,0.012414191,-0.013852013,0.008830697,-0.02192863,-0.011012007,-0.039989818,-0.016098168,-0.019958211,-0.0008821226,0.012982991,-0.