4421ab8e-6e1c-4418-bff2-ebe86efaac15	\N	C:\\2026\\AI-Q\\Jul2026\\14July2026_bkp\\AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf	AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf	6	● Multiply by 4 spreads scores across 0-100 range\n● Without normalization, most scores cluster in 10-30 range (poor discrimination)\n● With normalization, achieves healthy distribution across full scale\nCalculation Example:\nHealthcare chatbot inherent risk:\n1. Base Risk = L × I = 4.0 × 4.0 = 16.0\n2. Contextual Risk = 16.0 × CM = 16.0 × 0.9167 = 14.67\n3. Domain-Weighted Risk = 14.67 × DW = 14.67 × 1.17 = 17.16\n4. Sector-Adjusted Risk = 17.16 + SM = 17.16 + 8 = 25.16\n5. Normalized Risk = 25.16 × 4 = 100.64\n6. Capped Risk = MIN(100, 100.64) = 100.0\nInherent_Risk = 100.0 (maximum inherent risk before mitigations)\nInterpretation: Healthcare patient communication chatbot handling PHI with production deployment\nrepresents maximum inherent risk (100/100) before considering vendor's security controls.\n1.2.8 Mitigation Effectiveness Calculation\nMaster Formula:\nMitigation_Effectiveness = (Category_Coverage × 0.6) + (Evidence_Quality × 0.4)\nWhere:\nCategory_Coverage = Implemented_Categories / Required_Categories\nEvidence_Quality = AVERAGE(Relevance_Scores)\nRationale for Weights:\n● Category Coverage (60%): Breadth of controls matters most—defense-in-depth principle\n● Evidence Quality (40%): Quality of implementation matters—poorly configured controls provide false\nsecurity\nCategory Coverage Calculation\nFormula:\nCategory_Coverage = COUNT(Unique_implemented_categories) /\nCOUNT(Required_categories)\nRequired_categories determined by:\nFOR EACH applicable_risk:\nrequired_categories_for_risk = LOOKUP(risk.mitigation_requirements)\nunique_required_categories = DISTINCT(FLATTEN(required_categories_for_risk))\nimplemented_categories = INTERSECTION(\nvendor_implemented_mitigations.categories,\nunique_required_categories\n)\n13 Mitigation Categories:\n1. Data Governance & Privacy Controls\n2. Model Security & Integrity\n3. Access Management & Authentication\n4. Testing & Auditing Procedures\n5. Post-Deployment Monitoring\n6. Incident Response & Recovery\n7. Transparency & Documentation\n8. Human Oversight Mechanisms\n9. Bias Detection & Mitigation\n10. Adversarial Robustness\n11. Supply Chain Security\n12. Compliance & Regulatory Adherence\n13. User Education & Awareness\nCalculation Example:\nHealthcare chatbot mitigation coverage:\nRequired categories for 210 applicable risks:\n1. Data Governance (Privacy risks)\n2. Access Management (PHI protection)\n3. Testing & Auditing (Safety validation)\n4. Post-Deployment Monitoring (Drift detection)\n5. Incident Response (Breach procedures)\n6. Transparency (HIPAA accountability)\n7. Human Oversight (Clinical review)\n8. Bias Detection (Fair treatment)\nRequired_Categories = 8\nVendor implemented mitigations:\n- MIT-001: Data Governance\n- MIT-045: Access Management\n- MIT-112: Testing & Auditing\n- MIT-203: Incident Response\n- MIT-287: Transparency\n- MIT-334: Bias Detection\nImplemented_Categories = 6\nCategory_Coverage = 6 / 8 = 0.75 (75% coverage)\nEvidence Quality Calculation\nFormula:\nEvidence_Quality = SUM(Relevance_Score_i × Risk_Count_i) / SUM(Risk_Count_i)\nWhere for each implemented mitigation:\nRelevance_Score_i = Mitigation relevance to applicable risks (from Mitigation\nDB)\nRisk_Count_i = Number of applicable risks addressed by mitigation_i\nRelevance Score Derivation:\nThe Enhanced Risk Database Mitigations CSV contains pre-calculated relevance scores (0.0 to 1.0) for\neach risk-mitigation pairing:\nMitigation_Relevance_Score calculation (performed during database curation):\nHigh Relevance (0.8-1.0):	[-0.05441241,0.034768954,0.047095347,0.018217979,0.028906357,0.004547737,0.041747667,-0.033702176,0.012049554,0.051980134,0.016694654,-0.013208339,-0.029432075,-0.011390106,-0.018858625,-0.08106116,0.00071990094,-0.007933228,-0.017343264,-0.029291166,-0.011740944,-0.0