0b739867-52bd-4b79-a664-a14c3f31dbc4	\N	C:\\2026\\AI-Q\\Jul2026\\14July2026_bkp\\AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf	AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf	7	The Enhanced Risk Database Mitigations CSV contains pre-calculated relevance scores (0.0 to 1.0) for\neach risk-mitigation pairing:\nMitigation_Relevance_Score calculation (performed during database curation):\nHigh Relevance (0.8-1.0):\n- Mitigation directly prevents or detects the specific risk\n- Industry best practice for this exact risk type\n- Regulatory requirement for this risk\nMedium Relevance (0.5-0.79):\n- Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact but doesn't eliminate risk\n- Applicable but not specifically designed for this risk\n- Complementary control that supports primary mitigations\nLow Relevance (0.2-0.49):\n- Mitigation provides minimal risk reduction\n- Tangentially related to risk\n- Generic control with broad but shallow coverage\nNegligible Relevance (0.0-0.19):\n- Mitigation doesn't address this risk\n- Wrong risk domain or category\n- Theoretical connection only\nCalculation Example:\nHealthcare chatbot evidence quality:\nVendor's 6 implemented mitigations matched to 210 applicable risks:\nMIT-001 (Data Governance):\n- Addresses 85 Privacy risks\n- Average relevance: 0.82\n- Weighted contribution: 85 × 0.82 = 69.7\nMIT-045 (Access Management):\n- Addresses 73 Privacy + Security risks\n- Average relevance: 0.78\n- Weighted contribution: 73 × 0.78 = 56.94\nMIT-112 (Testing & Auditing):\n- Addresses 134 Safety + Fairness risks\n- Average relevance: 0.71\n- Weighted contribution: 134 × 0.71 = 95.14\nMIT-203 (Incident Response):\n- Addresses 89 Security + Operational risks\n- Average relevance: 0.68\n- Weighted contribution: 89 × 0.68 = 60.52\nMIT-287 (Transparency):\n- Addresses 62 Accountability risks\n- Average relevance: 0.65\n- Weighted contribution: 62 × 0.65 = 40.3\nMIT-334 (Bias Detection):\n- Addresses 42 Fairness risks\n- Average relevance: 0.74\n- Weighted contribution: 42 × 0.74 = 31.08\nTotal weighted: 69.7 + 56.94 + 95.14 + 60.52 + 40.3 + 31.08 = 353.68\nTotal risk instances: 85 + 73 + 134 + 89 + 62 + 42 = 485\nEvidence_Quality = 353.68 / 485 = 0.729\nMitigation Effectiveness Result\nFormula:\nMitigation_Effectiveness = (Category_Coverage × 0.6) + (Evidence_Quality × 0.4)\nCalculation Example:\nHealthcare chatbot mitigation effectiveness:\nMitigation_Effectiveness = (0.75 × 0.6) + (0.729 × 0.4)\n= 0.45 + 0.2916\n= 0.7416\nRounded: 0.74 (74% mitigation effectiveness)\nInterpretation: Vendor's controls reduce inherent risk by 74%, primarily through broad category\ncoverage (75%) with strong evidence quality (73%).\nGap Analysis for Vendor:\n● Missing categories: Post-Deployment Monitoring, Human Oversight\n● Recommendation: Implement continuous model monitoring and clinical review workflow\n● Impact: Would increase coverage to 100% (8/8 categories)\n1.2.9 Confidence Factor\nFormula:\nConfidence_Factor = LOOKUP(assessmentMethod, {\n"third_party_audit": 0.90,\n"third_party_review": 0.93,\n"internal_audit": 0.97,\n"self_reported_verified": 1.00,\n"self_reported_unverified": 1.10,\n"no_formal_assessment": 1.15\n}) × Evidence_availability_adjustment\nEvidence_availability_adjustment = {\nIF compliance_documentation_complete = "yes": ×0.98\nIF penetration_test_report_available = "yes": ×0.97\nIF soc2_type2_current = "yes": ×0.95\n}\nRationale:\n● Third-party audit (0.90): Independent verification increases confidence, reduces final risk\n● Self-reported unverified (1.10): No verification increases uncertainty, elevates final risk\n● Evidence adjustments: Objective artifacts (pen test reports, SOC2) reduce uncertainty\nConfidence Factor vs Confidence in Score:	[-0.03585946,-0.0022302573,0.06788319,0.04819584,-0.040044256,0.012592855,0.051592536,-0.018726502,-0.021793017,0.04977505,0.0055884495,0.006429298,-0.0113730375,-0.036541224,0.017174767,-0.056480207,0.0096272975,-0.016405843,-0.048326835,-0.04834