4bf81610-a5ba-4884-bc9c-91892ef7462f	\N	C:\\2026\\AI-Q\\Jul2026\\14July2026_bkp\\AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 2 (1).pdf	AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 2 (1).pdf	11	2. Configure auto-scaling before production\n3. Conduct stress test at 200,000 claims (133% of expected peak)\n3.4.8 Total Integration Risk\nFormula:\nIntegration_Risk = (MIN(100, Technical_Integration) x 0.15) +\n(MIN(100, Feature_Gap) x 0.25) +\n(MIN(100, Data_Interoperability) x 0.20) +\n(MIN(100, Vendor_Lock_in) x 0.15) +\n(MIN(100, Rollback_Capability) x 0.10) +\n(MIN(100, Scaling_Risk) x 0.15)\nWeight Rationale:\nComponent Weight Rationale\nTechnical Integration 0.15 Foundational complexity; typically a known quantity\nduring evaluation\nFeature Gap 0.25 Missing features have the highest direct impact on\ndeployment success\nData Interoperability 0.20 Data format and quality issues drive the bulk of\nintegration effort\nVendor Lock-in 0.15 Long-term strategic concern that affects total cost of\nownership\nRollback Capability 0.10 Safety net; important but lower-frequency\nconsideration\nScaling Risk 0.15 Critical for production but addressable through phased\nrollout\nCalculation Example:\nHealthFirst Insurance total integration risk:\nIR = (25 x 0.15) + (63 x 0.25) + (67 x 0.20) + (42 x 0.15) + (40 x 0.10) + (50\nx 0.15)\n= 3.75 + 15.75 + 13.4 + 6.3 + 4.0 + 7.5 = 50.7 ~ 51\nInterpretation: Maximum integration complexity (critical)\nEvery integration dimension shows significant risk:\nInterpretation: Moderate-High integration complexity\n1. Feature Gap: 63 raw (15.75 weighted)\n2. Data Interoperability: 67 raw (13.4 weighted)\n3. Scaling Risk: 50 raw (7.5 weighted)\n4. Vendor Lock-in: 42 raw (6.3 weighted)\n5. Rollback Capability: 40 raw (4.0 weighted)\n6. Technical Integration: 25 raw (3.75 weighted)\n3.5 Final Implementation Readiness Score\n3.5.1 Master Formula\nImplementation_Readiness_Score = 100 - [(Vendor_Risk × 0.35) +\n(Organizational_Readiness_Gap × 0.35) +\n(Integration_Risk × 0.30)]\nThis inversion aligns the Implementation Readiness Score with the Vendor Trust Score (Assessment Type\n1) and Sales Confidence Score (Assessment Type 2), ensuring all platform scores follow a consistent\nhigher-is-better paradigm.\n3.5.2 Comprehensive Calculation\nFormula:\nIRS = 100 - [(12 x 0.35) + (40 x 0.35) + (51 x 0.30)]\n= 100 - [4.2 + 14.0 + 15.3]\n= 100 - 33.5\n= 66.5\nRounded: IRS = 67\n3.5.3 Implementation Readiness Interpretation Framework\nScore Range Classification Readiness Profile Recommended Action\n76-100 High Readiness Organization ready; vendor Proceed with standard\ncapable; integration implementation timeline\nstraightforward\n51-75 Moderate Readiness Some gaps exist; Proceed with gap mitigation\nmanageable with planning plan; extend timeline 20-30%\n26-50 Low Readiness Significant gaps; risk of Proceed with caution; extend\nfailure if not addressed timeline 50-100%; pilot first\n0-25 Readiness Review Major gaps across Do not proceed until critical\nRequired dimensions; additional gaps are resolved; reassess\npreparation needed after remediation\n3.5.4 HealthFirst Insurance Implementation Assessment Example\nComplete Score Breakdown:\nVendor Risk: 12\nBase Vendor Risk: 9 x 0.35 = 3.15\nMaturity Gap: 35 x 0.25 = 8.75\nCertification Gap: 0 x 0.20 = 0\nReference Check: 0 x 0.10 = 0\nFinancial Stability: 0 x 0.10 = 0\nOrganizational Readiness Gap: 40\nAI Governance Gap: 48 x 0.25 = 12\nData Governance Gap: 29 x 0.20 = 5.8\nSkills Gap: 53 x 0.25 = 13.25\nChange Management: 21 x 0.15 = 3.15\nBudget Adequacy: 39 x 0.15 = 5.85\nIntegration Risk: 51\nTechnical Integration: 25 x 0.15 = 3.75\nFeature Gap: 63 x 0.25 = 15.75\nData Interoperability: 67 x 0.20 = 13.4\nVendor Lock-in: 42 x 0.15 = 6.3	[0.0036059462,0.060160823,0.036221907,-0.017726397,0.056942143,0.026498143,0.06629769,-0.011499458,-0.026555484,-0.021031987,-0.055746406,-0.022766443,-0.037277564,-0.0054205977,-0.048028503,-0.007720086,-0.023809042,-0.0247422