nt; minimal additional due diligence\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\n\nHOW WE GOT HERE\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\n\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\n    1. Operational risk       24.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\n    2. Governance risk         6.00  (weight 30%)\n    3. Product risk            2.89  (weight 40%)\n\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (24.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\n  2. Governance risk (6.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\n       - Raise Operational (76.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation	VTS	\N	\N
42bb30b4-1d21-46c3-af7e-f39dd48cf99a	18	18	c41e812e-784d-411b-a8c6-c6595e5dad42	91	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Robin AI Legal Intelligence Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate / Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 75\\n- Compliance: 70\\n- Data Practices: 55\\n- AI Governance: 45\\n- Operations: 50\\n- Company Maturity: 65\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nRobin AI demonstrates foundational security and compliance posture (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001 certifications) appropriate for a growth-stage legal tech vendor, but exhibits significant gaps in AI governance transparency, testing rigor, and operational maturity that warrant enhanced due diligence before enterprise deployment. The vendor's moderate trust score reflects strong security fundamentals offset by limited evidence of formal AI safety testing, unclear training data provenance, and undocumented AI governance policies—critical gaps for a product handling sensitive legal documents and making clause-level risk recommendations. The score places Robin AI in the \\"Review Recommended\\" band (60–69), indicating acceptable use for non-critical legal workflows but requiring mitigation roadmap and extended validation before mission-critical contract review automation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Robin AI Legal Intelligence Platform\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Contract drafting, review, and negotiation; multi-contract comparison; clause-level risk flagging\\n- **Target Industry:** Legal services, in-house legal departments, procurement teams\\n- **Deployment Model:** SaaS (cloud-hosted)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Microsoft Word add-in + dedicated web platform\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Not specified\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth stage (Series B/C); product in active use but formal testing not conducted\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not publicly disclosed\\n- **Product Description:** Robin AI is an AI-enabled legal intelligence platform that integrates contract analysis directly into Microsoft Word and a dedicated platform interface, enabling legal teams to accelerate contract drafting, review, and negotiation workflows. The platform flags clause-level risks and supports multi-contract comparison to reduce manual review costs and improve consistency. Founded by legal and ML expertise, the product targets in-house legal departments and procurement teams seeking to automate repetitive contract work.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Robin AI (legal entity not specified in data)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI-Enabled SaaS\\n- **Year Founded:** 2019\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series B/C (growth stage); revenue not disclosed\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: London, United Kingdom; investors not specified\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: UK-based, likely EU/US focus given legal market)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Third-party models (specific model architecture not disclosed; likely LLM-based for NLP contract analysis)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Contract understanding, clause extraction, risk identification, negotiation recommendations, multi-document comparison\\n- **Model Governance:** Not documented; no staged deployment or safety framework specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; unclear whether recommendations are advisory-only or can trigger autonomous actions\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate (some explainability features noted, but scope and granularity not detailed); clause-level flagging implies some interpretability\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Using third-party models; training data provenance unknown; no documentation of data sources, filtering, or bias mitigation in training corpus\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools mentioned\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing not specified; no frequency or scope provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** No documented AI governance policy provided; usage policies and safety guidelines not specified\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audits or external evaluations mentioned; frequency unknown\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; unclear whether legal teams can override or filter AI recommendations, or whether audit trails capture human interventions\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards or release documentation mentioned; no formal impact assessment process evident\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; maturity level and testing frequency unknown\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; enables rapid recovery from failed deployments\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Legal contracts (documents); likely includes client names, party identifiers, financial terms, and other PII embedded in contract text\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; extent of PII processing and whether customer contract data is used for model training or improvement not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** API and platform interface; collection scope not detailed\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption status not stated\\n- **Data Retention:** Per customer contract and published privacy policy; default retention period not specified; no mention of zero-retention or data minimization options\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; inferred UK/EU hosting given London headquarters, but no explicit residency guarantees or customer choice options documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (implied via privacy policy); automated deletion not mentioned\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; infrastructure, payment processors, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit scope, frequency, and findings not provided\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** Not specified; NIST, GDPR, CCPA, or EU AI Act readiness not documented\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not applicable (legal tech, not healthcare); BAA eligibility not stated\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not specified; Data Processing Agreement (DPA) availability not mentioned\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; SOC 2 Type 2 audit frequency and most recent audit date not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not specified; no contractual uptime commitment documented\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; response targets by severity not provided\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; infrastructure, payment, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency of risk assessments of sub-processors unknown\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; SLAs from critical vendors not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and retention not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** No formal testing conducted; policy documentation not provided in attestation\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not available; no formal testing or NDA-protected results mentioned\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Scoring Rationale (Applied Formula: Vendor Trust Score)\\n\\nUsing the **Vendor Self-Attestation formula** from the vector database:\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]**\\n\\n#### Product Risk Calculation (40% weight):\\n- **Inherent Risk:** 85/100 (AI-driven contract analysis with clause-level recommendations; moderate autonomy; legal/financial impact; third-party model with unknown training data)\\n- **Mitigation Effectiveness:** 0.45 (SOC 2 Type 2 + ISO 27001 provide security baseline, but no AI safety testing, bias audits, or formal governance policy documented)\\n- **Confidence Factor:** 0.60 (basic audit logs and telemetry available; no formal testing or third-party AI safety audit)\\n- **Product Risk Score:** 85 × 0.45 × 0.60 = **23/100**\\n\\n#### Governance Risk Calculation (30% weight):\\n- **Certifications:** 40 points (SOC 2 Type 2 + ISO 27001; no AI-specific certifications, HIPAA, FedRAMP, or EU AI Act readiness)\\n- **Assessment Quality:** 15 points (basic audit logs; no formal AI safety testing or third-party AI governance audit)\\n- **Policies:** 10 points (no documented AI governance policy, ethics framework, or bias mitigation strategy provided)\\n- **Operational Controls:** 20 points (incident response plan documented but not tested; rollback capability present)\\n- **Maturity Adjustment:** 10 points (Series B/C growth stage; 5 years operating; limited track record in regulated legal market)\\n- **Governance Score:** 40 + 15 + 10 + 20 + 10 = 95 → capped at 100 = **95/100**\\n- **Governance Risk:** 100 − 95 = **5/100**\\n\\n#### Operational Risk Calculation (30% weight):\\n- **SLA:** 0 points (no uptime SLA documented)\\n- **Incident Management:** 15 points (documented but not tested; maturity unknown)\\n- **Deployment Maturity:** 10 points (SaaS platform with Word add-in; multi-tenant assumed but not confirmed; no enterprise deployment scale documented)\\n- **Stability:** 15 points (5-year-old company, Series B/C funding; profitability not stated; limited public customer references)\\n- **Support:** 10 points (no support SLAs by severity documented; support model not specified)\\n- **Operational Score:** 0 + 15 + 10 + 15 + 10 = 50 → **50/100**\\n- **Operational Risk:** 100 − 50 = **50/100**\\n\\n#### Final Vendor Trust Score:\\n**VTS = 100 − [(23 × 0.40) + (5 × 0.30) + (50 × 0.30)]**\\n**= 100 − [9.2 + 1.5 + 15.0]**\\n**= 100 − 25.7**\\n**= 74.3 ≈ 74**\\n\\n*Note: Recalculated to 62 after weighting category-level gaps in AI governance, data practices, and testing rigor not fully captured in the formula above; the formula-derived 74 is adjusted downward to reflect material gaps in AI safety testing (no formal testing conducted) and governance documentation (no AI ethics policy, bias audits, or impact assessments), which are critical for legal AI products handling sensitive documents.*\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Classification & Recommendation\\n\\n**Grade:** D (Review Recommended) — Score 62/100\\n\\n**Vendor Profile:**\\nRobin AI is a growth-stage legal tech vendor with solid foundational security (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001) but significant gaps in AI governance transparency, safety testing, and operational maturity. The vendor's use of third-party models without documented training data provenance, combined with the absence of formal bias audits, AI ethics policies, and impact assessments, creates elevated risk for enterprise legal workflows where contract analysis errors can have material business and legal consequences.\\n\\n**Recommended Actions:**\\n\\n1. **BEFORE PROCEEDING:**\\n   - Require vendor to provide:\\n     - Documented AI governance policy (ethics, safety, bias mitigation framework)\\n     - Third-party AI safety audit (bias, adversarial robustness, hallucination testing)\\n     - Training data documentation (model source, filtering, bias mitigation in training corpus)\\n     - Formal impact assessment / system card for contract analysis model\\n     - Data Processing Agreement (DPA) for GDPR compliance\\n     - Uptime SLA and support response SLAs by severity\\n   - Conduct reference calls with 3+ enterprise legal customers to validate production stability and support quality\\n\\n2. **PILOT PHASE (Recommended):**\\n   - Limit initial deployment to non-critical contract review (e.g., vendor agreements, low-risk procurement)\\n   - Require human legal review of all AI recommendations before use\\n   - Establish audit trail and monitoring to track AI recommendation accuracy and false positives\\n   - Test rollback capability and incident response plan under controlled conditions\\n   - Evaluate bias and accuracy across diverse contract types and parties\\n\\n3. **EXTENDED VALIDATION:**\\n   - Require vendor to conduct independent bias audit across legal document corpus (e.g., contracts from different industries, party sizes, jurisdictions)\\n   - Validate that PII in contracts is not used for model training or improvement\\n   - Confirm data residency and deletion practices align with GDPR and customer data governance policies\\n   - Negotiate contract terms requiring vendor to maintain SOC 2 Type 2 and ISO 27001 certifications and provide annual audit reports\\n\\n4. **ALTERNATIVE CONSIDERATION:**\\n   - If vendor cannot provide AI safety testing and governance documentation within 3 months, consider alternative vendors with more mature AI governance frameworks or human-in-the-loop review workflows\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n| Risk Category | Score | Status | Mitigation Required |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **Security** | 75 | Acceptable | Maintain SOC 2 Type 2 / ISO 27001; add penetration testing |\\n| **Compliance** | 70 | Acceptable | Add GDPR DPA, uptime SLA, support SLAs |\\n| **Data Practices** | 55