k drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\n    1. Operational risk       21.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\n    2. Governance risk         6.00  (weight 30%)\n    3. Product risk            2.60  (weight 40%)\n\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (21.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\n  2. Governance risk (6.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\n       - Raise Operational (79.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation	VTS	\N	\N
0d248988-792a-41e3-93e9-09b3c813b4ea	23	23	0142d25c-0243-46da-baa2-d85df27a03a2	92	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Cresta Agent Intelligence Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 72 (Acceptable Vendor)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 78\\n- Compliance: 82\\n- Data Practices: 68\\n- AI Governance: 55\\n- Operations: 65\\n- Company Maturity: 80\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nCresta demonstrates solid compliance posture and company maturity (Series D+, profitable, 201–1,000 employees, established 2017), supported by comprehensive certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001/27701/42001, HIPAA, GDPR, PCI DSS, TISAX). However, the trust score is constrained by significant gaps in AI governance transparency—no documented AI governance policy, no training data documentation, no formal bias testing or adversarial security testing results, and no disclosed human oversight mechanisms. Data practices are moderate: while data retention follows customer contracts and GDPR/HIPAA frameworks are in place, specific data residency options, PII handling detail, and sub-processor transparency are not specified. Operations lack published uptime SLAs and support response targets, and incident response is documented but untested. The product's proprietary/closed model transparency and lack of formal safety testing further limit confidence in residual AI risk mitigation. Strengths include strong certifications, established company stability, and real-world customer ROI evidence (Brinks Home case study). Recommendation: Acceptable for deployment with enhanced due diligence on AI governance, bias testing, and incident response validation before production use.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Cresta Agent Intelligence Platform\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Real-time agent assist, autonomous voice/virtual agents, conversation intelligence, automated QA/quality management, agent coaching\\n- **Target Industry:** Contact centers (financial services, insurance, healthcare, e-commerce, telecommunications)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (SaaS)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; on-premises deployment options not specified\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise contact centers; typical customer base includes mid-market to large enterprises\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Established product (Series D+, 3+ years profitable); no formal testing results disclosed\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (customer count not disclosed); case study: Brinks Home (50% QM cost reduction, FCR to 75%, NPS +30, transfers 30%→8%)\\n- **Product Description:** Cresta is a generative-AI platform purpose-built for contact centers that unifies human and AI agents through real-time agent assist, autonomous agents, conversation intelligence, and automated QA on a single no-code interface. The platform combines proprietary models tuned for contact-center conversations with business-user-friendly tooling. Security and compliance are supported via SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001/27701/42001, HIPAA, GDPR, PCI DSS, and TISAX certifications.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Cresta (legal entity name not specified)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Platform Provider\\n- **Year Founded:** 2017\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: Sunnyvale, California, USA; investors not specified\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: US-based with likely global reach given GDPR/HIPAA certifications)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Generative AI models; proprietary, contact-center-tuned; specific model architecture (LLM, custom-trained, etc.) not disclosed\\n- **Model Purpose:** Agent assist (real-time guidance), autonomous agent operation (voice/virtual), conversation intelligence (analysis and insights), automated quality assurance\\n- **Model Governance:** No documented AI governance policy provided; governance framework not transparent\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; human-in-the-loop mechanisms, admin controls, and intervention workflows not disclosed\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Proprietary/closed model; no prompt-level control, citation, or explainability documentation provided\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach documented; red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools not specified\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency and scope unknown\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** No documented AI ethics or usage policy provided; safety guidelines not disclosed\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audit frequency or external evaluation process specified\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Human oversight mechanisms not documented; admin controls, content filtering, and intervention workflows not specified\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model cards, or release impact documentation provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented; however, plan has not been tested in production scenarios\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated rollback with manual trigger option available; rollback testing and readiness practices not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Contact center conversations (voice and text), agent interactions, customer communications, quality assurance data\\n- **PII Handling:** Extent of PII processing and handling procedures not specified; customer data use for model training/improvement not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** API and platform interface; retention options per customer contract\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption standards (in-transit, at-rest) not disclosed\\n- **Data Retention:** Per customer contract and published privacy policy; specific default retention periods not specified\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Data residency options (US, EU, customer choice) not specified; likely US-based with GDPR compliance mechanisms\\n- **Data Deletion:** On-request deletion implied by GDPR compliance; automated deletion policies not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 27701, ISO 42001, PCI DSS, HIPAA, GDPR, TISAX\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit methodology and scope not disclosed\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** HIPAA, GDPR, PCI DSS, TISAX compliance claimed; NIST and EU AI Act readiness not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** HIPAA certification held; BAA eligibility implied but not explicitly confirmed\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** GDPR certification held; Data Processing Agreement (DPA) availability not specified\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; engagement or preparation status unknown\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Audit frequency not specified; last audit date and findings not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not specified; contractual uptime commitment not published\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; response targets by severity not disclosed\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Release process, frequency, and rollback readiness practices not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not identified\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Vendor risk assessment frequency and methodology not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Key vendor SLAs not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and retention not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** No formal testing results, AI governance policies, or safety documentation provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; NDA-protected results not available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT NOTES & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Strengths\\n1. **Compliance & Certifications:** Comprehensive security and compliance certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001/27701/42001, HIPAA, GDPR, PCI DSS, TISAX) demonstrate commitment to enterprise standards.\\n2. **Company Maturity:** Established vendor (founded 2017, Series D+, profitable 3+ years, 201–1,000 employees) with proven market presence and customer ROI evidence.\\n3. **Incident Response & Rollback:** Documented incident response plan and automated rollback capability reduce operational risk.\\n4. **Audit Logging:** Basic audit logs and interaction telemetry available for compliance and monitoring.\\n\\n### Critical Gaps\\n1. **AI Governance & Transparency:** No documented AI governance policy, training data documentation, bias testing, or adversarial security testing. Proprietary/closed model limits transparency and residual risk assessment.\\n2. **Safety Testing:** No formal AI safety testing conducted; no model cards, system cards, or impact assessments provided.\\n3. **Human Oversight:** No disclosure of human-in-the-loop mechanisms, admin controls, or intervention workflows for autonomous agent operation.\\n4. **Operations Transparency:** Uptime SLA, support response SLAs, and release cadence not published; limits operational risk assessment.\\n5. **Data Practices Detail:** Data residency options, PII handling procedures, sub-processor transparency, and training data use policies not specified.\\n\\n### Recommended Actions Before Production Deployment\\n1. **Require AI Governance Documentation:** Request documented AI governance policy, training data curation practices, and bias testing methodology.\\n2. **Conduct Bias & Safety Audit:** Commission third-party bias audit and adversarial security testing; require results before production use.\\n3. **Validate Incident Response:** Test documented incident response plan in staging environment; confirm rollback procedures.\\n4. **Clarify Data Practices:** Obtain written confirmation of data residency options, PII handling, sub-processor list, and training data use restrictions.\\n5. **Publish Operational SLAs:** Request uptime SLA, support response SLAs (P1/P2/P3), and release cadence documentation.\\n6. **Human Oversight Specification:** Document human-in-the-loop controls, escalation workflows, and admin intervention capabilities for autonomous agents.\\n\\n### Classification\\n**Grade: C (Acceptable Vendor)** — Moderate capabilities with governance and transparency gaps. Suitable for deployment with enhanced due diligence and contractual mitigation of AI governance and safety testing gaps. Recommend pilot phase with dedicated monitoring before full-scale production rollout.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Cresta Agent Intelligence Platform", "Customer Base": "Not specified (customer count not disclosed); case study: Brinks Home (50% QM cost reduction, FCR to 75%, NPS +30, transfers 30%→8%)", "Target Industry": "Contact centers (financial services, insurance, healthcare, e-commerce, telecommunications)", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted (SaaS)", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise contact centers; typical customer base includes mid-market to large enterprises", "Primary Us