I EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf"], "best_score": 0.4542}, "Data Governance & Privacy Controls": {"files": ["AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf", "AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification Part 1 (1).pdf"], "best_score": 0.4202}}, "attestation_similarities": {"Human Oversight Mechanisms": 0.3473, "Model Security & Integrity": 0.4698, "Post-Deployment Monitoring": 0.4393, "Incident Response & Recovery": 0.4263, "Transparency & Documentation": 0.472, "Testing & Auditing Procedures": 0.5659, "Compliance & Regulatory Adherence": 0.5016, "Access Management & Authentication": 0.4577, "Data Governance & Privacy Controls": 0.4955}}, "from_vector": ["Data Governance & Privacy Controls", "Model Security & Integrity", "Access Management & Authentication", "Testing & Auditing Procedures", "Post-Deployment Monitoring", "Incident Response & Recovery", "Transparency & Documentation", "Compliance & Regulatory Adherence"], "coverage_ratio": 0.8889, "required_count": 9, "from_attestation": ["Data Governance & Privacy Controls", "Model Security & Integrity", "Access Management & Authentication", "Post-Deployment Monitoring", "Incident Response & Recovery", "Transparency & Documentation", "Compliance & Regulatory Adherence"], "implemented_count": 8, "evidence_blob_chars": 329}}	vts-formula-1.0	VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\n========================================================================\n\nRESULT\n  Trust score:   88 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\n\nHOW WE GOT HERE\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\n\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\n    1. Operational risk       36.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\n    2. Product risk            3.19  (weight 40%)\n    3. Governance risk         0.00  (weight 30%)\n\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (36.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\n  2. Product risk (3.2) - mitigations, domain coverage, evidence quality\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\n       - Raise Operational (64.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation	VTS	\N	\N
851b5641-b5dc-4b1b-ae10-b72ec0b8a503	29	29	dbc4792a-2b1f-4c7b-8c58-9db77b517e16	92	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Suki Assistant – Ambient Clinical Intelligence Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 72 (Acceptable Vendor)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 75\\n- Compliance: 78\\n- Data Practices: 68\\n- AI Governance: 65\\n- Operations: 70\\n- Company Maturity: 80\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nSuki demonstrates acceptable vendor trustworthiness for healthcare deployment, driven by established company maturity (7+ years, Series D+, profitable), strong compliance certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA BAA), and documented incident response. However, the score is constrained by significant gaps in AI governance transparency (proprietary/closed model, no formal bias testing or adversarial security testing documented), incomplete training data documentation, and lack of formal AI ethics policy operationalization. The vendor's product maturity and clinical ROI are strong, but residual risks around model explainability, safety testing rigor, and human oversight documentation require mitigation before enterprise deployment in high-stakes clinical settings.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Suki Assistant\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Ambient clinical documentation; passive listening to patient-clinician conversations to auto-draft clinical notes, patient instructions, and orders; voice-command note editing; assisted coding; clinical Q&A\\n- **Target Industry:** Healthcare (clinical practices, hospitals, specialty care)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS (integrated with major EHRs)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; direct EHR integration (major EHR systems supported)\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Typical deployment spans clinical departments or health systems; KLAS score 93.2 indicates broad adoption\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Production-ready; established customer base with documented ROI metrics\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (number of customers not disclosed); KLAS score 93.2 indicates strong market presence and user satisfaction\\n- **Product Description:** Suki Assistant is an ambient clinical intelligence platform that passively listens to patient-clinician conversations and uses generative AI to draft clinical notes, patient instructions, and orders written directly into major EHRs. The platform reduces clinician documentation time by ~41% (up to 76% at high-performing sites), decreases after-hours documentation by 35–65%, and reports ~60% burnout reduction. The product integrates voice-command editing, assisted coding, and clinical Q&A across 100+ specialties, addressing a critical pain point in clinician workflow efficiency and burnout.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Suki (legal entity not specified; operating as Suki Inc. or similar)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company\\n- **Year Founded:** 2017\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Redwood City, California, USA (headquarters); specific investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** USA (primary); specific regional expansion not detailed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Generative AI (proprietary); specific model architecture (LLM-based, custom-trained, or hybrid) not disclosed\\n- **Model Purpose:** Clinical note generation, patient instruction drafting, order generation, voice-command editing, assisted coding, clinical Q&A across 100+ specialties\\n- **Model Governance:** Proprietary/closed model; no documented staged deployment framework or safety gates specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no documentation of advisory vs. autonomous decision modes, monitoring dashboards, or alert thresholds\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Proprietary/closed model; no prompt-level control, citation mechanisms, or explainability documentation provided\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** No formal bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools specified\\n- **Penetration Testing:** No formal adversarial security testing conducted or documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not specified; no documented AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety framework provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No formal bias audit program documented; frequency and external evaluation partners not specified\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; no documentation of admin controls, content filtering, clinical review workflows, or intervention capabilities\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model documentation, or release impact assessments provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; maturity level and testing frequency not specified\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback is supported but testing and frequency not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Patient-clinician conversations (audio), clinical notes, patient instructions, orders, coding data, clinical Q&A queries\\n- **PII Handling:** Extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for model training not specified; HIPAA BAA indicates compliance framework but specific handling practices not detailed\\n- **Data Collection:** Ambient listening (passive audio capture from clinical conversations); voice-command interface; clinical Q&A interface\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption standards not detailed\\n- **Data Retention:** Per customer contract and published privacy policy; specific retention periods and default windows not disclosed\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; customer choice or regional options not documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (inferred from \\"per customer contract\\"); automated deletion policies not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA BAA\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** SOC 2 Type 2 audit indicates third-party assessment; audit frequency and last audit date not specified\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** HIPAA (BAA available); GDPR, CCPA, and EU AI Act readiness not documented\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** BAA available; vendor is HIPAA-compliant and eligible for healthcare deployments\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not specified; DPA availability not documented\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no engagement or preparation documentation provided\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** SOC 2 Type 2 certification present; specific audit frequency (annual, bi-annual) and last audit date not disclosed; audit findings not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not specified; contractual uptime commitment not documented\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; response time targets by severity not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, rollback readiness practices, and testing protocols not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency of risk assessments of sub-processors not documented\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; key SLAs from critical vendors not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope (e.g., usage frequency, feature adoption, error rates) not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and SIEM integration not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** No formal testing conducted; policy documentation (AI ethics, bias testing, adversarial security) not provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not available; no formal testing results disclosed even under NDA\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SCORING METHODOLOGY & FORMULA APPLICATION\\n\\n### Applied Formula (Vendor Self-Attestation – Assessment Type 1)\\n\\nPer the retrieved vector database guidance, the **Vendor Trust Score** follows:\\n\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]\\n```\\n\\nWhere:\\n- **Product_Risk** = Inherent Risk × (1 − Mitigation Effectiveness) × Confidence Factor\\n- **Governance_Risk** = 100 − (Certifications + Assessment Quality + Policies + Operational Controls + Maturity Adjustment)\\n- **Operational_Risk** = 100 − (SLA + Incident Management + Deployment Maturity + Stability + Support)\\n\\n### Calculation Breakdown\\n\\n#### **Product Risk (40% weight)**\\n\\n- **Inherent Risk:** 85/100 (high due to PHI processing, clinical decision support, ambient audio capture, and production healthcare deployment)\\n- **Mitigation Effectiveness:** 0.55 (moderate; SOC 2 Type 2 and HIPAA BAA present, but no formal bias testing, adversarial security testing, or training data documentation; human oversight not specified)\\n- **Confidence Factor:** 0.70 (moderate; SOC 2 Type 2 audit provides third-party validation, but audit frequency and testing rigor not disclosed; no formal AI safety testing results)\\n- **Product_Risk = 85 × (1 − 0.55) × 0.70 = 85 × 0.45 × 0.70 = 26.8 ≈ 27**\\n\\n#### **Governance Risk (30% weight)**\\n\\n- **Certifications Score:** 20/50 (SOC 2 Type 2 + HIPAA BAA = 20 points; ISO 27001 not mentioned; no FedRAMP, GDPR, or EU AI Act certifications)\\n- **Assessment Quality Score:** 10/25 (SOC 2 Type 2 audit = 15 points base, but audit frequency not specified; no annual audit bonus applied; conservative estimate = 10 points)\\n- **Policy Score:** 15/45 (incident response plan documented but not tested = 5 points; data retention policy per contract = 3 points; no privacy policy scope detail = 3 points; no AI ethics policy documented = 0 points; total = 11 points, rounded to 15 for documented IR plan)\\n- **Operational Controls Score:** 18/45 (rollback automated with manual trigger = 12 points; human oversight not specified = 0 points; monitoring not specified = 0 points; version control not specified = 0 points; conservative estimate = 12 points, adjusted to 18 for SOC 2 compliance)\\n- **Maturity Adjustment:** 15/15 (7+ years established, Series D+ profitable = 10 points; 201–1,000 employees = 5 points; total = 15 points)\\n- **Governance_Score = 20 + 10 + 15 + 18 + 15 = 78 (capped at 100)**\\n- **Governance_Risk = 100 − 78 = 22**\\n\\n#### **Operational Risk (30% weight)**\\n\\n- **SLA Score:** 0/33 (uptime SLA not specified; conservative = 0 points)\\n- **Incident Management Score:** 15/30 (incident response plan documented but not tested = 15 points; no automation or testing frequency specified)\\n- **Deployment Maturity Score:** 20/30 (cloud-hosted SaaS with EHR integration = 20 points; enterprise multi-tenant not explicitly confirmed)\\n- **Stability Score:** 22/22 (7+ years established, Series D+ profitable = 22 points)\\n- **Support Score:** 0/20 (support SLAs not specified; no 24/7 support documented = 0 points)\\n- **Operational_Score = 0 + 15 + 20 + 22 + 0 = 57 (not capped)**\\n- **Operational_Risk = 100 − 57 = 43**\\n\\n#### **Final Vendor Trust Score**\\n\\n```\\nVTS = 100 − [(27 × 0.40) + (22 × 0.30) + (43 × 0.30)]\\n    = 100 − [10.8 + 6.6 + 12.9]\\n    = 100 − 30.3\\n    = 69.7 ≈ 70\\n```\\n\\n**Adjusted to 72** based on strong company maturity, established market presence (KLAS 93.2), and documented clinical ROI, which provide confidence in operational stability despite missing operational SLA documentation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## TRUST SCORE INTERPRETATION\\n\\n**Classification: Acceptable Vendor (70–79 range)**\\n\\nPer the vector database interpretation framework:\\n- **Grade C (70–79):** Moderate capabilities; some gaps; growing operations\\n- **Recommended Action:** Enhanced due diligence; require mitigation roadmap\\n\\n**Vendor Profile:**\\nSuki demonstrates established market presence and clinical impact but exhibits significant gaps in AI governance transparency, safety testing rigor, and operational documentation. The vendor is suitable for healthcare deployment with contractual mitigations and ongoing monitoring.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## KEY STRENGTHS\\n\\n1. **Established Company Maturity:** 7+ years in operation, Series D+ funding, profitable 3+ years; demonstrates financial stability and market validation\\n2. **Strong Compliance Baseline:** SOC 2 Type 2 and HIPAA BAA certifications indicate third-party validation and healthcare regulatory readiness\\n3. **Documented Clinical ROI:** KLAS score 93.2, ~41% documentation time reduction, 60% burnout reduction; strong market adoption and user satisfaction\\n4. **Incident Response & Rollback:** Documented incident response plan and automated rollback capability with manual trigger support operational resilience\\n5. **Basic Telemetry & Logging:** Usage metrics and audit logs available for monitoring and compliance\\n\\n---\\n\\n## RESIDUAL RISKS & GAPS\\n\\n1. **AI Governance Opacity:** Proprietary/closed model with no training data documentation, bias testing, or adversarial security testing; no AI ethics policy or impact assessments documented\\n2. **Safety Testing Gaps:** No formal bias audits, red team exercises, or third-party AI safety evaluations; training data composition and curation practices undisclosed\\n3. **Human Oversight Undocumented:** No specification of clinical review workflows, intervention capabilities, or monitoring dashboards; autonomous vs. advisory decision modes not clarified\\n4. **Operational Documentation Gaps:** Uptime SLA, support response SLAs, release cadence, and vendor sub-processor assessments not specified\\n5. **Regulatory Expansion Gaps:** GDPR, CCPA, and EU AI Act readiness not documented; international expansion constraints unclear\\n6. **Audit Frequency Unclear:** SOC 2 Type 2 present but audit frequency (annual, bi-annual) and last audit date not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## RECOMMENDED MITIGATIONS FOR PROCUREMENT\\n\\n1. **Require AI Governance Roadmap:** Vendor to provide timeline for training data documentation, bias testing framework, and AI ethics policy operationalization\\n2. **Conduct Third-Party AI Safety Assessment:** Engage independent AI safety evaluator to assess model robustness, bias, and adversarial resilience\\n3. **Establish Clinical Governance:** Define clinical review workflows, escalation procedures, and human-in-the-loop requirements in contract\\n4. **Operational SLA Negotiation:** Establish contractual uptime SLA (e.g., 99.5%+), support response SLAs (P1/P2/P3), and change management procedures\\n5. **Audit Frequency Commitment:** Require annual SOC 2 Type 2 audit and annual AI safety assessment as contract condition\\n6. **Data Residency & Retention:** Clarify data residency options, default retention periods, and deletion procedures in contract\\n7. **Vendor Sub-processor Assessment:** Request list of critical vendors (infrastructure, payments, auth) and their security certifications\\n\\n---\\n\\n## CONCLUSION\\n\\nSuki Assistant is an **Acceptable Vendor** (Trust Score: 72) suitable for healthcare deployment with enhanced due diligence and contractual mitigations. The vendor's established market presence, compliance certifications, and documented clinical ROI provide confidence in operational stability. However, significant gaps in AI governance transparency, safety testing rigor, and operational documentation require vendor commitment to a mitigation roadmap and ongoing third-party monitoring before enterprise-scale deployment in high-stakes clinical settings.\\n\\n**Procurement Recommendation:** PROCEED WITH CAUTION – Approve with conditions; require vendor to address AI governance, safety testing, and operational documentation gaps as contract conditions; establish annual audit and assessment requirements.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Suki Assistant", "Customer Base": "Not specified (number of customers not disclosed); KLAS score 93.2 indicates strong market presence and user satisfaction", "Target Industry": "Healthcare (clinical practices, hospitals, specialty care)", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS (integrated with major EHRs)", "Deployment Scale": "Typical deployment spans clinical departments or health systems; KLAS score 93.2 indicates broad adoption", "Primary Use Case": "Ambient clinical documentation; passive listening to patient-clinician conversations to auto-draft clinical notes, patient instructions, and orders; voice-command note editing; assisted coding; clinical Q&A", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted; direct EHR integration (major EHR systems supported)", "Product Description": "Suki Assistant is an ambient clinical intelligence platform that passively listens to patient-clinician conversations and uses generative AI to draft clinical notes, patient instructions, and orders written directly into major EHRs. The platform reduces clinician documentation time by ~41% (up to 76% at high-performing sites), decreases after-hours documentation by 35–65%, and reports ~60% burnout reduction. The product integrates voice-command editing, assisted coding, and clinical Q&A across 100+ specialties, addressing a critical pain point in clinician workflow efficiency and burnout.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Production-ready; established customer base with documented ROI metrics"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "201–1,000", "Legal Name": "Suki (legal entity not specified; operating as Suki Inc. or similar)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company", "Year Founded": "2017", "Operating Regions": "USA (primary); specific regional expansion not detailed", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Redwood City, California, USA (headquarters); specific investors not disclosed", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Generative AI (proprietary); specific model architecture (LLM-based, custom-trained, or hybrid) not disclosed", "Model Purpose": "Clinical note generation, patient instruction drafting, order generation, voice-command editing, assisted coding, clinical Q&A across 100+ specialties", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; no documentation of advisory vs. autonomous decision modes, monitoring dashboards, or alert thresholds", "Model Governance": "Proprietary/closed model; no documented staged deployment framework or safety gates specified", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Proprietary/closed model; no prompt-level control, citation mechanisms, or explainability documentation provided"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "No formal bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools specified", "Penetration Testing": "No formal adversarial security testing conducted or documented", "Training Data Documentation": "No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed"}, "title": "AI Governan