ion status tracking, claims processing optimization, and staff workload reduction\\n- **Model Governance:** No documented AI governance policy provided; model updates and staged deployment procedures not specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no documented human-in-the-loop controls, monitoring alerts, or clinical review workflows described\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Proprietary/closed model; no explainability documentation, prompt-level control, or citation/reasoning transparency provided\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation methodology not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** No formal bias testing approach documented; no red-team evaluations, third-party audits, or statistical bias monitoring tools described\\n- **Penetration Testing:** No adversarial security testing documented; frequency and scope not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** No documented AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety framework provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No formal bias audit program; frequency and external evaluation methodology not specified\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** No documented human oversight capabilities, admin controls, content filtering, or intervention mechanisms described\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model documentation, or release impact assessments provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Documented but **not tested**; maturity level unclear; no evidence of annual testing or tabletop exercises\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger (moderate maturity); enables rapid recovery but requires manual intervention for activation\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Healthcare documentation (clinical notes, authorization records, claims data), Epic EHR workflow data, and related revenue cycle information\\n- **PII Handling:** Processes Protected Health Information (PHI) as part of revenue cycle automation; extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for model training not specified\\n- **Data Collection:** API and workflow integration (specific collection mechanisms not detailed); retention options not clearly specified\\n- **Data Storage:** US-hosted infrastructure (specific cloud provider and encryption standards not disclosed)\\n- **Data Retention:** AKASA retains customer data for as long as necessary to fulfill collection purposes, including legal compliance and dispute resolution; no default zero-retention option documented\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** US only; no EU or customer-choice residency options available\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request and when no longer needed; procedures documented but specific timelines and verification mechanisms not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; infrastructure provider, payment processor, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** \\n  - SOC 2 Type 2 ✓\\n  - HITRUST ✓\\n  - HIPAA BAA-eligible ✓\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit scope, frequency, and most recent audit date not provided\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - HIPAA: Compliant (BAA available)\\n  - GDPR: Not specified; no DPA mentioned\\n  - CCPA: Not specified\\n  - EU AI Act: Not specified; no readiness assessment provided\\n  - NIST: Not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** BAA-eligible; SOC 2 Type 2 and HITRUST certifications support healthcare compliance posture\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not specified; no Data Processing Agreement (DPA) mentioned; US-only data residency may limit GDPR applicability\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; SOC 2 Type 2 and HITRUST certifications imply periodic audits, but frequency and findings not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime allowance)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support tiers and response targets not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, change notification procedures, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; infrastructure provider, payment processor, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency of third-party vendor risk assessments not documented\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; SLAs from critical infrastructure and service vendors not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; specific telemetry scope (e.g., claims processed, accuracy rates, latency) not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and SIEM integration not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited; no formal testing results, policy documentation, or model safety validation provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected safety or performance validation available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Compliance Foundation:** SOC 2 Type 2, HITRUST, and HIPAA BAA demonstrate commitment to healthcare regulatory requirements.\\n2. **Operational Stability:** 99.9% SLA and automated rollback capability provide operational reliability for production healthcare environments.\\n3. **Proven Customer Impact:** Corroborated case study (22% reduction in work queue volume, 13% A/R improvement, 300+ staff hours saved) demonstrates tangible business value.\\n4. **Data Governance Basics:** Documented data retention and deletion procedures; US-only residency simplifies compliance for domestic healthcare organizations.\\n\\n### Critical Gaps (Mitigation Required Before Production Deployment)\\n1. **AI Governance Vacuum:** No documented AI ethics policy, bias testing, or explainability framework—critical for healthcare AI systems handling clinical workflows.\\n2. **Untested Incident Response:** Incident response plan exists but has **never been tested**; no evidence of annual tabletop exercises or readiness validation.\\n3. **Training Data Opacity:** No documentation of training data sources, composition, or curation; raises concerns about model bias and generalization to diverse healthcare settings.\\n4. **No Formal Safety Validation:** Absence of third-party testing, adversarial security testing, or formal bias audits; reliance on proprietary/closed model limits transparency.\\n5. **Limited Explainability:** Closed model architecture with no documented explainability, citations, or reasoning transparency—problematic for clinical decision support and compliance audits.\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**APPROVED FOR PROCUREMENT WITH MANDATORY MITIGATION CONDITIONS**\\n\\nAKASA is acceptable for healthcare revenue cycle automation use cases **conditional on the following contract requirements:**\\n\\n1. **Pre-Deployment Bias Audit:** Vendor must commission independent bias audit (e.g., by third-party AI safety firm) covering demographic parity, equalized odds, and calibration across patient populations and provider types. Audit must be completed and findings remediated before production go-live.\\n\\n2. **Continuous Model Drift Monitoring:** Vendor must implement and document real-time monitoring for model performance degradation (e.g., accuracy drift, output distribution shifts) with automated alerting and escalation to customer clinical teams.\\n\\n3. **Clinical Review Workflow:** Vendor must establish documented human-in-the-loop procedures requiring clinical review of high-risk automation decisions (e.g., coding changes, authorization denials) with audit trails and override capabilities.\\n\\n4. **Incident Response Testing:** Vendor must conduct and document annual incident response tabletop exercises; results and remediation plans must be shared with customer security teams.\\n\\n5. **Explainability Documentation:** Vendor must provide model card or system documentation describing model architecture, training data characteristics, known limitations, and decision factors (at appropriate level of proprietary protection).\\n\\n6. **Support SLA Definition:** Vendor must define and contractually commit to P1/P2/P3 response and resolution SLAs aligned with healthcare operational criticality.\\n\\n7. **Vendor Sub-processor Disclosure:** Vendor must disclose all critical infrastructure, payment, and authentication sub-processors and provide evidence of their SOC 2 / ISO 27001 compliance.\\n\\n### Risk Classification\\n- **Product Risk:** 45/100 (Moderate-High) — Proprietary model, no formal testing, governance gaps\\n- **Governance Risk:** 22/100 (Low-Moderate) — Strong certifications, weak policy operationalization\\n- **Operational Risk:** 30/100 (Low-Moderate) — Solid SLA, untested incident response\\n- **Overall Trust Score:** 62/100 (Moderate / Review Recommended)\\n\\n### Use Case Fit\\n✓ **Suitable for:** Regional hospitals and provider groups with mature IT/security teams, existing HIPAA compliance infrastructure, and capacity to enforce contractual mitigation conditions.\\n\\n✗ **Not suitable for:** Highly regulated health systems requiring formal model validation, EU operations (no GDPR/DPA), or organizations unable to implement clinical review workflows.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Report Generated:** [Date]  \\n**Assessment Type:** Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1)  \\n**Scoring Framework:** AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification (Vector DB 