g": 0.2175, "Incident Response & Recovery": 0.1992, "Transparency & Documentation": 0.2189, "Testing & Auditing Procedures": 0.242, "Compliance & Regulatory Adherence": 0.2139, "Access Management & Authentication": 0.2238, "Data Governance & Privacy Controls": 0.2836}}, "from_vector": ["Testing & Auditing Procedures", "Transparency & Documentation", "Compliance & Regulatory Adherence"], "coverage_ratio": 0.7778, "required_count": 9, "from_attestation": ["Data Governance & Privacy Controls", "Access Management & Authentication", "Post-Deployment Monitoring", "Incident Response & Recovery", "Transparency & Documentation", "Compliance & Regulatory Adherence"], "implemented_count": 7, "evidence_blob_chars": 525}}	vts-formula-1.0	VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\n========================================================================\n\nRESULT\n  Trust score:   86 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\n\nHOW WE GOT HERE\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\n\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\n    1. Operational risk       34.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\n    2. Governance risk         6.00  (weight 30%)\n    3. Product risk            4.43  (weight 40%)\n\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (34.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\n  2. Governance risk (6.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\n       - Raise Operational (66.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - 