315d2758-a9c0-4dcb-98a7-55ae124f7add	35	35	8be3ada3-f7dc-4dbd-8b89-b8a7c798868d	87	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Aidoc aiOS Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 72 (Acceptable Vendor)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 68\\n- Compliance: 75\\n- Data Practices: 70\\n- AI Governance: 65\\n- Operations: 78\\n- Company Maturity: 80\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nAidoc demonstrates solid operational maturity (established company, profitable 3+ years, 201–1,000 employees) and acceptable compliance posture (NIST CSF certification, GDPR-aligned data retention, annual incident response testing). However, the vendor exhibits moderate gaps in AI governance transparency, bias testing documentation, and explainability—critical for healthcare PHI handling. Applying the vector-DB formula *VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]*, estimated Product Risk is ~45 (moderate inherent risk from healthcare AI + PHI, partially mitigated by FDA clearance and CARE™ foundation model but limited bias/adversarial testing evidence), Governance Risk is ~35 (documented incident response and NIST CSF, but incomplete AI ethics policy and bias audit documentation), and Operational Risk is ~22 (strong 99.95% SLA, automated rollback, annual testing). This yields VTS ≈ 72, placing Aidoc in the \\"Acceptable Vendor\\" band (70–79). **Main strengths:** FDA-cleared clinical AI, established company, strong uptime SLA, GDPR-aligned data practices. **Main gaps:** Limited transparency on bias testing methodology, incomplete AI governance policy documentation, no evidence of third-party adversarial security testing or comprehensive model cards.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** aiOS\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Clinical AI triage, anomaly detection in medical imaging, care pathway activation, diagnostic support in hospital workflows\\n- **Target Industry:** Healthcare (hospitals, imaging centers, diagnostic facilities)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (infrastructure details not specified)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; on-premises deployment options not specified\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise hospital networks; typical deployment supports large volumes of medical imaging data\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** FDA-cleared product; clinical validation documented\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (customer count and deployment metrics not provided)\\n- **Product Description:** aiOS is an FDA-cleared AI platform designed to improve clinical workflows by detecting anomalies in medical imaging and activating care pathways. The platform integrates with existing healthcare IT infrastructure and leverages Aidoc's CARE™ AI foundation model to support multiple clinical indications. It emphasizes data protection, GDPR compliance, and international regulatory alignment to meet healthcare privacy and security standards.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Aidoc (legal entity name not specified; operating as Aidoc)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company\\n- **Year Founded:** 2016\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: New York, NY, USA; specific investors not listed\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (global operations implied by GDPR compliance and international standards alignment)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** CARE™ AI foundation model (proprietary); supports multiple clinical indications; underlying architecture (LLM, CNN, hybrid) not specified\\n- **Model Purpose:** Medical image analysis, anomaly detection, clinical decision support, care pathway activation\\n- **Model Governance:** FDA-cleared pathway indicates regulatory validation; staged deployment approach not explicitly documented\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not fully specified; clinical workflows imply physician review, but extent of autonomous vs. advisory operation not detailed\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate (some explainability features); specific methods (saliency maps, attention visualization, confidence scores) not documented\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Partial documentation available; comprehensive training data provenance, labeling methodology, and demographic balance not detailed\\n- **Bias Detection:** Not specified; no evidence of systematic red-teaming, third-party bias audits, or statistical fairness monitoring provided\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Not specified; no evidence of adversarial security testing or frequency documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Documented AI governance policy not explicitly provided; clinical safety and regulatory compliance implied but formal ethics framework not detailed\\n- **Bias Audits:** Not specified; no documented frequency or external bias evaluation process provided\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Clinical workflows imply physician oversight; specific admin controls, content filtering, or intervention mechanisms not documented\\n- **Impact Assessment:** FDA clearance indicates clinical validation; system cards or detailed release documentation not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, tested annually; formal incident response plan in place with annual testing cadence\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback procedures documented and operationalized\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Medical imaging data (DICOM, proprietary formats); patient identifiers and clinical metadata\\n- **PII Handling:** Extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for model training not specified; healthcare context implies PHI handling\\n- **Data Collection:** API and clinical workflow integration; retention options not detailed\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption standards (at-rest, in-transit) not documented\\n- **Data Retention:** Aidoc retains customer data for the duration necessary to fulfill stated purposes, with deletion procedures aligned with GDPR and other regulatory requirements; zero-retention option not specified\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; GDPR compliance implies EU data residency options, but customer choice not documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** Procedures aligned with GDPR; on-request deletion implied; automated deletion schedule not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure provider, payments, and authentication vendors not identified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** NIST CSF (NIST Cybersecurity Framework); SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA BAA, or FedRAMP status not specified\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit methodology (third-party, internal, frequency) not documented\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** NIST CSF alignment confirmed; GDPR compliance documented; CCPA and EU AI Act readiness not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** BAA eligibility not explicitly stated; healthcare data handling implies HIPAA applicability, but formal BAA availability not confirmed\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Data retention and deletion procedures aligned with GDPR; DPA (Data Processing Agreement) availability not specified\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; engagement or preparation status not documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Incident response plan tested annually; comprehensive compliance audit frequency and last audit date not provided; audit findings not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.95% (4.4 hours/year downtime allowance); strong enterprise-grade commitment\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; severity-based response targets not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, testing procedures, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure provider, payments processor, and authentication vendors not identified\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Frequency of vendor risk assessments not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Key vendor SLAs not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; detailed telemetry scope and export capability not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; comprehensive audit log export and SIEM integration not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Basic test results available; comprehensive policy documentation, model cards, and safety testing reports not provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not specified; availability of detailed testing results under NDA not confirmed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Strengths\\n1. **Established company maturity:** Founded 2016, Series D+, profitable 3+ years, 201–1,000 employees—demonstrates stability and market validation.\\n2. **FDA clearance:** Clinical validation through regulatory pathway; significant barrier to entry and evidence of safety review.\\n3. **Strong operational SLA:** 99.95% uptime commitment (4.4 hrs/year) meets enterprise healthcare expectations.\\n4. **GDPR-aligned data practices:** Documented data retention and deletion procedures aligned with EU privacy regulations.\\n5. **Incident response discipline:** Annual testing of incident response plan indicates operational maturity.\\n6. **Automated rollback capability:** Deployment rollback with manual trigger supports rapid incident recovery.\\n\\n### Gaps & Risks\\n1. **Limited AI governance transparency:** No documented AI ethics policy, bias audit framework, or formal governance structure provided. Critical for healthcare AI.\\n2. **Incomplete bias testing:** No evidence of systematic bias detection, red-teaming, or third-party fairness audits. High-risk gap for clinical AI handling sensitive patient populations.\\n3. **Moderate explainability:** \\"Some explainability features\\" insufficient detail; no documentation of saliency maps, confidence scores, or clinical interpretability methods.\\n4. **Partial training data documentation:** Incomplete provenance, labeling methodology, and demographic balance documentation; insufficient for clinical validation transparency.\\n5. **Missing compliance certifications:** No SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, or formal HIPAA BAA confirmation; NIST CSF alone insufficient for healthcare PHI.\\n6. **No adversarial security testing:** No evidence of penetration testing or adversarial robustness validation; critical for clinical decision-support systems.\\n7. **Incomplete support SLAs:** No severity-based response targets (P1/P2/P3) documented; critical for healthcare incident response.\\n8. **Vendor sub-processor opacity:** Infrastructure, payments, and auth vendors not identified; supply chain risk not transparent.\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**CONDITIONAL APPROVAL – ENHANCED DUE DILIGENCE REQUIRED**\\n\\nAidoc demonstrates acceptable operational maturity and FDA-cleared clinical validation, placing it in the \\"Acceptable Vendor\\" (70–79) trust band. However, healthcare PHI handling and clinical decision-support criticality require enhanced governance and testing transparency before production deployment.\\n\\n**Mandatory Pre-Deployment Conditions:**\\n\\n1. **AI Governance & Bias Testing (Highest Priority)**\\n   - Provide documented AI ethics policy (board-approved, operationalized)\\n   - Deliver comprehensive bias audit report (third-party, covering demographic fairness across patient populations)\\n   - Establish continuous model drift monitoring and clinical review workflow\\n   - Document red-teaming methodology and adversarial robustness testing results\\n\\n2. **Compliance & Security Certification**\\n   - Confirm HIPAA BAA eligibility and provide executed BAA template\\n   - Obtain SOC 2 Type 2 audit (or equivalent third-party security assessment)\\n   - Provide evidence of annual penetration testing and remediation tracking\\n   - Document sub-processor list (infrastructure, payments, auth) with SLAs\\n\\n3. **Transparency & Documentation**\\n   - Provide system cards or model documentation detailing training data, performance metrics, and known limitations\\n   - Document explainability methods (saliency maps, confidence scores, clinical interpretability)\\n   - Publish support SLAs by severity (P1/P2/P3 response targets)\\n   - Provide change management and release cadence documentation\\n\\n4. **Pilot & Validation**\\n   - Conduct extended pilot (3–6 months) with clinical review workflow enabled\\n   - Validate rollback procedures in non-production environment\\n   - Establish clinical governance board with hospital stakeholders for ongoing oversight\\n\\n**Timeline:** 6–9 months for gap remediation + 3–6 month pilot = 9–15 months to production readiness.\\n\\n**Alternative:** If vendor cannot meet governance and testing requirements within 6 months, escalate to executive review and consider alternative vendors with more mature AI governance frameworks.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "aiOS", "Customer Base": "Not specified (customer count and deployment metrics not provided)", "Target Industry": "Healthcare (hospitals, imaging centers, diagnostic facilities)", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted (infrastructure details not specified)", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise hospital networks; typical deploy