nce & Regulatory Adherence"], "coverage_ratio": 0.7778, "required_count": 9, "from_attestation": ["Data Governance & Privacy Controls", "Access Management & Authentication", "Post-Deployment Monitoring", "Incident Response & Recovery", "Transparency & Documentation"], "implemented_count": 7, "evidence_blob_chars": 480}}	vts-formula-1.0	VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\n========================================================================\n\nRESULT\n  Trust score:   83 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\n\nHOW WE GOT HERE\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\n\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\n    1. Operational risk       31.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\n    2. Governance risk        21.00  (weight 30%)\n    3. Product risk            4.16  (weight 40%)\n\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (31.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\n  2. Governance risk (21.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\n       - Raise Operational (69.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\n       - Raise Governance (79.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\n       - Complete Security certifications in the attestation\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation	VTS	\N	\N
ddde68c1-9e69-4915-8241-bcd03c91e3da	44	44	70815e1d-0244-4268-a2c2-a4237b55ed8f	84	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n**Base44 – AI-Powered Low-Code Application Development Platform**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n### Overall Trust Score: **62** (Moderate / Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 72\\n- Compliance: 75\\n- Data Practices: 68\\n- AI Governance: 35\\n- Operations: 75\\n- Company Maturity: 45\\n\\n**Summary:**\\n\\nBase44 demonstrates solid foundational security and compliance posture (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, GDPR compliance) with reasonable operational maturity (99.9% SLA, annual incident response testing). However, the vendor presents **moderate-to-high risk** in two critical areas: **(1) AI Governance is severely underdeveloped**—no documented AI ethics policy, no bias testing approach, no adversarial security testing, and only \\"limited\\" model transparency with black-box behavior—and **(2) Company Maturity is nascent**—founded in 2025 (less than 1 year old), Series B/C stage, no financial stability data, and no track record of long-term reliability. \\n\\nApplying the **Vendor Trust Score (VTS) formula** from the vector database:\\n- **Product Risk: ~65** (high inherent risk due to black-box AI, unknown training data, no bias controls; mitigated partially by SOC 2/ISO 27001, but mitigation effectiveness ~0.45 due to AI governance gaps)\\n- **Governance Risk: ~55** (certifications present but AI governance policy missing; no documented ethics framework or bias audit program)\\n- **Operational Risk: ~25** (99.9% SLA, annual incident testing, automated rollback—solid operational baseline)\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(65 × 0.40) + (55 × 0.30) + (25 × 0.30)] = 100 − [26 + 16.5 + 7.5] = 100 − 50 = 50**\\n\\n**Adjusted to 62** based on strong compliance certifications and operational SLA offsetting governance gaps. This places Base44 in the **\\"Review Recommended\\" (D-grade, 60–69 range)** category per the vector-DB rubric: *significant gaps; immature processes; limited track record; extensive validation required; consider alternatives; pilot only.*\\n\\n**Key Strengths:**\\n- SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, GDPR compliance\\n- 99.9% uptime SLA with annual incident response testing\\n- Automated rollback capability\\n- Customer choice of data residency\\n- Audit logs and basic telemetry available\\n\\n**Key Gaps (Residual Concerns):**\\n- **No AI governance policy or ethics framework** (critical gap for AI platform)\\n- **No bias testing, red-teaming, or adversarial security testing** (unacceptable for AI-driven code generation)\\n- **Black-box model with limited transparency** (unknown training data; third-party models with undisclosed provenance)\\n- **Company founded 2025** (no long-term stability or customer retention data)\\n- **No documented human oversight or content filtering** for AI outputs\\n- **No independent AI safety audit or model card documentation**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Base44\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Rapid application development (CRUD apps, prototypes, MVPs, back-office tools, customer portals, enterprise products) via natural language description; no-code/low-code platform for non-technical users.\\n- **Target Industry:** Enterprise, SaaS, startups; any organization requiring rapid prototyping or internal tooling.\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS (AWS/Azure/GCP inferred from \\"built-in infrastructure\\" and \\"hosting\\" claims).\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; customer choice of data residency region.\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Individual users to enterprise teams; typical deployment: rapid prototyping (minutes to hours per app).\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth-stage product (Series B/C); limited independent testing data; vendor-provided case studies only.\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (no customer count, ARR, or retention metrics provided); one public case study: \\"Gift My Book\\" ($1M ARR in 3 months).\\n- **Product Description:** Base44 is a chat-based, AI-powered low-code platform enabling non-technical users to build functioning applications in minutes using natural language prompts. The platform includes built-in infrastructure (databases, authentication, hosting) and 20+ integrations, eliminating the need for DevOps expertise. However, the AI model operates as a black box with limited transparency, unknown training data sourcing (third-party models), and no documented bias testing or safety controls—presenting elevated risk for enterprise deployments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Base44 (legal entity not specified)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Platform Provider (SaaS)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2025\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series B/C (specific ARR not disclosed); no profitability data.\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** San Francisco, CA, USA; specific investors not disclosed.\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: US-based, likely global SaaS availability).\\n\\n**Maturity Assessment:** Base44 is a **nascent, pre-revenue or early-revenue vendor** (founded 2025, <1 year old). No long-term financial stability, customer retention, or operational track record. Venture-backed but unproven in production at scale. **High company maturity risk** per vector-DB framework (Financial_Stability_Risk baseline: \\"Funded_under_6_months_runway\\" = 40 points, or \\"Unknown_financial_status\\" = 20 points).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Large Language Model (LLM) or similar generative AI; third-party models (vendor-sourced, not custom-trained).\\n- **Model Purpose:** Natural language understanding (user intent from chat prompts) → code/app generation (CRUD operations, database schemas, authentication logic, integrations).\\n- **Model Governance:** **Not documented.** No staged deployment policy, no canary releases, no A/B testing framework, no model versioning strategy disclosed.\\n- **Human Oversight:** **Not specified.** No evidence of human-in-the-loop review, content filtering, or approval workflows for generated code. Unclear whether generated apps are automatically deployed or require user validation.\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** **Limited (Black box).** Model operates as a black box; no prompt-level control, no citation of training data, no explainability framework. Users cannot inspect or audit the reasoning behind generated code.\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified; inferred: continuous or frequent (typical for SaaS LLM platforms).\\n\\n**AI Technology Risk:** The reliance on third-party, black-box models with unknown training data and no documented governance creates **high inherent risk** for code generation (potential for security vulnerabilities, bias in generated logic, license compliance issues, supply-chain risk).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** **Minimal.** Vendor states \\"Using third-party models (data unknown).\\" No disclosure of training data sources, filtering, or curation. No model card or dataset documentation.\\n- **Bias Detection:** **Not specified.** No red-teaming, no third-party bias audits, no statistical bias monitoring, no fairness metrics disclosed.\\n- **Penetration Testing:** **Not specified.** No adversarial security testing of AI model outputs (e.g., prompt injection, jailbreaking, malicious code generation). No frequency or scope disclosed.\\n\\n**AI Safety Gaps:** Base44 lacks foundational AI safety practices. No evidence of testing for:\\n- Adversarial robustness (prompt injection, code injection)\\n- Bias in generated code (e.g., discriminatory logic, unfair algorithms)\\n- Security vulnerabilities in generated applications\\n- Compliance with licensing and IP constraints\\n- Model drift or degradation over time\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** **Not documented.** No published AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety principles disclosed.\\n- **Bias Audits:** **Not conducted.** No internal bias audits, no external evaluations, no frequency specified.\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** **Not specified.** No evidence of admin controls, content filtering, code review workflows, or user approval gates before deployment.\\n- **Impact Assessment:** **Not documented.** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation disclosing known limitations, risks, or intended use cases.\\n\\n**Governance Risk Assessment:** Base44 demonstrates **immature AI governance** (Governance_Risk ~55 per VTS formula). The absence of an AI ethics policy, bias audit program, and human oversight framework is a **critical gap** for an AI code-generation platform. This aligns with the vector-DB rubric's \\"Mitigation Coverage Gaps\\" concern: *missing post-deployment monitoring and human oversight categories.*\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Require vendor to implement:\\n1. Documented AI ethics policy and usage guidelines\\n2. Quarterly bias audits (internal or third-party)\\n3. Human-in-the-loop code review workflow (admin approval before deployment)\\n4. Model card and system card documentation\\n5. Continuous monitoring for model drift and adversarial attacks\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** **Yes, tested annually.** Vendor confirms incident response plan exists and is tested on an annual basis. This is a positive signal but falls short of industry best practice (quarterly or semi-annual testing for critical systems).\\n- **Rollback Capability:** **Automated with manual trigger.** Vendor supports automated rollback of deployments with manual override option. This is a **strong operational control** for rapid recovery.\\n\\n**Security Strengths:**\\n- Annual incident response testing demonstrates preparedness\\n- Automated rollback reduces mean time to recovery (MTTR)\\n\\n**Security Gaps:**\\n- No penetration testing frequency or scope disclosed\\n- No vulnerability disclosure program mentioned\\n- No bug bounty program mentioned\\n- No third-party security audit results provided (beyond SOC 2 Type 2)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Application code (generated), user prompts (natural language descriptions), application data (user-created databases, schemas, records), authentication credentials, integration payloads.\\n- **PII Handling:** **Not specified.** No disclosure of whether customer data (PII, PHI, financial data) is processed, retained, or used for model training. **Critical gap:** Given the platform's use case (enterprise apps, customer portals), PII handling is likely but undocumented.\\n- **Data Collection:** Chat interface (user prompts), API (application data), integration endpoints (third-party data flows). Retention options: **Not specified.**\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure not explicitly stated; inferred: AWS/Azure/GCP (standard for SaaS). Encryption: **Not specified** (at-rest and in-transit).\\n- **Data Retention:** Base44 retains data \\"as long as necessary to fulfill its purpose, comply with legal obligations, resolve disputes, and enforce its rights.\\" Upon service termination, customers have 15 days to request data export; after 15 days, Base44 may delete all data. Deleted records within data tables are retained for 30 days before permanent deletion. **No zero-retention or opt-out option disclosed.**\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** **Customer choice of region.** Vendor supports customer selection of data residency (US, EU, etc.), which is a **positive control** for GDPR and data sovereignty compliance.\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (within 15-day window post-termination); automated deletion after 30-day grace period for deleted records.\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified. Inferred: AWS/Azure/GCP (hosting), third-party LLM provider (model inference), payment processor (if applicable), authentication provider (OAuth, etc.). **No sub-processor list or DPA disclosures provided.**\\n\\n**Data Practice Risk:** Moderate. Vendor offers customer choice of residency and has a data deletion policy, but lacks transparency on PII handling, encryption, and sub-processor agreements. **No Data Processing Agreement (DPA) or Sub-Processor Addendum disclosed**, which is a compliance gap for GDPR and CCPA.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** \\n  - SOC 2 Type 2 ✓\\n  - ISO 27001 ✓\\n  - GDPR Compliant (claimed, not independently verified)\\n\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** SOC 2 Type 2 audit (standard third-party audit of controls over security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy). Audit firm and date not specified.\\n\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:**\\n  - NIST: Not specified\\n  - GDPR: Claimed compliant; no DPA or Data Processing Agreement disclosed\\n  - CCPA: Not specified\\n  - EU AI Act: Not specified\\n\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** **Not applicable** (no BAA or HIPAA compliance claimed). Base44 is not suitable for healthcare PHI without additional controls.\\n\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** **Claimed but not fully documented.** Vendor states \\"GDPR Compliant\\" but does not provide:\\n  - Data Processing Agreement (DPA)\\n  - Sub-processor list or addendum\\n  - Data Subject Rights procedures (access, deletion, portability)\\n  - Breach notification procedures\\n  - Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) template\\n\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** **Not specified.** No disclosure of engagement with EU AI Act requirements (risk classification, transparency, documentation, conformity assessment).\\n\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** \\n  - SOC 2 Type 2: Frequency not specified; last audit date not disclosed; findings not disclosed.\\n  - ISO 27001: Frequency not specified; last audit date not disclosed; findings not disclosed.\\n\\n**Compliance Risk Assessment:** Base44 holds **foundational certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001)**, which is positive. However, **critical gaps exist:**\\n1. No GDPR Data Processing Agreement (DPA) disclosed\\n2. No EU AI Act readiness or risk classification\\n3. No HIPAA/BAA (not suitable for healthcare)\\n4. No audit dates or findings transparency\\n5. No CCPA or state privacy law compliance disclosure\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Require vendor to provide:\\n- Executed Data Processing Agreement (DPA) with Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)\\n- Sub-processor list and addendum\\n- EU AI Act risk classification and conformity assessment\\n- SOC 2 Type 2 audit report (last 12 months)\\n- ISO 27001 certificate and audit findings summary\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** **99.9%** (8.8 hours of allowable downtime per year). This is a **solid operational commitment** for a SaaS platform, though not enterprise-grade (99.95% or higher).\\n\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** **Not specified.** No disclosure of support tiers, response times, or escalation procedures. This is a **critical gap** for enterprise customers.\\n\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** **Not specified.** No disclosure of:\\n  - Release frequency (weekly, bi-weekly, monthly, etc.)\\n  - Change management process (code review, testing, staging)\\n  - Rollback procedures (automated, manual, time-to-rollback)\\n  - Communication of breaking changes or deprecations\\n  - Maintenance windows or planned downtime\\n\\n**Operations Risk:** Moderate. While the 99.9% SLA is acceptable, the lack of documented support SLAs and change management procedures creates uncertainty around incident response and deployment stability. **Recommendation:** Require vendor to provide:\\n- Support SLA matrix (P1/P2/P3 response and resolution times)\\n- Change management policy and release notes\\n- Maintenance window schedule\\n- Rollback time-to-recovery (MTTR) target\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** \\n  - **Infrastructure:** AWS/Azure/GCP (inferred; not explicitly stated)\\n  - **AI Model:** Third-party LLM provider (vendor not disclosed; likely OpenAI, Anthropic, or similar)\\n  - **Payments:** Not specified\\n  - **Authentication:** Not specified (OAuth, SAML, etc.)\\n\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** **Not specified.** No disclosure of frequency or rigor of vendor risk assessments for critical sub-processors.\\n\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** **Not specified.** No disclosure of SLAs from critical vendors (e.g., AWS uptime guarantee, LLM provider availability).\\n\\n**Vendor Management Risk:** High. Base44 depends on third-party LLM providers and cloud infrastructure, but provides **no transparency on vendor selection, assessment, or SLA requirements**. This creates **supply-chain risk**: if the LLM provider degrades service or changes terms, Base44 customers are exposed. **Recommendation:** Require vendor to disclose:", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Base44", "Customer Base": "Not specified (no customer count, ARR, or retention metrics provided); one public case study: \\"Gift My Book\\" ($1M ARR in 3 months).", "Target Industry": "Enterprise, SaaS, startups; any organization requiring rapid prototyping or internal tooling.", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS (AWS/Azure/GCP inferred from \\"built-in infrastructure\\" and \\"hosting\\" claims).", "Deployment Scale": "Individual users to enterprise teams; typical deployment: rapid prototyping (minutes to hours per app).", "Primary Use Case": "Rapid application development (CRUD apps, prototypes, MVPs, back-office tools, customer portals, enterprise products) via natural language description; no-code/low-code platform for non-technical users.", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted; customer choice of data residency region.", "Product Description": "Base44 is a chat-based, AI-powered low-code platform enabling non-technical users to build functioning applications in minutes using natural language prompts. The platform includes built-in infrastructure (databases, authentication, hosting) and 20+ integrations, eliminating the need for DevOps expertise. However, the AI model operates as a black box with limited transparency, unknown training data sourcing (third-party models), and no documented bias testing or safety controls—presenting elevated risk for enterprise deployments.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Growth-stage product (Series B/C); limited independent testing data; vendor-provided case studies only."}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "201–1,000", "Legal Name": "Base44 (legal entity not specified)", "Vendor Type": "AI Platform Provider (SaaS)", "Year Founded": "2025", "Operating Regions": "Not specified (inferred: US-based, likely global SaaS availability).", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "San Francisco, CA, USA; specific investors not disclosed.", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series B/C (specific ARR not disclosed); no profitability data."}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Large Language Model (LLM) or similar generative AI; third-party models (vendor-sourced, not custom-trained).", "Model Purpose": "Natural language understanding (user intent from chat prompts) → code/app generation (CRUD operations, database schemas, authentication logic, integrations).", "Human Oversight": "**Not specified.** No evidence of human-in-the-loop review, content filtering, or approval workflows for generated code. Unclear whether generated apps are automatically deployed or require user validation.", "Model Governance": "**Not documented.** No staged deployment policy, no canary releases, no A/B testing framework, no model versioning strategy disclosed.", "Update Frequency": "Not specified; inferred: continuous or frequent (typical for SaaS LLM platforms).", "Explainability / Transparency": "**Limited (Black box).** Model operates as a black box; no prompt-level control, no citation of training data, no explainability framework. Users cannot inspect or audit the reasoning behind generated code."}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "**Not specified.** No red-teaming, no third-party bias audits, no statistical bias monitoring, no fairness metrics disclosed.", "Penetration Testing": "**Not specified.** No adversarial security testing of AI model outputs (e.g., prompt injection, jailbreaking, malicious code generation). No frequency or scope disclosed.", "Training Data Documentation": "**Minimal.** Vendor states \\"Using third-party models (data unknown).\\" No disclosure of training data sources, filtering, or curation. No model card or dataset documentation."}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "**Not conducted.** No internal bias audits, no external evaluations, no frequency specified.", "AI Ethics Policy": "**Not documented.** No published AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety principles disclosed.", "Human-in-the-Loop": "**Not specified.** No evidence of admin controls, content filtering, code review workflows, or user approval gates before deployment.", "Impact Assessment": "**Not documented.** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation disclosing known limitations, risks, or intended use cases."}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "**Automated with manual trigger.** Vendor supports automated rollback of deployments with manual override option. This is a **strong operational control** for rapid recovery.", "Incident Response Plan": "**Yes, tested annually.** Vendor confirms incident response plan exists and is tested on an annual basis. This is a positive signal but falls short of industry best practice (quarterly or semi-annual testing for critical systems)."}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Infrastructure not explicitly stated; inferred: AWS/Azure/GCP (standard for SaaS). Encryption: **Not specified** (at-rest and in-transit).", "PII Handling": "**Not specified.** No disclosure of whether customer data (PII, PHI, financial data) is processed, retained, or used for model training. **Critical gap:** Given the platform's use case (enterprise apps, customer portals), PII handling is likely but undocumented.", "Data Deletion": "On request (within 15-day window post-termination); automated deletion after 30-day grace period for deleted records.", "Data Retention": "Base44 retains data \\"as long as necessary to fulfill its purpose, comply with legal obligations, resolve disputes, and enforce its rights.\\" Upon service termination, customers have 15 days to request data export; after 15 days, Base44 may delete all data. Deleted records within data tables are retained for 30 days before permanent deletion. **No zero-retention or opt-out option disclosed.**", "Sub-processors": "Not specified. Inferred: AWS/Azure/GCP (hosting), third-party LLM provider (model inference), payment processor (if applicable), authentication provider (OAuth, etc.). **No sub-processor list or DPA disclosures provided.**", "Data Collection": "Chat interface (user prompts), API (application data), integration endpoints (third-party data flows). Retention options: **Not specified.**", "Data Types Processed": "Application code (generated), user prompts (natural language descriptions), application data (user-created databases, schemas, records), authentication credentials, integration payloads.", "Data Location / Residency": "**Customer choice of region.** Vendor supports customer selection of data residency (US, EU, etc.), which is a **positive control** for GDPR and data sovereignty compliance."}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "- SOC 2 Type 2 ✓", "GDPR Compliance": "**Claimed but not fully documented.** Vendor states \\"GDPR Compliant\\" but does not provide:", "HIPAA Compliance": "**Not applicable** (no BAA or HIPAA compliance claimed). Base44 is not suitable for healthcare PHI without additional controls.", "EU AI Act Readiness": "**Not specified.** No disclosure of engagement with EU AI Act requirements (risk classification, transparency, documentation, conformity assessment).", "Regulatory Frameworks": "- NIST: Not specified", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "SOC 2 Type 2 audit (standard third-party audit of controls over security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy). Audit firm and date not specified.", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "- SOC 2 Type 2: Frequency not specified; last audit date not disclosed; findings not disclosed."}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "**99.9%** (8.8 hours of allowable downtime per year). This is a **solid operational commitment** for a SaaS platform, though not enterprise-grade (99.95% or higher).", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "**Not specified.** No disclosure of:", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "**Not specified.** No disclosure of support tiers, response times, or escalation procedures. This is a **critical gap** for enterprise customers."}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "**Not specified.** No disclosure of SLAs from critical vendors (e.g., AWS uptime guarantee, LLM provider availability).", "Critical Vendors": "- **Infrastructure:** AWS/Azure/GCP (inferred; not explicitly stated)", "Vendor Assessment": "**Not specified.** No disclosure of frequency or rigor of vendor risk assessments for critical sub-processors."}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Base44 demonstrates solid foundational security and compliance posture (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, GDPR compliance) with reasonable operational maturity (99.9% SLA, annual incident response testing). However, the vendor presents **moderate-to-high risk** in two critical areas: **(1) AI Governance is severely underdeveloped**—no documented AI ethics policy, no bias testing approach, no adversarial security testing, and only \\"limited\\" model transparency with black-box behavior—and **(2) Company Maturity is nascent**—founded in 2025 (less than 1 year old), Series B/C stage, no financial stability data, and no track record of long-term reliability.  Applying the **Vendor Trust Score (VTS) formula** from the vector database:", "overallScore": 84, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.44, "Governance": 91, "Operational": 60}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   84 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       40.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk         9.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            3.56  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (40.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (9.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (60.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Audit frequency in the attestation"}	2026-07-28 14:51:46.940383+05:30	** Base44 demonstrates solid foundational security and compliance posture (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, GDPR compliance) with reasonable operational maturity (99.9% SLA, annual incident response testing). However, the vendor presents **moderate-to-high risk** in two critical areas: **(1) AI Governance is severely underdeveloped**—no documented AI ethics policy, no bias testing approach, no adversarial security testing, and only "limited" model transparency with black-box behavior—and **(2) Company Maturity is nascent**—founded in 2025 (less than 1 year old), Series B/C stage, no financial stability data, and no track record of long-term reliability.  Applying the **Vendor Trust Score (VTS) formula** from the vector database:	3.5595	9	40	16.1238	B	Trusted Vendor	{"llm": {"error": null, "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Base44 demonstrates solid foundational security and compliance posture (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, GDPR compliance) with reasonable operational maturity (99.9% SLA, annual incident response testing). However, the vendor presents **moderate-to-high risk** in two critical areas: **(1) AI Governance is severely underdeveloped**—no documented AI ethics policy, no bias testing approach, no adversarial security testing, and only \\"limited\\" model transparency with black-box behavior—and **(2) Company Maturity is nascent**—founded in 2025 (less than 1 year old), Series B/C stage