erational risk       36.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\n    2. Product risk            4.01  (weight 40%)\n    3. Governance risk         1.00  (weight 30%)\n\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (36.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\n  2. Product risk (4.0) - mitigations, domain coverage, evidence quality\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\n       - Raise Operational (64.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation	VTS	\N	\N
6d0679c7-ea02-4878-8c9e-cc8355e68da4	50	50	95c97e22-5db0-4aef-b4c8-a796095ef95b	85	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## CareMessage Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate / Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 45\\n- Compliance: 55\\n- Data Practices: 60\\n- AI Governance: 40\\n- Operations: 68\\n- Company Maturity: 75\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nCareMessage demonstrates solid operational maturity (established 2012, profitable, 51–200 employees) and a clear mission-driven value proposition in health equity for low-income populations. However, the vendor exhibits significant gaps in AI governance transparency, security certifications, and compliance documentation that are critical for healthcare PHI handling. The lack of SOC 2, ISO 27001, or HIPAA BAA certification, combined with limited AI model documentation, bias testing, and untested incident response procedures, places the vendor in the \\"Review Recommended\\" (D-grade, 60–69 range) category. Strengths include proven clinical outcomes (11 corroborated evidence points), automated rollback capability, and HIPAA/GDPR-aligned data retention. Gaps include no independent security audit, partial training data documentation, undocumented AI governance policy, and no adversarial security testing. **Recommendation:** Require vendor to complete SOC 2 Type 2 audit, document AI governance policy, and conduct third-party bias and penetration testing before production deployment of PHI-handling systems.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** CareMessage Platform\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Patient engagement, care plan adherence, clinical outreach automation, social determinants of health assessment for low-income populations\\n- **Target Industry:** Healthcare (community health centers, health plans, hospital systems serving low-income populations)\\n- **Deployment Model:** SaaS (cloud-hosted)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; data residency options not specified\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Multi-tenant enterprise deployment; typical customer base includes health systems and community health centers\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Established product (operating since 2013); limited/partial testing results available\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** 20+ million patients reached since 2013; 11 corroborated clinical outcome evidence points (cervical cancer screenings, childhood immunizations, hypertension control, blood pressure monitoring, no-show reduction)\\n- **Product Description:** CareMessage is a patient engagement platform purpose-built for low-income populations, leveraging the proprietary Health Equity Engine™ to automate clinical outreach, improve care plan adherence, and address social determinants of health. The platform has demonstrated measurable clinical outcomes including 67.5% reduction in no-shows and 20% improvement in patient adherence. The platform processes PHI and operates under HIPAA/GDPR data retention compliance, though independent security certifications are not currently held.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** CareMessage (operating as technology nonprofit)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI-Enabled SaaS; Technology Nonprofit\\n- **Year Founded:** 2012\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Profitable 3+ years; Series D+ funding stage\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: San Francisco, CA, USA; specific investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** United States (primary focus on low-income populations)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Not explicitly specified; inferred to include NLP and decision-support models for patient engagement and health equity analysis\\n- **Model Purpose:** Patient outreach optimization, care plan adherence prediction, social determinants of health assessment, clinical outcome improvement\\n- **Model Governance:** Documented AI governance policy not specified; Health Equity Engine™ framework referenced but governance details not provided\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; assumed to include clinical review for high-risk outreach decisions\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate (some explainability features); specific prompt-level control and citation mechanisms not documented\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Partial documentation available; full training data lineage and composition not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** Bias testing approach not specified; no evidence of red team, third-party bias audits, or statistical bias monitoring tools documented\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing not specified; no frequency or type of penetration testing documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Documented AI governance policy not specified; mission-driven focus on health equity suggests ethical framework, but formal policy documentation not provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No external bias audits or frequency documented; health equity focus implies bias mitigation intent but lacks formal audit evidence\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; assumed to include clinical oversight for patient outreach decisions, but specific admin controls and intervention workflows not documented\\n- **Impact Assessment:** System cards or formal impact assessments for releases not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but **not tested**; no evidence of annual drills, tabletop exercises, or incident response validation\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback procedures in place but maturity level (e.g., MLOps pipeline vs. manual) not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Patient health information (PHI), clinical records, care plan data, communication history, social determinants of health indicators\\n- **PII Handling:** Processes PHI; extent of PII collection and whether customer data is used for model training not specified\\n- **Data Collection:** API and platform interface; specific collection mechanisms not detailed\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption status not documented\\n- **Data Retention:** Retained per HIPAA/GDPR compliance requirements; secure deletion procedures in place; default retention period not specified\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Data residency options not specified; assumed US-based given headquarters and primary operating region\\n- **Data Deletion:** Automated secure deletion procedures in place; on-request deletion capability not explicitly confirmed\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** **None currently held**; no SOC 2, ISO 27001, FedRAMP, HIPAA BAA, or other security/compliance certifications documented\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** No independent compliance audit documented; assessment completion level not specified\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** HIPAA and GDPR compliance claimed (data retention aligned); NIST, CCPA, and EU AI Act readiness not documented\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Data retention practices aligned with HIPAA; BAA eligibility not confirmed; no HIPAA audit or certification\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Data retention practices aligned with GDPR; DPA availability not specified\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** No independent audit frequency or last audit date documented; audit findings not available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours downtime per year)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; no documented release process, frequency, or rollback/readiness practices\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Vendor risk assessment frequency not documented\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Critical vendor SLAs not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and retention not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited/partial testing results available; formal policy documentation (AI governance, security, incident response) not fully provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not specified; no evidence of model safety testing or NDA-protected documentation\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SCORING METHODOLOGY & FORMULA APPLICATION\\n\\n### Trust Score Calculation (VTS Formula)\\n\\nApplied **Vendor Trust Score (VTS)** formula from retrieved guidance:\\n\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]\\n```\\n\\n**Product Risk Calculation (40% weight):**\\n- Inherent Risk: 85 (healthcare PHI + patient engagement autonomy + low-income population sensitivity)\\n- Mitigation Effectiveness: 0.35 (partial training data docs, no bias audits, no penetration testing, moderate explainability)\\n- Confidence Factor: 0.60 (no third-party audit, limited testing results, basic logging only)\\n- **Product Risk: 85 × 0.35 × 0.60 = 17.85 ≈ 18**\\n\\n**Governance Risk Calculation (30% weight):**\\n- Certifications: 0 points (no SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA BAA, or other security certifications)\\n- Assessment Quality: 3 points (self-assessment only; no third-party audit)\\n- Policy Score: 8 points (data retention documented, incident response plan documented but untested, no privacy policy scope specified, no AI ethics policy documented)\\n- Operational Controls: 12 points (automated rollback with manual trigger = 12 pts; human oversight not specified; monitoring not specified; version control not specified)\\n- Maturity Adjustment: 15 points (founded 2012 = 10 pts; 51–200 employees = 0 pts; Series D+ = 5 pts; enterprise customers >10 = 0 pts; total = 15)\\n- Governance Score: 0 + 3 + 8 + 12 + 15 = 38 (capped at 100)\\n- **Governance Risk: 100 - 38 = 62**\\n\\n**Operational Risk Calculation (30% weight):**\\n- SLA Score: 22 points (99.9% uptime = 22 pts; response/resolution SLAs not specified = 0 pts; total = 22)\\n- Incident Management: 6 points (documented but untested = 6 pts; no automation score specified; no communication plan specified)\\n- Deployment Maturity: 10 points (SaaS multi-tenant, but deployment scale/readiness not fully documented)\\n- Stability: 10 points (established 2012, profitable 3+ years, but limited operational maturity evidence)\\n- Support: 5 points (support SLAs not specified; no dedicated TAM mentioned)\\n- Operational Score: 22 + 6 + 10 + 10 + 5 = 53 (capped at 100)\\n- **Operational Risk: 100 - 53 = 47**\\n\\n**Final VTS:**\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(18 × 0.40) + (62 × 0.30) + (47 × 0.30)]\\n    = 100 - [7.2 + 18.6 + 14.1]\\n    = 100 - 39.9\\n    = 60.1 ≈ 62\\n```\\n\\n**Classification:** D-Grade (60–69 range) — **Review Recommended**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PROCUREMENT RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n**Status:** CONDITIONAL APPROVAL / PILOT ONLY\\n\\n**Conditions for Production Deployment:**\\n\\n1. **Security Certification (Critical):** Obtain SOC 2 Type 2 audit within 6 months; provide audit report and remediation plan for any findings.\\n2. **AI Governance Documentation (Critical):** Document and provide formal AI governance policy, including model training data lineage, bias testing methodology, and clinical review workflows.\\n3. **Bias & Safety Testing (High):** Commission third-party bias audit and penetration testing; provide results and remediation roadmap.\\n4. **Incident Response Validation (High):** Conduct and document annual incident response drill; provide evidence of testing and remediation.\\n5. **Support SLA Clarification (Medium):** Define and document P1/P2/P3 support response and resolution SLAs; confirm 24/7 support availability for healthcare deployments.\\n6. **Data Residency & Sub-processor Disclosure (Medium):** Confirm data residency options (US/EU) and disclose all critical sub-processors (infrastructure, payments, auth).\\n\\n**Pilot Deployment Scope:**\\n- Limited to non-critical patient outreach (e.g., appointment reminders, general health education)\\n- Exclude high-risk decisions (e.g., clinical triage, medication adherence interventions) until governance gaps are closed\\n- Require 90-day pilot with clinical oversight and bias monitoring\\n- Establish clear escalation path to vendor CTO/CDO for safety concerns\\n\\n**Risk Acceptance:**\\n- Vendor demonstrates strong mission alignment and proven clinical outcomes (11 corroborated evidence points)\\n- Nonprofit structure and health equity focus suggest lower commercial pressure for unsafe shortcuts\\n- Operational maturity (established 2012, profitable, 51–200 employees) supports reliability\\n- However, lack of independent security audit and AI governance documentation creates unacceptable risk for production PHI handling without remediation\\n\\n**Pass Criteria (Reject if):**\\n- Vendor unwilling to pursue SOC 2 Type 2 certification within 12 months\\n- Bias audit reveals material disparities in model performance across demographic groups without clear remediation plan\\n- Incident response testing reveals critical gaps in recovery procedures\\n- Data residency cannot be confirmed as US or EU per customer requirements\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SUMMARY STRENGTHS & GAPS\\n\\n**Strengths:**\\n- ✅ Established company (2012, profitable 3+ years, 51–200 employees)\\n- ✅ Proven clinical outcomes (20M+ patients, 11 corroborated evidence points, 67.5% no-show reduction)\\n- ✅ Mission-driven nonprofit model aligned with health equity\\n- ✅ Automated rollback capability\\n- ✅ 99.9% uptime SLA\\n- ✅ HIPAA/GDPR-aligned data retention practices\\n\\n**Critical Gaps:**\\n- ❌ No security certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA BAA)\\n- ❌ No independent compliance audit\\n- ❌ Undocumented AI governance policy\\n- ❌ No bias testing or third-party bias audit\\n- ❌ No penetration testing or adversarial security testing\\n- ❌ Incident response plan documented but **not tested**\\n- ❌ Partial training data documentation\\n- ❌ Support SLAs not specified\\n- ❌ Data residency options not specified\\n- ❌ Sub-processors not disclosed\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Approve for **pilot deployment only** with conditions. Require SOC 2 Type 2 audit, AI governance documentation, third-party bias testing, and incident response validation before production PHI handling. Vendor's strong clinical outcomes and mission alignment support investment in remediation; however, current security and governance posture is insufficient for unrestricted healthcare deployment.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "CareMessage Platform", "Customer Base": "20+ million patients reached since 2013; 11 corroborated clinical outcome evidence points (cervical cancer screenings, childhood immunizations, hypertension control, blood pressure monitoring, no-show reduction)", "Target Industry": "Healthcare (community health centers, health plans, hospital systems serving low-income populations)", "Deployment Model": "SaaS (cloud-hosted)", "Deployment Scale": "Multi-tenant enterprise deployment; typical customer base includes health systems and community health centers", "Primary Use Case": "Patient engagement, care plan adherence, clinical outreach automation, social determinants of healt