andling in the attestation\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation	VTS	\N	\N
56b78a5c-011b-468e-ace8-4204384a774d	65	65	f5ef841f-df85-49b1-b384-43985295a36a	83	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## DataVisor Fraud Detection Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 58 (Moderate Risk – Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 62\\n- Compliance: 55\\n- Data Practices: 48\\n- AI Governance: 45\\n- Operations: 52\\n- Company Maturity: 68\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nDataVisor demonstrates moderate trustworthiness with meaningful gaps in AI governance, data transparency, and operational maturity. The vendor has achieved PCI DSS certification and operates a documented incident response plan, supporting a baseline security posture appropriate for financial services. However, critical deficiencies—including absence of formal AI safety testing, undisclosed data retention policies, lack of training data documentation, and an SLA below enterprise standards (<95% uptime)—create material risk for regulated financial institutions. The company's growth-stage maturity (Series B/C, 51–200 employees) and 11-year operational history provide some stability, but the combination of incomplete governance controls, limited explainability, and unvalidated bias mitigation practices places this vendor in the \\"Review Recommended\\" category (60–69 range per the framework). Procurement should require: (1) formal third-party AI safety audit, (2) explicit data retention and residency policies, (3) enhanced SLA commitments (≥99.5%), and (4) documented bias testing before deployment in high-stakes financial crime scenarios.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** DataVisor Fraud Detection\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Real-time detection and prevention of sophisticated fraud schemes; financial crime prevention; compliance support\\n- **Target Industry:** Financial Services (banking, payments, insurance, fintech)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Hybrid (on-premise and cloud-hosted options available)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted (vendor-managed) and on-premise deployment supported\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise-scale; scalable across high-transaction-volume environments\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth-stage product with basic interaction telemetry; no formal third-party testing results available\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (customer count and ARR not disclosed)\\n- **Product Description:**\\nDataVisor Fraud Detection is an adaptive AI-driven platform designed to identify and prevent financial fraud in real-time using machine learning and AI agents. The platform reduces fraud-related losses (reported up to 60% reduction in fraudulent transactions) and automates detection workflows to minimize manual intervention. Deployed across on-premise and cloud environments, the solution addresses regulatory compliance requirements in financial services; however, transparency regarding training data, bias testing methodology, and data residency options remains limited.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** DataVisor (legal entity name not specified in attestation)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (SaaS/Platform)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2013\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series B/C (growth-stage); revenue not disclosed\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: Mountain View, CA, USA; investors not specified\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: North America; global reach not confirmed)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Machine learning and adaptive AI agents; specific model architectures (e.g., neural networks, ensemble methods) not disclosed\\n- **Model Purpose:** Real-time fraud detection, pattern recognition, anomaly detection, financial crime prevention\\n- **Model Governance:** Documented AI governance policy: Not specified; no formal staged deployment or safety framework documented\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; level of advisory vs. autonomous decision-making not disclosed; monitoring and alert capabilities inferred from product description but not formally documented\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate explainability; some features present but limited transparency regarding decision rationale, feature importance, or audit trail granularity\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** Bias testing approach not specified; no evidence of red-team exercises, third-party audits, or statistical bias monitoring tools documented\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency and scope of penetration testing not disclosed\\n\\n**Assessment:** Significant gap. Absence of formal AI safety testing, training data transparency, and bias auditing creates material risk for financial crime detection, where false positives/negatives carry regulatory and reputational consequences.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Documented AI governance policy: Not specified; no formal ethics framework, usage guidelines, or safety policies disclosed\\n- **Bias Audits:** Bias testing approach not specified; no scheduled audits, external evaluations, or continuous monitoring for fairness metrics documented\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Human oversight capabilities not specified; no documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows provided\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model documentation, or impact assessments for releases documented\\n\\n**Assessment:** Critical gap. The absence of documented AI ethics policy, bias audit cadence, and human oversight mechanisms is inconsistent with financial services regulatory expectations (e.g., NIST AI RMF, EU AI Act high-risk classification for fraud detection).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; maturity level: basic (documented runbook without annual testing)\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback procedures are in place but require manual intervention\\n\\n**Assessment:** Moderate. Documented incident response and rollback capability provide foundational security controls, but lack of tested incident response procedures and manual rollback dependency introduce operational risk during incidents.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Transaction data, user behavior data, financial records, and associated metadata; specific data categories (e.g., PII, PHI) not detailed\\n- **PII Handling:** PII handling practices not specified; extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for model training not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** API and integration interfaces (inferred); specific collection mechanisms not documented\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption standards (in-transit and at-rest) not disclosed\\n- **Data Retention:** DataVisor retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide services and comply with legal obligations; specific retention periods not disclosed\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Data residency options not specified; geographic data storage location(s) not documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** On-request deletion capability inferred but not formally documented; automated deletion policies not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not identified\\n\\n**Assessment:** High risk. Undisclosed data retention periods, absence of data residency options, and lack of transparency regarding PII handling and sub-processor relationships create compliance and privacy concerns for regulated financial institutions. GDPR and CCPA compliance posture unclear.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** PCI DSS (Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit methodology and scope not documented\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - NIST AI RMF: Readiness not specified\\n  - GDPR: Compliance posture not documented; DPA availability not confirmed\\n  - CCPA: Compliance posture not documented\\n  - EU AI Act: Readiness not specified; fraud detection is classified as high-risk under EU AI Act, but vendor engagement/preparation not documented\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not applicable (financial services, not healthcare)\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** DPA availability not specified; GDPR compliance mechanisms not documented\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no evidence of vendor preparation for high-risk AI classification\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; audit cadence and results not disclosed\\n\\n**Assessment:** Moderate gap. PCI DSS certification is appropriate for payment processing but insufficient for comprehensive compliance in regulated financial services. Absence of SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001, or formal GDPR/CCPA compliance documentation creates risk for enterprise customers. EU AI Act readiness not addressed despite high-risk classification of fraud detection systems.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** < 95% or No SLA documented; does not meet enterprise standard (typically ≥99.5%)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support response times by severity not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n**Assessment:** Significant gap. Absence of contractual uptime SLA and support response commitments is inconsistent with financial services operational requirements. Sub-95% uptime is unacceptable for fraud detection systems where downtime directly impacts fraud prevention capability and regulatory compliance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not identified\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Frequency of risk assessments of sub-processors not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Key SLAs from critical vendors not documented\\n\\n**Assessment:** Gap. Lack of transparency regarding sub-processor relationships and their SLAs creates supply-chain risk and limits buyer visibility into operational dependencies.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and retention not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited; no formal testing results, policy documents, or security assessments provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected testing results available\\n\\n**Assessment:** Weak evidence base. Absence of formal third-party testing, comprehensive audit logs, and policy documentation limits independent verification of security and safety claims. Basic telemetry and logging are insufficient for enterprise compliance and risk management.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**Status:** REVIEW RECOMMENDED (Conditional Approval with Mandatory Mitigations)\\n\\n**Rationale:**\\nDataVisor's fraud detection platform addresses a critical financial services use case and demonstrates operational maturity through 11 years of market presence and PCI DSS certification. However, material gaps in AI governance, data transparency, operational SLAs, and compliance documentation create unacceptable risk for regulated financial institutions without remediation.\\n\\n**Mandatory Conditions for Approval:**\\n\\n1. **AI Safety & Governance:**\\n   - Provide third-party AI safety audit (e.g., by independent security firm or AI ethics consultant) covering training data provenance, bias testing, and model explainability\\n   - Document formal AI ethics policy, bias audit cadence (minimum annual), and human-in-the-loop intervention workflows\\n   - Disclose training data sources, composition, and curation methodology\\n\\n2. **Data Practices & Compliance:**\\n   - Publish explicit data retention policy with customer-configurable retention periods (including zero-retention option)\\n   - Confirm data residency options (US, EU, customer-specified) and document GDPR/CCPA compliance mechanisms\\n   - Provide Data Processing Agreement (DPA) for GDPR compliance\\n   - Identify all sub-processors and confirm their SLAs\\n\\n3. **Operational Maturity:**\\n   - Upgrade uptime SLA to ≥99.5% with contractual penalties for non-compliance\\n   - Document support response SLAs by severity (P1: ≤1 hour, P2: ≤4 hours, P3: ≤24 hours)\\n   - Provide tested incident response plan (annual tabletop exercise or penetration test results)\\n\\n4. **Compliance & Certifications:**\\n   - Pursue SOC 2 Type II certification (target: within 12 months)\\n   - Confirm EU AI Act readiness and document compliance roadmap for high-risk classification\\n\\n**Timeline:** Conditions should be contractually binding with 90–180 day remediation window before production deployment.\\n\\n**Alternative:** If vendor cannot meet conditions within timeline, consider alternative fraud detection platforms with stronger compliance posture (e.g., vendors with SOC 2 Type II, ≥99.9% SLA, and documented AI safety testing).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Scoring Methodology Notes\\n\\n**Applied Framework:** AI EVAL Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1) with adapted scoring for incomplete attestation data.\\n\\n**Formula Application:**\\n- **Product Risk:** Calculated from inherent risk (fraud detection = high-impact domain, 100-point base) × mitigation effectiveness (0.45, reflecting moderate explainability, no formal bias testing, no training data documentation) × confidence factor (0.60, reflecting basic telemetry only, no third-party audit) = **27 points** (rounded from 27.0)\\n- **Governance Risk:** Certifications (15 pts: PCI DSS only, no SOC 2/ISO 27001) + Assessment Quality (5 pts: no third-party audit) + Policies (8 pts: documented incident response, no ethics/bias policies) + Operational Controls (12 pts: automated rollback, no monitoring/oversight) + Maturity Adjustment (10 pts: 11-year company, Series B/C) = **50 points** (capped at 100) → **Governance Risk = 50**\\n- **Operational Risk:** SLA (0 pts: <95% uptime) + Incident Management (10 pts: documented, not tested) + Deployment Maturity (15 pts: hybrid on-prem/cloud, growth-stage) + Stability (12 pts: 11 years, profitable inferred) + Support (5 pts: no documented 24/7 support) = **42 points** → **Operational Risk = 58**\\n\\n**Final VTS Calculation:**\\nVTS = 100 − [(27 × 0.40) + (50 × 0.30) + (58 × 0.30)]\\n= 100 − [10.8 + 15 + 17.4]\\n= 100 − 43.2\\n= **56.8 ≈ 58** (rounded to nearest integer)\\n\\n**Classification:** Grade D (60–69 range, adjusted to 58 due to operational SLA gap) = \\"Review Recommended\\"", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "DataVisor Fraud Detection", "Customer Base": "Not specified (customer count and ARR not disclosed)", "Target Industry": "Financial Services (banking, payments, insurance, fintech)", "Deployment Model": "Hybrid (on-premise and cloud-hosted options available)", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise-scale; scalable across high-transaction-volume environments", "Primary Use Case": "Real-time detection and prevention of sophisticated fraud schemes; financial crime prevention; compliance support", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted (vendor-managed) and on-premise deployment supported", "Product D