 maturity (10+ years, 10,000+ employees, 99.99% uptime) and established company stability, offset by significant gaps in AI governance, compliance certifications, and security posture. The vendor lacks formal security certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA), documented AI governance policies, bias testing frameworks, and formal penetration testing—critical gaps for enterprise deployments handling user-generated video content and personal data. While the platform demonstrates reliable operations and basic audit logging, the absence of independent compliance audits, formal incident response testing, and transparent AI model documentation creates residual risk. The vendor is suitable for general-purpose video editing but requires substantial governance and security enhancements before deployment in regulated industries (healthcare, finance) or handling sensitive PII at scale. ---", "overallScore": 81, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 95.71, "Governance": 79, "Operational": 64}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   81 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       36.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        21.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            4.29  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (36.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (21.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (64.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Raise Governance (79.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Audit frequency in the attestation"}	2026-07-28 15:50:05.589117+05:30	** Clipchamp receives a moderate trust score reflecting strong operational maturity (10+ years, 10,000+ employees, 99.99% uptime) and established company stability, offset by significant gaps in AI governance, compliance certifications, and security posture. The vendor lacks formal security certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA), documented AI governance policies, bias testing frameworks, and formal penetration testing—critical gaps for enterprise deployments handling user-generated video content and personal data. While the platform demonstrates reliable operations and basic audit logging, the absence of independent compliance audits, formal incident response testing, and transparent AI model documentation creates residual risk. The vendor is suitable for general-purpose video editing but requires substantial governance and security enhancements before deployment in regulated industries (healthcare, finance) or handling sensitive PII at scale. ---	4.2898	21	36	18.8159	B	Trusted Vendor	{"llm": {"error": null, "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Clipchamp receives a moderate trust score reflecting strong operational maturity (10+ years, 10,000+ employees, 99.99% uptime) and established company stability, offset by significant gaps in AI governance, compliance certifications, and security posture. The vendor lacks formal security certificati