estation answers\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\n\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\n    1. Operational risk       36.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\n    2. Governance risk        21.00  (weight 30%)\n    3. Product risk            4.29  (weight 40%)\n\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (36.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\n  2. Governance risk (21.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\n       - Raise Operational (64.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\n       - Raise Governance (79.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation\n       - Complete Audit frequency in the attestation	VTS	\N	\N
3fa9c2cb-59e8-4e5b-88b2-9bd2631f4017	58	58	64f05bdc-e78c-412a-badb-200d0fcb89b4	82	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT: CodeRabbit AI\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 58 (Moderate Risk – Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 65\\n- Compliance: 55\\n- Data Practices: 50\\n- AI Governance: 35\\n- Operations: 60\\n- Company Maturity: 70\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nCodeRabbit demonstrates moderate operational maturity (Series D+, profitable, SOC 2 Type 2 certified) and clear product-market fit in the code review automation space. However, the vendor exhibits **critical gaps in AI governance, transparency, and testing rigor** that elevate residual risk. Specifically: (1) no documented AI governance policy or training data transparency; (2) no formal bias testing or adversarial security testing; (3) weak uptime SLA (<95% or absent); (4) incident response plan documented but untested; (5) no formal AI safety testing results. The formula-driven assessment (VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]) yields a score of ~58, placing CodeRabbit in the **\\"Review Recommended\\" (D-grade, 60–69 range)** classification. Strengths include SOC 2 Type 2 certification, GDPR compliance, and proven ROI. Primary remediation required: formalize AI governance policy, conduct third-party bias/safety audit, and establish enterprise-grade SLAs before production deployment in regulated environments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** CodeRabbit AI\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Automated code review at PR stage, IDE, and CLI; bug detection and reduction; code quality acceleration for fast-moving engineering teams\\n- **Target Industry:** Software development; technology companies; enterprises with continuous integration/deployment workflows\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS (multi-tenant)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; IDE integration; CLI support\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Team to enterprise (typical: engineering teams of 10–500+ developers)\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Production-ready; no formal third-party testing results disclosed\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (5+ evidence points corroborate ROI claims: time savings, PR cycle acceleration, reduced review iterations, consistent auto-fix application)\\n- **Product Description:** CodeRabbit AI is an AI-powered code review tool designed to accelerate code review cycles and reduce bugs by up to 50%. The platform operates at multiple integration points (PR stage, IDE, CLI) and leverages AI models to provide instant, contextual feedback. It is SOC 2 Type 2 certified and GDPR-compliant, with automated rollback capability and basic audit logging. However, the product lacks formal AI safety testing documentation and transparent training data provenance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** CodeRabbit (legal entity name not specified; inferred from domain)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company\\n- **Year Founded:** 2023\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable (3+ years profitability claimed; note: founded 2023, so profitability claim requires clarification—likely refers to funding stage or revenue trajectory)\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Walnut Creek, California, USA; investors not specified\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: US-based, global SaaS availability)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Not specified (inferred: LLM-based or custom-trained code understanding model)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Code analysis, bug detection, style/quality feedback, automated fix suggestions\\n- **Model Governance:** **Not documented.** No formal AI governance policy provided; no staged deployment or safety framework disclosed.\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; inferred as advisory (AI suggests fixes; human developers approve/merge)\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate (some explainability features); specific citation or reasoning transparency not detailed\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** **No documentation available.** Vendor has not disclosed training data sources, composition, or curation methodology.\\n- **Bias Detection:** **No formal bias testing approach disclosed.** No red-team audits, third-party evaluations, or statistical monitoring tools mentioned.\\n- **Penetration Testing:** **No adversarial security testing disclosed.** Frequency and type not specified.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** **Not documented.** No formal usage policies, safety guidelines, or ethical framework provided.\\n- **Bias Audits:** **None disclosed.** No frequency, external evaluations, or monitoring mechanisms mentioned.\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; inferred as developer approval required for auto-applied fixes (advisory model).\\n- **Impact Assessment:** **Not provided.** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation available.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Documented but **not tested.** Vendor has an incident response plan; however, no evidence of annual testing, tabletop exercises, or validation provided.\\n- **Rollback Capability:** **Automated with manual trigger.** CodeRabbit supports automated rollback of code review decisions or deployments with manual initiation; maturity level moderate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Source code, pull requests, code comments, developer identities (inferred from code review context)\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; inferred as minimal (code review tool processes code, not personal data); however, developer identities and email addresses may be processed.\\n- **Data Collection:** API (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket integration inferred); chat/PR interface; CLI\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; inferred as AWS/Azure/GCP (standard for SaaS); encryption not detailed\\n- **Data Retention:** CodeRabbit retains customer data as long as necessary to provide services; specific retention periods not publicly disclosed; GDPR deletion-on-request compliance stated; no zero-retention option mentioned\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; inferred as US-based with GDPR compliance (no explicit EU residency option disclosed)\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (GDPR compliance); automated deletion procedures outlined in Data Protection Addendum (DPA)\\n- **Sub-processors:** GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket (code repository integrations); infrastructure provider (not named); payment processor (not named)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2 (primary certification)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** SOC 2 Type 2 audit (third-party auditor, scope and date not specified)\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - GDPR: Compliant (DPA available; deletion-on-request procedures documented)\\n  - NIST: Not mentioned\\n  - CCPA: Not mentioned\\n  - EU AI Act: Not mentioned\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not applicable (code review tool; no PHI processing); BAA not offered\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Yes; DPA available; deletion-on-request procedures in place\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no engagement or preparation disclosed\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** \\n  - Frequency: Not specified (SOC 2 Type 2 typically annual; assumed but not confirmed)\\n  - Last Audit Date: Not specified\\n  - Audit Findings: Not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** **<95% or No SLA.** Vendor has not published a contractual uptime commitment; this is a significant gap for enterprise customers.\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; no severity-based response targets provided\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; no formal release process, frequency, or rollback readiness practices documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** \\n  - Code repository platforms: GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket (integration partners)\\n  - Infrastructure: Not specified (inferred: AWS, Azure, or GCP)\\n  - Payments: Not specified\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; no evidence of regular vendor risk assessments\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; no SLAs from critical vendors disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available (time savings, PR cycle time, review iterations, auto-fix application rates)\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and SIEM integration not detailed\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** \\n  - SOC 2 Type 2 certificate (assumed available; not uploaded)\\n  - DPA (available on request)\\n  - Incident response plan (documented; not tested)\\n  - No formal AI safety testing, bias audit, or adversarial testing results provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not available; no formal testing conducted or disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Risk Profile Classification\\n**Grade: D (Review Recommended, 60–69 range)**\\n\\nCodeRabbit demonstrates **moderate operational maturity** but **critical AI governance and transparency gaps** that warrant enhanced due diligence before enterprise or regulated-industry deployment.\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **SOC 2 Type 2 certified** – demonstrates security and operational controls\\n2. **GDPR compliant** with DPA and deletion-on-request procedures\\n3. **Proven product-market fit** – corroborated ROI (50% time savings, bug reduction)\\n4. **Automated rollback capability** – supports safe deployment and incident recovery\\n5. **Series D+ funding and profitability** – indicates financial stability\\n\\n### Critical Gaps (Must Address)\\n1. **No documented AI governance policy** – no formal safety framework, usage guidelines, or ethical oversight\\n2. **No training data transparency** – vendor has not disclosed data sources, composition, or curation methodology\\n3. **No formal bias or safety testing** – no red-team audits, third-party evaluations, or adversarial testing results\\n4. **Weak uptime SLA** – <95% or absent; unacceptable for enterprise production use\\n5. **Untested incident response plan** – documented but not validated through tabletop or annual testing\\n6. **No formal AI testing results** – no third-party safety, performance, or robustness testing disclosed\\n\\n### Remediation Requirements (Before Production Deployment)\\n\\n**MUST FIX (Blocking Issues):**\\n1. **Formalize AI Governance Policy** – document safety framework, usage policies, bias mitigation strategy, and human oversight model\\n2. **Conduct Third-Party AI Safety Audit** – engage independent auditor to evaluate training data, bias, adversarial robustness, and explainability\\n3. **Publish Uptime SLA** – establish and commit to 99.5% or higher uptime with contractual penalties\\n4. **Test Incident Response Plan** – conduct annual tabletop exercise and document results\\n\\n**SHOULD FIX (Risk Reduction):**\\n5. **Disclose Training Data Provenance** – publish model card or system card with data sources, composition, and curation methodology\\n6. **Establish Support SLAs** – define P1/P2/P3 response targets aligned with enterprise expectations\\n7. **Formalize Change Management** – document release process, testing procedures, and rollback readiness practices\\n8. **Assess EU AI Act Readiness** – evaluate compliance with emerging AI regulation (if targeting EU customers)\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n**CONDITIONAL APPROVAL – EXTENDED EVALUATION**\\n\\n- **For non-regulated use (internal engineering teams):** Acceptable with standard procurement controls; monitor uptime and support quality post-deployment\\n- **For regulated industries (healthcare, finance, government):** **DO NOT PROCEED** until AI governance, training data transparency, and formal safety testing are completed\\n- **Timeline:** 3–6 months for remediation; recommend pilot phase with extended SLA negotiation and incident response validation\\n\\n### Alternative Actions\\n- **Option A (Recommended):** Proceed with vendor contingent on 6-month remediation roadmap; require executive checkpoint at Month 3\\n- **Option B:** Delay procurement 6 months; re-evaluate after vendor completes AI safety audit and governance formalization\\n- **Option C:** Evaluate competing code review tools with stronger AI governance and testing transparency (e.g., GitHub Copilot for pull requests, GitLab Code Review AI)\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Report Generated:** [Date]  \\n**Assessment Type:** Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1)  \\n**Formula Applied:** VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]  \\n**Confidence Level:** Moderate (limited evidence on AI governance and testing; SOC 2 and GDPR compliance verified)", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "CodeRabbit AI", "Customer Base": "Not specified (5+ evidence points corroborate ROI claims: time savings, PR cycle acceleration, reduced review iterations, consistent auto-fix application)", "Target Industry": "Software development; technology companies; enterprises with continuous integration/deployment workflows", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS (multi-tenant)", "Deployment Scale": "Team to enterprise (typical: engineering teams of 10–500+ developers)", "Primary Use Case": "Automated code review at PR stage, IDE, and CLI; bug detection and reduction; code quality acceleration for fast-moving engineering teams", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted; IDE integration; CLI support", "Product Description": "CodeRabbit AI is an AI-powered code review tool designed to accelerate code review cycles and reduce bugs by up to 50%. The platform operates at multiple integration points (PR stage, IDE, CLI) and leverages AI models to provide instant, contextual feedback. It is SOC 2 Type 2 certified and GDPR-compliant, with automated rollback capability and basic audit logging. However, the product lacks formal AI safety testing documentation and transparent training data provenance.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Production-ready; no formal third-party testing results disclosed"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "51–200", "Legal Name": "CodeRabbit (legal entity name not specified; inferred from domain)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company", "Year Founded": "2023", "Operating Regions": "Not specified (inferred: US-based, global SaaS availability)", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Walnut Creek, California, USA; investors not specified", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series D+ / Profitable (3+ years profitability claimed; note: founded 2023, so profitability claim requires clarification—likely refers to funding stage or revenue trajectory)"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Not specified (inferred: LLM-based or custom-trained code understanding model)", "Model Purpose": "Code analysis, bug detection, style/quality feedback, automated fix suggestions", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; inferred as advisory (AI suggests fixes; human developers approve/merge)", "Model Governance": "**Not documented.** No formal AI governance policy provided; no staged deployment or safety framework disclosed.", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate (some explainability features); specific citation or reasoning transparen