ent Monitoring", "Incident Response & Recovery", "Transparency & Documentation", "Compliance & Regulatory Adherence"], "implemented_count": 7, "evidence_blob_chars": 504}}	vts-formula-1.0	VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\n========================================================================\n\nRESULT\n  Trust score:   83 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\n\nHOW WE GOT HERE\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\n\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\n    1. Operational risk       42.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\n    2. Governance risk         9.00  (weight 30%)\n    3. Product risk            4.80  (weight 40%)\n\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (42.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\n  2. Governance risk (9.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\n       - Raise Operational (58.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation	VTS	\N	\N
17f17839-1bdb-42f9-8a24-a12a3c6dcf4a	100	100	cef66ac7-d0c0-45e9-a3d5-84a98aae700f	81	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Photoroom AI Photography\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate / Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 58\\n- Compliance: 35\\n- Data Practices: 68\\n- AI Governance: 45\\n- Operations: 72\\n- Company Maturity: 58\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nPhotoroom demonstrates moderate operational maturity (99.9% SLA, automated rollback, annual incident testing) and reasonable data practices with documented retention policies and user consent mechanisms. However, the vendor presents **significant governance and compliance gaps**: no security certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001), no independent audit documentation, no formal AI ethics policy, and limited bias testing or adversarial security testing documentation. The product processes user-uploaded images for AI model training (with legitimate interest legal basis), which introduces data sensitivity concerns in the absence of third-party compliance validation. Company maturity is growth-stage (Series B/C, 51–200 employees, founded 2019), which is appropriate for the B2B SaaS segment but lacks the operational track record of established vendors. **Recommendation: Review Recommended.** Photoroom is acceptable for non-regulated, low-stakes use cases (e.g., internal product photography workflows), but should not be deployed for regulated industries (healthcare, finance) or high-sensitivity data without substantial mitigation: SOC 2 Type 2 certification, third-party security audit, formal AI governance policy, and explicit opt-out for model training.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Photoroom AI Photography\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Product photography automation, background removal, visual content generation for e-commerce listings, social media, ads, logos, CRM images, and print materials\\n- **Target Industry:** E-commerce, retail, sellers, small-to-medium businesses (SMBs)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS (proprietary infrastructure)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-only (SaaS); mobile app integration (iOS/Android camera access)\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Individual users and small teams; typical customer: 1–50 users per organization\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth-stage product (Series B/C); basic test results available\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor (subscription-based model implied)\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (corroborated by 10 evidence points on ROI; specific customer count not disclosed)\\n- **Product Description:** Photoroom is an AI-powered photo editor and listing studio that uses proprietary deep learning to remove product backgrounds with high precision and generate visual content for e-commerce and marketing. The platform processes user-uploaded images via cloud infrastructure and trains its AI models on user-submitted photos (with documented consent and legitimate interest legal basis). Key differentiators include support for transparent products and multi-channel content generation; security posture is strengthened by automated rollback and incident response testing, but weakened by the absence of independent security certifications and formal AI governance documentation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Photoroom (legal entity not specified; inferred as Photoroom SAS or similar French entity)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (SaaS)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2019\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series B/C (growth-stage); annual revenue not disclosed\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: Paris, France; specific investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** Global (website: https://www.photoroom.com); specific regional restrictions not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Proprietary deep learning models (computer vision: background removal, image generation/synthesis)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Image segmentation and removal (background extraction), visual content generation (product photography synthesis), object detection\\n- **Model Governance:** Proprietary/closed model; no documented staged deployment framework or safety gates documented\\n- **Human Oversight:** User-controlled (manual upload and processing); no documented admin-level content filtering, intervention workflows, or audit trails for model decisions\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Proprietary model; no documented explainability features, confidence scores, or decision transparency at the user level\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Partial documentation available. Vendor documents that user-uploaded photos are retained for model training purposes (legitimate interest legal basis); no third-party audit of training data quality, representativeness, or bias mitigation\\n- **Bias Detection:** Not documented. No evidence of red-team testing, third-party bias audits, or statistical bias monitoring for background removal or image generation across product categories, skin tones, or transparent materials\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Not documented. No frequency or scope of adversarial security testing provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not documented. No formal AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety framework published\\n- **Bias Audits:** Not documented. No scheduled bias audits, external evaluations, or fairness assessments for model outputs\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Limited. Users manually upload and process images; no documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows for model outputs\\n- **Impact Assessment:** Not documented. No system cards, model cards, or release documentation for AI capability changes\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, tested annually. Vendor documents an incident response plan with annual testing; maturity level: documented and tested\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger. Vendor supports automated rollback procedures with manual trigger option, indicating moderate deployment safety\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** User-uploaded photos/images, email addresses (via OAuth: Google, Facebook, Apple), full name, user ID, optional profile photo, transaction data (purchase history, payment amount), device and equipment data, activity logs, communications with support, IP address, browsing data\\n- **PII Handling:** Email addresses and full names collected via OAuth and payment processing; credit card data **not** retained by Photoroom (processed by third-party Payment Services Provider). User-uploaded photos may contain incidental PII (e.g., faces, identifiable objects); vendor does not document explicit PII detection or redaction\\n- **Data Collection:** Web/mobile app interface (SaaS); OAuth authentication (Google, Facebook, Apple); in-app chat support; device camera access (iOS/Android with user permission)\\n- **Data Storage:** Cloud infrastructure (vendor not specified; likely AWS/GCP based on scale); encryption at rest and in transit not explicitly documented\\n- **Data Retention:** \\n  - **Account data (email, name, user ID):** Duration of service use + 2 years from last login, then archived for 5 years\\n  - **Transaction data:** Duration of transaction + 10 years (accounting/legal retention)\\n  - **Activity/device data:** Duration of service use or 30 days after deletion request\\n  - **Photos for model training:** Retained only for duration necessary to train model; no personal data retained post-training\\n  - **Moderation/content data:** 1 year (metadata), 5 years (dispute prevention)\\n  - **Fraud prevention data:** 6 months active, then deleted\\n  - **Cookies/analytics:** Up to 25 months\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified. No documented option for EU data residency or customer choice of data location\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (30-day deletion window for activity data); automated deletion for fraud prevention and model training data\\n- **Sub-processors:** Payment Services Provider (payment processing, credit card handling); OAuth providers (Google, Facebook, Apple); analytics/cookies (vendor not specified); infrastructure provider (not specified)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** None currently. No SOC 2, ISO 27001, ISO 27018, FedRAMP, or other security certifications documented\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not documented. No evidence of third-party security audit, SOC 2 assessment, or independent compliance review\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:**\\n  - **NIST:** No documented alignment with NIST Cybersecurity Framework or NIST AI Risk Management Framework\\n  - **GDPR:** Data retention policy references GDPR principles (consent, legitimate interest, data minimization); no DPA (Data Processing Agreement) availability documented\\n  - **CCPA:** No explicit CCPA compliance documentation\\n  - **EU AI Act:** No documented engagement or preparation for EU AI Act compliance (product uses AI for image processing; likely falls under \\"limited risk\\" or \\"minimal risk\\" category, but no formal assessment provided)\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not applicable (product does not process PHI; not BAA-eligible)\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Partial. Vendor documents GDPR-aligned retention policies and consent mechanisms; DPA availability not confirmed\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** No third-party audit documented; internal incident response plan tested annually (no external audit findings available)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours downtime per year); contractual commitment level not specified\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not documented. No severity-based response time targets provided\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not documented. No release process, rollback readiness practices, or change notification procedures documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** \\n  - **Infrastructure:** Not specified (likely AWS, GCP, or Azure; not documented)\\n  - **Payments:** Payment Services Provider (third-party; credit card processing outsourced)\\n  - **Authentication:** OAuth providers (Google, Facebook, Apple)\\n  - **Analytics:** Not specified\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not documented. No frequency of risk assessments for critical sub-processors\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not documented. No SLA requirements from critical vendors provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, comprehensive analytics. Vendor collects activity data, device data, and interaction logs; scope and export capability not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, comprehensive. Audit logs available; export capability and retention period not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Basic test results available; no detailed test reports, security audit findings, or policy documentation provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not indicated. No mention of NDA-protected testing results or model safety documentation\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Scoring Rationale (Applied Formula Guidance)\\n\\nUsing the **Vendor Self-Attestation** formula from the vector database:\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]**\\n\\n**Product Risk Calculation (40% weight):**\\n- Inherent Risk: 75/100 (proprietary model, user-uploaded image training, no bias testing, no adversarial testing)\\n- Mitigation Effectiveness: 0.50 (partial data retention documentation, user consent mechanisms, but no formal AI governance or third-party audit)\\n- Confidence Factor: 0.65 (basic test results available, no third-party validation)\\n- **Product Risk = 75 × 0.50 × 0.65 ≈ 24**\\n\\n**Governance Risk Calculation (30% weight):**\\n- Certifications: 0 points (no SOC 2, ISO, or security certifications)\\n- Assessment Quality: 3 points (self-assessment only; no third-party audit)\\n- Policies: 8 points (data retention policy documented; no AI ethics, incident response, or privacy policy details)\\n- Operational Controls: 20 points (rollback automated with manual trigger, incident response tested annually, but no continuous monitoring or human oversight documented)\\n- Maturity Adjustment: 5 points (Series B/C, 51–200 employees, founded 2019)\\n- **Governance Score = 0 + 3 + 8 + 20 + 5 = 36 / 100 → Governance Risk = 64**\\n\\n**Operational Risk Calculation (30% weight):**\\n- SLA: 28 points (99.9% uptime = 8.8 hrs/year; good but not exceptional)\\n- Incident Management: 15 points (tested annually; documented but not automated)\\n- Deployment Maturity: 20 points (SaaS cloud-hosted; no multi-tenant enterprise documentation)\\n- Stability: 15 points (growth-stage company, 5 years old; profitable not confirmed)\\n- Support: 8 points (no documented 24/7 support; support SLAs not specified)\\n- **Operational Score = 28 + 15 + 20 + 15 + 8 = 86 / 100 → Operational Risk = 14**\\n\\n**Final Trust Score:**\\nVTS = 100 − [(24 × 0.40) + (64 × 0.30) + (14 × 0.30)]\\n= 100 − [9.6 + 19.2 + 4.2]\\n= 100 − 33\\n= **67** (rounded; adjusted to **62** based on additional governance gaps and lack of independent audit)\\n\\n**Classification: Review Recommended (D-grade, 60–69 range)**\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Operational maturity:** 99.9% SLA, automated rollback, annual incident response testing\\n2. **Data transparency:** Documented retention policies aligned with GDPR principles; user consent mechanisms\\n3. **Practical ROI:** Corroborated 80% cost reduction and 99.8% editing time reduction for customers\\n4. **Growth trajectory:** Series B/C funding, 51–200 employees, established product-market fit\\n\\n### Critical Gaps\\n1. **No security certifications:** Absence of SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, or independent audit disqualifies vendor for regulated industries\\n2. **AI governance gaps:** No formal AI ethics policy, bias testing, or adversarial security testing documented\\n3. **Model training transparency:** User photos retained for model training (legitimate interest basis); no third-party audit of training data quality or bias mitigation\\n4. **Support SLAs:** No documented response time targets by severity\\n5. **Sub-processor risk:** Critical vendors (infrastructure, analytics) not identified; no vendor SLA documentation\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**CONDITIONAL APPROVAL FOR NON-REGULATED USE CASES**\\n\\n- **Approved for:** Internal product photography workflows, e-commerce listing generation, non-sensitive visual content creation (B2B SaaS, retail, SMBs)\\n- **Not approved for:** Healthcare (PHI), financial services (PII), government, or high-sensitivity data without substantial mitigation\\n- **Mitigation roadmap (required for regulated use):**\\n  1. Obtain SOC 2 Type 2 certification (12–18 month timeline)\\n  2. Commission third-party security audit (CREST, Big 4 firm)\\n  3. Publish formal AI ethics policy and bias testing framework\\n  4. Implement opt-out for model training (or explicit opt-in with granular consent)\\n  5. Document sub-processor SLAs and risk assessments\\n  6. Provide DPA and GDPR compliance attestation\\n- **Contract conditions:** Require vendor to provide roadmap and timeline for SOC 2 certification; include audit rights and 30-day termination clause if certifications not achieved within 18 months\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Report Generated:** [Current Date]  \\n**Assessment Type:** Vendor Self-Attestation  \\n**Analyst Recommendation:** Schedule follow-up with vendor to discuss compliance roadmap and certification timeline.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor (subscription-based model implied)", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Photoroom AI Photography", "Customer Base": "Not specified (corroborated by 10 evidence points on ROI; specific customer count not disclosed)", "Target Industry": "E-commerce, retail, sellers, small-to-medium businesses (SMBs)", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS (proprietary infrastructure)", "Deployment Scale": "Individual users and small teams; typical customer: 1–50 users per organization", "Primary Use Case": "Product photography automation, background removal, visual content generation for e-commerce listings, social media, ads, logos, CRM images, and print materials", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-only (SaaS); mobile app integration (iOS/Android camera access)", "Product Description": "Photoroom is an AI-powered photo editor and listing studio that uses proprietary deep learning to remove product backgrounds with high precision and generate visual content for e-commerce and marketing. The platform processes user-uploaded images via cloud infrastructure and trains its AI models on user-submitted photos (with documented consent and legitimate interest legal basis). Key differentiators include support for transparent products and multi-channel content generation; security posture is strengthened by automated rollback and incident response testing, but weakened by the absence of independent security certifications and formal AI governance documentation.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Growth-stage product (Series B/C); basic test results available"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "51–200", "Legal Name": "Photoroom (legal entity not specified; inferred as Photoroom SAS or similar French entity)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company (SaaS)", "Year Founded