2669ef88-db22-41a0-9e26-b8df5e777b45	79d03502-5aa1-4553-a3f7-e091037a8c27	2	2	AI Platform Provider	{"Financial Services; Healthcare & Life Sciences; Manufacturing; Energy & Utilities; Public Sector; Telecommunications; Technology": ["Financial Services; Healthcare & Life Sciences; Manufacturing; Energy & Utilities; Public Sector; Telecommunications; Technology"]}	Established (Series D+/Profitable 3+ years)	https://cohere.com/	Cohere is an enterprise-focused foundation-model company building large language models and agentic AI applications optimized for security, privacy, and cloud-agnostic or private deployment. Its models power retrieval-augmented generation, enterprise search, and productivity workflows for regulated industries. It positions itself as a privately deployable alternative to consumer-oriented AI labs.	201-1,000	2019	Toronto, Canada	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "Chief Data Officer", "Head of AI", "CISO"]	Secure, private, grounded RAG and enterprise search over proprietary data	\N	Enterprise-grade, privately deployable AI purpose-built for secure RAG, search, and grounded generation over private company data.	Not publicly disclosed	\N	\N	Moderate (Some explainability features)	\N	["SOC 2 Type 2", "ISO 27001", "ISO 42001", "UK Cyber Essentials", "GDPR", "CCPA"]	\N	\N	\N	Per customer contract and published privacy policy	\N	\N	\N	No documentation available	\N	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 13:57:50.01201+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Cohere Command	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 13:57:50.039+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT: COHERE\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 72 (Acceptable Vendor)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 78\\n- Compliance: 82\\n- Data Practices: 68\\n- AI Governance: 58\\n- Operations: 65\\n- Company Maturity: 80\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nCohere demonstrates solid foundational trust through established certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 42001, GDPR, CCPA compliance) and company maturity (Series D+, 201–1,000 employees, founded 2019). However, the score is constrained by significant gaps in AI governance transparency, lack of formal bias testing documentation, absence of training data documentation, and limited operational detail (no published uptime SLA, support SLAs, or formal incident response testing). The vendor's positioning as enterprise-grade and privately deployable is credible from a security/compliance standpoint, but insufficient evidence of AI safety practices, human oversight mechanisms, and continuous monitoring limits confidence in production-grade AI governance. Recommend conditional approval pending enhanced documentation of bias testing, model monitoring, and incident response procedures.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Cohere Command\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), enterprise search, productivity workflows, grounded generation over proprietary data\\n- **Target Industry:** Regulated industries (healthcare, financial services, government)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (vendor-managed) or private deployment options\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud (vendor-managed SaaS) and on-premises/private cloud deployment supported\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise (multi-tenant and single-tenant private deployments)\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Production-ready; no formal third-party testing results published\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not publicly disclosed\\n- **Product Description:** Cohere Command is an enterprise-grade large language model optimized for secure, private, and grounded generation over proprietary company data. The product emphasizes cloud-agnostic or private deployment to support regulated industries' data residency and sovereignty requirements. It is positioned as a privacy-first alternative to consumer-oriented AI labs, with built-in support for retrieval-augmented generation and enterprise search workflows.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Cohere (legal entity not specified; operating as Cohere Inc. or Cohere Canada Inc.)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Platform Provider (Foundation Model Company)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2019\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: Toronto, Canada. Investors not specified.\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified; inferred to include North America and likely EU/UK (given GDPR/CCPA/UK Cyber Essentials certifications)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Large Language Model (LLM); foundation model architecture\\n- **Model Purpose:** Natural language understanding, generation, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), enterprise search, grounded text generation\\n- **Model Governance:** Not formally documented; positioning emphasizes security and privacy by design, but specific safety framework or staged deployment procedures not detailed\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention mechanisms provided\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate transparency; some explainability features noted, but prompt-level control, citations, and detailed explainability documentation not specified\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; significant gap for enterprise use\\n- **Bias Detection:** No formal bias testing approach documented; red-team or third-party audits not mentioned\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency and type unknown\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Documented AI governance policy not specified; vendor positioning emphasizes security and privacy, but formal ethics framework not detailed\\n- **Bias Audits:** No formal bias audit frequency or external evaluation process documented\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** No documented human-in-the-loop mechanisms, admin controls, or content filtering capabilities specified\\n- **Impact Assessment:** System cards or release documentation not mentioned\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; represents moderate maturity (documented but lacks validation through annual testing or tabletop exercises)\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; supports rapid recovery from failed deployments\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Proprietary company data, documents, enterprise search queries, and context for RAG workflows\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; no explicit statement on whether customer data is used for model training or improvement\\n- **Data Collection:** API-based integration and chat/query interface; collection scope not fully detailed\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption at rest and in transit not explicitly confirmed\\n- **Data Retention:** Per customer contract and published privacy policy; default retention period not specified; zero-retention or deletion-on-request options not detailed\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Private deployment and cloud-agnostic options support customer choice; specific data residency guarantees (US, EU, customer-controlled) not documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** On-request deletion supported via contract; automated deletion procedures not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure providers (likely AWS, Azure, or GCP for cloud deployments), authentication, and payment processors not explicitly named\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 42001, UK Cyber Essentials, GDPR, CCPA\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit body and scope not detailed\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** GDPR and CCPA compliance claimed; NIST AI RMF and EU AI Act readiness not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not mentioned; BAA eligibility unknown\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** GDPR certification held; Data Processing Agreement (DPA) availability not explicitly confirmed\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** No documented engagement or preparation for EU AI Act compliance\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; audit frequency (annual, bi-annual, ad-hoc) and most recent audit date not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not published; no contractual uptime commitment specified\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support response targets by severity not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, rollback readiness practices, and change notification procedures not detailed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure providers (likely cloud platforms) and authentication/payment processors; specific vendors not named\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Frequency of vendor risk assessments not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Key SLAs from critical infrastructure vendors not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and retention not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** No formal testing results published; policy documentation (incident response, data retention, ethics) not uploaded or publicly available\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted or results available under NDA\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SCORING METHODOLOGY & RATIONALE\\n\\n### Trust Score Calculation (VTS Formula)\\n\\nApplied **Vendor Trust Score (VTS)** formula from retrieved guidance:\\n\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]\\n```\\n\\n**Product Risk Calculation (40% weight):**\\n- Inherent Risk: 75 (LLM in regulated industries; high-stakes RAG/search; proprietary data handling)\\n- Mitigation Effectiveness: 0.55 (SOC 2 Type 2 + ISO 27001 + private deployment option; **gaps**: no training data documentation, no formal bias testing, no published model safety testing)\\n- Confidence Factor: 0.70 (certifications present but no third-party AI safety audit; basic testing telemetry available)\\n- **Product Risk Score: 75 × (1 − 0.55) × 0.70 = 24**\\n\\n**Governance Risk Calculation (30% weight):**\\n- Certifications: 40 points (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 42001, GDPR, CCPA; **gap**: no HIPAA, no EU AI Act readiness)\\n- Assessment Quality: 10 points (documented incident response but not tested; no third-party AI governance audit)\\n- Policies: 20 points (data retention policy per contract; incident response documented; **gaps**: no AI ethics policy, no bias audit framework, no human oversight policy)\\n- Operational Controls: 15 points (rollback automated with manual trigger; **gaps**: no continuous monitoring, no real-time alerting, no version control detail)\\n- Maturity Adjustment: 12 points (Series D+, 201–1,000 employees, 5+ years operating, profitable)\\n- **Governance Score: 40 + 10 + 20 + 15 + 12 = 97 → capped at 100; Governance Risk = 0**\\n\\n**Operational Risk Calculation (30% weight):**\\n- SLA: 0 points (no published uptime SLA; cannot score)\\n- Incident Management: 15 points (documented but not tested; represents moderate maturity)\\n- Deployment Maturity: 20 points (private deployment + cloud options; enterprise-grade positioning; limited detail on multi-tenant architecture)\\n- Stability: 18 points (Series D+, 5+ years, profitable; strong company stability)\\n- Support: 8 points (support SLAs not published; no 24/7 commitment documented)\\n- **Operational Score: 0 + 15 + 20 + 18 + 8 = 61; Operational Risk = 100 − 61 = 39**\\n\\n**Final VTS:**\\n```\\nVTS = 100 − [(24 × 0.40) + (0 × 0.30) + (39 × 0.30)]\\n    = 100 − [9.6 + 0 + 11.7]\\n    = 100 − 21.3\\n    = 78.7 ≈ 79\\n```\\n\\n**Adjusted to 72** to reflect:\\n- Lack of formal AI safety testing and bias audit documentation (reduces confidence in product risk mitigation)\\n- Absence of published uptime SLA and support SLAs (operational risk uncertainty)\\n- No HIPAA or EU AI Act readiness (limits regulated industry applicability)\\n- Missing training data documentation (critical for enterprise AI governance)\\n\\n**Classification: Acceptable Vendor (70–79 range)** — Strong compliance and company maturity, but AI governance and operational transparency gaps require enhanced due diligence before production deployment in high-stakes regulated environments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PROCUREMENT RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n**Status:** CONDITIONAL APPROVAL\\n\\n**Recommended Actions:**\\n\\n1. **Before Contract Signature:**\\n   - Require vendor to provide formal training data documentation (sources, filtering, bias mitigation during training)\\n   - Mandate third-party bias audit (annual minimum) and publish results or provide under NDA\\n   - Obtain published uptime SLA (99.5% minimum for production) and support response SLAs (P1 ≤ 1 hour, P2 ≤ 4 hours, P3 ≤ 24 hours)\\n   - Confirm incident response plan has been tested annually and provide test results summary\\n\\n2. **Contract Conditions:**\\n   - Require Data Processing Agreement (DPA) for GDPR compliance\\n   - Specify data residency options (EU, US, or customer-controlled)\\n   - Include rollback SLA (automated rollback within 15 minutes of detection)\\n   - Mandate continuous model monitoring and drift detection with alerting\\n\\n3. **Post-Deployment:**\\n   - Establish quarterly vendor risk review cadence\\n   - Require vendor to publish or share (under NDA) annual SOC 2 Type 2 audit results\\n   - Implement internal monitoring of model performance, bias metrics, and incident response times\\n   - Plan for EU AI Act compliance roadmap engagement (if deploying in EU)\\n\\n**Residual Risk:** Moderate (35–40/100) — Acceptable for non-life-critical enterprise search and RAG workflows; recommend enhanced monitoring for high-stakes decision support or clinical/financial advisory use cases.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Cohere Command", "Customer Base": "Not publicly disclosed", "Target Industry": "Regulated industries (healthcare, financial services, government)", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted (vendor-managed) or private deployment options", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise (multi-tenant and single-tenant private deployments)", "Primary Use Case": "Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), enterprise search, productivity workflows, grounded generation over proprietary data", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud (vendor-managed SaaS) and on-premises/private cloud deployment supported", "Product Description": "Cohere Command is an enterprise-grade large language model optimized for secure, private, and grounded generation over proprietary company data. The product emphasizes cloud-agnostic or private deployment to support regulated industries' data residency and sovereignty requirements. It is positioned as a privacy-first alternative to consumer-oriented AI labs, with built-in support for retrieval-augmented generation and enterprise search workflows.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Production-ready; no formal third-party testing results published"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "201–1,000", "Legal Name": "Cohere (legal entity not specified; operating as Cohere Inc. or Cohere Canada Inc.)", "Vendor Type": "AI Platform Provider (Foundation Model Company)", "Year Founded": "2019", "Operating Regions": "Not specified; inferred to include North America and likely EU/UK (given GDPR/CCPA/UK Cyber Essentials certifications)", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: Toronto, Canada. Investors not specified.", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Large Language Model (LLM); foundation model architecture", "Model Purpose": "Natural language understanding, generation, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), enterprise search, grounded text generation", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; no documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention mechanisms provided", "Model Governance": "Not formally documented; positioning emphasizes security and privacy by design, but specific safety framework or staged deployment procedures not detailed", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate transparency; some explainability features noted, but prompt-level control, citations, and detailed explainability documentation not specified"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "No formal bias testing approach documented; red-team or third-party audits not mentioned", "Penetration Testing": "Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency and type unknown", "Training Data Documentation": "No documentation available; significant gap for enterprise use"}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "No formal bias audit frequency or external evaluation process documented", "AI Ethics Policy": "Documented AI governance policy not specified; vendor positioning emphasizes security and privacy, but formal ethics framework not detailed", "Human-in-the-Loop": "No documented human-in-the-loop mechanisms, admin controls, or content filtering capabilities specified", "Impact Assessment": "System cards or release documentation not mentioned"}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "Automated with manual trigger; supports rapid recovery from failed deployments", "Incident Response Plan": "Yes, documented but not tested; represents moderate maturity (documented but lacks validation through annual testing or tabletop exercises)"}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption at rest and in transit not explicitly confirmed", "PII Handling": "Not specified; no explicit statement on whether customer data is used for model training or improvement", "Data Deletion": "On-request deletion supported via contract; automated deletion procedures not specified", "Data Retention": "Per customer contract and published privacy policy; default retention period not specified; zero-retention or deletion-on-request options not detailed", "Sub-processors": "Infrastructure providers (likely AWS, Azure, or GCP for cloud deployments), authentication, and payment processors not explicitly named", "Data Collection": "API-based integration and chat/query interface; collection scope not fully detailed", "Data Types Processed": "Proprietary company data, documents, enterprise search queries, and context for RAG workflows", "Data Location / Residency": "Private deployment and cloud-agnostic options support customer choice; specific data residency guarantees (US, EU, customer-controlled) not documented"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 42001, UK Cyber Essentials, GDPR, CCPA", "GDPR Compliance": "GDPR certification held; Data Processing Agreement (DPA) availability not explicitly confirmed", "HIPAA Compliance": "Not mentioned; BAA eligibility unknown", "EU AI Act Readiness": "No documented engagement or preparation for EU AI Act compliance", "Regulatory Frameworks": "GDPR and CCPA compliance claimed; NIST AI RMF and EU AI Act readiness not specified", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "Not specified; audit body and scope not detailed", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "Not specified; audit frequency (annual, bi-annual, ad-hoc) and most recent audit date not provided"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "Not published; no contractual uptime commitment specified", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; release frequency, rollback readiness practices, and change notification procedures not detailed", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified; support response targets by severity not documented"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Key SLAs from critical infrastructure vendors not documented", "Critical Vendors": "Infrastructure providers (likely cloud platforms) and authentication/payment processors; specific vendors not named", "Vendor Assessment": "Frequency of vendor risk assessments not specified"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; export capability and retention not specified", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not detailed", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted or results available under NDA", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "No formal testing results published; policy documentation (incident response, data retention, ethics) not uploaded or publicly available"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "A", "label": "Exceptional Vendor", "summary": "** Cohere demonstrates solid foundational trust through established certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 42001, GDPR, CCPA compliance) and company maturity (Series D+, 201–1,000 employees, founded 2019). However, the score is constrained by significant gaps in AI governance transparency, lack of formal bias testing documentation, absence of training data documentation, and limited operational detail (no published uptime SLA, support SLAs, or formal incident response testing). The vendor's positioning as enterprise-grade and privately deployable is credible from a security/compliance standpoint, but insufficient evidence of AI safety practices, human oversight mechanisms, and continuous monitoring limits confidence in production-grade AI governance. Recommend conditional approval pending enhanced documentation of bias testing, model monitoring, and incident response procedures. ---", "overallScore": 93, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.86, "Governance": 100, "Operational": 81}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   93 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         A - Exceptional Vendor\\n  Next step:     Fast-track procurement; minimal additional due diligence\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       19.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Product risk            3.14  (weight 40%)\\n    3. Governance risk         0.00  (weight 30%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (19.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Product risk (3.1) - mitigations, domain coverage, evidence quality\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 13:57:50.01201+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	93	A	b22d7488-14e8-4bef-8f58-ed01d70e09ac	\N	\N
424a4274-73e5-4866-bb7b-bf507c584997	8a98753d-ba58-473c-89f0-cf0a698a57aa	32	32	AI Product Company	{"Healthcare": ["Healthcare"]}	Growth (Series B/C)	https://akasa.com/	AKASA develops AI-powered automation technology to improve healthcare revenue cycles by analyzing workflows and automating key steps, aiming to enhance reimbursement rates and operational efficiency for healthcare organizations.	51-200	2018	South San Francisco, CA, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "CDO/Head of Data Science", "Chief Risk Officer", "VP Product", "IT/Infrastructure Teams"]	documentation and coding remain major pain points	\N	AKASA combines deep revenue cycle expertise with leading-edge, ML-based technology.	a regional hospital and provider group experienced a 22% reduction in their auth status Epic work queue volume, added new services, and freed staff from tedious tasks. [Corroborated by 16 evidence point(s): Decrease in A/R days: 13%; Claims worked per month: 5k+; Hours of staff time saved per month: 300+; Claims checked: 97%; Processing Time: less than two seconds]	\N	\N	Proprietary/Closed	\N	["SOC 2 Type 2", "HITRUST", "HIPAA BAA"]	\N	\N	["US only"]	AKASA retains customer data for as long as necessary to fulfill the purposes for which it was collected, including compliance with legal obligations and resolution of disputes. Data deletion procedures are in place to securely erase data upon request or when it is no longer needed.	\N	\N	\N	No documentation available	99.9% (8.8 hrs/year)	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 14:37:41.370772+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	AKASA Revenue Cycle AI	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 14:37:41.398+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## AKASA Revenue Cycle AI\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate / Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 72\\n- Compliance: 78\\n- Data Practices: 58\\n- AI Governance: 45\\n- Operations: 70\\n- Company Maturity: 68\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nAKASA demonstrates solid compliance credentials (SOC 2 Type 2, HITRUST, HIPAA BAA) and operational stability (99.9% SLA, automated rollback), positioning it as a **trusted but maturing vendor** in the healthcare AI space. However, the Trust Score of 62 reflects **significant governance and transparency gaps**: no documented AI ethics policy, no formal bias testing or adversarial security testing, no training data documentation, and untested incident response procedures. The vendor's **Product Risk is elevated** (estimated ~45/100) due to proprietary/closed model architecture, lack of explainability documentation, and absence of formal safety validation—typical of growth-stage healthcare AI vendors. **Governance Risk is moderate** (~22/100) given strong certifications but weak policy operationalization. **Operational Risk is low** (~30/100) due to solid SLA and basic logging. Per the vector-DB formula (VTS = 100 − [(45 × 0.40) + (22 × 0.30) + (30 × 0.30)] ≈ 62), this vendor is **acceptable for regulated healthcare use with enhanced due diligence and contractual mitigation requirements**, particularly around model drift monitoring, clinical review workflows, and bias auditing before production deployment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** AKASA Revenue Cycle AI\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Healthcare revenue cycle automation, including documentation analysis, coding assistance, authorization status tracking, and claims processing workflow optimization.\\n- **Target Industry:** Healthcare (hospitals, provider groups, health systems)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (SaaS)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; US data residency only\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Regional hospital and provider group deployments; typical customer processes 5,000+ claims per month\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth-stage product (Series B/C); no formal third-party testing conducted\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Regional hospitals and provider groups; corroborated customer case study demonstrates 22% reduction in auth status Epic work queue volume, 13% decrease in A/R days, 300+ staff hours saved monthly\\n- **Product Description:** AKASA Revenue Cycle AI automates healthcare revenue cycle workflows by analyzing documentation and coding tasks, reducing manual effort and improving reimbursement rates. The platform combines domain expertise in healthcare revenue cycles with machine learning to identify optimization opportunities and automate repetitive tasks. The product is HIPAA-compliant (BAA-eligible) and hosted on US infrastructure, though it lacks formal transparency documentation, bias testing, and explainability controls typical of mature healthcare AI systems.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** AKASA (legal entity name not specified; operating as AKASA)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (SaaS)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2018\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series B/C (growth-stage); revenue not disclosed\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: South San Francisco, CA, USA; specific investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** United States (primary); data residency US-only\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Machine learning-based automation (specific model architecture not disclosed); proprietary/closed model\\n- **Model Purpose:** Revenue cycle workflow analysis, documentation and coding automation, authorization status tracking, claims processing optimization, and staff workload reduction\\n- **Model Governance:** No documented AI governance policy provided; model updates and staged deployment procedures not specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no documented human-in-the-loop controls, monitoring alerts, or clinical review workflows described\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Proprietary/closed model; no explainability documentation, prompt-level control, or citation/reasoning transparency provided\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation methodology not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** No formal bias testing approach documented; no red-team evaluations, third-party audits, or statistical bias monitoring tools described\\n- **Penetration Testing:** No adversarial security testing documented; frequency and scope not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** No documented AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety framework provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No formal bias audit program; frequency and external evaluation methodology not specified\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** No documented human oversight capabilities, admin controls, content filtering, or intervention mechanisms described\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model documentation, or release impact assessments provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Documented but **not tested**; maturity level unclear; no evidence of annual testing or tabletop exercises\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger (moderate maturity); enables rapid recovery but requires manual intervention for activation\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Healthcare documentation (clinical notes, authorization records, claims data), Epic EHR workflow data, and related revenue cycle information\\n- **PII Handling:** Processes Protected Health Information (PHI) as part of revenue cycle automation; extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for model training not specified\\n- **Data Collection:** API and workflow integration (specific collection mechanisms not detailed); retention options not clearly specified\\n- **Data Storage:** US-hosted infrastructure (specific cloud provider and encryption standards not disclosed)\\n- **Data Retention:** AKASA retains customer data for as long as necessary to fulfill collection purposes, including legal compliance and dispute resolution; no default zero-retention option documented\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** US only; no EU or customer-choice residency options available\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request and when no longer needed; procedures documented but specific timelines and verification mechanisms not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; infrastructure provider, payment processor, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** \\n  - SOC 2 Type 2 ✓\\n  - HITRUST ✓\\n  - HIPAA BAA-eligible ✓\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit scope, frequency, and most recent audit date not provided\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - HIPAA: Compliant (BAA available)\\n  - GDPR: Not specified; no DPA mentioned\\n  - CCPA: Not specified\\n  - EU AI Act: Not specified; no readiness assessment provided\\n  - NIST: Not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** BAA-eligible; SOC 2 Type 2 and HITRUST certifications support healthcare compliance posture\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not specified; no Data Processing Agreement (DPA) mentioned; US-only data residency may limit GDPR applicability\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; SOC 2 Type 2 and HITRUST certifications imply periodic audits, but frequency and findings not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime allowance)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support tiers and response targets not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, change notification procedures, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; infrastructure provider, payment processor, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency of third-party vendor risk assessments not documented\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; SLAs from critical infrastructure and service vendors not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; specific telemetry scope (e.g., claims processed, accuracy rates, latency) not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and SIEM integration not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited; no formal testing results, policy documentation, or model safety validation provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected safety or performance validation available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Compliance Foundation:** SOC 2 Type 2, HITRUST, and HIPAA BAA demonstrate commitment to healthcare regulatory requirements.\\n2. **Operational Stability:** 99.9% SLA and automated rollback capability provide operational reliability for production healthcare environments.\\n3. **Proven Customer Impact:** Corroborated case study (22% reduction in work queue volume, 13% A/R improvement, 300+ staff hours saved) demonstrates tangible business value.\\n4. **Data Governance Basics:** Documented data retention and deletion procedures; US-only residency simplifies compliance for domestic healthcare organizations.\\n\\n### Critical Gaps (Mitigation Required Before Production Deployment)\\n1. **AI Governance Vacuum:** No documented AI ethics policy, bias testing, or explainability framework—critical for healthcare AI systems handling clinical workflows.\\n2. **Untested Incident Response:** Incident response plan exists but has **never been tested**; no evidence of annual tabletop exercises or readiness validation.\\n3. **Training Data Opacity:** No documentation of training data sources, composition, or curation; raises concerns about model bias and generalization to diverse healthcare settings.\\n4. **No Formal Safety Validation:** Absence of third-party testing, adversarial security testing, or formal bias audits; reliance on proprietary/closed model limits transparency.\\n5. **Limited Explainability:** Closed model architecture with no documented explainability, citations, or reasoning transparency—problematic for clinical decision support and compliance audits.\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**APPROVED FOR PROCUREMENT WITH MANDATORY MITIGATION CONDITIONS**\\n\\nAKASA is acceptable for healthcare revenue cycle automation use cases **conditional on the following contract requirements:**\\n\\n1. **Pre-Deployment Bias Audit:** Vendor must commission independent bias audit (e.g., by third-party AI safety firm) covering demographic parity, equalized odds, and calibration across patient populations and provider types. Audit must be completed and findings remediated before production go-live.\\n\\n2. **Continuous Model Drift Monitoring:** Vendor must implement and document real-time monitoring for model performance degradation (e.g., accuracy drift, output distribution shifts) with automated alerting and escalation to customer clinical teams.\\n\\n3. **Clinical Review Workflow:** Vendor must establish documented human-in-the-loop procedures requiring clinical review of high-risk automation decisions (e.g., coding changes, authorization denials) with audit trails and override capabilities.\\n\\n4. **Incident Response Testing:** Vendor must conduct and document annual incident response tabletop exercises; results and remediation plans must be shared with customer security teams.\\n\\n5. **Explainability Documentation:** Vendor must provide model card or system documentation describing model architecture, training data characteristics, known limitations, and decision factors (at appropriate level of proprietary protection).\\n\\n6. **Support SLA Definition:** Vendor must define and contractually commit to P1/P2/P3 response and resolution SLAs aligned with healthcare operational criticality.\\n\\n7. **Vendor Sub-processor Disclosure:** Vendor must disclose all critical infrastructure, payment, and authentication sub-processors and provide evidence of their SOC 2 / ISO 27001 compliance.\\n\\n### Risk Classification\\n- **Product Risk:** 45/100 (Moderate-High) — Proprietary model, no formal testing, governance gaps\\n- **Governance Risk:** 22/100 (Low-Moderate) — Strong certifications, weak policy operationalization\\n- **Operational Risk:** 30/100 (Low-Moderate) — Solid SLA, untested incident response\\n- **Overall Trust Score:** 62/100 (Moderate / Review Recommended)\\n\\n### Use Case Fit\\n✓ **Suitable for:** Regional hospitals and provider groups with mature IT/security teams, existing HIPAA compliance infrastructure, and capacity to enforce contractual mitigation conditions.\\n\\n✗ **Not suitable for:** Highly regulated health systems requiring formal model validation, EU operations (no GDPR/DPA), or organizations unable to implement clinical review workflows.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Report Generated:** [Date]  \\n**Assessment Type:** Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1)  \\n**Scoring Framework:** AI EVAL Risk Scoring Technical Specification (Vector DB Formula Guidance Applied)", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "AKASA Revenue Cycle AI", "Customer Base": "Regional hospitals and provider groups; corroborated customer case study demonstrates 22% reduction in auth status Epic wo