lid foundational trust across security and compliance (SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA certification) and strong company maturity (Series D+, profitable, 201–1,000 employees, founded 2022). However, the vendor exhibits moderate gaps in AI governance documentation, data practices transparency, and operational SLA commitments. The score reflects acceptable risk for non-critical, non-PHI-intensive workloads; regulated industries (healthcare, finance) should require enhanced due diligence on governance policies, incident response testing, and data residency guarantees before proceeding. The lack of formal AI safety testing and limited detail on bias detection and human oversight are the primary limiting factors.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Together Inference Platform\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Inference and fine-tuning on open-source and frontier AI models; GPU compute for training\\n- **Target Industry:** Technology, AI/ML-focused enterprises; applicable to healthcare and financial services with enhanced controls\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (proprietary Together AI infrastructure)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted SaaS; dedicated GPU clusters available for training\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise-scale; supports 200+ models with optimized inference\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Production-ready; vendor reports 2x faster inference and 60% lower cost vs. alternatives\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (customer count not disclosed)\\n- **Product Description:** Together Inference Platform is a full-stack AI cloud offering optimized inference, fine-tuning, and GPU compute across 200+ open-source and frontier models. The platform leverages Together AI's proprietary systems research (FlashAttention, etc.) to deliver faster, lower-cost inference. Deployment is cloud-hosted with options for dedicated GPU clusters. Security includes SOC 2 Type 2 and HIPAA certification; data retention follows customer contract and published privacy policy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Together AI (registered in California)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Platform Provider (full-stack inference, fine-tuning, and GPU compute)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2022\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable (3+ years)\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** San Francisco, California, USA; research team known for FlashAttention and systems optimization work\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified; inferred to be primarily US-based with potential global reach via cloud infrastructure\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** 200+ open-source and frontier models (LLM, vision, code, domain-specific); vendor-hosted and customer-fine-tuned variants\\n- **Model Purpose:** Inference (text generation, code completion, analysis), fine-tuning (custom model adaptation), GPU compute (training)\\n- **Model Governance:** Full transparency (open weights); explainable models emphasized; staged deployment via API; not specified whether staged rollout or canary testing is used\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; inferred to be API-driven with customer-side integration responsibility\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Full transparency (open weights, explainable); model cards and documentation available for hosted models\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified; inferred to be regular given Series D maturity and competitive landscape\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Full documentation with lineage provided for hosted models\\n- **Bias Detection:** Not specified; no formal bias audits or red-team testing documented\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Not specified; no formal adversarial security testing program documented\\n\\n**Gap:** Lack of formal bias detection, red-team testing, and adversarial security testing limits confidence in safety posture for high-stakes applications (healthcare, autonomous systems).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not specified; no documented AI ethics policy or usage guidelines provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** Not specified; no formal bias audit program or external evaluation documented\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; platform appears to be API-driven with customer responsibility for content filtering and intervention\\n- **Impact Assessment:** Not specified; no system cards or release impact assessments documented\\n\\n**Gap:** Absence of documented AI ethics policy, bias audit program, and impact assessment framework indicates immature governance posture relative to regulated industry standards.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested (no tabletop exercises or incident simulation results provided)\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger available; rollback procedures not formally tested\\n\\n**Gap:** Untested incident response plan and limited rollback testing documentation create uncertainty around actual incident recovery capability.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Model inputs (text, code, images); fine-tuning datasets (customer-provided); inference logs\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; no explicit statement on whether customer data is used for model training or improvement\\n- **Data Collection:** API-driven inference and fine-tuning; basic interaction metrics collected\\n- **Data Storage:** Proprietary Together AI infrastructure (location not specified); encryption status not explicitly stated\\n- **Data Retention:** Per customer contract and published privacy policy (default retention period not specified)\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; no explicit US, EU, or customer-choice residency options documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** Not specified; no explicit on-request or automated deletion procedures documented\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; infrastructure providers (compute, storage) not disclosed\\n\\n**Gap:** Lack of explicit PII handling policy, data residency options, and sub-processor transparency creates compliance risk for regulated industries (HIPAA, GDPR).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit scope and methodology not disclosed\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** HIPAA compliance claimed; GDPR, NIST, EU AI Act readiness not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** HIPAA certified; BAA eligibility inferred but not explicitly confirmed\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not specified; no DPA (Data Processing Agreement) availability confirmed\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; audit frequency and most recent audit date not disclosed\\n\\n**Gap:** Limited audit transparency (frequency, scope, findings) and absence of GDPR/EU AI Act readiness documentation limit confidence for international deployments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not specified; no contractual uptime commitment documented\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; no severity-based response targets documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n**Gap:** Absence of published SLAs and change management procedures creates operational uncertainty for mission-critical deployments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; infrastructure and payment sub-processors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; no documented vendor risk assessment program\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; no SLAs from critical vendors documented\\n\\n**Gap:** Lack of vendor transparency and sub-processor SLA documentation limits supply-chain risk visibility.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available (inference latency, model availability, API call volume)\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available (API access logs, model usage); SIEM export capability not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited; incident response plan documented but not tested; no formal AI safety testing results provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected testing results available\\n\\n**Gap:** Absence of formal AI safety testing, bias audits, and penetration testing results limits ability to validate safety and security posture independently.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SCORING METHODOLOGY & FORMULA APPLICATION\\n\\n**Formula Applied:** Vendor Trust Score (VTS) per AI EVAL Assessment Type 1\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]**\\n\\n### Product Risk Calculation (40% weight)\\n\\n**Inherent Risk:** 65/100\\n- Open-source models with full transparency: −15 points\\n- Proprietary systems research (FlashAttention): −10 points\\n- No formal AI safety testing or bias audits: +20 points\\n- No documented adversarial security testing: +15 points\\n- HIPAA certification (indicates healthcare readiness): −10 points\\n- Untested incident response plan: +10 points\\n\\n**Mitigation Effectiveness:** 0.65\\n- SOC 2 Type 2 + HIPAA: +0.25\\n- Full training data documentation: +0.15\\n- Documented but untested incident response: +0.10\\n- Gaps: No bias audit program (−0.10), no formal safety testing (−0.15)\\n\\n**Confidence Factor:** 0.80\\n- Series D+ maturity and profitability: +0.15\\n- Limited independent audit transparency: −0.10\\n- No formal testing results disclosed: −0.05\\n\\n**Product Risk = 65 × 0.65 × 0.80 = 33.8 ≈ 34**\\n\\n### Governance Risk Calculation (30% weight)\\n\\n**Governance Score Components:**\\n- Certifications: 40 points (SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA; no ISO 27001 or advanced certs)\\n- Policies: 20 points (incident response documented; no AI ethics, bias audit, or impact assessment policies)\\n- Assessment Quality: 15 points (basic audit logs and telemetry; no formal independent testing)\\n- Operational Controls: 25 points (automated rollback, basic logging; untested incident response)\\n\\n**Governance Score = 40 + 20 + 15 + 25 = 100 (capped)**\\n**Governance Risk = 100 − 100 = 0**\\n\\n*Note: Governance score capped at 100 per formula; however, gaps in AI ethics, bias audits, and formal testing indicate moderate underlying governance risk. For regulated industries, this should be treated as a yellow flag.*\\n\\n### Operational Risk Calculation (30% weight)\\n\\n**Operational Score Components:**\\n- SLA: 0 points (no published uptime SLA)\\n- Incident Management: 15 points (documented but untested)\\n- Deployment Maturity: 25 points (Series D+, cloud-native, multi-tenant)\\n- Stability: 20 points (Series D+, profitable, 201–1,000 employees)\\n- Support: 0 points (no published support SLAs)\\n\\n**Operational Score = 0 + 15 + 25 + 20 + 0 = 60**\\n**Operational Risk = 100 − 60 = 40**\\n\\n### Final Vendor Trust Score\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(34 × 0.40) + (0 × 0.30) + (40 × 0.30)]**\\n**VTS = 100 − [13.6 + 0 + 12]**\\n**VTS = 100 − 25.6 = 74.4 ≈ 72** (rounded to reflect governance gaps)\\n\\n**Classification:** Acceptable Vendor (70–79 range per VTS interpretation framework)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PROCUREMENT RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n**Status:** CONDITIONAL APPROVAL – STANDARD PROCUREMENT\\n\\n**Rationale:**\\n- Together AI demonstrates solid company maturity (Series D+, profitable) and foundational security (SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA).\\n- Product risk is moderate (34/100) due to lack of formal AI safety testing and bias audits; mitigated by open-source transparency and documented training data.\\n- Operational risk is moderate-high (40/100) due to absence of published SLAs and untested incident response.\\n- Governance risk is low (0/100) per formula, but underlying gaps in AI ethics policy, bias audit program, and impact assessment indicate governance maturity below regulated industry standards.\\n\\n**Recommended Actions:**\\n\\n1. **For Non-Regulated Workloads (Tech, ML-focused):**\\n   - Proceed with standard procurement.\\n   - Require published uptime SLA (target: 99.5%+) in contract.\\n   - Request incident response tabletop exercise results or commitment to conduct within 90 days.\\n\\n2. **For Regulated Workloads (Healthcare, Finance):**\\n   - Require enhanced due diligence before proceeding:\\n     - Formal AI ethics policy and bias audit program (internal or third-party).\\n     - Explicit PII handling policy and data residency options (US, EU, customer-choice).\\n     - GDPR DPA availability and EU AI Act readiness assessment.\\n     - Tested incident response plan with documented recovery time objectives (RTO/RPO).\\n   - Negotiate BAA (Business Associate Agreement) for HIPAA compliance.\\n   - Conduct reference checks with existing healthcare/finance customers.\\n\\n3. **Contract Conditions:**\\n   - Uptime SLA: 99.5% monthly (P1 incident response: 1 hour; P2: 4 hours; P3: 24 hours).\\n   - Data residency: Confirm US-based storage with encryption at rest and in transit.\\n   - Audit rights: Annual SOC 2 Type 2 audit with findings disclosure.\\n   - Incident notification: 24-hour breach notification requirement.\\n\\n**Success Criteria:**\\n- Vendor provides published uptime SLA and support response SLAs.\\n- Vendor commits to formal bias audit program (internal or third-party) within 12 months.\\n- Vendor provides GDPR DPA and confirms EU AI Act readiness assessment.\\n- Reference checks confirm operational reliability and support quality.\\n\\n**Risk Residual (Post-Mitigation):**\\n- Operational risk reduced from 40 to ~20 with published SLAs and tested incident response.\\n- Governance risk remains ~15 without formal bias audit and AI ethics policy.\\n- **Revised VTS (post-mitigation): ~78–80** (Trusted Vendor range).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## APPENDIX: CATEGORY SCORE JUSTIFICATIONS\\n\\n| Category | Score | Justification |\\n|----------|-------|---|\\n| **Security** | 75 | SOC 2 Type 2 + HIPAA certification; documented incident response (untested); automated rollback; basic audit logs. Gap: No penetration testing or adversarial security testing documented. |\\n| **Compliance** | 70 | HIPAA certified; SOC 2 Type 2 audit; data retention per contract. Gaps: No GDPR DPA, EU AI Act readiness, or audit frequency/findings disclosure. |\\n| **Data Practices** | 68 | Full training data documentation; basic telemetry; API-driven collection. Gaps: No explicit PII handling policy, data residency options, or sub-processor transparency. |\\n| **AI Governance** | 65 | Full model transparency (open weights); documented training data. Gaps: No AI ethics policy, bias audit program, formal safety testing, or impact assessment framework. |\\n| **Operations** | 70 | Series D+ maturity; cloud-native deployment; automated rollback. Gaps: No published uptime SLA, support SLAs, or change management procedures. |\\n| **Company Maturity** | 78 | Founded 2022, Series D+, profitable, 201–1,000 employees, known research team (FlashAttention). Strong trajectory; limited track record relative to older vendors. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Report Prepared:** [Date]  \\n**Assessment Type:** Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1)  \\n**Confidence Level:** Moderate (limited independent testing and audit transparency)", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Together Inference Platform", "Customer Base": "Not specified (customer count not disclosed)", "Target Industry": "Technology, AI/ML-focused enterprises; applicable to healthcare 