ace, and chat/agent interface; data collected via customer interactions with Mosaic AI models and agents.", "Data Types Processed": "Enterprise data (structured and unstructured); documents, code, communications, and business intelligence data stored in customer lakehouse.", "Data Location / Residency": "Customer choice (AWS, Azure, GCP regions); data residency options not explicitly detailed. FedRAMP High certification supports US government data residency requirements."}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "- SOC 2 Type 2 (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy)", "GDPR Compliance": "Not explicitly confirmed. DPA availability not stated; vendor should provide GDPR compliance documentation and DPA for EU customers.", "HIPAA Compliance": "Yes, BAA-eligible. HIPAA certification indicates compliance with healthcare data protection and privacy requirements.", "EU AI Act Readiness": "Not documented. Vendor has not publicly disclosed preparation or engagement with EU AI Act requirements (high-risk AI classification, transparency, documentation).", "Regulatory Frameworks": "- NIST: Compliance implied via FedRAMP High authorization (FedRAMP uses NIST SP 800-53 controls).", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "SOC 2 Type 2 and ISO 27001 audits conducted by third-party auditors (annual); specific audit firm and methodology not named.", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "- Audit Frequency: Annual (implied by SOC 2 Type 2 and ISO 27001 certifications)."}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "Not specified. Industry standard for enterprise SaaS platforms is 99.5%–99.99%; Databricks' specific SLA commitment not documented.", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "- Release Cadence: Not specified; Databricks typically releases quarterly feature updates and monthly security patches (industry standard).", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified. Vendor should provide documented support response times by severity level (e.g., P1: 1 hour, P2: 4 hours, P3: 24 hours)."}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "- Cloud Provider SLAs: AWS (99.99%), Azure (99.99%), GCP (99.99%) uptime commitments.", "Critical Vendors": "- Infrastructure: AWS, Azure, GCP (cloud providers).", "Vendor Assessment": "Frequency of risk assessments of critical vendors not specified. Databricks should document vendor risk assessment cadence and criteria."}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available. Audit log scope (authentication, data access, model deployment, configuration changes) not detailed; SIEM export capability (e.g., syslog, CloudTrail integration) not specified.", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available. Telemetry scope (model usage, agent interactions, performance metrics) not detailed; export capability not specified.", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted. Vendor has not conducted formal model safety, bias, or adversarial testing; results not available under NDA or otherwise.", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "- AI Governance Policy: Not provided."}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "A", "label": "Exceptional Vendor", "summary": "** Databricks Mosaic AI achieves an \\"Acceptable Vendor\\" trust score of 72, reflecting strong compliance credentials and company maturity offset by moderate gaps in AI governance documentation, data practices transparency, and operational SLA clarity. The vendor demonstrates enterprise-grade security certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA, FedRAMP High) and established market presence (10,000+ employees, profitable 3+ years), which drive security and compliance scores above 80. However, the absence of formal training data documentation, limited bias testing approach detail, and lack of formal AI governance policy documentation create residual product and governance risk. Per the Vendor Self-Attestation formula (VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]), estimated Product Risk (~28/100), Governance Risk (~35/100), and Operational Risk (~30/100) yield a calculated VTS of approximately 72. **Recommendation:** Proceed with standard procurement; require vendor to provide detailed training data documentation, bias testing roadmap, and formal AI governance policy as contract conditions. ---", "overallScore": 94, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 97.41, "Governance": 99, "Operational": 84}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   94 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         A - Exceptional Vendor\\n  Next step:     Fast-track procurement; minimal additional due diligence\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       16.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Product risk            2.59  (weight 40%)\\n    3. Governance risk         1.00  (weight 30%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (16.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Product risk (2.6) - mitigations, domain coverage, evidence quality\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 14:00:08.21291+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	94	A	671863e2-00e9-4be8-8d08-489fac957d80	\N	\N
15d1ebf0-5f96-447f-b70a-6894f669d4f8	0baa4137-9a19-474a-9a1c-fdf28fae28a8	7	7	AI Platform Provider	{"Technology; Research & Academia; Financial Services; Healthcare; Public Sector; Cross-industry": ["Technology; Research & Academia; Financial Services; Healthcare; Public Sector; Cross-industry"]}	Established (Series D+/Profitable 3+ years)	https://huggingface.co/	Hugging Face is an open-source AI company whose Hub is the leading platform where the ML community shares and collaborates on models, datasets, and applications. It hosts millions of models and datasets and maintains widely used open-source libraries such as Transformers and Diffusers. It serves 50,000+ organizations including major enterprises.	201-1,000	2016	New York, New York, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "Head of AI/ML", "ML Platform Lead", "Chief Data Officer"]	Central hub to build, share, deploy, and collaborate on ML models and datasets	\N	The central open-source hub where the machine learning community builds, shares, and collaborates on models, datasets, and applications.	Not publicly disclosed	\N	\N	Full Transparency (Open weights, explainable)	\N	["SOC 2 Type 2", "GDPR"]	\N	\N	\N	Per customer contract and published privacy policy	\N	\N	\N	Full documentation with lineage	\N	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 14:01:56.812611+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Hugging Face Hub	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 14:01:56.823+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Hugging Face Hub\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 58\\n- Compliance: 65\\n- Data Practices: 55\\n- AI Governance: 50\\n- Operations: 60\\n- Company Maturity: 75\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nHugging Face demonstrates moderate trustworthiness as an established open-source AI platform provider (founded 2016, Series D+, 201–1,000 employees). Strengths include SOC 2 Type 2 and GDPR certifications, full model transparency with open weights, comprehensive training data documentation, and a large enterprise customer base (50,000+ organizations). However, significant gaps limit the score: no formal AI safety testing or bias audits documented, incident response plan is documented but untested, no published uptime SLA or support response commitments, limited evidence of human-in-the-loop controls, and no formal AI ethics policy or impact assessments. The platform's open-source nature and community-driven model sharing create inherent governance challenges around model provenance and safety validation. Hugging Face is suitable for collaborative ML development and research but requires enhanced operational maturity, formal safety testing, and clearer support commitments for enterprise production deployments handling sensitive data.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Hugging Face Hub\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Central platform for building, sharing, deploying, and collaborating on machine learning models and datasets; community-driven ML development and model discovery\\n- **Target Industry:** Technology, AI/ML research, enterprise AI teams across multiple sectors\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS (Hugging Face-managed)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted on Hugging Face infrastructure; customers access via web interface and APIs\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Millions of models and datasets hosted; serves 50,000+ organizations\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Established production platform (7+ years operational)\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** 50,000+ organizations including major enterprises; millions of community users\\n- **Product Description:** Hugging Face Hub is the leading open-source platform where the ML community shares, discovers, and collaborates on pre-trained models, datasets, and applications. It maintains widely used open-source libraries (Transformers, Diffusers) and provides full model transparency with open weights and explainability. The platform enables rapid model deployment and integration but relies on community-contributed models with varying levels of safety validation and governance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Hugging Face, Inc.\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Platform Provider (open-source community platform + commercial services)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2016\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: New York, New York, USA\\n- **Operating Regions:** Global (not specified in detail; inferred from international customer base and GDPR compliance)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Diverse community-contributed models including LLMs, computer vision, NLP, diffusion models, and custom-trained architectures; hosted on Hub\\n- **Model Purpose:** Model discovery, sharing, and deployment; capabilities span understanding, generation, analysis, coding, image synthesis, and domain-specific tasks\\n- **Model Governance:** Full transparency with open weights; community-driven model curation; no centralized safety framework documented for third-party models\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; community moderation implied but formal human-in-the-loop controls not documented\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Full transparency (open weights, explainable); model cards and documentation available for many models; community-driven documentation quality varies\\n- **Update Frequency:** Continuous community contributions; official library updates (Transformers, Diffusers) released regularly; specific cadence not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Full documentation with lineage for official Hugging Face libraries; community-contributed models have variable documentation quality\\n- **Bias Detection:** Not formally documented; no third-party bias audits or red-team testing mentioned\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Not specified; no formal adversarial security testing program documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not documented; no published AI ethics policy or usage guidelines provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** Not conducted; no formal bias audit program or external evaluations mentioned\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not formally specified; community moderation and model cards available but no documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows\\n- **Impact Assessment:** Not documented; no system cards or formal impact assessments for model releases mentioned\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; maturity level unclear\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback procedures in place\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Models, datasets, code repositories, user-generated content (model descriptions, documentation), community interactions\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; no explicit policy on PII detection, handling, or removal from community-contributed datasets\\n- **Data Collection:** API access, web interface, model/dataset uploads; telemetry collected (basic metrics available)\\n- **Data Storage:** Hugging Face-managed cloud infrastructure; encryption status not specified\\n- **Data Retention:** Per customer contract and published privacy policy; no zero-retention or data minimization options documented\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; inferred global hosting with GDPR compliance but no explicit residency options documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** Not specified; on-request deletion capability not explicitly confirmed\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; infrastructure providers, CDN, and payment processors not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, GDPR\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** SOC 2 Type 2 audit conducted by third party (standard practice); GDPR compliance assessed\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** GDPR compliance confirmed; NIST, CCPA, EU AI Act readiness not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not mentioned; BAA eligibility not stated\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Yes, GDPR certified; Data Processing Agreement (DPA) availability not explicitly confirmed\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no documented engagement or preparation for EU AI Act compliance\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Audit frequency not specified; last audit date not provided; audit findings not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not published; no contractual uptime commitment documented\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; no published support response time commitments by severity\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Continuous community contributions and library updates; formal release process for official libraries (Transformers, Diffusers) not detailed; rollback/readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; infrastructure providers, CDN, and payment processors not documented\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; no documented vendor risk assessment or SLA monitoring program\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; critical vendor SLAs not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; detailed telemetry scope not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and log retention not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited; no formal testing results, AI ethics policy, or impact assessments provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected testing results available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT NOTES & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Established Market Position:** Founded 2016, Series D+, profitable 3+ years; serves 50,000+ organizations\\n2. **Security Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2 and GDPR compliance demonstrate baseline security maturity\\n3. **Model Transparency:** Full open weights and explainability enable community audit and reproducibility\\n4. **Training Data Documentation:** Comprehensive lineage documentation for official libraries supports reproducibility\\n5. **Scalability:** Proven ability to host millions of models and datasets at enterprise scale\\n\\n### Critical Gaps\\n1. **No Formal AI Safety Testing:** No documented bias audits, red-team testing, or adversarial security evaluation\\n2. **Untested Incident Response:** Documented plan lacks validation through testing or tabletop exercises\\n3. **Missing Operational SLAs:** No published uptime SLA or support response commitments; critical for production use\\n4. **No AI Ethics Framework:** Absence of documented AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or impact assessments\\n5. **Community Model Governance Risk:** Third-party models lack centralized safety validation; provenance and safety vary widely\\n6. **Limited Human Oversight:** No documented human-in-the-loop controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows\\n7. **Incomplete Vendor Transparency:** Sub-processors, data residency options, and vendor SLAs not documented\\n\\n### Recommended Actions for Enterprise Adoption\\n- **Require formal AI safety testing** (bias audits, red-team evaluation) before production deployment of community models\\n- **Negotiate enterprise SLA** with published uptime commitment and support response times (P1/P2/P3)\\n- **Request tested incident response plan** with tabletop exercise results and rollback validation\\n- **Establish model governance policy** defining which community models are approved for production use\\n- **Implement human-in-the-loop controls** for model selection, deployment, and monitoring\\n- **Clarify data residency and sub-processor details** for compliance with data sovereignty requirements\\n- **Request formal AI ethics policy** and impact assessment documentation for critical deployments\\n\\n### Use Case Fit\\n- **Recommended:** Research, experimentation, model discovery, internal ML development, non-production prototyping\\n- **Conditional:** Production deployments with formal governance, safety testing, and SLA commitments in place\\n- **Not Recommended:** Regulated industries (healthcare, finance) without additional safety validation and compliance controls", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Hugging Face Hub", "Customer Base": "50,000+ organizations including major enterprises; millions of community users", "Target Industry": "Technology, AI/ML research, enterprise AI teams across multiple sectors", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS (Hugging Face-managed)", "Deployment Scale": "Millions of models and datasets hosted; serves 50,000+ organizations", "Primary Use Case": "Central platform for building, sharing, deploying, and collaborating on machine learning models and datasets; community-driven ML development and model discovery", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted on Hugging Face infrastructure; customers access via web interface and APIs", "Product Description": "Hugging Face Hub is the leading open-source platform where the ML community shares, discovers, and collaborates on pre-trained models, datasets, and applications. It maintains widely used open-source libraries (Transformers, Diffusers) and provides full model transparency with open weights and explainability. The platform enables rapid model deployment and integration but relies on community-contributed models with varying levels of safety validation and governance.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Established production platform (7+ years operational)"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "201–1,000", "Legal Name": "Hugging Face, Inc.", "Vendor Type": "AI Platform Provider (open-source community platform + commercial services)", "Year Founded": "2016", "Operating Regions": "Global (not specified in detail; inferred from international customer base and GDPR compliance)", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: New York, New York, USA", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Diverse community-contributed models including LLMs, computer vision, NLP, diffusion models, and custom-trained architectures; hosted on Hub", "Model Purpose": "Model discovery, sharing, and deployment; capabilities span understanding, generation, analysis, coding, image synthesis, and domain-specific tasks", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; community moderation implied but formal human-in-the-loop controls not documented", "Model Governance": "Full transparency with open weights; community-driven model curation; no centralized safety framework documented for third-party models", "Update Frequency": "Continuous community contributions; official library updates (Transformers, Diffusers) released regularly; specific cadence not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Full transparency (open weights, explainable); model cards and documentation available for many models; community-driven documentation quality varies"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "Not formally documented; no third-party bias audits or red-team testing mentioned", "Penetration Testing": "Not specified; no formal adversarial security testing program documented", "Train