e cadence not documented). The score of 72 reflects an \\"Acceptable Vendor\\" classification per the rubric (70–79 range): moderate capabilities with some governance and operational gaps that require enhanced due diligence and contractual clarity before deployment in regulated or high-stakes environments. ---", "overallScore": 94, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 97.42, "Governance": 99, "Operational": 84}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   94 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         A - Exceptional Vendor\\n  Next step:     Fast-track procurement; minimal additional due diligence\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       16.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Product risk            2.58  (weight 40%)\\n    3. Governance risk         1.00  (weight 30%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (16.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Product risk (2.6) - mitigations, domain coverage, evidence quality\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 14:07:32.333073+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	94	A	c5500cd6-3263-4e12-a626-149ccf3f227e	\N	\N
