 Retention": "Per customer contract and published privacy policy (flexible, customer-controlled)", "Sub-processors": "Not specified; infrastructure, payments, and auth vendors not identified", "Data Collection": "Not specified; inferred: API and platform interface", "Data Types Processed": "Not specified; inferred: structured data (predictive AI), text/documents (generative AI)", "Data Location / Residency": "Not specified; critical gap—vendor emphasizes data sovereignty but specific residency options (US, EU, customer choice) not documented"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA/HITECH, FedRAMP High", "GDPR Compliance": "DPA availability not specified; critical gap for EU data processing", "HIPAA Compliance": "HIPAA/HITECH certified; BAA eligibility inferred but not explicitly confirmed", "EU AI Act Readiness": "Not addressed; no engagement or preparation statement provided", "Regulatory Frameworks": "FedRAMP High (federal government readiness); HIPAA/HITECH (healthcare); SOC 2 Type 2 (general security and availability)", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "Not specified; SOC 2 Type 2 implies annual third-party audit, but audit scope and findings not provided", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "Not specified; SOC 2 Type 2 implies annual audit, but dates and findings not provided"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "Not specified", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; no formal release process or rollback/readiness practices documented", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Not specified; key SLAs from critical vendors not provided", "Critical Vendors": "Not specified; infrastructure, payments, and auth vendors not identified", "Vendor Assessment": "Not specified; frequency of risk assessments of critical vendors not documented"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"SLA": "0 points (not specified; no published uptime commitment)", "Support": "0 points (support SLAs not specified)", "Policies": "20 points (incident response documented but not tested; no formal AI ethics policy)", "Stability": "20 points (12+ years, profitable, Series D+)", "Inherent Risk": "70 (high due to GenAI + regulated data + no formal AI safety testing)", "Certifications": "50 points (SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA, FedRAMP High—strong but no AI-specific certifications)", "Confidence Factor": "0.75 (established vendor, 12+ years; but no formal testing results or third-party AI safety audit)", "Assessment Quality": "15 points (no formal AI governance policy, bias audits, or impact assessments documented)", "Deployment Maturity": "25 points (on-premises, cloud, air-gapped options; enterprise-ready)", "Incident Management": "15 points (documented but not tested)", "Maturity Adjustment": "15 points (12+ years, Series D+, profitable)", "Operational Controls": "25 points (rollback capability, basic logging; but no formal change management or SLAs)", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available", "Mitigation Effectiveness": "0.65 (strong: SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA, FedRAMP High; gaps: no bias testing, no training data documentation, no formal adversarial testing)", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "Limited; no formal AI safety testing results, governance policies, or bias audit documentation provided"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "A", "label": "Exceptional Vendor", "summary": "** H2O.ai demonstrates solid foundational trust through established company maturity (12+ years, Series D+, profitable), strong compliance certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, HIPAA/HITECH, FedRAMP High), and a clear security posture aligned with regulated industry requirements. However, the trust score is constrained by significant gaps in AI governance documentation (no formal AI ethics policy or bias testing approach documented), incomplete data practices transparency (PII handling and data residency options not specified), and absence of formal AI safety testing results. The vendor's emphasis on sovereign and on-premises deployment for data privacy is a material strength, but lack of training data documentation and no formal adversarial testing undermine confidence in AI safety controls. **Recommendation:** Acceptable for regulated industry deployment with enhanced due diligence on AI governance, bias testing protocols, and data handling specifics before contract execution. ---", "overallScore": 94, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.84, "Governance": 100, "Operational": 84}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   94 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         A - Exceptional Vendor\\n  Next step:     Fast-track procurement; minimal additional due diligence\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       16.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Product risk            3.16  (weight 40%)\\n    3. Governance risk         0.00  (weight 30%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (16.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Product risk (3.2) - mitigations, domain coverage, evidence quality\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 14:08:28.336476+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	94	A	41d48a94-b972-4e5d-87aa-a26f85fc8440	\N	\N
fd76703c-8958-4707-9612-f367fa9da148	ba07aaf3-3801-4cf4-9718-495abb08f99b	16	16	AI Platform Provider	{"Autonomous vehicles, financial services, healthcare & life sciences, media/entertainment, public sector, scientific research": ["Autonomous vehicles, financial services, healthcare & life sciences, media/entertainment, public sector, scientific research"]}	Established (Series D+/Profitable 3+ years)	https://wandb.ai	Weights & Biases builds MLOps and AI developer tools used by thousands of businesses and millions of practitioners. The W&B Platform covers ML experiment tracking, hyperparameter tuning, artifact/model versioning and collaborative dashboards, while W&B Weave adds LLM/GenAI tracing, evaluation, and production monitoring. It became part of CoreWeave following its acquisition completed in May 2025.	201-1,000	2017	San Francisco, California, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["VP of Machine Learning/AI", "Head of ML Engineering", "CTO", "Director of Data Science", "ML Platform lead"]	Untracked ML experiments, poor model reproducibility, fragmented ML collaboration, LLM observability and evaluation	\N	An end-to-end AI developer platform that serves as the system of record across both ML model training and LLM/GenAI application development in one collaborative platform.	Not publicly disclosed	\N	\N	Moderate (Some explainability features)	\N	["SOC 2 Type 2", "ISO 27001", "ISO 27017", "ISO 27018", "HIPAA"]	\N	\N	\N	Per customer contract and published privacy policy	\N	\N	\N	Using third-party models (data unknown)	\N	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 14:09:22.984767+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	W&B Platform (Models & Weave)	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 14:09:22.996+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Weights & Biases (W&B Platform: Models & Weave)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 72 (Acceptable Vendor)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 82\\n- Compliance: 85\\n- Data Practices: 68\\n- AI Governance: 60\\n- Operations: 65\\n- Company Maturity: 80\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nWeights & Biases demonstrates solid foundational trust through established company maturity (8+ years, Series D+, profitable), strong security certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001/27017/27018, HIPAA), and comprehensive compliance posture. However, the Trust Score is constrained by moderate gaps in AI governance documentation (no formal documented AI governance policy stated), limited AI safety testing rigor (no formal testing conducted, bias testing approach not specified), and incomplete operational transparency (uptime SLA, support SLAs, and release cadence not disclosed). The platform's role as an MLOps/observability tool—rather than a production AI model—positions it lower-risk than autonomous AI systems, but enterprise customers in regulated sectors will require enhanced due diligence on data handling practices and incident response testing before deployment.\\n\\n**Formula Applied:** VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)], where Product_Risk ≈ 28 (moderate; MLOps platform with third-party model dependencies), Governance_Risk ≈ 40 (gaps in formal AI governance and bias testing), Operational_Risk ≈ 35 (mature company and certifications offset by undisclosed SLAs and untested incident response).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** W&B Platform (Models & Weave)\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** ML experiment tracking, hyperparameter tuning, model versioning, LLM/GenAI tracing, evaluation, and production monitoring for collaborative AI development teams\\n- **Target Industry:** Technology, Financial Services, Healthcare, Life Sciences, and other sectors deploying ML/AI at scale\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS (primary); self-hosted options not specified\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted (AWS/Azure/GCP infrastructure); on-premise deployment not stated\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Thousands of businesses and millions of practitioners globally\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Established product (8+ years in market); production-grade MLOps platform\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Thousands of businesses; millions of individual practitioners using the platform\\n- **Product Description:** W&B Platform is an end-to-end MLOps and AI developer platform serving as a system of record for both ML model training (experiment tracking, hyperparameter tuning, artifact/model versioning) and LLM/GenAI application development (tracing, evaluation, production monitoring). The platform emphasizes collaborative dashboards and reproducibility. W&B Weave extends capabilities to LLM observability and evaluation. The platform integrates with third-party models and relies on external model providers for AI capabilities; W&B itself provides infrastructure, logging, and governance tooling rather than proprietary production models.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Weights & Biases, Inc.\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Platform Provider (MLOps and AI Developer Tools)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2017\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: San Francisco, California, USA; acquired by CoreWeave (completed May 2025)\\n- **Operating Regions:** Global (specific regions not detailed; US-based headquarters)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Platform integrates third-party models (LLMs, custom-trained models); W&B does not develop proprietary production AI models\\n- **Model Purpose:** W&B provides infrastructure for experiment tracking, model versioning, hyperparameter optimization, LLM tracing, and evaluation; does not execute autonomous AI decisions\\n- **Model Governance:** Not formally documented; platform supports governance workflows (versioning, artifact tracking, collaborative review) but vendor's own AI governance policy not specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Platform designed for human-in-the-loop workflows; users retain full control over model training, evaluation, and deployment decisions; W&B provides logging and audit trails for transparency\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate explainability features; platform logs experiments, hyperparameters, and model artifacts for reproducibility and audit; LLM tracing captures prompts and outputs for review\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Using third-party models; training data provenance and documentation delegated to model providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.); W&B does not document or control training data for integrated models\\n- **Bias Detection:** Bias testing approach not specified; no formal bias audits or red-team testing documented\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing approach not specified; no frequency or results disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Documented AI governance policy not stated; platform provides governance tooling (versioning, audit logs, collaborative review) but vendor's own ethics framework not disclosed\\n- **Bias Audits:** No formal bias audits or external evaluations documented\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Platform designed to support human oversight; users control model training, evaluation, and deployment; W&B provides logging and dashboards for review and intervention\\n- **Impact Assessment:** System cards or formal impact assessments not mentioned; platform supports user documentation of experiments and model cards through artifact versioning\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented; however, not tested (no tabletop exercises, simulations, or validation results disclosed)\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated rollback with manual trigger capability for model versions and deployments\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** ML experiment metadata, model artifacts, hyperparameters, training logs, LLM prompts, outputs, and evaluation results; customer code and data passed through platform for tracking and analysis\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; platform may process PII embedded in customer data, code, or LLM interactions; extent of PII filtering or masking not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** API, web interface, SDK integrations; data collected from user experiments, model training runs, and LLM interactions\\n- **Data Storage:** Cloud infrastructure (AWS/Azure/GCP); encryption in transit and at rest assumed (SOC 2 Type 2 certification implies encryption controls) but specific encryption standards not stated\\n- **Data Retention:** Per customer contract and published privacy policy; default retention period not specified; zero-retention or short-retention options not explicitly stated\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; likely US-based (San Francisco headquarters) with potential multi-region options; customer choice or EU residency not confirmed\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (per privacy policy); automated deletion timelines not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure providers (AWS/Azure/GCP); payment processors; authentication providers; specific sub-processor list not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 27017, ISO 27018, HIPAA\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** SOC 2 Type 2 audit conducted by independent third party (standard for SaaS platforms); frequency and last audit date not specified\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** HIPAA compliance certified; GDPR, CCPA, NIST, and EU AI Act readiness not explicitly stated\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** HIPAA certification held; BAA (Business Associate Agreement) availability not confirmed\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not explicitly stated; DPA (Data Processing Agreement) availability not confirmed\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not stated; no engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Audit frequency not specified; last audit date not disclosed; audit findings not available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not specified; no contractual uptime commitment disclosed\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support response targets by severity not disclosed\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, rollback readiness practices, and change notification procedures not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure providers (AWS/Azure/GCP); payment processors; authentication/identity providers; specific vendor names and SLAs not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Frequency of risk assessments of critical vendors not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Key SLAs from critical infrastructure vendors not disclosed; reliance on cloud provider SLAs (e.g., AWS, Azure) assumed but not confirmed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not specified (e.g., user activity, API call volumes, error rates)\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and SIEM integration not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** No formal testing results documented; incident response plan documented but not tested; AI governance policy not publicly disclosed\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected results available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Key Gaps & Recommendations for Enterprise Buyers\\n\\n| Gap | Severity | Recommendation |\\n|-----|----------|-----------------|\\n| No disclosed uptime SLA or support SLAs | High | Require contractual SLA commitments (e.g., 99.9% uptime, P1 response <1 hour) before production deployment |\\n| Incident response plan not tested | High | Request tabletop exercise results or schedule joint incident response drill before go-live |\\n| AI governance policy not documented | Medium | Request formal AI governance framework covering model validation, bias monitoring, and LLM safety practices |\\n| Bias testing approach not specified | Medium | Require bias detection and monitoring roadmap; consider third-party bias audit for LLM tracing features |\\n| Data residency and GDPR/DPA not confirmed | Medium | Confirm DPA availability, EU data residency options, and GDPR compliance before processing EU customer data |\\n| Release cadence and change management not disclosed | Medium | Request change management policy, release schedule, and rollback procedures documentation |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**APPROVED FOR STANDARD PROCUREMENT** with conditions:\\n\\n1. **Require contractual SLA commitments** (uptime, support response times) aligned with customer criticality\\n2. **Obtain tested incident response plan** or schedule joint tabletop exercise\\n3. **Confirm GDPR/DPA compliance** and data residency options if processing EU data\\n4. **Request formal AI governance and bias monitoring roadmap** for LLM tracing features\\n5. **Validate change management and release procedures** with security and operations teams\\n\\n**Rationale:** W&B is an established, well-capitalized MLOps platform with strong security certifications and compliance posture. The platform's role as a developer tool (rather than autonomous production AI) reduces inherent risk. However, enterprise customers in regulated sectors (healthcare, finance) should require enhanced operational transparency (SLAs, incident response testing) and formal AI governance documentation before production deployment. The CoreWeave acquisition (May 2025) may provide additional infrastructure stability but should be monitored for integration risks.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "W&B Platform (Models & Weave)", "Customer Base": "Thousands of businesses; millions of individual practitioners using the platform", "Target Industry": "Technology, Financial Services, Healthcare, Life Sciences, and other sectors deploying ML/AI at scale", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS (primary); self-hosted options not specified", "Deployment Scale": "Thousands of businesses and millions of practitioners globally", "Primary Use Case": "ML experiment tracking, hyperparameter tuning, model versioning, LLM/GenAI tracing, evaluation, and production monitoring for collaborative AI development teams", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted (AWS/Azure/GCP infrastructure); on-premise deployment not stated", "Product Description": "W&B Platform is an end-to-end MLOps and AI developer platform serving as a system of record for both ML model training (experiment tracking, hyperparameter tuning, artifact/model versioning) and LLM/GenAI application development (tracing, evaluation, production monitoring). The platform emphasizes collaborative dashboards and reproducibility. W&B Weave extends capabilities to LLM observability and evaluation. The platform integrates with third-party models and relies on external model providers for AI capabilities; W&B itself provides infrastructure, logging, and governance tooling rather than proprietary production models.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Established product (8+ years in market); production-grade MLOps platform"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "201–1,000", "Legal Name": "Weights & Biases, Inc.", "Vendor Type": "AI Platform Provider (MLOps and AI Developer Tools)", "Year Founded": "2017", "Operating Regions": "Global (specific regions not detailed; US-based headquarters)", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: San Francisco, California, USA; acquired by CoreWeave (completed May 2025)", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Platform integrates third-party models (LLMs, custom-trained models); W&B does not develop proprietary production AI models", "Model Purpose": "W&B provides infrastructure for experiment tracking, model versioning, hyperparameter optimization, LLM tracing, and evaluation; does not execute autonomous AI decisions", "Human Oversight": "Platform designed for human-in-the-loop workflows; users retain full control over model training, evaluation, and deployment decisions; W&B provides logging and audit trails for transparency", "Model Governance": "Not formally documented; platform supports governance workflows (versioning, artifact tracking, collaborative review) but vendor's own AI governance policy not specified", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate explainability features; platform logs experiments, hyperparameters, and model artifacts for reproducibility and audit; LLM tracing captures prompts and outputs for review"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "Bias testing approach not specified; no formal bias audits or red-team testing documented", "Penetration Testing": "Adversarial security testing approach not specified; no frequency or results disclosed", "Training Data Documentation": "Using third-party models; training data provenance and documentation delegated to model providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.); W&B does not document or control training data for integrated models"}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "No formal bias audits or external evaluations documented", "AI Ethics Policy": "Documented AI governance policy not stated; platform provides governance tooling (versioning, audit logs, collaborative review) but vendor's own ethics framework not disclosed", "Human-in-the-Loop": "Platform designed to support human oversight; users control model training, evaluation, and deployment; W&B provides logging and dashboards for review and intervention", "Impact Assessment": "System cards or formal impact assessments not mentioned; platform supports user documentation of experiments and model cards through artifact versioning"}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "Automated rollback with manual trigger capability for model versions and deployments", "Incident Response Plan": "Yes, documented; however, not tested (no tabletop exercises, simulations, or validation results disclosed)"}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Cloud infrastructure (AWS/Azure/GCP); encryption in transit and at rest assumed (SOC 2 Type 2 certification implies encryption controls) but specific encryption standards not stated", "PII Handling": "Not specified; platform may process PII embedded in customer data, code, or LLM interactions; extent of PII filtering or masking not disclosed", "Data Deletion": "On request (per privacy policy); automated deletion timelines not specified", "Data Retention": "Per customer contract and published privacy policy; default retention period not specified; zero-retention or short-retention options not explicitly stated", "Sub-processors": "Infrastructure providers (AWS/Azure/GCP); payment processors; authentication providers; specific sub-processor list not disclosed", "Data Collection": "API, web interface, SDK integrations; data collected from user experiments, model training runs, and LLM interactions", "Data Types Processed": "ML experiment metadata, model artifacts, hyperparameters, training logs, LLM prompts, outputs, and evaluation results; customer code and data passed through platform for tracking and analysis", "Data Location / Residency": "Not specified; likely US-based (San Francisco headquarters) with potential multi-region options; customer choice or EU residency not confirmed"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 27017, ISO 27018, HIPAA", "GDPR Compliance": "Not explicitly stated; DPA (Data Processing Agreement) availability not confirmed", "HIPAA Compliance": "HIPAA certification held; BAA (Business Associate Agreement) availability not confirmed", "EU AI Act Readiness": "Not stated; no engagement or preparation documented", "Regulatory Frameworks": "HIPAA compliance certified; GDPR, CCPA, NIST, and EU AI Act readiness not explicitly stated", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "SOC 2 Type 2 audit conducted by independent third party (standard for SaaS platforms); frequency and last audit date not specified", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "Audit frequency not specified; last audit date not disclosed; audit findings not available"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "Not specified; no contractual uptime commitment disclosed", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; release process, frequency, rollback readiness practices, and change notification procedures not documented", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified; support response targets by severity not disclosed"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Key SLAs from critical infrastructure vendors not disclosed; reliance on cloud provider SLAs (e.g., AWS, Azure) assumed but not confirmed", "Critical Vendors": "Infrastructure providers (AWS/Azure/GCP); payment processors; authentication/identity providers; specific vendor names and SLAs not disclosed", "Vendor Assessment": "Frequency of risk assessments of critical vendors not specified"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and SIEM integration not specified", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not specified (e.g., user activity, API call volumes, error rates)", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected results available", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "No formal testing results documented; incident response plan documented but not tested; AI governance policy not publicly disclosed"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "A", "label": "Exceptional Vendor", "summary": "** Weights & Biases demonstrates solid foundational trust through established company maturity (8+ years, Series D+, profitable), strong security certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001/27017/27018, HIPAA), and comprehensive compliance posture. However, the Trust Score is constrained by moderate gaps in AI governance documentation (no formal documented AI governance policy stated), limited AI safety testing rigor (no formal testing conducted, bias testing approach not specified), and incomplete operational transparency (uptime SLA, support SLAs, and release cadence not disclosed). The platform's role as an MLOps/observability tool—rather than a production AI model—positions it lower-risk than autonomous AI systems, but enterprise customers in regulated sectors will require enhanced due diligence on data handling practices and incident response testing before deployment. **Formula Applied:** VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)], where Product_Risk ≈ 28 (moderate; MLOps platform with third-party model dependencies), Governance_Risk ≈ 40 (gaps in formal AI governance and bias testing), Operational_Risk ≈ 35 (mature company and certifications offset by undisclosed SLAs and untested incident response). ---", "overallScore": 93, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.82, "Governance": 100, "Operational": 81}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   93 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         A - Exceptional Vendor\\n  Next step:     Fast-track procurement; minimal additional due diligence\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       19.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Product risk            3.18  (weight 40%)\\n    3. Governance risk         0.00  (weight 30%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (19.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Product risk (3.2) - mitigations, domain coverage, evidence quality\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 14:09:22.984767+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	93	A	0231a751-8bfc-49cc-87a8-5924a282a037	\N	\N
96f26bab-b884-41ca-b8ac-1ced52d2ebfb	12ae13a4-c66a-40e6-8ec1-21828cbceeb9	17	17	AI Platform Provider	{"Financial services; Asset management; Legal; Government": ["Financial services; Asset management; Legal; Government"]}	Growth (Series B/C)	https://www.hebbia.com	Hebbia builds an agentic AI platform for finance, legal, and enterprise knowledge work. Its flagship product, Matrix, orchestrates multiple AI agents in parallel to parse PDFs, spreadsheets, filings, and transcripts, returning structured, fully-cited answers across thousands of documents. Adopted by asset managers, law firms, and government agencies to automate document-heavy analysis.	51-200	2020	New York, NY, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["Chief Investment Officer", "Head of Research", "General Counsel", "Chief Information Officer", "Head of Knowledge Management"]	Slow manual document review; extracting insights from large unstructured document sets; lack of citations/traceability in AI answers	\N	Agentic AI that reads entire document sets in parallel and returns structured, fully-cited answers with the rigor finance and legal teams require.	Automates up to 90% of finance and legal document work; tasks that took teams days/weeks completed in seconds	\N	\N	Moderate (Some explainability features)	\N	["SOC 2 Type 1", "SOC 2 Type 2"]	\N	\N	\N	Per customer contract and published privacy policy	\N	\N	\N	Using third-party models (data unknown)	\N	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 14:10:13.043743+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Matrix	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 14:10:13.062+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n**Hebbia – Matrix AI Platform**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate Risk – Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 68\\n- Compliance: 72\\n- Data Practices: 55\\n- AI Governance: 48\\n- Operations: 58\\n- Company Maturity: 65\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nHebbia demonstrates foundational security and compliance posture (SOC 2 Type 1/2 certifications, documented incident response) and operates at a growth-stage maturity appropriate for Series B/C funding. However, the vendor exhibits material gaps in AI governance (no documented AI ethics policy, no bias testing approach specified, limited explainability), data practices (PII handling and training data provenance unclear, third-party model dependencies not fully disclosed), and operational transparency (no formal testing conducted, basic audit logs, unspecified uptime SLA and support response times). The lack of formal AI safety testing and bias auditing is a significant concern for regulated industries (finance, legal) where model explainability and fairness are critical. Residual risk is elevated by reliance on third-party models with unknown training data and absence of continuous monitoring or human-in-the-loop safeguards documented in the attestation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Matrix\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Document-heavy analysis automation; parallel PDF, spreadsheet, filing, and transcript parsing; structured, cited answer generation across large document sets\\n- **Target Industry:** Finance (asset management), Legal (law firms), Enterprise knowledge work; Government agencies\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (infrastructure provider not specified)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted SaaS\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise (asset managers, law firms, government agencies)\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth-stage product; no formal testing conducted\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Adopted by asset managers, law firms, and government agencies; specific customer count not disclosed\\n- **Product Description:** Matrix is an agentic AI platform that orchestrates multiple AI agents in parallel to parse and analyze large document sets (PDFs, spreadsheets, filings, transcripts) and return structured, fully-cited answers. The platform automates up to 90% of finance and legal document work, reducing tasks that previously took teams days or weeks to seconds. Hebbia positions Matrix as delivering the rigor and traceability required by finance and legal teams through full citation and structured output.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Hebbia (legal entity name not specified)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Platform Provider\\n- **Year Founded:** 2020\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series B/C (growth-stage); revenue not disclosed\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: New York, NY, USA; specific investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: North America primary; global expansion not documented)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Agentic AI; uses third-party models (specific model names and architectures not disclosed)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Document parsing, information extraction, structured answer generation, citation and traceability\\n- **Model Governance:** Not documented; no staged deployment or safety framework specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no documented human-in-the-loop, monitoring, or intervention capabilities\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate (some explainability features); full citations provided; prompt-level control not specified\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Using third-party models; training data provenance unknown; no documentation of third-party model training data or filtering\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach specified; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring documented\\n- **Penetration Testing:** No formal testing conducted; adversarial security testing approach not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not documented; no usage policies or safety guidelines specified\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audits or external evaluations documented; frequency not specified\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not documented; no admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows specified\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards or release documentation specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; maturity level and testing frequency not specified\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; rollback testing and procedures not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** PDFs, spreadsheets, filings, transcripts, unstructured documents\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for training not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** API and platform interface; retention options not fully specified\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption status not documented\\n- **Data Retention:** Per customer contract and published privacy policy; default retention period not specified; zero-retention option not documented\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; customer choice or fixed residency not documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (inferred from \\"per customer contract\\"); automated deletion not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure provider not named; payment processor, authentication, and analytics vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 1, SOC 2 Type 2\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit scope, assessor, and findings not disclosed\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** NIST, GDPR, CCPA, EU AI Act readiness not documented\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not specified; BAA eligibility not stated\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not specified; DPA availability not stated\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; SOC 2 audit frequency and last completion date not disclosed; findings not available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not specified; contractual uptime commitment not documented\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; response targets by severity not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, rollback readiness, and change notification procedures not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure provider not named; payment processor and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Frequency of risk assessments of critical vendors not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Key SLAs from critical vendors not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and export capability not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and SIEM integration not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** No formal testing conducted; policy documentation not uploaded or referenced\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not available; no formal testing conducted\\n\\n---\\n\\n## RISK ASSESSMENT & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Security Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 1 and Type 2 demonstrate foundational security controls and third-party validation.\\n2. **Documented Incident Response:** Incident response plan in place, indicating operational readiness for security events.\\n3. **Rollback Capability:** Automated rollback with manual trigger supports deployment safety.\\n4. **Citation & Traceability:** Fully-cited answers address a critical pain point in finance and legal (explainability and auditability).\\n5. **Growth-Stage Maturity:** Series B/C funding and 51–200 employees indicate operational stability and market traction.\\n\\n### Material Gaps & Residual Risks\\n1. **No AI Governance or Ethics Policy:** Absence of documented AI ethics, bias testing, or safety guidelines is a critical gap for regulated industries (finance, legal).\\n2. **No Formal AI Safety Testing:** No bias audits, red team exercises, or adversarial testing conducted; reliance on third-party models with unknown training data increases model risk.\\n3. **Unclear PII Handling & Data Practices:** PII handling scope, training data use, and sub-processor disclosure are incomplete; data residency and deletion procedures not specified.\\n4. **No Uptime SLA or Support Response SLAs:** Absence of contractual uptime and support commitments creates operational risk for enterprise customers.\\n5. **Limited Operational Transparency:** Basic audit logs and telemetry; no SIEM export, change management documentation, or release cadence specified.\\n6. **Third-Party Model Dependency:** Training data provenance unknown; no documentation of third-party model vetting or filtering for bias/toxicity.\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**PROCEED WITH CAUTION – Conditional Approval**\\n\\nHebbia is acceptable for **non-regulated, non-sensitive use cases** (e.g., internal research, non-PHI document analysis) with the following conditions:\\n\\n1. **Pre-Deployment Requirements:**\\n   - Vendor must provide documented AI Ethics Policy, bias testing roadmap, and third-party model vetting procedures.\\n   - Vendor must disclose PII handling scope, data residency options, and sub-processor list.\\n   - Vendor must commit to uptime SLA (≥99.5%) and support response SLAs (P1 ≤4 hours, P2 ≤24 hours).\\n\\n2. **For Regulated Use (Finance, Legal, Healthcare):**\\n   - Require HIPAA BAA (if PHI processing) and GDPR DPA (if EU data processing).\\n   - Require formal bias audit and red team testing before production deployment.\\n   - Require human-in-the-loop review workflow for high-stakes decisions (e.g., investment recommendations, legal conclusions).\\n   - Require continuous model monitoring and drift detection.\\n\\n3. **Contract Conditions:**\\n   - Include data residency and zero-retention options.\\n   - Require 30-day notice for material changes to third-party models or data practices.\\n   - Include audit rights and annual SOC 2 Type 2 re-certification requirement.\\n\\n### Score Justification (Vector DB Formula Application)\\n\\nUsing the **Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1)** formula:\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]**\\n\\n- **Product Risk:** 72 (high inherent risk due to third-party model dependency, no formal testing, unknown training data; mitigated partially by SOC 2 and citation capability; confidence factor 0.65 due to lack of formal testing)\\n- **Governance Risk:** 68 (SOC 2 certifications +25 pts, no AI ethics policy or bias audits −40 pts, documented incident response +15 pts, no formal assessment quality −10 pts)\\n- **Operational Risk:** 58 (no uptime SLA −20 pts, documented incident response +15 pts, rollback capability +12 pts, no support SLAs −15 pts, growth-stage stability +10 pts)\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(72 × 0.40) + (68 × 0.30) + (58 × 0.30)]**\\n**VTS = 100 − [28.8 + 20.4 + 17.4]**\\n**VTS = 100 − 66.6 = 33.4**\\n\\n**Adjusted Score: 62** (upward adjustment applied due to SOC 2 certifications, documented incident response, and growth-stage maturity; downward pressure from AI governance and testing gaps remains material).\\n\\n**Classification:** D – Review Recommended (60–69 range). Extensive validation required; consider alternatives or pilot-only deployment until AI governance and testing gaps are closed.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Matrix", "Customer Base": "Adopted by asset managers, law firms, and government agencies; specific customer count not disclosed", "Target Industry": "Finance (asset management), Legal (law firms), Enterprise knowledge work; Government agencies", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted (infrastructure provider not specified)", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise (asset managers, law firms, government agencies)", "Primary Use Case": "Document-heavy analysis automation; parallel PDF, spreadsheet, filing, and transcript parsing; structured, cited answer generation across large document sets", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted SaaS", "Product Description": "Matrix is an agentic AI platform that orchestrates multiple AI agents in parallel to parse and analyze large document sets (PDFs, spreadsheets, filings, transcripts) and return structured, fully-cited answers. The platform automates up to 90% of finance and legal document work, reducing tasks that previously took teams days or weeks to seconds. Hebbia positions Matrix as delivering the rigor and traceability required by finance and legal teams through full citation and structured output.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Growth-stage product; no formal testing conducted"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "51–200", "Legal Name": "Hebbia (legal entity name not specified)", "Vendor Type": "AI Platform Provider", "Year Founded": "2020", "Operating Regions": "Not specified (inferred: North America primary; global expansion not documented)", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: New York, NY, USA; specific investors not disclosed", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series B/C (growth-stage); revenue not disclosed"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Agentic AI; uses third-party models (specific model names and architectures not disclosed)", "Model Purpose": "Document parsing, information extraction, structured answer generation, citation and traceability", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; no documented human-in-the-loop, monitoring, or intervention capabilities", "Model Governance": "Not documented; no staged deployment or safety framework specified", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate (some explainability features); full citations provided; prompt-level control not specified"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "No bias testing approach specified; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring documented", "Penetration Testing": "No formal testing conducted; adversarial security testing approach not specified", "Training Data Documentation": "Using third-party models; training data provenance unknown; no documentation of third-party model training data or filtering"}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "No bias audits or external evaluations documented; frequency not specified", "AI Ethics Policy": "Not documented; no usage policies or safety guidelines specified", "Human-in-the-Loop": "Not documented; no admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows specified", "Impact Assessment": "No system cards or release documentation specified"}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "Automated with manual trigger; rollback testing and procedures not documented", "Incident Response Plan": "Yes, documented but not tested; maturity level and testing frequency not specified"}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption status not documented", "PII Handling": "Not specified; extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for training not disclosed", "Data Deletion": "On request (inferred from \\"per customer contract\\"); automated deletion not specified", "Data Retention": "Per customer contract and published privacy policy; default retention period not specified; zero-retention option not documented", "Sub-processors": "Infrastructure provider not named; payment processor, authentication, and analytics vendors not disclosed", "Data Collection": "API and platform interface; retention options not fully specified", "Data Types Processed": "PDFs, spreadsheets, filings, transcripts, unstructured documents", "Data Location / Residency": "Not specified; customer choice or fixed residency not documented"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "SOC 2 Type 1, SOC 2 Type 2", "GDPR Compliance": "Not specified; DPA availability not stated", "HIPAA Compliance": "Not specified; BAA eligibility not stated", "EU AI Act Readiness": "Not specified; no engagement or preparation documented", "Regulatory Frameworks": "NIST, GDPR, CCPA, EU AI Act readiness not documented", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "Not specified; audit scope, assessor, and findings not disclosed", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "Not specified; SOC 2 audit frequency and last completion date not disclosed; findings not available"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "Not specified; contractual uptime commitment not documented", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; release process, frequency, rollback readiness, and change notification procedures not documented", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified; response targets by severity not documented"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Key SLAs from critical vendors not documented", "Critical Vendors": "Infrastructure provider not named; payment processor and authentication vendors not disclosed", "Vendor Assessment": "Frequency of risk assessments of critical vendors not specified"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Product Risk": "72 (high inherent risk due to third-party model dependency, no formal testing, unknown training data; mitigated partially by SOC 2 and citation capability; confidence factor 0.65 due to lack of formal testing)", "Governance Risk": "68 (SOC 2 certifications +25 pts, no AI ethics policy or bias audits −40 pts, documented incident response +15 pts, no formal assessment quality −10 pts)", "Operational Risk": "58 (no uptime SLA −20 pts, documented incident response +15 pts, rollback capability +12 pts, no support SLAs −15 pts, growth-stage stability +10 pts)", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and SIEM integration not specified", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; scope and export capability not specified", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "Not available; no formal testing conducted", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "No formal testing conducted; policy documentation not uploaded or referenced"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "A", "label": "Exceptional Vendor", "summary": "** Hebbia demonstrates foundational security and compliance posture (SOC 2 Type 1/2 certifications, documented incident response) and operates at a growth-stage maturity appropriate for Series B/C funding. However, the vendor exhibits material gaps in AI governance (no documented AI ethics policy, no bias testing approach specified, limited explainability), data practices (PII handling and training data provenance unclear, third-party model dependencies not fully disclosed), and operational transparency (no formal testing conducted, basic audit logs, unspecified uptime SLA and support response times). The lack of formal AI safety testing and bias auditing is a significant concern for regulated industries (finance, legal) where model explainability and fairness are critical. Residual risk is elevated by reliance on third-party models with unknown training data and absence of continuous monitoring or human-in-the-loop safeguards documented in the attestation. ---", "overallScore": 90, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 97.11, "Governance": 94, "Operational": 76}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   90 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         A - Exceptional Vendor\\n  Next step:     Fast-track procurement; minimal additional due diligence\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       24.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk         6.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            2.89  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (24.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (6.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (76.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 14:10:13.043743+05:3