ion vendors not disclosed.", "Vendor Assessment": "Not specified; no frequency or methodology for assessing critical vendor risk stated."}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Product Risk": "High (proprietary model, no bias testing, no training data documentation, no governance framework).", "Governance Risk": "High (no AI ethics policy, no impact assessments, no compliance framework).", "Operational Risk": "Moderate (SOC 2 Type 2, incident response documented but untested, no SLAs).", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging; audit logs are available but export capability and SIEM integration not detailed.", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics; vendor collects and can provide basic usage telemetry (e.g., API calls, session counts) but detailed interaction data not specified.", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted; no safety testing results, bias reports, or adversarial robustness evaluations available (even under NDA).", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "No formal testing conducted; no uploaded evidence files, policy documents, or compliance artifacts provided."}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "A", "label": "Exceptional Vendor", "summary": "** Runway demonstrates moderate trustworthiness with established company maturity (Series E, profitable, 201–1,000 employees) and foundational security controls (SOC 2 Type 2), but significant gaps in AI governance, transparency, and compliance documentation limit enterprise readiness—particularly for regulated use cases. The vendor lacks documented AI governance policies, formal bias testing, training data documentation, and comprehensive compliance frameworks (no HIPAA BAA, GDPR DPA, or EU AI Act readiness stated). While the company is operationally mature and incident response is documented, the absence of formal AI safety testing, explainability controls, and human-in-the-loop mechanisms, combined with proprietary/closed model transparency, places this vendor in the \\"Review Recommended\\" category (60–69 range per VTS rubric). Strengths include SOC 2 Type 2 certification and automated rollback capability; primary gaps are AI governance maturity, bias auditing, and regulatory compliance readiness for high-stakes deployments. ---", "overallScore": 90, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.85, "Governance": 96, "Operational": 76}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   90 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         A - Exceptional Vendor\\n  Next step:     Fast-track procurement; minimal additional due diligence\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       24.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Product risk            3.15  (weight 40%)\\n    3. Governance risk         4.00  (weight 30%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (24.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Product risk (3.2) - mitigations, domain coverage, evidence quality\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (76.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 14:12:1