multi-tenant; rollback capability; no multi-region or enterprise deployment details)", "Incident Management": "12 points (Documented incident response plan; not tested; no automation or testing evidence)", "Maturity Adjustment": "8 points (Founded 2023, Series D+, 51–200 employees; early-stage company with investor backing but limited operational history)", "Operational Controls": "18 points (Rollback capability automated with manual trigger; no explicit human oversight, continuous monitoring, or version control details)", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; SIEM export capability or comprehensive audit trail export not specified", "Mitigation Effectiveness": "0.50 (SOC 2 Type 2 provides baseline security controls; no AI-specific safety mitigations, testing, or governance documented)", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; scope limited (e.g., PR reviews completed, time saved, bug detection rate); detailed telemetry export not confirmed", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected results available", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "Compliance documentation available via website (https://compliance.coderabbit.com); formal AI safety testing results, model cards, or bias audit reports not provided"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** CodeRabbit demonstrates moderate trustworthiness with solid foundational security (SOC 2 Type 2) and reasonable company maturity (Series D+, founded 2023), but significant gaps in AI governance, testing rigor, and operational transparency limit confidence for enterprise deployment. The vendor lacks formal AI safety documentation, bias testing frameworks, and documented training data provenance—critical gaps for an AI code review tool that influences developer workflows and security decisions. While the product shows market traction and operational rollback capability, the absence of formal testing results, unclear uptime SLA, and minimal AI governance policy place this vendor in the \\"Review Recommended\\" category (D-grade, 60–69 range per vector-DB rubric). Recommend enhanced due diligence and contractual SLA clarification before production use in regulated or high-stakes environments. ---", "overallScore": 82, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 95.63, "Governance": 89, "Operational": 58}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   82 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       42.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        11.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            4.37  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (42.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (11.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (58.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:1