aw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Augmedix Go – AI-Powered Medical Documentation\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 68 **(Review Recommended)**\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 72\\n- Compliance: 75\\n- Data Practices: 65\\n- AI Governance: 52\\n- Operations: 74\\n- Company Maturity: 78\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nAugmedix demonstrates solid operational and compliance foundations (HITRUST certification, 99.9% SLA, established company maturity), but significant gaps in AI governance, transparency, and testing undermine overall trustworthiness. The vendor lacks documented AI ethics policies, formal bias testing, training data documentation, and adversarial security testing—critical controls for a healthcare AI system processing PHI. While the company's 12-year track record and profitability are strengths, the \\"black box\\" model transparency and absence of formal testing results create material residual risk. The score reflects a **Review Recommended (D-band, 60–69)** classification: acceptable for pilot or limited deployment with enhanced due diligence and contractual mitigation roadmap requirements.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Augmedix Go\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Ambient AI-powered clinical documentation; automated transcription and EHR data entry for clinicians\\n- **Target Industry:** Healthcare (clinical practices, hospitals, health systems)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (AWS/Azure/GCP not specified; US-only residency)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud SaaS; on-premises deployment options not specified\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise health systems and mid-market clinical practices\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Production-stage product (12-year company); formal testing results not available\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (customer count or revenue metrics not disclosed)\\n- **Product Description:** Augmedix Go leverages proprietary NLP combined with Google's MedLM to automate clinical documentation and reduce clinician administrative burden by up to 50%. The product integrates deeply with major EHR systems (Epic, Cerner) and holds HITRUST certification for healthcare data security. However, the system operates as a black box with limited transparency into decision logic and lacks formal bias testing or adversarial security validation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Augmedix (legal entity name not specified)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (healthcare-focused)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2012\\n- **Employees:** 1,001–5,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: San Francisco, CA, USA; specific investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** US (primary); international expansion not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Proprietary NLP + Google MedLM (large language model); ambient AI (audio/voice-to-text with clinical context)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Medical documentation generation, clinical note synthesis, EHR data extraction and population, clinician-patient interaction transcription\\n- **Model Governance:** Not documented; no staged deployment framework or safety gates specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no explicit human-in-the-loop controls, monitoring alerts, or intervention workflows documented\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Limited (black box model); basic logging available; no prompt-level control, citation mechanisms, or explainability framework disclosed\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools specified\\n- **Penetration Testing:** No adversarial security testing or penetration testing frequency documented; formal testing results not available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not documented; no usage policies, safety guidelines, or ethical framework for clinical AI disclosed\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audit frequency or external evaluation program specified\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; no documented admin controls, content filtering, clinical review workflows, or intervention capabilities\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation available; clinical impact assessment process not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but **not tested**; maturity level and annual testing cadence not specified\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; rollback procedures documented but testing frequency not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Clinical audio (ambient voice recordings), transcribed clinical notes, EHR data (patient demographics, medical history, diagnoses, medications, lab results)\\n- **PII Handling:** Processes PHI (Protected Health Information) as core function; extent of customer data use for model training or improvement not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** Ambient audio capture (clinical encounters); API integration with EHR systems (Epic, Cerner); retention options not specified\\n- **Data Storage:** US-only data residency; encryption standards (in-transit, at-rest) not specified\\n- **Data Retention:** Default retention not explicitly specified; vendor notes likely compliance with standard healthcare retention (6–10 years per state regulations) but does not commit to specific policy\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** US only; no EU, customer-choice, or multi-region options\\n- **Data Deletion:** On-request deletion process not explicitly documented; automated deletion policies not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Google (MedLM partnership); infrastructure providers (AWS/Azure/GCP not confirmed); payment processors and auth vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** HITRUST (healthcare-specific security certification); SOC 2, ISO 27001 status not specified\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit type (third-party, internal, frequency) not documented\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** HIPAA compliance claimed (BAA eligibility implied); GDPR, CCPA, NIST, EU AI Act readiness not addressed\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Implied (HITRUST certification requires HIPAA compliance); BAA availability not explicitly confirmed\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not addressed; DPA availability not specified; EU operations not mentioned\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not addressed; no engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; audit cadence and most recent audit results not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime allowance)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support tiers and response targets not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, change approval process, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Google (MedLM partnership); infrastructure provider (AWS/Azure/GCP not confirmed); payment and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Frequency of risk assessments for critical vendors not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Google MedLM SLA terms not disclosed; infrastructure provider SLAs not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and SIEM integration not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited; no formal testing results, AI ethics policies, or bias audit reports provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected testing results available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## RISK ASSESSMENT & MITIGATION RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Established company maturity** (12 years, profitable 3+ years, 1,001–5,000 employees): Reduces operational and financial risk.\\n2. **HITRUST certification**: Demonstrates healthcare-grade security controls and compliance commitment.\\n3. **Strong operational SLA** (99.9% uptime): Meets enterprise healthcare availability expectations.\\n4. **Deep EHR integration** (Epic, Cerner): Reduces implementation friction and workflow disruption.\\n5. **Documented incident response plan**: Foundational security governance in place.\\n\\n### Critical Gaps (Residual Risk)\\n1. **No AI governance policy or ethics framework**: Black-box model with no documented safety guidelines, usage policies, or ethical oversight for clinical AI.\\n2. **No bias testing or training data documentation**: No evidence of bias detection, red-teaming, or training data curation for medical accuracy or demographic fairness.\\n3. **No formal adversarial security testing**: Ambient AI systems (voice capture, transcription) are high-value targets; no penetration testing or adversarial robustness validation documented.\\n4. **Untested incident response plan**: While documented, the plan has not been validated through annual testing or tabletop exercises.\\n5. **Limited model transparency**: Black-box architecture with basic logging; no explainability, prompt-level control, or citation mechanisms for clinical decision support.\\n6. **Vague data retention and deletion policies**: No explicit retention schedule or on-request deletion SLA; reliance on inferred compliance rather than contractual commitment.\\n7. **No GDPR or EU AI Act readiness**: US-only deployment and no international compliance roadmap; limits expansion and creates regulatory risk for global health systems.\\n8. **Support SLAs not specified**: No documented response times for critical incidents; support model and escalation procedures unclear.\\n\\n### Recommended Mitigation Roadmap (Contract Conditions)\\n\\n**Before Deployment:**\\n1. **AI Governance & Safety Documentation**\\n   - Provide board-approved AI ethics policy with clinical safety guidelines, usage restrictions, and escalation procedures.\\n   - Document model governance framework: staged deployment gates, clinical review workflows, and human-in-the-loop controls.\\n\\n2. **Bias & Fairness Testing**\\n   - Conduct third-party bias audit across demographic groups (age, gender, race, language, socioeconomic status) relevant to clinical populations.\\n   - Document training data composition, curation practices, and exclusion criteria.\\n   - Establish ongoing bias monitoring dashboard with monthly reporting.\\n\\n3. **Adversarial Security & Penetration Testing**\\n   - Conduct annual third-party penetration testing of ambient audio capture, transcription, and EHR integration components.\\n   - Document adversarial robustness testing (e.g., accent robustness, background noise, medical terminology edge cases).\\n\\n4. **Incident Response Validation**\\n   - Conduct annual tabletop exercise or full-scale incident response drill; provide test results and remediation evidence.\\n\\n5. **Data Practices Clarification**\\n   - Provide explicit data retention schedule (e.g., \\"PHI retained for 7 years per HIPAA, then deleted within 30 days\\").\\n   - Document on-request deletion SLA (e.g., \\"Customer-requested PHI deletion completed within 15 business days\\").\\n   - Clarify customer data use for model training/improvement; confirm opt-out capability.\\n\\n6. **Support & Change Management SLAs**\\n   - Define P1/P2/P3 response and resolution targets (e.g., P1: 1-hour response, 4-hour resolution).\\n   - Document release cadence, change approval process, and rollback procedures.\\n\\n**Post-Deployment:**\\n- Require quarterly compliance attestation and annual third-party audit (SOC 2 Type 2 recommended).\\n- Establish continuous model drift monitoring and clinical review workflow (as noted in vector-DB guidance for healthcare AI).\\n- Implement SIEM integration for audit log export and real-time alerting.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PROCUREMENT RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n**Status:** **CONDITIONAL APPROVAL FOR PILOT DEPLOYMENT**\\n\\n**Rationale:**\\n- Augmedix Go demonstrates acceptable operational and compliance foundations (HITRUST, 99.9% SLA, established company) suitable for **limited pilot use** in non-critical clinical workflows.\\n- However, **material gaps in AI governance, bias testing, and model transparency** require contractual mitigation before production deployment or expansion to high-stakes clinical decisions (e.g., diagnostic support, treatment recommendations).\\n- The vendor's 12-year track record and profitability reduce financial and operational risk, but the absence of formal testing and documented safety controls create unacceptable residual risk for unsupervised autonomous deployment.\\n\\n**Conditions for Approval:**\\n1. Vendor commits to AI governance and bias testing roadmap (completion within 6 months).\\n2. Pilot deployment limited to **documentation-only use cases** (e.g., ambient note transcription) with mandatory human clinician review before EHR submission.\\n3. Incident response plan validated through annual tabletop exercise; results provided before go-live.\\n4. Data retention and deletion policies explicitly documented in BAA; customer data use for model training prohibited or explicitly opt-in.\\n5. Quarterly compliance attestation and annual SOC 2 Type 2 audit (or equivalent) required.\\n\\n**Escalation Triggers (Reassess Deployment):**\\n- Bias audit reveals material demographic disparities (>5% accuracy variance) without documented mitigation.\\n- Penetration testing identifies critical vulnerabilities in audio capture or EHR integration.\\n- Incident response drill fails or reveals gaps in clinical escalation procedures.\\n- Vendor declines to commit to AI governance or bias testing roadmap.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SCORING METHODOLOGY NOTES\\n\\n**Trust Score Calculation (Vendor Self-Attestation Framework):**\\n\\nApplied the vector-DB formula structure:\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]\\n```\\n\\n**Product Risk (40% weight):** \\n- Inherent risk: 85/100 (healthcare AI, PHI processing, black-box model, autonomous documentation)\\n- Mitigation effectiveness: 0.55 (HITRUST + incident response documented, but no bias testing, no AI ethics policy, untested incident response)\\n- Confidence factor: 0.70 (established company, but no formal testing results; limited transparency)\\n- **Product Risk Score: 32/100** (residual risk after mitigation)\\n\\n**Governance Risk (30% weight):**\\n- Certifications: 20/50 (HITRUST only; SOC 2, ISO 27001 not confirmed)\\n- Assessment quality: 8/25 (incident response documented but untested; no third-party audit frequency disclosed)\\n- Policies: 15/45 (data retention vague; no AI ethics policy, no bias audit policy, no human-in-the-loop policy)\\n- Operational controls: 22/45 (rollback automated; human oversight not documented; monitoring basic; version control not specified)\\n- Maturity adjustment: 15/15 (12-year company, profitable, 1,001–5,000 employees)\\n- **Governance Score: 80/100 → Governance Risk: 20/100**\\n\\n**Operational Risk (30% weight):**\\n- SLA: 33/33 (99.9% uptime = excellent)\\n- Incident management: 20/30 (documented but untested)\\n- Deployment maturity: 25/30 (enterprise SaaS, deep EHR integration, but no multi-region/multi-tenant details)\\n- Stability: 18/22 (12-year company, profitable, but no customer retention metrics)\\n- Support: 12/20 (support SLAs not specified; no 24/7 dedicated support documented)\\n- **Operational Score: 108 → capped at 100 → Operational Risk: 0/100**\\n\\n**Final VTS:**\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(32 × 0.40) + (20 × 0.30) + (0 × 0.30)]\\n    = 100 - [12.8 + 6 + 0]\\n    = 100 - 18.8\\n    = 81.2 ≈ 81\\n```\\n\\n**Adjustment for Healthcare AI Specificity:**\\nThe formula-derived score of 81 (B-band, \\"Trusted Vendor\\") was adjusted downward to **68 (D-band, \\"Review Recommended\\")** to reflect vector-DB guidance for healthcare AI systems:\\n- Vector guidance emphasizes \\"continuous model drift monitoring and clinical review workflow\\" as **mandatory post-deployment controls** for healthcare AI.\\n- Augmedix lacks documented model drift monitoring, clinical review workflows, and formal bias testing—critical gaps for healthcare PHI handling.\\n- The absence of training data documentation and adversarial testing creates material residual risk despite strong operational posture.\\n\\n**Category Scores (0–100 scale):**\\n- **Security: 72** — HITRUST certified, incident response documented, rollback automated; gaps: untested incident response, no adversarial testing\\n- **Compliance: 75** — HITRUST certification, HIPAA-compliant; gaps: no SOC 2/ISO 27001 confirmed, audit frequency not specified, no GDPR/EU AI Act readiness\\n- **Data Practices: 65**", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Augmedix Go", "Customer Base": "Not specified (customer count or revenue metrics not disclosed)", "Target Industry": "Healthcare (clinical practices, hospitals, health systems)", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted (AWS/Azure/GCP not specified; US-only residency)", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise health systems and mid-market clinical practices", "Primary Use Case": "Ambient AI-powered clinical 