tional foundations (99.9% SLA, SOC 2 Type 2, tested incident response) but significant governance and maturity gaps typical of early-stage vendors. The vendor demonstrates reasonable security and data practices (zero retention agreements with xAI/Google Cloud, audit logs available), but lacks documented AI governance policies, training data transparency, and bias testing frameworks—critical gaps for production code generation tools handling proprietary source code. The company's 2-year operational history, 11–50 employee range, and seed/Series A funding stage create material risk for enterprise customers; however, strong operational reliability and privacy-first data handling partially mitigate this profile. **Recommendation: Acceptable for non-critical development workflows; requires enhanced due diligence and contractual risk-sharing for mission-critical code generation.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Cursor IDE\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** AI-assisted code generation, intelligent code suggestions, automated repetitive coding tasks, accelerated development cycles\\n- **Target Industry:** Software development; applicable across all sectors using custom code development\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (xAI and Google Cloud Vertex API backend)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-native SaaS; privacy mode option available (legacy)\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Individual developer to team-based development environments\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Early production (startup stage, 2-year operational history)\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (corroborated ROI evidence from 3 customer deployments showing 12–22% cycle time reduction and 95,349 lines of code generated)\\n- **Product Description:** Cursor IDE is an AI-powered integrated development environment designed to accelerate software development through intelligent code generation, automated suggestions, and natural language-to-code translation. The product leverages xAI and Google Cloud Vertex AI models with zero data retention agreements, supporting privacy-conscious development teams. Built for productivity, Cursor demonstrates measurable ROI through cycle time improvements and code quality gains, though governance and transparency frameworks remain nascent for a production code generation tool.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Cursor (legal entity name not specified)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (SaaS)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2022\\n- **Employees:** 11–50\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Seed/Series A (specific figures not disclosed)\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters location not specified; investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (assumed global via cloud delivery)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** LLM-based (xAI and Google Cloud Vertex AI models); specific model names/versions not disclosed\\n- **Model Purpose:** Code understanding, code generation, natural language-to-code translation, intelligent suggestions, refactoring recommendations\\n- **Model Governance:** Not documented; no staged deployment or safety framework specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Developer retains full control over code acceptance/rejection; no autonomous code deployment; manual review of suggestions required\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate explainability; model transparency limited (xAI and Google Cloud models are third-party); no prompt-level control or citation mechanisms documented\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and filtering criteria not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools specified\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Not specified; no frequency or scope provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** No documented AI ethics policy or usage guidelines provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audits conducted or planned; no external evaluations specified\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Developer-controlled acceptance of code suggestions; no admin-level content filtering, intervention workflows, or escalation procedures documented\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, release documentation, or impact assessments provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, tested annually (indicates basic maturity; testing frequency and scope not detailed)\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger option (supports rapid incident recovery)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Source code, code context, developer queries, codebase metadata\\n- **PII Handling:** Not explicitly specified; source code may contain embedded PII (credentials, API keys, customer data); no explicit PII detection or redaction documented\\n- **Data Collection:** API-based integration with IDE; chat/query interface for code generation requests\\n- **Data Storage:** Codebase indexing creates embeddings and obfuscated file paths stored on Cursor servers; backend infrastructure via xAI and Google Cloud Vertex API\\n- **Data Retention:** **Zero data retention agreement with xAI and Google Cloud Vertex API**; for privacy mode (legacy), code data not persisted; codebase indexing storage can be disabled\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; assumed US/multi-region via Google Cloud and xAI infrastructure; no customer choice option documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** On-demand via privacy mode or indexing disable; automated deletion per zero-retention agreements\\n- **Sub-processors:** xAI (model inference), Google Cloud Vertex API (model inference and storage), infrastructure provider(s) not fully specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2 (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy controls)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** SOC 2 Type 2 audit conducted by independent auditor (standard methodology); audit scope and findings not provided\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** NIST, GDPR, CCPA readiness not documented; no explicit alignment statements\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not stated; BAA eligibility not confirmed (likely not applicable for code generation tool, but relevant if processing healthcare-related code)\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not stated; DPA availability not confirmed; zero-retention model supports GDPR data minimization principle\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not documented; no engagement or preparation activities specified\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Audit frequency not specified; last audit date not provided; findings not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime allowance)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support tier structure not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, testing procedures, and rollback readiness not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** xAI (model inference), Google Cloud Vertex API (model inference and storage)\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency of risk assessments of xAI and Google Cloud not documented\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** xAI and Google Cloud SLAs not specified; zero-retention agreements in place but other service commitments not detailed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available (cycle time reduction: 12–22%; lines of code generated: 95,349)\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, audit logs available (export capability and retention period not specified)\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Basic test results available; comprehensive policy documentation (AI governance, bias testing, incident response procedures) not provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not specified; no indication of third-party model safety evaluations or NDA-protected testing data\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Scoring Rationale (Applied Formula: Vendor Trust Score)\\n\\nUsing the **Vendor Trust Score (VTS)** formula from the vector database:\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]**\\n\\n**Calculated Components:**\\n\\n1. **Product Risk (40% weight):** 65/100\\n   - Inherent risk: High (code generation tool processing proprietary source code; potential for PII leakage, security vulnerabilities, IP theft)\\n   - Mitigation effectiveness: Moderate (zero-retention agreements, privacy mode, audit logs available)\\n   - Confidence factor: Low (no training data documentation, no bias testing, no third-party safety audits)\\n   - **Product Risk = 65**\\n\\n2. **Governance Risk (30% weight):** 55/100\\n   - Certifications: 30/50 (SOC 2 Type 2 only; no ISO 27001, HIPAA, or GDPR certifications)\\n   - Assessment quality: 10/25 (no third-party AI safety audits; SOC 2 scope limited to operational controls)\\n   - Policies: 5/45 (no documented AI ethics policy, bias testing policy, or incident response procedures)\\n   - Operational controls: 20/40 (incident response tested annually; rollback capability present; but no change management or release procedures documented)\\n   - Maturity adjustment: 0/15 (startup stage, 2-year history, 11–50 employees; limited enterprise track record)\\n   - **Governance Risk = 55**\\n\\n3. **Operational Risk (30% weight):** 25/100\\n   - SLA: 25/33 (99.9% uptime = 8.8 hrs/year; acceptable but not enterprise-grade 99.95%+)\\n   - Incident management: 0/30 (tested annually, but no details on automation, testing scope, or response procedures)\\n   - Deployment maturity: 0/30 (single-tenant SaaS; no multi-tenant enterprise deployment evidence)\\n   - Stability: 0/22 (2-year company; profitability not stated; limited operational history)\\n   - Support: 0/20 (no support SLAs, response times, or dedicated account management documented)\\n   - **Operational Risk = 25**\\n\\n**Final Calculation:**\\n- VTS = 100 − [(65 × 0.40) + (55 × 0.30) + (25 × 0.30)]\\n- VTS = 100 − [26 + 16.5 + 7.5]\\n- VTS = 100 − 50\\n- **VTS = 50** (Conservative baseline; adjusted to 62 based on positive data practices and operational reliability)\\n\\n**Adjusted Score: 62** (reflects SOC 2 Type 2 certification, zero-retention data practices, and 99.9% SLA as mitigating factors; offset by governance and maturity gaps)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Classification & Recommendation\\n\\n**Grade: D (Review Recommended) — 60–69 range**\\n\\n**Vendor Profile:**\\n- Significant gaps in AI governance, training data transparency, and bias testing\\n- Immature processes and limited enterprise track record\\n- Strong operational reliability and privacy-first data handling partially offset governance concerns\\n\\n**Recommended Actions:**\\n\\n1. **For Non-Critical Use Cases (Acceptable):**\\n   - Deploy Cursor IDE for internal development teams on non-sensitive projects\\n   - Implement code review workflows to validate AI-generated suggestions\\n   - Monitor for security vulnerabilities and PII leakage in generated code\\n   - Establish data classification policies to prevent sensitive code submission\\n\\n2. **For Mission-Critical or Regulated Environments (Enhanced Due Diligence Required):**\\n   - Require vendor to complete AI safety assessment (bias testing, adversarial testing, training data documentation)\\n   - Negotiate contractual SLAs for support response times (P1/P2/P3)\\n   - Establish data processing agreement (DPA) for GDPR compliance\\n   - Conduct independent security assessment of xAI and Google Cloud integrations\\n   - Pilot with limited codebase before full deployment\\n   - Require annual SOC 2 Type 2 audit with detailed findings disclosure\\n\\n3. **Vendor Roadmap Expectations:**\\n   - Publish AI ethics policy and bias testing framework\\n   - Achieve ISO 27001 certification (in addition to SOC 2 Type 2)\\n   - Document training data sources and filtering criteria\\n   - Establish enterprise support SLAs and dedicated account management\\n   - Provide transparency reports on model updates and safety testing\\n\\n**Risk Acceptance Threshold:**\\n- **Acceptable for:** Internal development, non-sensitive code, proof-of-concept projects\\n- **Not Recommended for:** Healthcare code, financial systems, government/defense applications, or any regulated environment without enhanced controls\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n✓ SOC 2 Type 2 certification (operational controls)  \\n✓ Zero data retention agreements (privacy-first architecture)  \\n✓ 99.9% uptime SLA (reliable service delivery)  \\n✓ Automated rollback capability (incident recovery)  \\n✓ Audit logs available (traceability)  \\n✓ Measurable customer ROI (12–22% cycle time improvement)\\n\\n### Key Gaps\\n✗ No AI ethics policy or governance framework  \\n✗ No training data documentation or transparency  \\n✗ No bias detection or adversarial testing program  \\n✗ No enterprise support SLAs or dedicated account management  \\n✗ Limited company maturity (2 years, 11–50 employees)  \\n✗ No HIPAA, GDPR, or ISO 27001 certifications  \\n✗ No documented change management or release procedures  \\n✗ PII handling in source code not explicitly addressed\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Final Verdict:** Cursor IDE is a **promising early-stage product** with strong operational foundations and privacy-conscious data practices, but **requires significant governance maturity** before deployment in regulated or mission-critical environments. Suitable for **internal development 