anagement / Release Cadence": "Not specified; release process, frequency, rollback readiness practices, and change management procedures not documented", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified; response time targets by severity level not documented"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Not specified; key SLAs from critical vendors not provided", "Critical Vendors": "Not specified; infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed", "Vendor Assessment": "Not specified; frequency of risk assessments for critical vendors not documented"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; full SIEM export capability and log retention not specified", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; telemetry scope limited to basic usage data", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected safety or adversarial testing results available", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "No formal testing results or policy documentation uploaded; vendor attestation incomplete"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Cedar demonstrates solid operational maturity (99.99% uptime SLA, quarterly incident response testing, automated rollback) and established company credentials (Series D+, profitable 3+ years, 201–1,000 employees), but faces significant gaps in compliance certifications, AI governance documentation, and formal safety testing that are critical for healthcare PHI handling. The lack of SOC 2, HIPAA BAA, or ISO 27001 certifications—combined with no formal adversarial testing, incomplete training data documentation, and undocumented bias testing—places this vendor in the \\"Review Recommended\\" category (60–69 range). While Cedar's healthcare domain expertise and strong ROI metrics are strengths, the absence of third-party audit evidence and formal AI safety controls creates residual risk that must be addressed before enterprise healthcare deployment, particularly for regulated use cases involving patient financial data (PII/PHI). ---", "overallScore": 85, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.33, "Governance": 86, "Operational": 69}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   85 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       31.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        14.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            3.67  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (31.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (14.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (69.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 14:58:04.919869+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	85	B	