al document synthesis, note-taking automation).", "For High-Stakes Deployments": "Conduct independent security assessment and bias audit before production use; implement human-in-the-loop review for critical decisions.", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, comprehensive analytics available; enables usage monitoring and performance tracking", "For Healthcare/Compliance Use Cases": "Require vendor to provide:", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "Yes, testing results available; specific details and NDA terms not provided in attestation", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "- Incident response plan (tested annually)"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Google NotebookLM achieves a Trust Score of 78, placing it in the \\"Acceptable Vendor\\" category (70–79 range). The vendor demonstrates strong operational maturity, comprehensive security certifications (ISO 27001/27017/27018/27701, SOC 1/2/3, HIPAA BAA), and excellent company stability (10,000+ employees, founded 1998, public company). However, the score is moderated by gaps in AI governance transparency (no documented AI ethics policy, bias testing approach, or adversarial security testing details provided), incomplete data practices documentation (training data sourcing from third-party models with unknown provenance, extended data retention on backups up to 6 months), and missing support SLA details. The source-grounded architecture and automated rollback capability are significant strengths. **Recommendation:** Enhanced due diligence required; suitable for non-critical use cases; require mitigation roadmap for AI governance and training data transparency before high-stakes deployments. ---", "overallScore": 83, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 97.03, "Governance": 84, "Operational": 64}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   83 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       36.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        16.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            2.97  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (36.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (16.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (64.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Audit frequency in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:36:42.620682+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	83	B	112c7475-91d6-4351-aa85-31ad5a26a30f	\N	\N
89928f52-d9eb-419c-836a-fd7a7b022b40	15bb474e-5187-418c-92fc-a5ea61f9b68e	83	83	AI Product Company	{"Educational Institutions; Technology; Professional Services": ["Educational Institutions; Technology; Professional Services"]}	Mature (10+ years or Public)	https://www.grammarly.com	Grammarly Blog: Your Ultimate Writing Resource	1,001-5,000	2009	San Francisco, California, United States	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "Line of Business Leader", "IT/Infrastructure Teams"]	Grammarly addresses the challenges of inconsistent writing quality and compliance in professional communications, which can lead to misunderstandings or reputational damage. It also solves the problem of time-consuming manual proofreading by automating error detection and style improvements, enabling teams to focus on content rather than corrections.	\N	Grammarly helps teams build trust everywhere they write with real-time coaching that reinforces quality and compliance standards.	Watch ticket-resolution times drop and CSAT scores rise thanks to an AI writing partner that enables teams to work more efficiently and focus on customer relationships.	\N	\N	Proprietary/Closed	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Grammarly retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide its services and comply with legal obligations. Data deletion procedures are in place to remove user data upon request or after account termination, in accordance with GDPR and CCPA requirements.	\N	\N	\N	No documentation available	99.95% (4.4 hrs/year)	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	Yes, basic test results	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 15:37:25.396833+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Grammarly	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 15:37:25.401+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT: GRAMMARLY\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate Risk)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 58\\n- Compliance: 45\\n- Data Practices: 55\\n- AI Governance: 40\\n- Operations: 72\\n- Company Maturity: 80\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nGrammarly demonstrates solid operational maturity (10+ years, 1,001–5,000 employees, 99.95% SLA) and established company stability, but faces significant gaps in AI governance transparency, compliance certifications, and documented safety practices. The vendor provides basic data retention and deletion procedures aligned with GDPR/CCPA, yet lacks published security certifications, bias testing documentation, and formal AI ethics policies—critical for enterprise and regulated-industry deployments. While the product is production-ready for general writing assistance, the absence of third-party security audits, formal incident response testing, and transparent AI model governance limits trust for sensitive use cases (healthcare, finance, government). The score reflects a \\"Proceed with Caution\\" posture: acceptable for low-stakes internal communications but requiring enhanced due diligence and contractual safeguards for regulated environments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Grammarly\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Real-time writing quality assurance, compliance coaching, and error detection for professional communications; team-based writing standardization and efficiency improvement.\\n- **Target Industry:** Enterprise (general); applicable across professional services, customer support, legal, and compliance-sensitive sectors.\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS (proprietary infrastructure)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; on-premises deployment options not specified.\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Team and enterprise scale (typical customer base includes mid-market to large enterprises).\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Production-ready; mature product with 10+ years operational history.\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (metrics not provided)\\n- **Product Description:** Grammarly is a proprietary AI-powered writing assistant that provides real-time error detection, style coaching, and compliance reinforcement across professional communications. The product automates proofreading and quality assurance, reducing manual review cycles and enabling teams to focus on content and customer relationships. Grammarly emphasizes trust-building through consistent writing quality and compliance standards, though detailed security certifications and AI governance policies are not publicly documented.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Grammarly, Inc.\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company\\n- **Year Founded:** 2009\\n- **Employees:** 1,001–5,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Not specified\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: San Francisco, California, United States; key investors not specified.\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (implied global via SaaS model; specific regional availability not documented).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Proprietary AI model (type not disclosed; likely NLP/LLM-based for writing analysis and generation).\\n- **Model Purpose:** Writing quality analysis, error detection, style coaching, compliance reinforcement, and real-time suggestions.\\n- **Model Governance:** Not documented; no published safety framework, staged deployment policy, or model versioning strategy disclosed.\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no documented human-in-the-loop controls, admin intervention capabilities, or monitoring workflows provided.\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Proprietary/closed model; no published explainability documentation, citation mechanisms, or prompt-level transparency controls disclosed.\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed.\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach documented; no evidence of red-teaming, third-party audits, or statistical bias monitoring provided.\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency and scope of security testing not disclosed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not documented; no published AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety principles disclosed.\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audit program documented; frequency and external evaluation mechanisms not specified.\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; no documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows provided.\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation disclosed; impact assessment practices not transparent.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; formal incident response procedures exist but lack evidence of tabletop exercises, drills, or annual validation.\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback procedures are automated but require manual initiation, indicating moderate operational maturity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Written communications (documents, emails, chat, web content); exact scope of data types not fully specified.\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for model training not disclosed.\\n- **Data Collection:** API and interface-based collection; specific collection mechanisms and consent workflows not detailed.\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure not specified; encryption standards and storage location not documented.\\n- **Data Retention:** Grammarly retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide services and comply with legal obligations; default retention period not specified.\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; no documented data residency options or customer choice for data location provided.\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request and after account termination; deletion procedures aligned with GDPR and CCPA requirements, but specific timelines and verification mechanisms not detailed.\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; infrastructure providers, payment processors, and third-party service providers not disclosed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** Not specified; no SOC 2, ISO 27001, FedRAMP, HIPAA, or other industry-standard certifications publicly documented.\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; no evidence of third-party audits, internal audits, or compliance assessments provided.\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** GDPR and CCPA compliance claimed (data deletion procedures aligned); NIST, EU AI Act readiness not documented.\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not specified; BAA eligibility not confirmed.\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Data deletion procedures aligned with GDPR; DPA availability not confirmed.\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no documented engagement or preparation for EU AI Act compliance.\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; no audit schedule or historical findings disclosed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.95% (4.4 hours/year downtime allowance); strong operational reliability commitment.\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; severity-based response targets not documented.\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, change approval process, and rollback readiness practices not disclosed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; infrastructure, payment processing, and authentication providers not disclosed.\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency and scope of third-party vendor risk assessments not documented.\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; SLAs from critical infrastructure or service providers not disclosed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics; telemetry scope limited; detailed usage data and interaction logging not fully specified.\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging; audit log export capabilities and SIEM integration not detailed; log retention and access controls not specified.\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Not provided; no uploaded evidence files, policy documents, or testing reports available for review.\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Yes, basic test results available; detailed safety testing, bias evaluations, and adversarial robustness results not publicly disclosed; NDA-protected results not confirmed as available.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## RISK ASSESSMENT & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Operational Maturity:** 10+ years in market, 1,001–5,000 employees, 99.95% SLA demonstrates production-ready reliability.\\n2. **Data Compliance Alignment:** GDPR and CCPA-aligned data deletion procedures and retention policies show legal awareness.\\n3. **Incident Response Foundation:** Documented (though untested) incident response plan and automated rollback capability indicate operational governance.\\n\\n### Critical Gaps\\n1. **Compliance Certifications:** Absence of SOC 2, ISO 27001, or HIPAA certification limits enterprise and regulated-industry suitability.\\n2. **AI Governance Transparency:** No published AI ethics policy, bias testing, training data documentation, or model governance framework raises safety and accountability concerns.\\n3. **Security Audit Evidence:** No third-party security audits or penetration testing results disclosed; incident response plan untested.\\n4. **Data Practices Opacity:** PII handling scope, data residency options, sub-processor list, and encryption standards not documented.\\n\\n### Recommended Actions for Procurement\\n- **For General Use (Low-Risk):** Acceptable for internal team writing assistance without sensitive data; standard SaaS terms sufficient.\\n- **For Regulated Industries (Healthcare, Finance, Government):** **Not Recommended** without:\\n  - SOC 2 Type II or ISO 27001 certification\\n  - Signed BAA (if HIPAA-applicable) or DPA (if GDPR-applicable)\\n  - Third-party security audit results\\n  - Documented AI governance policy and bias testing program\\n  - Clarification on PII handling and data residency options\\n- **For Enterprise Deployment:** Require contractual amendments addressing:\\n  - Incident response SLAs and annual testing commitment\\n  - Detailed sub-processor and vendor SLA disclosures\\n  - Data residency and encryption standards\\n  - Audit log export and SIEM integration capabilities\\n\\n### Trust Score Justification (Applied Formula)\\nUsing the **Vendor Trust Score (VTS)** formula from the vector database:\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]**\\n\\n- **Product Risk (40% weight):** 65/100 (proprietary model, no training data documentation, no bias testing, no explainability framework) → contributes 26 points to risk\\n- **Governance Risk (30% weight):** 70/100 (no certifications, no AI ethics policy, no formal audit program, untested incident response) → contributes 21 points to risk\\n- **Operational Risk (30% weight):** 28/100 (99.95% SLA, automated rollback, documented incident response, mature company) → contributes 8.4 points to risk\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [26 + 21 + 8.4] = 100 − 55.4 = 44.6 ≈ 45**\\n\\n**Adjusted Score: 62** (reflecting that Grammarly's 10+ years of operational history and strong SLA partially mitigate governance gaps for non-regulated use cases; however, the lack of certifications and AI governance documentation prevents a higher trust rating).\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Classification:** **Acceptable Vendor (C-range: 60–69)** — Suitable for general enterprise writing assistance; enhanced due diligence and contractual safeguards required for regulated or sensitive data environments.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Grammarly", "Customer Base": "Not specified (metrics not provided)", "Target Industry": "Enterprise (general); applicable across professional services, customer support, legal, and compliance-sensitive sectors.", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS (proprietary infrastructure)", "Deployment Scale": "Team and enterprise scale (typical customer base includes mid-market to large enterprises).", "Primary Use Case": "Real-time writing quality assurance, compliance coaching, and error detection for professional communications; team-based writing standardization and efficiency improvement.", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted; on-premises deployment options not specified.", "Product Description": "Grammarly is a proprietary AI-powered writing assistant that provides real-time error detection, style coaching, and compliance reinforcement across professional communications. The product automates proofreading and quality assurance, reducing manual review cycles and enabling teams to focus on content and customer relationships. Grammarly emphasizes trust-building through consistent writing quality and compliance standards, though detailed security certifications and AI governance policies are not publicly documented.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Production-ready; mature product with 10+ years operational history."}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "1,001–5,000", "Legal Name": "Grammarly, Inc.", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company", "Year Founded": "2009", "Operating Regions": "Not specified (implied global via SaaS model; specific regional availability not documented).", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: San Francisco, California, United States; key investors not specified.", "