ocumented AI ethics policy, bias testing approach, or training data documentation are specified—and moderate data practice concerns around limited residency options (EU only) and unclear PII handling specifics. The proprietary/closed model transparency and lack of human oversight documentation further constrain the AI Governance category. Strengths in operational excellence and compliance posture support fast-track procurement for non-healthcare, non-regulated use cases; healthcare deployments require explicit mitigation of governance gaps before contract execution. ---", "overallScore": 84, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 95.84, "Governance": 89, "Operational": 64}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   84 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       36.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        11.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            4.16  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (36.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (11.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (64.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI g