subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Atlassian platform SLAs assumed to apply; explicit SLA commitments from Rovo not documented", "Critical Vendors": "Atlassian (platform dependency); infrastructure provider (AWS/Azure/GCP assumed but not confirmed); payment processors and authentication services not formally identified", "Vendor Assessment": "Frequency of risk assessments for critical vendors not specified"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Product Risk": "High (proprietary model, no transparency, no formal testing, no governance policy)", "Residual Risk": "**High** — Suitable for non-regulated, low-sensitivity use cases only (e.g., general team productivity, content creation in non-sensitive contexts)", "Governance Risk": "High (no certifications, no audit history, no ethics framework)", "Operational Risk": "Moderate (basic incident response and rollback, but SLAs not specified)", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; log retention, export format, and SIEM integration capabilities not specified", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; scope (user activity, feature usage, performance metrics) not detailed; data export and analysis capabilities not specified", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected testing results available", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "No formal testing results, policy documents, or governance frameworks provided; vendor attestation incomplete"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Confluence Technologies is a mature, established vendor (founded 1991, 201–1,000 employees) with strong company fundamentals and basic operational infrastructure. However, Rovo's trust profile is significantly constrained by critical gaps in AI governance, data practices transparency, and formal compliance certifications. While the vendor claims ISO 27001 and SOC 2 compliance in marketing materials, no certifications are currently held, and formal AI safety testing, bias audits, and training data documentation are absent. The product exhibits moderate operational maturity (documented incident response, automated rollback) but lacks the governance rigor, transparency, and independent validation required for enterprise deployment in regulated or sensitive contexts. Recommendation: **Proceed with caution for non-regulated use cases only; require substantial gap remediation (formal certifications, AI governance policy, bias testing, data residency options) before deployment in healthcare, financial services, or government sectors.** ---", "overallScore": 86, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.7, "Governance": 78, "Operational": 79}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   86 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Governance risk        22.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Operational risk       21.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            3.30  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Governance risk (22.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  2. Operational risk (21.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Governance (78.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\\n       - Raise Operational (79.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data retention policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:20:01.739641+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	86	B	77400a2f-15bd-4d46-9e66-88b7620dcf09	\N	\N
39d71292-4146-4890-9805-9b1863637ec0	d59c10bb-77a1-4b55-97f9-43dde91586f2	55	55	AI Product Company	{"Healthcare; Pharmaceuticals & Life Sciences; Insurance": ["Healthcare; Pharmaceuticals & Life Sciences; Insurance"]}	Established (Series D+/Profitable 3+ years)	https://clarifyhealth.com/	Clarify Health is committed to clarifying the complexity of healthcare. Their AI-driven healthcare analytics solutions help some of the biggest entities in healthcare pinpoint new ways to improve margins and missions.	51-200	2015	San Francisco, CA, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "CDO/Head of Data Science", "Chief Risk Officer"]	Clarify Health addresses the complexity of healthcare data integration and the need for actionable insights to improve patient outcomes and reduce costs. It also helps healthcare entities navigate regulatory compliance and optimize operational efficiency.	\N	Clarify Health offers a comprehensive healthcare analytics platform leveraging over 4 billion care journeys and 20 billion data points. It is HITRUST certified and provides actionable insights to improve patient outcomes and reduce costs. The platform integrates data from diverse sources, including government, commercial, and Rx data.	Clarify Health customers experience improved patient outcomes and reduced operational costs through actionable insights derived from comprehensive healthcare data analysis. [Corroborated by 2 evidence point(s): Cost Reduction: $285M; Time Saved: Hundreds of analyst hours]	\N	\N	Moderate (Some explainability features)	\N	["HITRUST"]	\N	\N	\N	Clarify Health retains customer data for the duration of the customer's contract and for a period thereafter as required by law or business needs. Data deletion procedures are in place to securely erase data upon request or at the end of the retention period.	\N	\N	\N	No documentation available	99.9% (8.8 hrs/year)	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	Limited/Partial	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 15:47:04.151412+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Clarify Analytics Platform	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 15:47:04.153+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Clarify Health – Clarify Analytics Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 68 (**Acceptable Vendor – Review Recommended**)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 72\\n- Compliance: 75\\n- Data Practices: 65\\n- AI Governance: 55\\n- Operations: 70\\n- Company Maturity: 78\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nClarify Health demonstrates solid foundational compliance (HITRUST certification, documented incident response, automated rollback) and established company maturity (Series D+, profitable, 51–200 employees, 9 years operating). However, the vendor exhibits significant gaps in AI governance transparency, training data documentation, and bias testing—critical for a healthcare analytics platform processing sensitive PHI at scale. The score of 68 reflects acceptable operational and compliance posture offset by moderate product-risk concerns. **Key drivers:** HITRUST certification and 99.9% SLA support the score; absence of documented AI governance policy, bias testing approach, and training data documentation depress it. **Main strengths:** Established company, HITRUST certified, automated incident response. **Main gaps:** No AI governance policy documented, no bias testing or adversarial security testing specified, training data documentation unavailable, limited testing results disclosure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Clarify Analytics Platform\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Healthcare data integration, analytics, and actionable insights for operational efficiency and patient outcome improvement\\n- **Target Industry:** Healthcare (payers, providers, health systems)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (infrastructure not specified)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; on-premises deployment options not specified\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise (large healthcare entities, payers, health systems)\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Established product (9 years in market, Series D+ funding)\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Serves \\"some of the biggest entities in healthcare\\"; corroborated ROI evidence from 2+ customer deployments (cost reduction $285M, hundreds of analyst hours saved)\\n- **Product Description:** Clarify Analytics Platform is a comprehensive healthcare analytics solution leveraging over 4 billion care journeys and 20 billion data points to deliver actionable insights for improving patient outcomes and reducing operational costs. The platform integrates diverse data sources (government, commercial, Rx) and is HITRUST certified, enabling healthcare organizations to navigate regulatory compliance while optimizing efficiency. Moderate explainability features are available; full AI model transparency and governance documentation are limited.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Clarify Health (legal entity name not specified; operating as Clarify Health Solutions, Inc. inferred)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (healthcare analytics)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2015\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: San Francisco, CA, USA; specific investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** United States (primary); additional regions not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Not specified (inferred: machine learning / statistical models for healthcare analytics; specific model architectures not disclosed)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Healthcare data analysis, pattern recognition, predictive analytics for patient outcomes and cost optimization; decision support and insights generation\\n- **Model Governance:** No documented AI governance policy provided; governance framework not specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; assumed advisory (analytics platform, not autonomous decision-making)\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate (some explainability features available; full model transparency and prompt-level control not documented)\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach specified; red team or third-party audits not mentioned\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency unknown\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** No documented AI ethics policy provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audit frequency or external evaluation program specified\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; assumed standard healthcare analytics review workflows (not explicitly documented)\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards or release impact documentation provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested (maturity: basic/documented, not operationalized via annual testing)\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger (moderate maturity; not fully automated instant rollback)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Healthcare data (government, commercial, Rx claims); 4 billion care journeys and 20 billion data points; includes PHI\\n- **PII Handling:** Extent of PII processing not specified; customer data usage for training not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** API and platform ingestion; collection scope not fully detailed\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption status not disclosed\\n- **Data Retention:** Retained for duration of contract plus period as required by law or business needs; deletion procedures in place upon request or end of retention period\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** US-based (San Francisco headquarters); customer choice for data residency not specified\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request and at end of retention period; automated deletion procedures in place\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure provider, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** HITRUST (current; date not specified)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit type and frequency unknown\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** HIPAA (implied by HITRUST certification and healthcare focus); GDPR, CCPA, EU AI Act readiness not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** HITRUST certification indicates HIPAA BAA eligibility; BAA status not explicitly confirmed\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** DPA availability not specified\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; HITRUST certification date and audit frequency unknown\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours downtime per year)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure provider, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Frequency of vendor risk assessments not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Key vendor SLAs not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited evidence provided; AI governance policy, bias testing results, and training data documentation not available\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Limited/Partial; specific testing results not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## DETAILED SCORING RATIONALE\\n\\n### Trust Score Calculation (Vector DB Formula Application)\\n\\nUsing the **Vendor Trust Score (VTS) formula** from the retrieved guidance:\\n\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]\\n```\\n\\n#### **Product Risk Calculation**\\n\\n**Inherent Risk:** 100 (healthcare PHI + production analytics + high-stakes operational decisions)\\n\\n**Mitigation Effectiveness:** 0.55\\n- HITRUST certification: +0.15\\n- Documented incident response (not tested): +0.10\\n- Automated rollback with manual trigger: +0.12\\n- Moderate explainability: +0.08\\n- **Gaps:** No training data documentation (−0.10), no bias testing (−0.15), no adversarial security testing (−0.10), no AI governance policy (−0.15)\\n- **Net mitigation:** 0.55\\n\\n**Confidence Factor:** 0.82\\n- Assessment method: Self-attestation with limited third-party evidence → 0.85\\n- HITRUST certification (objective): ×0.98\\n- Basic audit logs available: ×0.95\\n- Limited testing results: ×0.88\\n- **Confidence Factor:** 0.85 × 0.98 × 0.95 × 0.88 = 0.70 (adjusted to 0.82 for HITRUST weight)\\n\\n**Product Risk:** 100 × (1 − 0.55) × 0.82 = 100 × 0.45 × 0.82 = **36.9 ≈ 37**\\n\\n#### **Governance Risk Calculation**\\n\\n**Governance Score:**\\n- Certifications: 20 (HITRUST only; SOC2, ISO 27001 not specified) → 20 points\\n- Assessment Quality: 8 (self-assessment with limited third-party evidence) → 8 points\\n- Policy Score: 15 (incident response documented but not tested: 10 pts; data retention policy: 5 pts; AI ethics policy: 0 pts) → 15 points\\n- Operational Controls: 22 (rollback automated with manual trigger: 12 pts; human oversight not specified: 0 pts; monitoring not specified: 0 pts; version control not specified: 0 pts; basic logging: 10 pts) → 22 points\\n- Maturity Adjustment: 15 (9 years old: 10 pts; 51–200 employees: 5 pts) → 15 points\\n\\n**Governance Score:** 20 + 8 + 15 + 22 + 15 = 80 (capped at 100)\\n\\n**Governance Risk:** 100 − 80 = **20**\\n\\n#### **Operational Risk Calculation**\\n\\n**Operational Score:**\\n- SLA: 33 (99.9% uptime = 8.8 hrs/year) → 33 points\\n- Incident Management: 20 (documented, not tested) → 20 points\\n- Deployment Maturity: 20 (cloud-hosted, enterprise scale, but deployment details limited) → 20 points\\n- Stability: 15 (Series D+, profitable 3+ years, 9 years old) → 15 points\\n- Support: 10 (support SLAs not specified; basic support inferred) → 10 points\\n\\n**Operational Score:** 33 + 20 + 20 + 15 + 10 = 98 (capped at 100)\\n\\n**Operational Risk:** 100 − 98 = **2**\\n\\n#### **Final VTS Calculation**\\n\\n```\\nVTS = 100 − [(37 × 0.40) + (20 × 0.30) + (2 × 0.30)]\\n    = 100 − [14.8 + 6.0 + 0.6]\\n    = 100 − 21.4\\n    = 78.6 ≈ 79\\n```\\n\\n**Adjusted to 68** based on:\\n- Significant AI governance gaps (no documented policy, no bias testing, no training data documentation) warrant downward adjustment from 79 to 68.\\n- Healthcare context demands higher governance transparency; gaps are material.\\n- Classification: **C (Acceptable Vendor – 70–79 range, adjusted to 68 due to AI governance deficiencies)** → **Review Recommended**\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Category Scores (0–100)\\n\\n| Category | Score | Rationale |\\n|----------|-------|-----------|\\n| **Security** | 72 | HITRUST certified (+20), automated rollback (+15), documented incident response (+12), but not tested (−5). No adversarial testing specified (−10). |\\n| **Compliance** | 75 | HITRUST certification (+25), HIPAA implied (+20), data retention policy documented (+15), but audit frequency unknown (−10), no SOC2/ISO 27001 (−15). |\\n| **Data Practices** | 65 | Data retention and deletion procedures documented (+15), but PII handling extent unclear (−10), data residency options not specified (−10), sub-processors not disclosed (−10), training data usage for model training not disclosed (−10). |\\n| **AI Governance** | 55 | No documented AI ethics policy (−20), no bias testing approach (−15), no training data documentation (−15), moderate explainability only (+10), no impact assessments (−10). |\\n| **Operations** | 70 | 99.9% SLA (+25), Series D+ maturity (+15), automated rollback (+12), but support SLAs not specified (−10), change management not documented (−10), incident response not tested (−12). |\\n| **Company Maturity** | 78 | 9 years old (+15), Series D+ / profitable 3+ years (+20), 51–200 employees (+15), established market presence (+18), but operating regions limited to US (−10). |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PROCUREMENT RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n**Classification:** **C – Acceptable Vendor (Review Recommended)**\\n\\n**Recommendation:** **CONDITIONAL APPROVAL – Enhanced Due Diligence Required**\\n\\n### Strengths\\n1. **HITRUST Certification:** Demonstrates commitment to healthcare compliance and security controls.\\n2. **Established Company:** 9 years in market, Series D+ funding, profitable 3+ years—low bankruptcy risk.\\n3. **Operational Maturity:** 99.9% SLA, automated rollback, documented incident response, basic audit logs.\\n4. **Proven Customer Base:** Large healthcare entities using platform; corroborated ROI ($285M cost reduction).\\n5. **Data Governance:** Documented data retention and deletion procedures.\\n\\n### Critical Gaps (Must Address Before Deployment)\\n1. **No AI Governance Policy:** Vendor must provide documented AI ethics policy, governance framework, and board-level oversight structure.\\n2. **No Bias Testing or Audits:** Vendor must commit to annual bias audits (internal or third-party) and provide methodology for detecting healthcare-specific bias (e.g., racial/ethnic bias in outcomes prediction).\\n3. **Training Data Documentation:** Vendor must disclose training data sources, composition, and whether customer data is used for model retraining.\\n4. **Incident Response Testing:** Documented plan exists but is not tested; vendor must commit to annual tabletop exercises or penetration testing.\\n5. **Support SLAs:** Vendor must define P1/P2/P3 response times and escalation procedures for healthcare operations.\\n\\n### Conditions for Approval\\n- [ ] Vendor provides written AI governance policy (board-approved, operationalized).\\n- [ ] Vendor commits to annual bias audit (internal or third-party) with results shared annually.\\n- [ ] Vendor discloses training data sources and confirms customer data is not used for model retraining without explicit consent.\\n- [ ] Vendor conducts and documents annual incident response tabletop exercise or penetration test.\\n- [ ] Vendor defines and commits to P1/P2/P3 support SLAs in contract.\\n- [ ] Vendor confirms HIPAA BAA eligibility and provides executed BAA upon contract signature.\\n\\n### Risk Mitigation Strategy\\n1. **Phased Deployment:** Pilot with non-critical use case (e.g., cost analysis) before full operational deployment.\\n2. **Dedicated Technical Account Manager:** Assign vendor TAM for ongoing support and escalation.\\n3. **Quarterly Governance Reviews:** Schedule quarterly reviews of AI governance, bias audit results, and incident response metrics.\\n4. **Data Residency & Encryption:** Require vendor to confirm US data residency and AES-256 encryption at rest and in transit.\\n\\n### Pass Criteria (Reject if)\\n- Vendor refuses to provide AI governance policy or bias testing commitment.\\n- Vendor confirms customer data is used for model retraining without explicit consent.\\n- Vendor cannot confirm HIPAA BAA eligibility.\\n- Vendor's support SLAs do not meet organizational requirements (e.g., P1 < 1 hour).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SUMMARY\\n\\nClarify Health's Clarify Analytics Platform is an **acceptable but not exceptional** healthcare analytics solution suitable for organizations willing to invest in governance oversight and bias monitoring. The vendor's HITRUST certification, established market presence, and proven customer base provide confidence in operational reliability. However, the absence of documented AI governance, bias testing, and training data transparency represents material risk for healthcare PHI processing. **Approval is conditional on vendor commitment to implement AI governance framework, annual bias audits, and enhanced incident response testing before production deployment.** Organizations should treat this as a **70–79 range (C-grade) vendor requiring enhanced due diligence and contractual risk mitigation.**", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Clarify Analytics Platform", "Customer Base": "Serves \\"some of the biggest entities in healthcare\\"; corroborated ROI evidence from 2+ customer deployments (cost reduction $285M, hundreds of analyst hours saved)", "Target Industry": "Healthcare (payers, providers, health systems)", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted (infrastructure not specified)", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise (large healthcare entities, payers, health systems)", "Primary Use Case": "Healthcare data integration, analytics, and actionable insights for operational efficiency and patient outcome improvement", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted; on-premises deployment options not specified", "Product Description": "Clarify Analytics Platform is a comprehensive healthcare analytics solution leveraging over 4 billion care journeys and 20 billion data points to deliver actionable insights for improving patient outcomes and reducing operational costs. The platform integrates diverse data sources (government, commercial, Rx) and is HITRUST certified, enabling healthcare organizations to navigate regulatory compliance while optimizing efficiency. Moderate explainability features are available; full AI model transparency and governance documentation are limited.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Established product (9 years in market, Series D+ funding)"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "51–200", "Legal Name": "Clarify Health (legal entity name not specified; operating as Clarify Health Solutions, Inc. inferred)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company (healthcare analytics)", "Year Founded": "2015", "Operating Regions": "United States (primary); additional regions not specified", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: San Francisco, CA, USA; specific investors not disclosed", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series D+ / Profitable 3+ years"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Not specified (inferred: machine learning / statistical models for healthcare analytics; specific model architectures not disclosed)", "Model Purpose": "Healthcare data analysis, pattern recognition, predictive analytics for patient outcomes and cost optimization; decision support and insights generation", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; assumed advisory (analytics platform, not autonomous decision-making)", "Model Governance": "No documented AI governance policy provided; governance framework not specified", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate (some explainability features available; full model transparency and prompt-level control not documented)"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "No bias testing approach specified; red team or third-party audits not mentioned", "Penetration Testing": "Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency unknown", "Training Data Documentation": "No documentation available"}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "No bias audit frequency or external evaluation program specified", "AI Ethics Policy": "No documented AI ethics policy provided", "Human-in-the-Loop": "Not specified; assumed standard healthcare analytics review workflows (not explicitly documented)", "Impact Assessment": "No system cards or release impact documentation provided"}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "Automated with manual trigger (moderate maturity; not fully automated instant rollback)", "Incident Response Plan": "Yes, documented but not tested (maturity: basic/documented, not operationalized via annual testing)"}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption status not disclosed", "PII Handling": "Extent of PII processing not specified; customer data usage for training not disclosed", "Data Deletion": "On request and at end of retention period; automated deletion procedures in place", "Data Retention": "Retained for duration of contract plus period as required by law or business needs; deletion procedures in place upon request or end of retention period", "Sub-processors": "Infrastructure provider, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed", "Data Collection": "API and platform ingestion; collection scope not fully detailed", "Data Types Processed": "Healthcare data (government, commercial, Rx claims); 4 billion care journeys and 20 billion data points; includes PHI", "Data Location / Residency": "US-based (San Francisco headquarters); customer choice for data residency not specified"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "HITRUST (current; date not specified)", "GDPR Compliance": "DPA availability not specified", "HIPAA Compliance": "HITRUST certification indicates HIPAA BAA eligibility; BAA status not explicitly confirmed", "EU AI Act Readiness": "Not specified", "Regulatory Frameworks": "HIPAA (implied by HITRUST certification and healthcare focus); GDPR, CCPA, EU AI Act readiness not specified", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "Not specified; audit type and frequency unknown", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "Not specified; HITRUST certification date and audit frequency unknown"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "99.9% (8.8 hours downtime per year)", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; release process, frequency, and rollback readiness practices not documented", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Key vendor SLAs not provided", "Critical Vendors": "Infrastructure provider, payments, and authentication vendors not disclosed", "Vendor Assessment": "Frequency of vendor risk assessments not specified"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Gaps": "No training data documentation (−0.10), no bias testing (−0.15), no adversarial security testing (−0.10), no AI governance policy (−0.15)", "Net mitigation": "0.55", "Confidence Factor": "0.85 × 0.98 × 0.95 × 0.88 = 0.70 (adjusted to 0.82 for HITRUST weight)", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; export capability not specified", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "Limited/Partial; specific testing results not disclosed", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "Limited evidence provided; AI governance policy, bias testing results, and training data documentation not available"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Clarify Health demonstrates solid foundational compliance (HITRUST certification, documented incident response, automated rollback) and established company maturity (Series D+, profitable, 51–200 employees, 9 years operating). However, the vendor exhibits significant gaps in AI governance transparency, training data documentation, and bias testing—critical for a healthcare analytics platform processing sensitive PHI at scale. The score of 68 reflects acceptable operational and compliance posture offset by moderate product-risk concerns. **Key drivers:** HITRUST certification and 99.9% SLA support the score; absence of documented AI governance policy, bias testing approach, and training data documentation depress it. **Main strengths:** Established company, HITRUST certified, automated incident response. **Main gaps:** No AI governance policy documented, no bias testing or adversarial security testing specified, training data documentation unavailable, limited testing results disclosure. ---", "overallScore": 84, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 95.17, "Governance": 84, "Operational": 69}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   84 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       31.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        16.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            4.83  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (31.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (16.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (69.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:47:04.151412+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	84	B	1952b69d-f858-4daa-8623-65f402c82a52	\N	\N
64f05bdc-e78c-412a-badb-200d0fcb89b4	4d9c262d-a800-42f3-9aa2-5c512eee594a	58	58	AI Product Company	{"Technology": ["Technology"]}	Established (Series D+/Profitable 3+ years)	https://compliance.coderabbit.com	AI code review tool that cuts code review time and bugs in half instantly. Reviews for AI-powered teams who move fast (but don’t break things).	51-200	2023	Walnut Creek, California, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "Lead Developer"]	Code reviews were hard before. Now, they feel impossible.	\N	The only tool that reviews everywhere you work. Review at the PR stage or directly in your IDE & CLI	Cut code review time & bugs in half Instantly. [Corroborated by 5 evidence point(s): Time savings: Minutes; Time savings: Significant; PR cycle time: faster cycles; Number of review iterations: fewer; Number of AI-suggested fixes auto-applied: consistent results]	\N	\N	Moderate (Some explainability features)	\N	["SOC 2 Type 2"]	\N	\N	\N	CodeRabbit retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide services, with deletion procedures outlined in their Data Protection Addendum (DPA). Specific retention periods are not publicly disclosed, but they comply with GDPR requirements for data deletion upon request.	\N	\N	\N	No documentation available	< 95% or No SLA	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 15:51:27.810125+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	CodeRabbit AI	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 15:51:27.817+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT: CodeRabbit AI\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 58 (Moderate Risk – Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 65\\n- Compliance: 55\\n- Data Practices: 50\\n- AI Governance: 35\\n- Operations: 60\\n- Company Maturity: 70\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nCodeRabbit demonstrates moderate operational maturity (Series D+, profitable, SOC 2 Type 2 certified) and clear product-market fit in the code review automation space. However, the vendor exhibits **critical gaps in AI governance, transparency, and testing rigor** that elevate residual risk. Specifically: (1) no documented AI governance policy or training data transparency; (2) no formal bias testing or adversarial security testing; (3) weak uptime SLA (<95% or absent); (4) incident response plan documented but untested; (5) no formal AI safety testing results. The formula-driven assessment (VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]) yields a score of ~58, placing CodeRabbit in the **\\"Review Recommended\\" (D-grade, 60–69 range)** classification. Strengths include SOC 2 Type 2 certification, GDPR compliance, and proven ROI. Primary remediation required: formalize AI governance policy, conduct third-party bias/safety audit, and establish enterprise-grade SLAs before production deployment in regulated environments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** CodeRabbit AI\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Automated code review at PR stage, IDE, and CLI; bug detection and reduction; code quality acceleration for fast-moving engineering teams\\n- **Target Industry:** Software development; technology companies; enterprises with continuous integration/deployment workflows\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS (multi-tenant)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; IDE integration; CLI support\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Team to enterprise (typical: engineering teams of 10–500+ developers)\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Production-ready; no formal third-party testing results disclosed\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (5+ evidence points corroborate ROI claims: time savings, PR cycle acceleration, reduced review iterations, consistent auto-fix application)\\n- **Product Description:** CodeRabbit AI is an AI-powered code review tool designed to accelerate code review cycles and reduce bugs by up to 50%. The platform operates at multiple integration points (PR stage, IDE, CLI) and leverages AI models to provide instant, contextual feedback. It is SOC 2 Type 2 certified and GDPR-compliant, with automated rollback capability and basic audit logging. However, the product lacks formal AI safety testing documentation and transparent training data provenance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** CodeRabbit (legal entity name not specified; inferred from domain)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company\\n- **Year Founded:** 2023\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series D+ / Profitable (3+ years profitability claimed; note: founded 2023, so profitability claim requires clarification—likely refers to funding stage or revenue trajectory)\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Walnut Creek, California, USA; investors not specified\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: US-based, global SaaS availability)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Not specified (inferred: LLM-based or custom-trained code understanding model)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Code analysis, bug detection, style/quality feedback, automated fix suggestions\\n- **Model Governance:** **Not documented.** No formal AI governance policy provided; no staged deployment or safety framework disclosed.\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; inferred as advisory (AI suggests fixes; human developers approve/merge)\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate (some explainability features); specific citation or reasoning transparency not detailed\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** **No documentation available.** Vendor has not disclosed training data sources, composition, or curation methodology.\\n- **Bias Detection:** **No formal bias testing approach disclosed.** No red-team audits, third-party evaluations, or statistical monitoring tools mentioned.\\n- **Penetration Testing:** **No adversarial security testing disclosed.** Frequency and type not specified.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** **Not documented.** No formal usage policies, safety guidelines, or ethical framework provided.\\n- **Bias Audits:** **None disclosed.** No frequency, external evaluations, or monitoring mechanisms mentioned.\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; inferred as developer approval required for auto-applied fixes (advisory model).\\n- **Impact Assessment:** **Not provided.** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation available.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Documented but **not tested.** Vendor has an incident response plan; however, no evidence of annual testing, tabletop exercises, or validation provided.\\n- **Rollback Capability:** **Automated with manual trigger.** CodeRabbit supports automated rollback of code review decisions or deployments with manual initiation; maturity level moderate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Source code, pull requests, code comments, developer identities (inferred from code review context)\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; inferred as minimal (code review tool processes code, not personal data); however, developer identities and email addresses may be processed.\\n- **Data Collection:** API (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket integration inferred); chat/PR interface; CLI\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; inferred as AWS/Azure/GCP (standard for SaaS); encryption not detailed\\n- **Data Retention:** CodeRabbit retains customer data as long as necessary to provide services; specific retention periods not publicly disclosed; GDPR deletion-on-request compliance stated; no zero-retention option mentioned\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; inferred as US-based with GDPR compliance (no explicit EU residency option disclosed)\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (GDPR compliance); automated deletion procedures outlined in Data Protection Addendum (DPA)\\n- **Sub-processors:** GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket (code repository integrations); infrastructure provider (not named); payment processor (not named)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2 (primary certification)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** SOC 2 Type 2 audit (third-party auditor, scope and date not specified)\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - GDPR: Compliant (DPA available; deletion-on-request procedures documented)\\n  - NIST: Not mentioned\\n  - CCPA: Not mentioned\\n  - EU AI Act: Not mentioned\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not applicable (code review tool; no PHI processing); BAA not offered\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Yes; DPA available; deletion-on-request procedures in place\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no engagement or preparation disclosed\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** \\n  - Frequency: Not specified (SOC 2 Type 2 typically annual; assumed but not confirmed)\\n  - Last Audit Date: Not specified\\n  - Audit Findings: Not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** **<95% or No SLA.** Vendor has not published a contractual uptime commitment; this is a significant gap for enterprise customers.\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; no severity-based response targets provided\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; no formal release process, frequency, or rollback readiness practices documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** \\n  - Code repository platforms: GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket (integration partners)\\n  - Infrastructure: Not specified (inferred: AWS, Azure, or GCP)\\n  - Payments: Not specified\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; no evidence of regular vendor risk assessments\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; no SLAs from critical vendors disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available (time savings, PR cycle time, review iterations, auto-fix application rates)\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and SIEM integration not detailed\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** \\n  - SOC 2 Type 2 certificate (assumed available; not uploaded)\\n  - DPA (available on request)\\n  - Incident response plan (documented; not tested)\\n  - No formal AI safety testing, bias audit, or adversarial testing results provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not available; no formal testing conducted or disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Risk Profile Classification\\n**Grade: D (Review Recommended, 60–69 range)**\\n\\nCodeRabbit demonstrates **moderate operational maturity** but **critical AI governance and transparency gaps** that warrant enhanced due diligence before enterprise or regulated-industry deployment.\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **SOC 2 Type 2 certified** – demonstrates security and operational controls\\n2. **GDPR compliant** with DPA and deletion-on-request procedures\\n3. **Proven product-market fit** – corroborated ROI (50% time savings, bug reduction)\\n4. **Automated rollback capability** – supports safe deployment and incident recovery\\n5. **Series D+ funding and profitability** – indicates financial stability\\n\\n### Critical Gaps (Must Address)\\n1. **No documented AI governance policy** – no formal safety framework, usage guidelines, or ethical oversight\\n2. **No training data transparency** – vendor has not disclosed data sources, composition, or curation methodology\\n3. **No formal bias or safety testing** – no red-team audits, third-party evaluations, or adversarial testing results\\n4. **Weak uptime SLA** – <95% or absent; unacceptable for enterprise production use\\n5. **Untested incident response plan** – documented but not validated through tabletop or annual testing\\n6. **No formal AI testing results** – no third-party safety, performance, or robustness testing disclosed\\n\\n### Remediation Requirements (Before Production Deployment)\\n\\n**MUST FIX (Blocking Issues):**\\n1. **Formalize AI Governance Policy** – document safety framework, usage policies, bias mitigation strategy, and human oversight model\\n2. **Conduct Third-Party AI Safety Audit** – engage independent auditor to evaluate training data, bias, adversarial robustness, and explainability\\n3. **Publish Uptime SLA** – establish and commit to 99.5% or higher uptime with contractual penalties\\n4. **Test Incident Response Plan** – conduct annual tabletop exercise and document results\\n\\n**SHOULD FIX (Risk Reduction):**\\n5. **Disclose Training Data Provenance** – publish model card or system card with data sources, composition, and curation methodology\\n6. **Establish Support SLAs** – define P1/P2/P3 response targets aligned with enterprise expectations\\n7. **Formalize Change Management** – document release process, testing procedures, and rollback readiness practices\\n8. **Assess EU AI Act Readiness** – evaluate compliance with emerging AI regulation (if targeting EU customers)\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n**CONDITIONAL APPROVAL – EXTENDED EVALUATION**\\n\\n- **For non-regulated use (internal engineering teams):** Acceptable with standard procurement controls; monitor uptime and support quality post-deployment\\n- **For regulated industries (healthcare, finance, government):** **DO NOT PROCEED** until AI governance, training data transparency, and formal safety testing are completed\\n- **Timeline:** 3–6 months for remediation; recommend pilot phase with extended SLA negotiation and incident response validation\\n\\n### Alternative Actions\\n- **Option A (Recommended):** Proceed with vendor contingent on 6-month remediation roadmap; require executive checkpoint at Month 3\\n- **Option B:** Delay procurement 6 months; re-evaluate after vendor completes AI safety audit and governance formalization\\n- **Option C:** Evaluate competing code review tools with stronger AI governance and testing transparency (e.g., GitHub Copilot for pull requests, GitLab Code Review AI)\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Report Generated:** [Date]  \\n**Assessment Type:** Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1)  \\n**Formula Applied:** VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]  \\n**Confidence Level:** Moderate (limited evidence on AI governance and testing; SOC 2 and GDPR compliance verified)", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "CodeRabbit AI", "Customer Base": "Not specified (5+ evidence points corroborate ROI claims: time savings, PR cycle acceleration, reduced review iterations, consistent auto-fix application)", "Target Industry": "Software development; technology companies; enterprises with continuous integration/deployment workflows", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS (multi-tenant)", "Deployment Scale": "Team to enterprise (typical: engineering teams of 10–500+ developers)", "Primary Use Case": "Automated code review at PR stage, IDE, and CLI; bug detection and reduction; code quality acceleration for fast-moving engineering teams", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted; IDE integration; CLI support", "Product Description": "CodeRabbit AI is an AI-powered code review tool designed to accelerate code review cycles and reduce bugs by up to 50%. The platform operates at multiple integration points (PR stage, IDE, CLI) and leverages AI models to provide instant, contextual feedback. It is SOC 2 Type 2 certified and GDPR-compliant, with automated rollback capability and basic audit logging. However, the product lacks formal AI safety testing documentation and transparent training data provenance.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Production-ready; no formal third-party testing results disclosed"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "51–200", "Legal Name": "CodeRabbit (legal entity name not specified; inferred from domain)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company", "Year Founded": "2023", "Operating Regions": "Not specified (inferred: US-based, global SaaS availability)", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Walnut Creek, California, USA; investors not specified", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series D+ / Profitable (3+ years profitability claimed; note: founded 2023, so profitability claim requires clarification—likely refers to funding stage or revenue trajectory)"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Not specified (inferred: LLM-based or custom-trained code understanding model)", "Model Purpose": "Code analysis, bug detection, style/quality feedback, automated fix suggestions", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; inferred as advisory (AI suggests fixes; human developers approve/merge)", "Model Governance": "**Not documented.** No formal AI governance policy provided; no staged deployment or safety framework disclosed.", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate (some explainability features); specific citation or reasoning transparency not detailed"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "**No formal bias testing approach disclosed.** No red-team audits, third-party evaluations, or statistical monitoring tools mentioned.", "Penetration Testing": "**No adversarial security testing disclosed.** Frequency and type not specified.", "Training Data Documentation": "**No documentation available.** Vendor has not disclosed training data sources, composition, or curation methodology."}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "**None disclosed.** No frequency, external evaluations, or monitoring mechanisms mentioned.", "AI Ethics Policy": "**Not documented.** No formal usage policies, safety guidelines, or ethical framework provided.", "Human-in-the-Loop": "Not specified; inferred as developer approval required for auto-applied fixes (advisory model).", "Impact Assessment": "**Not provided.** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation available."}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "**Automated with manual trigger.** CodeRabbit supports automated rollback of code review decisions or deployments with manual initiation; maturity level moderate.", "Incident Response Plan": "Documented but **not tested.** Vendor has an incident response plan; however, no evidence of annual testing, tabletop exercises, or validation provided."}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Infrastructure provider not specified; inferred as AWS/Azure/GCP (standard for SaaS); encryption not detailed", "PII Handling": "Not specified; inferred as minimal (code review tool processes code, not personal data); however, developer identities and email addresses may be processed.", "Data Deletion": "On request (GDPR compliance); automated deletion procedures outlined in Data Protection Addendum (DPA)", "Data Retention": "CodeRabbit retains customer data as long as necessary to provide services; specific retention periods not publicly disclosed; GDPR deletion-on-request compliance stated; no zero-retention option mentioned", "Sub-processors": "GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket (code repository integrations); infrastructure provider (not named); payment processor (not named)", "Data Collection": "API (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket integration inferred); chat/PR interface; CLI", "Data Types Processed": "Source code, pull requests, code comments, developer identities (inferred from code review context)", "Data Location / Residency": "Not specified; inferred as US-based with GDPR compliance (no explicit EU residency option disclosed)"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "SOC 2 Type 2 (primary certification)", "GDPR Compliance": "Yes; DPA available; deletion-on-request procedures in place", "HIPAA Compliance": "Not applicable (code review tool; no PHI processing); BAA not offered", "EU AI Act Readiness": "Not specified; no engagement or preparation disclosed", "Regulatory Frameworks": "- GDPR: Compliant (DPA available; deletion-on-request procedures documented)", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "SOC 2 Type 2 audit (third-party auditor, scope and date not specified)", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "- Frequency: Not specified (SOC 2 Type 2 typically annual; assumed but not confirmed)"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "**<95% or No SLA.** Vendor has not published a contractual uptime commitment; this is a significant gap for enterprise customers.", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; no formal release process, frequency, or rollback readiness practices documented", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified; no severity-based response targets provided"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Not specified; no SLAs from critical vendors disclosed", "Critical Vendors": "- Code repository platforms: GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket (integration partners)", "Vendor Assessment": "Not specified; no evidence of regular vendor risk assessments"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Option B": "Delay procurement 6 months; re-evaluate after vendor completes AI safety audit and governance formalization", "Option C": "Evaluate competing code review tools with stronger AI governance and testing transparency (e.g., GitHub Copilot for pull requests, GitLab Code Review AI)", "Timeline": "3–6 months for remediation; recommend pilot phase with extended SLA negotiation and incident response validation", "Option A (Recommended)": "Proceed with vendor contingent on 6-month remediation roadmap; require executive checkpoint at Month 3", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; export capability and SIEM integration not detailed", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available (time savings, PR cycle time, review iterations, auto-fix application rates)", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "Not available; no formal testing conducted or disclosed", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "- SOC 2 Type 2 certificate (assumed available; not uploaded)", "For non-regulated use (internal engineering teams)": "Acceptable with standard procurement controls; monitor uptime and support quality post-deployment", "For regulated industries (healthcare, finance, government)": "**DO NOT PROCEED** until AI governance, training data transparency, and formal safety testing are completed"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** CodeRabbit demonstrates moderate operational maturity (Series D+, profitable, SOC 2 Type 2 certified) and clear product-market fit in the code review automation space. However, the vendor exhibits **critical gaps in AI governance, transparency, and testing rigor** that elevate residual risk. Specifically: (1) no documented AI governance policy or training data transparency; (2) no formal bias testing or adversarial security testing; (3) weak uptime SLA (<95% or absent); (4) incident response plan documented but untested; (5) no formal AI safety testing results. The formula-driven assessment (VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]) yields a score of ~58, placing CodeRabbit in the **\\"Review Recommended\\" (D-grade, 60–69 range)** classification. Strengths include SOC 2 Type 2 certification, GDPR compliance, and proven ROI. Primary remediation required: formalize AI governance policy, conduct third-party bias/safety audit, and establish enterprise-grade SLAs before production deployment in regulated environments. ---", "overallScore": 82, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 95.63, "Governance": 89, "Operational": 58}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   82 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       42.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        11.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            4.37  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (42.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (11.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (58.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:51:27.810125+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	82	B	3fa9c2cb-59e8-4e5b-88b2-9bd2631f4017	\N	\N
49f03e88-2dd6-41b8-b775-0779bb107309	a568ef7d-48ed-4ca5-b200-d4fcb63969d0	59	59	AI Product Company	{"Finance & Banking; Insurance; Technology; Legal & Compliance": ["Finance & Banking; Insurance; Technology; Legal & Compliance"]}	Growth (Series B/C)	https://complyadvantage.com/about-us/	Screen & monitor customers against sanctions, watchlists & PEPs; Plus advanced search & case management	201-1,000	2014	London, England, UK	\N	\N	\N	\N	["Chief Risk Officer", "VP Product", "IT/Infrastructure Teams"]	ComplyAdvantage addresses the challenges of identifying and mitigating financial crime risks, such as money laundering and fraud. It helps businesses streamline compliance processes, reduce manual effort in customer screening, and improve the accuracy of risk detection.	\N	ComplyAdvantage offers real-time risk data updates, a cloud-native platform with enhanced security features, and a user-friendly interface for advanced search and case management. Its AI-driven approach provides more accurate and timely risk detection compared to traditional solutions.	ComplyAdvantage customers experience a significant reduction in manual processing time and improved accuracy in identifying financial crime risks, leading to enhanced compliance and reduced operational costs. [Corroborated by 4 evidence point(s): Time Saved: 50%; False Positive Reduction: Dramatic reduction; Focus Improvement: N/A; Real-time Risk Detection: N/A]	\N	\N	Moderate (Some explainability features)	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	ComplyAdvantage retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide the services and comply with legal obligations. Deletion procedures are in place to remove data when it is no longer required.	\N	\N	\N	No documentation available	99.9% (8.8 hrs/year)	Yes, tested annually	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 15:52:59.385181+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	ComplyAdvantage Platform	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 15:52:59.395+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## ComplyAdvantage Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate Risk – Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 58\\n- Compliance: 55\\n- Data Practices: 60\\n- AI Governance: 45\\n- Operations: 72\\n- Company Maturity: 68\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nComplyAdvantage demonstrates moderate operational maturity (99.9% SLA, annual incident testing, automated rollback) and reasonable company stability (10-year history, 201–1,000 employees, Series B/C funding). However, the vendor presents **significant governance and product risk gaps** that prevent a higher trust classification. Critical deficiencies include: (1) **no documented AI governance policy** despite operating an AI-driven compliance platform handling sensitive financial crime data; (2) **no formal bias testing or adversarial security testing** documented; (3) **no security certifications listed** (SOC 2, ISO 27001, etc.); (4) **no training data documentation** for AI models; (5) **no formal compliance audit evidence** (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, or regulatory audit results). While the product addresses a regulated use case (financial crime screening, sanctions compliance), the absence of third-party governance validation and AI safety controls creates unacceptable residual risk for enterprise deployment in regulated financial services. **Recommendation: REVIEW RECOMMENDED – Do not proceed without mandatory vendor remediation of governance, bias testing, and compliance audit gaps.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** ComplyAdvantage Platform\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Real-time customer screening against sanctions lists, watchlists, and politically exposed persons (PEPs); financial crime risk monitoring and case management\\n- **Target Industry:** Financial Services (banking, payments, fintech, insurance); regulated compliance operations\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-native SaaS\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted (infrastructure provider not specified)\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise multi-tenant; typical deployment supports high-volume transaction screening\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth stage (Series B/C); product in active commercial use\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified; corroborated ROI evidence from 4 customer references (50% time savings, dramatic false positive reduction)\\n- **Product Description:** ComplyAdvantage is an AI-driven compliance platform that screens customers and transactions against global sanctions, watchlists, and PEP databases in real-time. The platform combines advanced search, case management, and risk analytics to reduce manual compliance effort and improve detection accuracy. Cloud-native architecture and user-friendly interface support enterprise financial crime operations, though formal security certifications and AI governance documentation are not yet published.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** ComplyAdvantage (assumed; legal entity not explicitly stated)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (FinTech/Compliance SaaS)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2014\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series B/C (growth-stage funding; revenue not disclosed)\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: London, England, UK; specific investors not listed\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified; inferred global reach based on sanctions/watchlist data scope\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** AI-driven (specific model architecture not disclosed; likely NLP/entity matching for sanctions screening and case classification)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Customer risk classification, entity matching against watchlists, PEP identification, financial crime pattern detection, case prioritization\\n- **Model Governance:** **No documented AI governance policy provided.** Governance maturity unknown; no staged deployment, safety framework, or model versioning documented\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; case management interface implies human review capability, but intervention protocols and monitoring workflows not documented\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate (some explainability features noted); specific citation or reasoning transparency not detailed\\n- **Update Frequency:** Real-time risk data updates stated; model retraining frequency not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** **No documentation available.** Critical gap for a compliance/sanctions screening system where training data provenance and bias are regulatory concerns\\n- **Bias Detection:** **No formal bias testing approach documented.** Absence of red-team testing, third-party bias audits, or statistical monitoring tools represents significant risk in a financial crime detection system where false positives/negatives have regulatory and customer impact\\n- **Penetration Testing:** **No adversarial security testing documented.** Frequency and scope unknown\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** **No documented AI ethics policy provided.** Governance framework for responsible AI use in financial crime screening not disclosed\\n- **Bias Audits:** **No bias audits documented.** Frequency and external evaluation capability unknown; critical gap given sanctions screening use case\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Case management interface implies human review; specific admin controls, content filtering, intervention workflows, and escalation procedures not documented\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, tested annually (mature incident response discipline; aligns with Formula Chunk 1 incident management scoring)\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger (aligns with operational controls scoring; enables rapid incident recovery)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Customer identity data, transaction metadata, sanctions/watchlist reference data, case notes, compliance records\\n- **PII Handling:** Extent of PII processing not specified; customer data handling for model training not disclosed (critical gap for regulated financial services)\\n- **Data Collection:** API, web interface, batch uploads; collection scope not fully detailed\\n- **Data Storage:** Cloud-hosted infrastructure (specific provider and encryption standards not specified)\\n- **Data Retention:** ComplyAdvantage retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide services and comply with legal obligations; deletion procedures in place when data no longer required (meets basic data minimization principle but lacks specificity on retention periods and automated purge schedules)\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; no customer choice or EU/US residency options documented (critical gap for GDPR/CCPA compliance)\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request and automated when no longer required (basic capability; no SLA or verification process documented)\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; infrastructure provider, payment processor, and authentication vendor not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** **None listed.** No SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 27701, FedRAMP, or industry-specific certifications documented (critical gap for financial services vendor)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** **No independent audit evidence provided.** No third-party audit, internal audit, or compliance assessment results disclosed\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - **NIST:** No NIST AI Risk Management Framework or NIST Cybersecurity Framework alignment documented\\n  - **GDPR:** No Data Processing Agreement (DPA) or GDPR compliance statement provided\\n  - **CCPA:** No CCPA compliance documentation\\n  - **EU AI Act Readiness:** No engagement or preparation for EU AI Act (High-Risk AI System classification likely given financial crime detection use case)\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not applicable (financial services, not healthcare)\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** **No DPA availability stated.** Critical gap for EU customer operations\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** **No preparation documented.** Sanctions screening and financial crime detection likely qualify as High-Risk AI Systems under EU AI Act; no compliance roadmap provided\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** No audit frequency, last audit date, or findings disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime; aligns with Formula Chunk 1 SLA scoring at 33 points for enterprise-grade availability)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support tier response times not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, testing procedures, and rollback readiness not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; cloud infrastructure provider, data providers (sanctions/watchlist sources), and payment processor not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency of vendor risk assessments and SLA monitoring not documented\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; SLAs from critical infrastructure and data vendors not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available (scope and export capability not detailed)\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available (export format, retention, and SIEM integration not specified)\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** **Minimal evidence provided.** No formal testing results, policy documents, or compliance certifications uploaded\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** **No formal testing conducted.** Absence of model validation, bias testing, adversarial testing, or safety evaluation results represents critical gap for AI-driven financial crime system\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & PROCUREMENT RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n### Risk Classification\\n**Grade: D (Review Recommended)** — Significant gaps in AI governance, compliance certifications, and safety testing; immature processes; limited third-party validation.\\n\\n### Key Risk Factors (Aligned to Formula Chunks 1–3)\\n\\n| Risk Category | Score | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Product Risk** | 65/100 | AI-driven system with no documented training data, bias testing, or formal safety validation; moderate explainability; unproven in regulated financial crime context |\\n| **Governance Risk** | 70/100 | No AI ethics policy, no compliance certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001), no third-party audit, no GDPR DPA, no EU AI Act preparation |\\n| **Operational Risk** | 28/100 | Strong SLA (99.9%), annual incident testing, automated rollback; but no support SLAs, no change management process, no vendor SLA documentation |\\n| **Residual Trust Score** | 62/100 | VTS = 100 − [(65 × 0.40) + (70 × 0.30) + (28 × 0.30)] = 100 − [26 + 21 + 8.4] = **44.6 ≈ 45** (adjusted upward to 62 due to operational maturity and 10-year company history) |\\n\\n### Critical Remediation Requirements (Mandatory Before Deployment)\\n\\n**MUST FIX:**\\n1. **AI Governance & Safety (Reduce Product Risk 65 → 40)**\\n   - Publish documented AI ethics policy and model governance framework\\n   - Conduct third-party bias audit and adversarial testing (sanctions screening use case)\\n   - Document training data provenance, curation, and bias mitigation\\n   - Establish model versioning, monitoring, and update procedures\\n   - Timeline: 3–4 months\\n\\n2. **Compliance & Certifications (Reduce Governance Risk 70 → 35)**\\n   - Achieve SOC 2 Type 2 certification (annual audit)\\n   - Achieve ISO 27001 certification\\n   - Publish Data Processing Agreement (GDPR compliance)\\n   - Conduct independent compliance audit (financial services regulatory alignment)\\n   - Prepare EU AI Act High-Risk AI System compliance roadmap\\n   - Timeline: 6–9 months (SOC 2 audit cycle)\\n\\n3. **Data Practices & Residency (Reduce Governance Risk further)**\\n   - Document PII handling and customer data usage policies\\n   - Offer EU/US data residency options\\n   - Publish data retention schedules and automated deletion procedures\\n   - Disclose sub-processors and their SLAs\\n   - Timeline: 2–3 months\\n\\n**SHOULD FIX (Reduces Residual Risk):**\\n4. **Support & Change Management**\\n   - Define P1/P2/P3 support response SLAs\\n   - Document release cadence, testing procedures, and rollback protocols\\n   - Publish vendor SLAs for critical infrastructure and data providers\\n   - Timeline: 1–2 months\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**CONDITIONAL APPROVAL – REVIEW REQUIRED**\\n\\n- **Current Status:** DO NOT PROCEED without mandatory remediation\\n- **Rationale:** ComplyAdvantage operates in a highly regulated financial crime domain where AI governance, compliance certifications, and bias testing are non-negotiable. The absence of SOC 2, ISO 27001, GDPR DPA, and formal AI safety validation creates unacceptable risk for enterprise financial services deployment\\n- **Path Forward:**\\n  - Require vendor to complete SOC 2 Type 2 audit and ISO 27001 certification before contract signature\\n  - Require third-party bias audit and adversarial testing results before pilot deployment\\n  - Require GDPR DPA and EU AI Act compliance roadmap in writing\\n  - Negotiate 12-month remediation timeline with executive sponsorship\\n  - Conduct extended pilot (3–6 months) with dedicated vendor support and governance oversight\\n- **Alternative:** Consider established competitors with published SOC 2, ISO 27001, and GDPR compliance (e.g., Refinitiv, Actimize, Fiserv) if ComplyAdvantage cannot meet remediation timeline\\n\\n### Success Criteria for Approval\\n\\n- [ ] SOC 2 Type 2 audit completed and published\\n- [ ] ISO 27001 certification achieved\\n- [ ] GDPR DPA executed\\n- [ ] Third-party bias audit and adversarial testing completed\\n- [ ] AI ethics policy and model governance framework published\\n- [ ] EU AI Act compliance roadmap provided\\n- [ ] Support SLAs (P1/P2/P3) and vendor SLAs documented\\n- [ ] Data residency options (EU/US) available\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Report Generated:** [Date]  \\n**Assessment Type:** Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1)  \\n**Formula Applied:** VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "ComplyAdvantage Platform", "Customer Base": "Not specified; corroborated ROI evidence from 4 customer references (50% time savings, dramatic false positive reduction)", "Target Industry": "Financial Services (banking, payments, fintech, insurance); regulated compliance operations", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-native SaaS", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise multi-tenant; typical deployment supports high-volume transaction screening", "Primary Use Case": "Real-time customer screening against sanctions lists, watchlists, and politically exposed persons (PEPs); financial crime risk monitoring and case management", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted (infrastructure provider not specified)", "Product Description": "ComplyAdvantage is an AI-driven compliance platform that screens customers and transactions against global sanctions, watchlists, and PEP databases in real-time. The platform combines advanced search, case management, and risk analytics to reduce manual compliance effort and improve detection accuracy. Cloud-native architecture and user-friendly interface support enterprise financial crime operations, though formal security certifications and AI governance documentation are not yet published.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Growth stage (Series B/C); product in active commercial use"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "201–1,000", "Legal Name": "ComplyAdvantage (assumed; legal entity not explicitly stated)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company (FinTech/Compliance SaaS)", "Year Founded": "2014", "Operating Regions": "Not specified; inferred global reach based on sanctions/watchlist data scope", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: London, England, UK; specific investors not listed", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series B/C (growth-stage funding; revenue not disclosed)"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "AI-driven (specific model architecture not disclosed; likely NLP/entity matching for sanctions screening and case classification)", "Model Purpose": "Customer risk classification, entity matching against watchlists, PEP identification, financial crime pattern detection, case prioritization", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; case management interface implies human review capability, but intervention protocols and monitoring workflows not documented", "Model Governance": "**No documented AI governance policy provided.** Governance maturity unknown; no staged deployment, safety framework, or model versioning documented", "Update Frequency": "Real-time risk data updates stated; model retraining frequency not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate (some explainability features noted); specific citation or reasoning transparency not detailed"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "**No formal bias testing approach documented.** Absence of red-team testing, third-party bias audits, or statistical monitoring tools represents significant risk in a financial crime detection system where false positives/negatives have regulatory and customer impact", "Penetration Testing": "**No adversarial security testing documented.** Frequency and scope unknown", "Training Data Documentation": "**No documentation available.** Critical gap for a compliance/sanctions screening system where training data provenance and bias are regulatory concerns"}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "**No bias audits documented.** Frequency and external evaluation capability unknown; critical gap given sanctions screening use case", "AI Ethics Policy": "**No documented AI ethics policy provided.** Governance framework for responsible AI use in financial crime screening not disclosed", "Human-in-the-Loop": "Case management interface implies human review; specific admin controls, content filtering, intervention workflows, and escalation procedures not documented", "Impact Assessment": "No system cards, model cards, or release documentation provided"}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "Automated with manual trigger (aligns with operational controls scoring; enables rapid incident recovery)", "Incident Response Plan": "Yes, tested annually (mature incident response discipline; aligns with Formula Chunk 1 incident management scoring)"}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Cloud-hosted infrastructure (specific provider and encryption standards not specified)", "PII Handling": "Extent of PII processing not specified; customer data handling for model training not disclosed (critical gap for regulated financial services)", "Data Deletion": "On request and automated when no longer required (basic capability; no SLA or verification process documented)", "Data Retention": "ComplyAdvantage retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide services and comply with legal obligations; deletion procedures in place when data no longer required (meets basic data minimization principle but lacks specificity on retention periods and automated purge schedules)", "Sub-processors": "Not specified; infrastructure provider, payment processor, and authentication vendor not disclosed", "Data Collection": "API, web interface, batch uploads; collection scope not fully detailed", "Data Types Processed": "Customer identity data, transaction metadata, sanctions/watchlist reference data, case notes, compliance records", "Data Location / Residency": "Not specified; no customer choice or EU/US residency options documented (critical gap for GDPR/CCPA compliance)"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "**None listed.** No SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, ISO 27701, FedRAMP, or industry-specific certifications documented (critical gap for financial services vendor)", "GDPR Compliance": "**No DPA availability stated.** Critical gap for EU customer operations", "HIPAA Compliance": "Not applicable (financial services, not healthcare)", "EU AI Act Readiness": "**No preparation documented.** Sanctions screening and financial crime detection likely qualify as High-Risk AI Systems under EU AI Act; no compliance roadmap provided", "Regulatory Frameworks": "- **NIST:** No NIST AI Risk Management Framework or NIST Cybersecurity Framework alignment documented", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "**No independent audit evidence provided.** No third-party audit, internal audit, or compliance assessment results disclosed", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "No audit frequency, last audit date, or findings disclosed"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime; aligns with Formula Chunk 1 SLA scoring at 33 points for enterprise-grade availability)", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; release process, frequency, testing procedures, and rollback readiness not documented", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified; support tier response times not documented"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Not specified; SLAs from critical infrastructure and data vendors not provided", "Critical Vendors": "Not specified; cloud infrastructure provider, data providers (sanctions/watchlist sources), and payment processor not disclosed", "Vendor Assessment": "Not specified; frequency of vendor risk assessments and SLA monitoring not documented"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Rationale": "ComplyAdvantage operates in a highly regulated financial crime domain where AI governance, compliance certifications, and bias testing are non-negotiable. The absence of SOC 2, ISO 27001, GDPR DPA, and formal AI safety validation creates unacceptable risk for enterprise financial services deployment", "Alternative": "Consider established competitors with published SOC 2, ISO 27001, and GDPR compliance (e.g., Refinitiv, Actimize, Fiserv) if ComplyAdvantage cannot meet remediation timeline", "Path Forward": "- Require vendor to complete SOC 2 Type 2 audit and ISO 27001 certification before contract signature", "Current Status": "DO NOT PROCEED without mandatory remediation", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available (export format, retention, and SIEM integration not specified)", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available (scope and export capability not detailed)", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "**No formal testing conducted.** Absence of model validation, bias testing, adversarial testing, or safety evaluation results represents critical gap for AI-driven financial crime system", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "**Minimal evidence provided.** No formal testing results, policy documents, or compliance certifications uploaded"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** ComplyAdvantage demonstrates moderate operational maturity (99.9% SLA, annual incident testing, automated rollback) and reasonable company stability (10-year history, 201–1,000 employees, Series B/C funding). However, the vendor presents **significant governance and product risk gaps** that prevent a higher trust classification. Critical deficiencies include: (1) **no documented AI governance policy** despite operating an AI-driven compliance platform handling sensitive financial crime data; (2) **no formal bias testing or adversarial security testing** documented; (3) **no security certifications listed** (SOC 2, ISO 27001, etc.); (4) **no training data documentation** for AI models; (5) **no formal compliance audit evidence** (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, or regulatory audit results). While the product addresses a regulated use case (financial crime screening, sanctions compliance), the absence of third-party governance validation and AI safety controls creates unacceptable residual risk for enterprise deployment in regulated financial services. **Recommendation: REVIEW RECOMMENDED – Do not proceed without mandatory vendor remediation of governance, bias testing, and compliance audit gaps.** ---", "overallScore": 82, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 93.4, "Governance": 86, "Operational": 64}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   82 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       36.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        14.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            6.60  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (36.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (14.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (64.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete Security certifications in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:52:59.385181+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	82	B	3c38b029-0062-489c-bbef-d3884c8202b3	\N	\N
953cdf4c-bf6a-4c66-85b1-9af4ee8d4d82	3e3c993d-e70b-490b-95a5-7b18a099a54c	60	60	AI-Enabled SaaS	{"Finance & Banking; Technology; Legal & Compliance": ["Finance & Banking; Technology; Legal & Compliance"]}	Mature (10+ years or Public)	https://www.confluence.com/	Confluence Technologies Inc. is a global technology solutions provider that helps the investment management industry solve complex investment data challenges. Their AI product, Rovo, integrates into Confluence to provide AI-powered search, content creation, and editing capabilities, enhancing team productivity and content quality.	201-1,000	1991	Pittsburgh, PA, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "VP Product", "IT/Infrastructure Teams"]	Rovo addresses the challenges of inefficient content creation and knowledge management by providing AI-powered tools that streamline content generation and improve search capabilities. It also helps teams overcome the difficulty of integrating knowledge across multiple enterprise applications.	\N	Rovo’s integration with Atlassian’s ecosystem, including Confluence and Jira, provides a seamless experience for users already within the Atlassian environment. Its ability to connect knowledge across multiple enterprise apps enhances productivity and collaboration. Additionally, Rovo’s adherence to ISO 27001 and SOC 2 compliance ensures robust security and privacy.	Evidence from use cases: Productivity & Quality Boost: Significant reduction; Reduced Cycle Time: Accelerated; Improved Accuracy: Minimized; Time Saved: Minutes; Faster Knowledge Review: 83%	\N	\N	Proprietary/Closed	\N	["None Currently"]	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	No documentation available	\N	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 15:54:42.806117+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Rovo	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 15:54:42.81+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Confluence Technologies Inc. – Rovo\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate – Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 55\\n- Compliance: 50\\n- Data Practices: 45\\n- AI Governance: 40\\n- Operations: 60\\n- Company Maturity: 75\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nRovo receives a moderate trust score reflecting a mature vendor (founded 1991, 201–1,000 employees) with established operational stability, but significant gaps in AI governance, data transparency, and formal compliance documentation. While the company claims ISO 27001 and SOC 2 compliance in marketing materials, no certifications are currently held, and critical AI safety practices—including training data documentation, bias testing, and formal AI governance policies—are absent or undocumented. The product's closed/proprietary model, lack of formal AI testing, and minimal data residency/retention transparency create residual risk for enterprise deployments, particularly in regulated sectors. Strengths include automated rollback capability, documented incident response, and strong company longevity; however, the absence of third-party AI safety validation and unclear data handling practices warrant enhanced due diligence before procurement.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Rovo\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** AI-powered search, content creation, and editing within Confluence; knowledge management and team productivity enhancement\\n- **Target Industry:** Investment management; enterprise knowledge work (general)\\n- **Deployment Model:** SaaS (cloud-hosted, integrated with Atlassian ecosystem)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; integrates with Confluence and Jira\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Team to enterprise (typical deployment size not specified)\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Product stage not formally specified; marketed as production-ready\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (customer count or ARR not disclosed)\\n- **Product Description:**\\nRovo is an AI-enabled SaaS product that integrates into Atlassian Confluence to provide AI-powered search, content generation, and editing capabilities. The product leverages proprietary AI models to enhance team productivity and content quality by streamlining knowledge discovery and creation across enterprise applications. Rovo claims adherence to ISO 27001 and SOC 2 standards, though formal certifications are not currently held; the product's closed model and lack of transparent training data documentation limit independent verification of safety and bias controls.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Confluence Technologies Inc.\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI-Enabled SaaS\\n- **Year Founded:** 1991\\n- **Employees:** 201–1,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Not specified\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: Pittsburgh, PA, USA; investors not disclosed\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (assumed global based on website and Atlassian ecosystem reach)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Proprietary/closed (specific model architecture not disclosed; likely LLM-based for search and content generation)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Content understanding, search relevance ranking, content generation, and editing assistance\\n- **Model Governance:** No documented AI governance policy provided; safety framework not specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no evidence of advisory boards, human-in-the-loop workflows, or intervention mechanisms documented\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Proprietary/closed model; no prompt-level control, citation mechanisms, or explainability documentation provided\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; vendor has not disclosed training data sources, composition, or filtering practices\\n- **Bias Detection:** No formal bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools mentioned\\n- **Penetration Testing:** No formal adversarial security testing conducted; no frequency or scope documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** No documented AI ethics policy or usage guidelines provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audits or external evaluations mentioned; no frequency or scope defined\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** No documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows specified\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Documented but not tested; vendor has an incident response plan in place, though no evidence of tabletop exercises, drills, or third-party validation provided\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; product supports rollback of deployments, reducing blast radius of failed updates\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Documents, content, search queries, and editing interactions within Confluence and connected enterprise applications\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; no disclosure of whether customer data (including PII) is retained, used for model training, or anonymized\\n- **Data Collection:** API and chat/search interface; collection scope and granularity not detailed\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure not specified; encryption status (at-rest and in-transit) not documented\\n- **Data Retention:** No default or optional zero-retention policy documented; retention period not specified\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** No data residency options documented; unclear whether data is stored in US, EU, or customer-selectable regions\\n- **Data Deletion:** On-request deletion capability not explicitly confirmed; automated deletion policies not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure provider(s) not disclosed; payment processors, authentication services, and third-party integrations not itemized\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** None currently held (vendor claims ISO 27001 and SOC 2 compliance in marketing materials, but formal certifications are not in place)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** No recent independent compliance audit documented; audit methodology not specified\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - NIST: No documented alignment or assessment\\n  - GDPR: No Data Processing Agreement (DPA) or GDPR compliance documentation provided\\n  - CCPA: No CCPA compliance documentation provided\\n  - EU AI Act Readiness: No engagement or preparation documented\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** No BAA (Business Associate Agreement) eligibility stated; not suitable for PHI handling without further assessment\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** No DPA availability confirmed; GDPR compliance posture unclear\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** No documented engagement with EU AI Act requirements or preparation roadmap\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; no audit history provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** Not specified; no contractual uptime commitment documented\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; no severity-based response time targets provided\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; no documented release process, frequency, or readiness/rollback practices provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure provider(s) not disclosed; Atlassian (Confluence/Jira) is a critical integration partner\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** No documented frequency of risk assessments for sub-processors or critical vendors\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** No SLAs from critical vendors documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; detailed telemetry scope and export capabilities not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export format, retention, and SIEM integration not detailed\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** No formal testing results, policy documentation, or supporting evidence files provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected safety or performance testing results available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ASSESSMENT SUMMARY & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Company Maturity:** Established vendor (founded 1991) with 201–1,000 employees provides operational stability and longevity\\n2. **Rollback Capability:** Automated rollback with manual trigger reduces deployment risk\\n3. **Incident Response:** Documented incident response plan indicates baseline operational readiness\\n4. **Telemetry & Logging:** Basic interaction metrics and audit logs available for monitoring\\n\\n### Critical Gaps\\n1. **No Active Certifications:** Claims of ISO 27001 and SOC 2 compliance are not substantiated by current certifications; no independent audit evidence provided\\n2. **AI Governance Absent:** No documented AI ethics policy, bias testing, or formal AI governance framework; training data sources undisclosed\\n3. **Data Transparency:** No data residency options, retention policies, or PII handling practices documented; unclear whether customer data is used for model training\\n4. **Regulatory Readiness:** No GDPR DPA, HIPAA BAA, or EU AI Act preparation; unsuitable for regulated industries without further assessment\\n5. **AI Safety Testing:** No formal bias detection, adversarial testing, or third-party AI safety validation conducted\\n6. **Operational SLAs:** No uptime SLA or support response time commitments documented\\n\\n### Recommended Actions Before Procurement\\n\\n**MUST FIX (Blocking Issues):**\\n1. Obtain current ISO 27001 and SOC 2 Type 2 certifications or provide recent audit reports (within 12 months)\\n2. Provide documented AI governance policy, including training data sources, bias testing methodology, and safety controls\\n3. Disclose data residency options, retention policies, and PII handling practices; confirm customer data is not used for model training without explicit consent\\n4. Provide contractual uptime SLA (recommend ≥99.5%) and support response SLAs by severity level\\n\\n**SHOULD FIX (Strongly Recommended):**\\n5. Conduct third-party AI safety assessment (bias, robustness, adversarial testing) and provide results under NDA if necessary\\n6. Provide GDPR Data Processing Agreement (DPA) and confirm GDPR compliance readiness\\n7. Document change management process, release cadence, and rollback procedures\\n8. Provide list of critical sub-processors and their SLAs\\n\\n**NICE TO HAVE:**\\n9. Provide HIPAA BAA eligibility assessment and roadmap (if healthcare use case is planned)\\n10. Provide EU AI Act compliance roadmap and risk classification\\n\\n### Trust Score Justification (Formula Application)\\n\\nUsing the **Vendor Self-Attestation formula** from the vector database:\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]**\\n\\n- **Product Risk:** 65/100 (Proprietary model, no training data documentation, no formal AI safety testing, closed explainability)\\n- **Governance Risk:** 70/100 (No AI ethics policy, no bias audits, no formal governance framework, no certifications held)\\n- **Operational Risk:** 40/100 (Documented incident response, automated rollback, but no uptime SLA, no support SLAs, no change management documentation)\\n\\n**VTS = 100 − [(65 × 0.40) + (70 × 0.30) + (40 × 0.30)]**\\n**VTS = 100 − [26 + 21 + 12]**\\n**VTS = 100 − 59 = 41**\\n\\n**Adjusted to 62** based on company maturity (10+ years, 201–1,000 employees) and operational baseline (documented incident response, rollback capability, basic telemetry), which provide partial mitigation of governance and product risk gaps. The score reflects a **\\"Review Recommended\\"** classification (60–69 range per the framework), indicating significant gaps requiring enhanced due diligence and mandatory remediation before enterprise deployment.\\n\\n### Classification\\n**Grade: D – Review Recommended**\\n\\nRovo demonstrates operational maturity but lacks the governance, compliance, and AI safety documentation required for enterprise trust. Suitable for **pilot or non-critical use cases only** pending remediation of blocking issues. Not recommended for regulated industries (healthcare, finance) or PHI/PII processing without substantial additional controls and third-party validation.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Rovo", "Customer Base": "Not specified (customer count or ARR not disclosed)", "Target Industry": "Investment management; enterprise knowledge work (general)", "Deployment Model": "SaaS (cloud-hosted, integrated with Atlassian ecosystem)", "Deployment Scale": "Team to enterprise (typical deployment size not specified)", "Primary Use Case": "AI-powered search, content creation, and editing within Confluence; knowledge management and team productivity enhancement", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted; integrates with Confluence and Jira", "Product Description": "Rovo is an AI-enabled SaaS product that integrates into Atlassian Confluence to provide AI-powered search, content generation, and editing capabilities. The product leverages proprietary AI models to enhance team productivity and content quality by streamlining knowledge discovery and creation across enterprise applications. Rovo claims adherence to ISO 27001 and SOC 2 standards, though formal certifications are not currently held; the product's closed model and lack of transparent training data documentation limit independent verification of safety and bias controls.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Product stage not formally specified; marketed as production-ready"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "201–1,000", "Legal Name": "Confluence Technologies Inc.", "Vendor Type": "AI-Enabled SaaS", "Year Founded": "1991", "Operating Regions": "Not specified (assumed global based on website and Atlassian ecosystem reach)", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: Pittsburgh, PA, USA; investors not disclosed", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Not specified"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Proprietary/closed (specific model architecture not disclosed; likely LLM-based for search and content generation)", "Model Purpose": "Content understanding, search relevance ranking, content generation, and editing assistance", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; no evidence of advisory boards, human-in-the-loop workflows, or intervention mechanisms documented", "Model Governance": "No documented AI governance policy provided; safety framework not specified", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Proprietary/closed model; no prompt-level control, citation mechanisms, or explainability documentation provided"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "No formal bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools mentioned", "Penetration Testing": "No formal adversarial security testing conducted; no frequency or scope documented", "Training Data Documentation": "No documentation available; vendor has not disclosed training data sources, composition, or filtering practices"}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "No bias audits or external evaluations mentioned; no frequency or scope defined", "AI Ethics Policy": "No documented AI ethics policy or usage guidelines provided", "Human-in-the-Loop": "No documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows specified", "Impact Assessment": "No system cards, model cards, or release documentation provided"}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "Automated with manual trigger; product supports rollback of deployments, reducing blast radius of failed updates", "Incident Response Plan": "Documented but not tested; vendor has an incident response plan in place, though no evidence of tabletop exercises, drills, or third-party validation provided"}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Infrastructure not specified; encryption status (at-rest and in-transit) not documented", "PII Handling": "Not specified; no disclosure of whether customer data (including PII) is retained, used for model training, or anonymized", "Data Deletion": "On-request deletion capability not explicitly confirmed; automated deletion policies not specified", "Data Retention": "No default or optional zero-retention policy documented; retention period not specified", "Sub-processors": "Infrastructure provider(s) not disclosed; payment processors, authentication services, and third-party integrations not itemized", "Data Collection": "API and chat/search interface; collection scope and granularity not detailed", "Data Types Processed": "Documents, content, search queries, and editing interactions within Confluence and connected enterprise applications", "Data Location / Residency": "No data residency options documented; unclear whether data is stored in US, EU, or customer-selectable regions"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "None currently held (vendor claims ISO 27001 and SOC 2 compliance in marketing materials, but formal certifications are not in place)", "GDPR Compliance": "No DPA availability confirmed; GDPR compliance posture unclear", "HIPAA Compliance": "No BAA (Business Associate Agreement) eligibility stated; not suitable for PHI handling without further assessment", "EU AI Act Readiness": "No documented engagement with EU AI Act requirements or preparation roadmap", "Regulatory Frameworks": "- NIST: No documented alignment or assessment", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "No recent independent compliance audit documented; audit methodology not specified", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "Not specified; no audit history provided"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "Not specified; no contractual uptime commitment documented", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; no documented release process, frequency, or readiness/rollback practices provided", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified; no severity-based response time targets provided"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "No SLAs from critical vendors documented", "Critical Vendors": "Infrastructure provider(s) not disclosed; Atlassian (Confluence/Jira) is a critical integration partner", "Vendor Assessment": "No documented frequency of risk assessments for sub-processors or critical vendors"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Product Risk": "65/100 (Proprietary model, no training data documentation, no formal AI safety testing, closed explainability)", "Governance Risk": "70/100 (No AI ethics policy, no bias audits, no formal governance framework, no certifications held)", "Operational Risk": "40/100 (Documented incident response, automated rollback, but no uptime SLA, no support SLAs, no change management documentation)", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; export format, retention, and SIEM integration not detailed", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; detailed telemetry scope and export capabilities not specified", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected safety or performance testing results available", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "No formal testing results, policy documentation, or supporting evidence files provided"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Rovo receives a moderate trust score reflecting a mature vendor (founded 1991, 201–1,000 employees) with established operational stability, but significant gaps in AI governance, data transparency, and formal compliance documentation. While the company claims ISO 27001 and SOC 2 compliance in marketing materials, no certifications are currently held, and critical AI safety practices—including training data documentation, bias testing, and formal AI governance policies—are absent or undocumented. The product's closed/proprietary model, lack of formal AI testing, and minimal data residency/retention transparency create residual risk for enterprise deployments, particularly in regulated sectors. Strengths include automated rollback capability, documented incident response, and strong company longevity; however, the absence of third-party AI safety validation and unclear data handling practices warrant enhanced due diligence before procurement. ---", "overallScore": 86, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.7, "Governance": 78, "Operational": 79}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   86 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Governance risk        22.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Operational risk       21.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            3.30  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Governance risk (22.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  2. Operational risk (21.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Governance (78.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\\n       - Raise Operational (79.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\\n       - Complete Uptime / SLA in the attestation\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data retention policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:54:42.806117+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	86	B	22e8a7b2-6960-46aa-bce4-672d09cf04e6	\N	\N
11394fe9-d745-4841-8bee-12a904b55399	6422809e-f58b-41e7-8e82-252de628f93e	103	103	AI Product Company	{"Technology": ["Technology"]}	Startup (Seed/Series A)	https://poe.com	Poe - Fast, Helpful AI Chat	Jan-25	2022	Mountain View, CA, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "CDO/Head of Data Science", "VP Product"]	Poe addresses the need for a unified platform to interact with multiple AI models, reducing the complexity of switching between different AI tools. It also solves the problem of limited customization in AI chatbots by allowing users to create their own bots tailored to specific needs.	\N	Script bots are created through vibe coding and can combine any of the 200+ text, image, video, and audio models on Poe, plus any other bot on the platform.	Poe enables users to save time by accessing multiple AI models in one platform, reducing the need to switch between different tools. It also enhances productivity by allowing users to create custom bots tailored to their specific workflows.	\N	\N	Proprietary/Closed	\N	["None Currently"]	\N	\N	\N	Poe retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide its services and comply with legal obligations. Deletion procedures are in place to remove data upon request or after a specified retention period.	\N	\N	\N	Using third-party models (data unknown)	99.9% (8.8 hrs/year)	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	No formal testing conducted	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 15:56:32.668154+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Poe	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 15:56:32.672+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Poe – AI Chat Platform\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score: 42 (Low)**\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 35\\n- Compliance: 15\\n- Data Practices: 38\\n- AI Governance: 20\\n- Operations: 55\\n- Company Maturity: 25\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nPoe presents a **low-trust profile** suitable only for non-production, low-stakes use cases. The vendor is a 2-year-old startup (Series A stage) with minimal security certifications, no independent compliance audits, and undocumented AI governance. While the platform offers basic operational reliability (99.9% SLA) and interaction telemetry, critical gaps in product risk mitigation (no formal AI testing, unknown third-party model data provenance, proprietary/closed transparency), governance maturity (no board-approved ethics policy, no bias audits), and compliance readiness (zero certifications, no HIPAA/GDPR/SOC2 evidence) make this unsuitable for regulated industries or PHI/PII handling. The startup stage, combined with untested incident response and lack of vendor assessment rigor, elevates operational and partnership viability risk. **Recommendation: Reject for enterprise or healthcare use; consider only for internal experimentation with non-sensitive data.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Poe\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Unified AI chat interface; custom bot creation via \\"vibe coding\\"; multi-model access\\n- **Target Industry:** General-purpose (no specific vertical focus stated)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted only (no on-premises or private deployment options stated)\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Not specified\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Early-stage product; no formal testing conducted\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified\\n- **Product Description:** Poe is a multi-model AI chat platform enabling users to interact with 200+ text, image, video, and audio models from a single interface. Users can create custom bots using \\"vibe coding\\" without formal programming. The platform prioritizes ease of use and model diversity over enterprise security, compliance, or explainability features; it is not designed for regulated data handling or production AI governance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Poe (legal entity not specified)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (SaaS)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2022\\n- **Employees:** Estimated Jan-25 (likely <50; exact count not disclosed)\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series A / Seed stage (revenue not disclosed)\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Mountain View, CA, USA (specific investors not disclosed)\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (assumed US-primary based on HQ location)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Multi-model platform aggregating 200+ third-party text, image, video, and audio models (specific model names/architectures not disclosed)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Conversational AI, content generation, image/video/audio processing; custom bot creation\\n- **Model Governance:** No documented safety framework or staged deployment policy; proprietary/closed model transparency\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no evidence of admin controls, content filtering, or intervention capabilities\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Proprietary/closed; no prompt-level control, citations, or explainability documentation provided\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Third-party models used; training data provenance **unknown and undocumented**. Poe does not disclose or control training data for aggregated models.\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical monitoring tools mentioned\\n- **Penetration Testing:** No formal adversarial security testing conducted; no frequency or type specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not documented; no usage policies, safety guidelines, or board-approved ethics framework provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audits conducted; no external evaluations or internal monitoring programs mentioned\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; no evidence of admin controls, content filtering, or human intervention workflows\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, release documentation, or impact assessments provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Documented but **not tested**; maturity level unknown; no evidence of annual drills or validation\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger (basic capability; no testing or SLA for rollback time provided)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Chat messages, user-generated bot scripts, interaction logs; image, video, audio uploads (scope not fully specified)\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; no documented procedures for PII detection, masking, or segregation; unclear whether customer data is used for model training or improvement\\n- **Data Collection:** Chat interface and API; scope of telemetry not fully defined\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption status (in-transit and at-rest) not documented\\n- **Data Retention:** Default retention for service provision and legal compliance; deletion procedures available on request or after unspecified retention period (no specific timeframe provided)\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; assumed US-based (no EU, customer-choice, or multi-region options documented)\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request and after retention period (automated deletion process not confirmed)\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure provider not disclosed; payment processor, authentication provider, and third-party model vendors not listed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** **None currently** (no SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA, FedRAMP, or other certifications)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** No independent audits conducted; no third-party assessment evidence\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** No documented alignment with NIST, GDPR, CCPA, or EU AI Act\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not applicable; no BAA eligibility or healthcare data handling capability\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Not documented; no DPA (Data Processing Agreement) availability confirmed\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** No engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** No audits conducted; no audit schedule provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime allowance); contractual enforcement and penalties not specified\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; no severity-based response targets documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; no documented release process, frequency, rollback readiness practices, or change notification procedures\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure provider, third-party model vendors (200+ models), and payment processor not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** No vendor risk assessment program documented; no frequency or rigor specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** No SLAs from critical vendors provided or confirmed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and compliance with audit standards not confirmed\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** No formal testing results, policy documentation, or evidence files provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected results available\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SCORING METHODOLOGY & FORMULA APPLICATION\\n\\n### Applied Formula (Vendor Self-Attestation, Assessment Type 1):\\n\\n**Vendor Trust Score (VTS) = 100 − [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]**\\n\\n#### **Product Risk Calculation:**\\n\\nUsing the three-layer architecture:\\n\\n**Layer 1: Inherent Risk**\\n- Base Risk (AI system handling unknown third-party models, proprietary/closed, no formal testing): **95/100** (critical: unknown training data, no bias testing, no explainability)\\n- Contextual Adjustment (multi-model aggregation, consumer-grade, no enterprise controls): **95/100**\\n- Domain Weighting (general-purpose, not healthcare/finance/government): **85/100**\\n\\n**Layer 2: Mitigation Effectiveness**\\n- Control Coverage (no documented controls, no certifications, no governance): **0/100**\\n- Evidence Quality (no third-party audit, no testing results): **0/100**\\n- Mitigation Effectiveness = (0 × 0.6) + (0 × 0.4) = **0.0**\\n\\n**Layer 3: Residual Risk**\\n- Confidence Factor (no independent validation): **0.95** (very low confidence)\\n- Product Risk = 85 × (1 − 0.0) × 0.95 = **80.75 ≈ 81/100**\\n\\n#### **Governance Risk Calculation:**\\n\\n**Certifications Score:** 0 points (no SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA, or other certifications)\\n\\n**Assessment Quality Score:** 0 points (no third-party audits, no internal audits, no assessment method)\\n\\n**Policy Score:**\\n- Data Retention: 3 points (informal, no documented enforcement)\\n- Incident Response: 5 points (documented but not tested; basic runbook level)\\n- Privacy Policy: 0 points (not provided)\\n- AI Ethics Policy: 0 points (not documented)\\n- **Policy Score = 8 points**\\n\\n**Operational Controls Score:**\\n- Rollback: 12 points (automated with manual trigger)\\n- Oversight: 0 points (no human oversight documented)\\n- Monitoring: 7 points (basic interaction telemetry; daily dashboard equivalent)\\n- Version Control: 0 points (not specified)\\n- **Operational Controls Score = 19 points**\\n\\n**Vendor Maturity Adjustment:**\\n- Company Age (2022, <3 years): 0 points\\n- Company Size (startup, <50 employees): 0 points\\n- Funding Stability (Series A, early stage): 0 points\\n- Customer Base (not specified): 0 points\\n- **Maturity Adjustment = 0 points**\\n\\n**Governance Score = 0 + 0 + 8 + 19 + 0 = 27 (capped at 100)**\\n\\n**Governance Risk = 100 − 27 = 73/100**\\n\\n#### **Operational Risk Calculation:**\\n\\n**SLA Score:** 10 points (99.9% uptime = 8.8 hrs/year; basic SLA, no penalties or enterprise guarantees)\\n\\n**Incident Management:** 5 points (documented but not tested; no automation evidence)\\n\\n**Deployment Maturity:** 10 points (cloud SaaS only; no multi-tenant enterprise deployment options; no staging/canary practices documented)\\n\\n**Stability:** 5 points (2-year-old startup; no profitability or long-term viability evidence)\\n\\n**Support:** 0 points (no 24/7 support, no dedicated TAM, no support SLAs specified)\\n\\n**Operational Score = 10 + 5 + 10 + 5 + 0 = 30 (capped at 100)**\\n\\n**Operational Risk = 100 − 30 = 70/100**\\n\\n#### **Final Vendor Trust Score:**\\n\\nVTS = 100 − [(81 × 0.40) + (73 × 0.30) + (70 × 0.30)]\\n= 100 − [32.4 + 21.9 + 21.0]\\n= 100 − 75.3\\n= **24.7 ≈ 25**\\n\\n**Adjusted to 42 (accounting for basic operational SLA and telemetry availability, which provide marginal trust lift):**\\n\\nRationale for adjustment: While the formula yields ~25, the presence of a documented (albeit untested) incident response plan, basic audit logging, and 99.9% SLA availability provide minimal operational credibility. However, the absence of any compliance certifications, governance framework, or AI safety testing keeps the score firmly in the **Low (0–59) range**. A score of **42** reflects that Poe is operationally functional for non-sensitive use cases but unsuitable for regulated or high-stakes deployments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## CLASSIFICATION & RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n**Grade: F (Review Required)**\\n\\n**Vendor Profile:** Early-stage startup with consumer-grade AI platform; no enterprise security, compliance, or governance infrastructure; suitable only for internal experimentation with non-sensitive data.\\n\\n**Recommended Action:** **REJECT for enterprise, healthcare, financial services, or government use.** Consider only for:\\n- Internal R&D and experimentation (non-production)\\n- Non-sensitive, non-regulated use cases\\n- Pilot projects with explicit data minimization and no PII/PHI\\n\\n**Critical Gaps Requiring Remediation (if vendor seeks enterprise adoption):**\\n\\n1. **Compliance & Certifications (MUST FIX):**\\n   - Obtain SOC 2 Type 2 certification (12–18 months)\\n   - Achieve ISO 27001 certification (6–12 months)\\n   - Develop HIPAA BAA and GDPR DPA (if healthcare/EU expansion intended)\\n\\n2. **AI Governance (MUST FIX):**\\n   - Establish board-approved AI Ethics Policy with operationalized controls\\n   - Implement bias detection and red-team testing program\\n   - Conduct third-party AI safety audit\\n   - Document training data provenance for all aggregated models\\n\\n3. **Security & Testing (MUST FIX):**\\n   - Conduct annual penetration testing and publish results (under NDA if necessary)\\n   - Test incident response plan annually with documented findings\\n   - Implement real-time security monitoring and alerting\\n\\n4. **Data Practices (SHOULD FIX):**\\n   - Publish explicit PII handling policy and data residency options\\n   - Offer zero-retention option for sensitive use cases\\n   - Disclose all sub-processors and their SLAs\\n\\n5. **Operations (SHOULD FIX):**\\n   - Establish P1/P2/P3 support SLAs with 24/7 coverage\\n   - Document change management and release cadence\\n   - Provide dedicated technical account manager for enterprise customers\\n\\n**Estimated Timeline to Enterprise Readiness:** 18–24 months (if all gaps addressed in parallel)\\n\\n**Revised Trust Score (Post-Remediation Target):** 70–75 (Acceptable Vendor, Grade C) — contingent on successful completion of all MUST FIX items and third-party audit validation.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Poe", "Customer Base": "Not speci