 Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "No SLAs from critical vendors provided or confirmed", "Critical Vendors": "Infrastructure provider, third-party model vendors (200+ models), and payment processor not disclosed", "Vendor Assessment": "No vendor risk assessment program documented; no frequency or rigor specified"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; export capability, retention, and compliance with audit standards not confirmed", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not specified", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected results available", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "No formal testing results, policy documentation, or evidence files provided"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "C", "label": "Acceptable Vendor", "summary": "** Poe presents a **low-trust profile** suitable only for non-production, low-stakes use cases. The vendor is a 2-year-old startup (Series A stage) with minimal security certifications, no independent compliance audits, and undocumented AI governance. While the platform offers basic operational reliability (99.9% SLA) and interaction telemetry, critical gaps in product risk mitigation (no formal AI testing, unknown third-party model data provenance, proprietary/closed transparency), governance maturity (no board-approved ethics policy, no bias audits), and compliance readiness (zero certifications, no HIPAA/GDPR/SOC2 evidence) make this unsuitable for regulated industries or PHI/PII handling. The startup stage, combined with untested incident response and lack of vendor assessment rigor, elevates operational and partnership viability risk. **Recommendation: Reject for enterprise or healthcare use; consider only for internal experimentation with non-sensitive data.** ---", "overallScore": 77, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 96.8, "Governance": 69, "Operational": 58}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   77 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         C - Acceptable Vendor\\n  Next step:     Enhanced due diligence; require mitigation roadmap\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       42.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        31.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            3.20  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (42.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (31.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (58.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Raise Governance (69.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:56:32.668154+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	77	C	5ec7061e-0aa9-4e76-9a46-f61a42a3d374	\N	\N
