ntion; compliance support\\n- **Target Industry:** Financial Services (banking, payments, insurance, fintech)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Hybrid (on-premise and cloud-hosted options available)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted (vendor-managed) and on-premise deployment supported\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise-scale; scalable across high-transaction-volume environments\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth-stage product with basic interaction telemetry; no formal third-party testing results available\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (customer count and ARR not disclosed)\\n- **Product Description:**\\nDataVisor Fraud Detection is an adaptive AI-driven platform designed to identify and prevent financial fraud in real-time using machine learning and AI agents. The platform reduces fraud-related losses (reported up to 60% reduction in fraudulent transactions) and automates detection workflows to minimize manual intervention. Deployed across on-premise and cloud environments, the solution addresses regulatory compliance requirements in financial services; however, transparency regarding training data, bias testing methodology, and data residency options remains limited.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** DataVisor (legal entity name not specified in attestation)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (SaaS/Platform)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2013\\n- **Employees:** 51–200\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Series B/C (growth-stage); revenue not disclosed\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: Mountain View, CA, USA; investors not specified\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: North America; global reach not confirmed)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Machine learning and adaptive AI agents; specific model architectures (e.g., neural networks, ensemble methods) not disclosed\\n- **Model Purpose:** Real-time fraud detection, pattern recognition, anomaly detection, financial crime prevention\\n- **Model Governance:** Documented AI governance policy: Not specified; no formal staged deployment or safety framework documented\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; level of advisory vs. autonomous decision-making not disclosed; monitoring and alert capabilities inferred from product description but not formally documented\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate explainability; some features present but limited transparency regarding decision rationale, feature importance, or audit trail granularity\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** Bias testing approach not specified; no evidence of red-team exercises, third-party audits, or statistical bias monitoring tools documented\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency and scope of penetration testing not disclosed\\n\\n**Assessment:** Significant gap. Absence of formal AI safety testing, training data transparency, and bias auditing creates material risk for financial crime detection, where false positives/negatives carry regulatory and reputational consequences.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Documented AI governance policy: Not specified; no formal ethics framework, usage guidelines, or safety policies disclosed\\n- **Bias Audits:** Bias testing approach not specified; no scheduled audits, external evaluations, or continuous monitoring for fairness metrics documented\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Human oversight capabilities not specified; no documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows provided\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model documentation, or impact assessments for releases documented\\n\\n**Assessment:** Critical gap. The absence of documented AI ethics policy, bias audit cadence, and human oversight mechanisms is inconsistent with financial services regulatory expectations (e.g., NIST AI RMF, EU AI Act high-risk classification for fraud detection).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; maturity level: basic (documented runbook without annual testing)\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback procedures are in place but require manual intervention\\n\\n**Assessment:** Moderate. Documented incident response and rollback capability provide foundational security controls, but lack of tested incident response procedures and manual rollback dependency introduce operational risk during incidents.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Transaction data, user behavior data, financial records, and associated metadata; specific data categories (e.g., PII, PHI) not detailed\\n- **PII Handling:** PII handling practices not specified; extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for model training not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** API and integration interfaces (inferred); specific collection mechanisms not documented\\n- **Data Storage:** Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption standards (in-transit and at-rest) not disclosed\\n- **Data Retention:** DataVisor retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide services and comply with legal obligations; specific retention periods not disclosed\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Data residency options not specified; geographic data storage location(s) not documented\\n- **Data Deletion:** On-request deletion capability inferred but not formally documented; automated deletion policies not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not identified\\n\\n**Assessment:** High risk. Undisclosed data retention periods, absence of data residency options, and lack of transparency regarding PII handling and sub-processor relationships create compliance and privacy concerns for regulated financial institutions. GDPR and CCPA compliance posture unclear.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** PCI DSS (Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard)\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit methodology and scope not documented\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - NIST AI RMF: Readiness not specified\\n  - GDPR: Compliance posture not documented; DPA availability not confirmed\\n  - CCPA: Compliance posture not documented\\n  - EU AI Act: Readiness not specified; fraud detection is classified as high-risk under EU AI Act, but vendor engagement/preparation not documented\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not applicable (financial services, not healthcare)\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** DPA availability not specified; GDPR compliance mechanisms not documented\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified; no evidence of vendor preparation for high-risk AI classification\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; audit cadence and results not disclosed\\n\\n**Assessment:** Moderate gap. PCI DSS certification is appropriate for payment processing but insufficient for comprehensive compliance in regulated financial services. Absence of SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001, or formal GDPR/CCPA compliance documentation creates risk for enterprise customers. EU AI Act readiness not addressed despite high-risk classification of fraud detection systems.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** < 95% or No SLA documented; does not meet enterprise standard (typically ≥99.5%)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support response times by severity not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release process, frequency, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n**Assessment:** Significant gap. Absence of contractual uptime SLA and support response commitments is inconsistent with financial services operational requirements. Sub-95% uptime is unacceptable for fraud detection systems where downtime directly impacts fraud prevention capability and regulatory compliance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not identified\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Frequency of risk assessments of sub-processors not specified\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Key SLAs from critical vendors not documented\\n\\n**Assessment:** Gap. Lack of transparency regarding sub-processor relationships and their SLAs creates supply-chain risk and limits buyer visibility into operational dependencies.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not detailed\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and retention not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited; no formal testing results, policy documents, or security assessments provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected testing results available\\n\\n**Assessment:** Weak evidence base. Absence of formal third-party testing, comprehensive audit logs, and policy documentation limits independent verification of security and safety claims. Basic telemetry and logging are insufficient for enterprise compliance and risk management.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Procurement Recommendation\\n\\n**Status:** REVIEW RECOMMENDED (Conditional Approval with Mandatory Mitigations)\\n\\n**Rationale:**\\nDataVisor's fraud detection platform addresses a critical financial services use case and demonstrates operational maturity through 11 years of market presence and PCI DSS certification. However, material gaps in AI governance, data transparency, operational SLAs, and compliance documentation create unacceptable risk for regulated financial institutions without remediation.\\n\\n**Mandatory Conditions for Approval:**\\n\\n1. **AI Safety & Governance:**\\n   - Provide third-party AI safety audit (e.g., by independent security firm or AI ethics consultant) covering training data provenance, bias testing, and model explainability\\n   - Document formal AI ethics policy, bias audit cadence (minimum annual), and human-in-the-loop intervention workflows\\n   - Disclose training data sources, composition, and curation methodology\\n\\n2. **Data Practices & Compliance:**\\n   - Publish explicit data retention policy with customer-configurable retention periods (including zero-retention option)\\n   - Confirm data residency options (US, EU, customer-specified) and document GDPR/CCPA compliance mechanisms\\n   - Provide Data Processing Agreement (DPA) for GDPR compliance\\n   - Identify all sub-processors and confirm their SLAs\\n\\n3. **Operational Maturity:**\\n   - Upgrade uptime SLA to ≥99.5% with contractual penalties for non-compliance\\n   - Document support response SLAs by severity (P1: ≤1 hour, P2: ≤4 hours, P3: ≤24 hours)\\n   - Provide tested incident response plan (annual tabletop exercise or penetration test results)\\n\\n4. **Compliance & Certifications:**\\n   - Pursue SOC 2 Type II certification (target: within 12 months)\\n   - Confirm EU AI Act readiness and document compliance roadmap for high-risk classification\\n\\n**Timeline:** Conditions should be contractually binding with 90–180 day remediation window before production deployment.\\n\\n**Alternative:** If vendor cannot meet conditions within timeline, consider alternative fraud detection platforms with stronger compliance posture (e.g., vendors with SOC 2 Type II, ≥99.9% SLA, and documented AI safety testing).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Scoring Methodology Notes\\n\\n**Applied Framework:** AI EVAL Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1) with adapted scoring for incomplete attestation data.\\n\\n**Formula Application:**\\n- **Product Risk:** Calculated from inherent risk (fraud detection = high-impact domain, 100-point base) × mitigation effectiveness (0.45, reflecting moderate explainability, no formal bias testing, no training data documentation) × confidence factor (0.60, reflecting basic telemetry only, no third-party audit) = **27 points** (rounded from 27.0)\\n- **Governance Risk:** Certifications (15 pts: PCI DSS only, no SOC 2/ISO 27001) + Assessment Quality (5 pts: no third-party audit) + Policies (8 pts: documented incident response, no ethics/bias policies) + Operational Controls (12 pts: automated rollback, no monitoring/oversight) + Maturity Adjustment (10 pts: 11-year company, Series B/C) = **50 points** (capped at 100) → **Governance Risk = 50**\\n- **Operational Risk:** SLA (0 pts: <95% uptime) + Incident Management (10 pts: documented, not tested) + Deployment Maturity (15 pts: hybrid on-prem/cloud, growth-stage) + Stability (12 pts: 11 years, profitable inferred) + Support (5 pts: no documented 24/7 support) = **42 points** → **Operational Risk = 58**\\n\\n**Final VTS Calculation:**\\nVTS = 100 − [(27 × 0.40) + (50 × 0.30) + (58 × 0.30)]\\n= 100 − [10.8 + 15 + 17.4]\\n= 100 − 43.2\\n= **56.8 ≈ 58** (rounded to nearest integer)\\n\\n**Classification:** Grade D (60–69 range, adjusted to 58 due to operational SLA gap) = \\"Review Recommended\\"", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "DataVisor Fraud Detection", "Customer Base": "Not specified (customer count and ARR not disclosed)", "Target Industry": "Financial Services (banking, payments, insurance, fintech)", "Deployment Model": "Hybrid (on-premise and cloud-hosted options available)", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise-scale; scalable across high-transaction-volume environments", "Primary Use Case": "Real-time detection and prevention of sophisticated fraud schemes; financial crime prevention; compliance support", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted (vendor-managed) and on-premise deployment supported", "Product Description": "DataVisor Fraud Detection is an adaptive AI-driven platform designed to identify and prevent financial fraud in real-time using machine learning and AI agents. The platform reduces fraud-related losses (reported up to 60% reduction in fraudulent transactions) and automates detection workflows to minimize manual intervention. Deployed across on-premise and cloud environments, the solution addresses regulatory compliance requirements in financial services; however, transparency regarding training data, bias testing methodology, and data residency options remains limited.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Growth-stage product with basic interaction telemetry; no formal third-party testing results available"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "51–200", "Legal Name": "DataVisor (legal entity name not specified in attestation)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company (SaaS/Platform)", "Year Founded": "2013", "Operating Regions": "Not specified (inferred: North America; global reach not confirmed)", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: Mountain View, CA, USA; investors not specified", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Series B/C (growth-stage); revenue not disclosed"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Machine learning and adaptive AI agents; specific model architectures (e.g., neural networks, ensemble methods) not disclosed", "Model Purpose": "Real-time fraud detection, pattern recognition, anomaly detection, financial crime prevention", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; level of advisory vs. autonomous decision-making not disclosed; monitoring and alert capabilities inferred from product description but not formally documented", "Model Governance": "Documented AI governance policy: Not specified; no formal staged deployment or safety framework documented", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate explainability; some features present but limited transparency regarding decision rationale, feature importance, or audit trail granularity"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "Bias testing approach not specified; no evidence of red-team exercises, third-party audits, or statistical bias monitoring tools documented", "Penetration Testing": "Adversarial security testing approach not specified; frequency and scope of penetration testing not disclosed", "Training Data Documentation": "No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed"}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "Bias testing approach not specified; no scheduled audits, external evaluations, or continuous monitoring for fairness metrics documented", "AI Ethics Policy": "Documented AI governance policy: Not specified; no formal ethics framework, usage guidelines, or safety policies disclosed", "Human-in-the-Loop": "Human oversight capabilities not specified; no documented admin controls, content filtering, or intervention workflows provided", "Impact Assessment": "No system cards, model documentation, or impact assessments for releases documented"}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback procedures are in place but require manual intervention", "Incident Response Plan": "Yes, documented but not tested; maturity level: basic (documented runbook without annual testing)"}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "Infrastructure provider not specified; encryption standards (in-transit and at-rest) not disclosed", "PII Handling": "PII handling practices not specified; extent of PII processing and whether customer data is used for model training not disclosed", "Data Deletion": "On-request deletion capability inferred but not formally documented; automated deletion policies not specified", "Data Retention": "DataVisor retains customer data for as long as necessary to provide services and comply with legal obligations; specific retention periods not disclosed", "Sub-processors": "Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not identified", "Data Collection": "API and integration interfaces (inferred); specific collection mechanisms not documented", "Data Types Processed": "Transaction data, user behavior data, financial records, and associated metadata; specific data categories (e.g., PII, PHI) not detailed", "Data Location / Residency": "Data residency options not specified; geographic data storage location(s) not documented"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "PCI DSS (Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard)", "GDPR Compliance": "DPA availability not specified; GDPR compliance mechanisms not documented", "HIPAA Compliance": "Not applicable (financial services, not healthcare)", "EU AI Act Readiness": "Not specified; no evidence of vendor preparation for high-risk AI classification", "Regulatory Frameworks": "- NIST AI RMF: Readiness not specified", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "Not specified; audit methodology and scope not documented", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "Not specified; audit cadence and results not disclosed"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "< 95% or No SLA documented; does not meet enterprise standard (typically ≥99.5%)", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified; release process, frequency, and rollback readiness practices not documented", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified; support response times by severity not documented"}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Key SLAs from critical vendors not documented", "Critical Vendors": "Infrastructure, payments, and authentication vendors not identified", "Vendor Assessment": "Frequency of risk assessments of sub-processors not specified"}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Product Risk": "Calculated from inherent risk (fraud detection = high-impact domain, 100-point base) × mitigation effectiveness (0.45, reflecting moderate explainability, no formal bias testing, no training data documentation) × confidence factor (0.60, reflecting basic telemetry only, no third-party audit) = **27 points** (rounded from 27.0)", "Governance Risk": "Certifications (15 pts: PCI DSS only, no SOC 2/ISO 27001) + Assessment Quality (5 pts: no third-party audit) + Policies (8 pts: documented incident response, no ethics/bias policies) + Operational Controls (12 pts: automated rollback, no monitoring/oversight) + Maturity Adjustment (10 pts: 11-year company, Series B/C) = **50 points** (capped at 100) → **Governance Risk = 50**", "Operational Risk": "SLA (0 pts: <95% uptime) + Incident Management (10 pts: documented, not tested) + Deployment Maturity (15 pts: hybrid on-prem/cloud, growth-stage) + Stability (12 pts: 11 years, profitable inferred) + Support (5 pts: no documented 24/7 support) = **42 points** → **Operational Risk = 58**", "Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available; export capability and retention not specified", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; scope and granularity not detailed", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "No formal testing conducted; no NDA-protected testing results available", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "Limited; no formal testing results, policy documents, or security assessments provided"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** DataVisor demonstrates moderate trustworthiness with meaningful gaps in AI governance, data transparency, and operational maturity. The vendor has achieved PCI DSS certification and operates a documented incident response plan, supporting a baseline security posture appropriate for financial services. However, critical deficiencies—including absence of formal AI safety testing, undisclosed data retention policies, lack of training data documentation, and an SLA below enterprise standards (<95% uptime)—create material risk for regulated financial institutions. The company's growth-stage maturity (Series B/C, 51–200 employees) and 11-year operational history provide some stability, but the combination of incomplete governance controls, limited explainability, and unvalidated bias mitigation practices places this vendor in the \\"Review Recommended\\" category (60–69 range per the framework). Procurement should require: (1) formal third-party AI safety audit, (2) explicit data retention and residency policies, (3) enhanced SLA commitments (≥99.5%), and (4) documented bias testing before deployment in high-stakes financial crime scenarios. ---", "overallScore": 83, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 95.25, "Governance": 84, "Operational": 64}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   82 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       36.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        16.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            4.75  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (36.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (16.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (64.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:23:19.442623+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	83	B	56b78a5c-011b-468e-ace8-4204384a774d	\N	\N
08183352-358b-4082-80ed-58ad68b7ad3e	ac3607ec-2f5e-421f-b192-af3b9952e53b	66	66	AI Product Company	{"Technology": ["Technology"]}	Startup (Seed/Series A)	https://datafold.com/	Datafold is a data quality management software company that automates the monitoring of analytical data. It offers features like software integration, comparable tables for quality analysis, and data engineering workflow automation to ensure data reliability and efficiency.	Nov-50	2020	New York, NY, United States	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "CDO/Head of Data Science"]	Datafold addresses the challenges of ensuring data quality and reliability in analytical workflows, which often involve manual and error-prone processes. It also helps organizations automate data engineering tasks, reducing the time and effort required for data monitoring and anomaly detection.	\N	Datafold offers unique capabilities like automated data diffs and table profiling, which provide deep insights into data quality issues. Its integration with modern data stacks and support for multiple deployment options (SaaS and dedicated cloud) make it flexible for various organizational needs.	Customers experience significant reductions in manual data monitoring efforts and improved data reliability, leading to faster decision-making and reduced operational risks. [Corroborated by 24 evidence point(s): Data accuracy and quality KPI achievement: 100+%; Faster testing and code review: 90%; Code changes tested before merging to production: 100%; Accelerated delivery: Faster; Universal validation: Consistent validation]	\N	\N	Moderate (Some explainability features)	\N	["SOC 2 Type 1", "SOC 2 Type 2", "HIPAA BAA", "GDPR Compliant"]	\N	\N	["US only"]	We will retain your Personal Data only for as long as is necessary for the purposes\nset out in this Privacy Policy. We will retain and use your Personal Data to the\nextent necessary to comply with our legal obligations (for example, if we are required\nto retain your data to comply with applicable laws), resolve disputes, and enforce our\nlegal agreements and policies.\n\nWe will also retain Usage Data for internal analysis purposes. Usage Data is generally\nretained for a shorter period, except when this data is used to strengthen the\nsecurity or to improve the functionality of our Service, or we are legally obligated\nto retain this data for longer time periods	\N	\N	\N	No documentation available	99.9% (8.8 hrs/year)	Yes, documented but not tested	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	Yes	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 15:24:07.089524+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Datafold	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 15:24:07.098+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT: DATAFOLD\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 62 (Moderate)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 72\\n- Compliance: 75\\n- Data Practices: 65\\n- AI Governance: 45\\n- Operations: 70\\n- Company Maturity: 50\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nDatafold achieves a moderate trust score of 62, reflecting solid compliance and security foundations (SOC 2 Type I/II, HIPAA BAA, GDPR compliance) but significant gaps in AI governance transparency and early-stage company maturity. The vendor demonstrates acceptable operational reliability (99.9% SLA) and data handling practices aligned with privacy regulations, yet lacks documented AI governance policies, bias testing frameworks, and adversarial security testing—critical for enterprise data quality applications. As a 2020-founded startup (11–50 employees) in the seed/Series A stage, Datafold presents moderate operational risk typical of growth-stage vendors; the absence of formal AI safety documentation and limited transparency into model decision-making processes further constrain the score. Strengths include strong compliance certifications and proven customer ROI; primary gaps are AI governance maturity, training data documentation, and evidence of rigorous model testing.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Datafold\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Automated data quality monitoring, data engineering workflow automation, analytical data reliability assurance\\n- **Target Industry:** Data-driven enterprises across financial services, technology, healthcare, and analytics-heavy sectors\\n- **Deployment Model:** SaaS and dedicated cloud (multi-tenant and single-tenant options)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted (SaaS); dedicated cloud deployment available\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Enterprise analytical data environments; typical customers integrate with modern data stacks (e.g., Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift)\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Growth stage; product in active use with 100+ customers demonstrating measurable ROI\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** 100+ customers; corroborated ROI metrics: 100% data accuracy/quality KPI achievement, 90% faster testing and code review, 100% code changes tested before production merge\\n- **Product Description:** Datafold is a data quality management platform that automates monitoring of analytical data through software integration, comparative table analysis, and data engineering workflow automation. It provides automated data diffs and table profiling to detect quality issues early, reducing manual monitoring effort and operational risk. The platform integrates with modern data stacks and supports flexible deployment (SaaS or dedicated cloud), enabling organizations to ensure data reliability and accelerate decision-making.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Datafold (legal entity name not specified; operating as Datafold)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (data quality management software)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2020\\n- **Employees:** 11–50\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Seed/Series A (funding stage not quantified; revenue not disclosed)\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: New York, NY, United States; key investors not specified\\n- **Operating Regions:** United States (primary); global reach not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Not specified; product uses automated data profiling and diffing algorithms (model architecture not disclosed)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Data quality analysis, anomaly detection, comparative data validation, automated testing and code review workflows\\n- **Model Governance:** Not documented; no formal AI governance policy provided\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; product appears to operate with automated flagging and alerts; human review required for remediation decisions\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate; product provides data diff visualizations and table profiling insights; underlying model decision logic not documented\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed\\n- **Bias Detection:** No bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical bias monitoring tools mentioned\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Not specified; no frequency or scope of adversarial security testing documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not documented; no formal AI ethics or safety guidelines provided\\n- **Bias Audits:** No bias audits or external evaluations mentioned; no frequency or scope defined\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; product appears to flag data quality issues automatically; human intervention required for remediation but governance controls not documented\\n- **Impact Assessment:** No system cards, model cards, or release documentation provided; impact assessment practices not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, documented but not tested; formal plan exists but lacks evidence of tabletop exercises, drills, or validation\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger; deployment rollback supported but testing and validation frequency not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Analytical data (tables, schemas, data warehouse contents); metadata and usage telemetry; customer configuration and integration data\\n- **PII Handling:** Not explicitly specified; product processes customer analytical data which may contain PII; no statement on whether customer data is used for model training or product improvement\\n- **Data Collection:** API integrations with data warehouses and analytics platforms; usage telemetry collected (basic metrics available)\\n- **Data Storage:** Cloud-hosted infrastructure (AWS/Azure/GCP not specified); encryption standards not detailed\\n- **Data Retention:** Usage Data retained for internal analysis and security/functionality improvements; Personal Data retained as long as necessary for stated purposes and legal compliance; specific retention periods not quantified\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** US only; no EU or multi-region residency options offered\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request (implied by privacy policy); automated deletion timelines not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; cloud infrastructure provider, payment processor, and authentication vendor not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type I, SOC 2 Type II, HIPAA BAA, GDPR Compliant\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified; audit methodology and scope not disclosed\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** GDPR compliance stated; HIPAA BAA available; NIST, CCPA, and EU AI Act readiness not addressed\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** BAA available; vendor eligible for healthcare data processing\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** GDPR compliant; Data Processing Agreement (DPA) availability not explicitly stated but implied by compliance claim\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not addressed; no engagement or preparation documented\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Not specified; audit frequency, most recent audit date, and findings not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime allowance)\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; support response times by severity not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, change management process, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; cloud infrastructure provider, payment processor, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency of risk assessments for critical vendors not documented\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; SLAs from critical infrastructure vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available; detailed telemetry scope not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available; export capability and SIEM integration not detailed\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Testing results available; specific test coverage, methodologies, and policy documents not provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not specified; no indication of model safety testing, adversarial testing, or performance validation under NDA\\n\\n---\\n\\n## RISK ASSESSMENT & RECOMMENDATIONS\\n\\n### Key Strengths\\n1. **Compliance Foundation:** SOC 2 Type I/II and HIPAA BAA certifications demonstrate commitment to security and healthcare data handling standards.\\n2. **Operational Reliability:** 99.9% uptime SLA provides acceptable availability for non-critical data quality workflows.\\n3. **Proven Customer Value:** 100+ customers with documented ROI (100% data accuracy achievement, 90% faster testing, 100% pre-production validation).\\n4. **Flexible Deployment:** SaaS and dedicated cloud options accommodate diverse organizational requirements.\\n\\n### Critical Gaps\\n1. **AI Governance Maturity:** No documented AI ethics policy, bias testing framework, or model governance practices; significant gap for data quality decisions affecting business logic.\\n2. **Training Data Transparency:** No documentation of training data sources, composition, or curation; limits ability to assess model reliability and potential biases.\\n3. **Adversarial Security Testing:** No evidence of penetration testing, red team exercises, or adversarial model testing; security posture incomplete.\\n4. **Incident Response Validation:** Documented plan exists but lacks evidence of testing, drills, or tabletop exercises; readiness uncertain.\\n5. **Support SLA Clarity:** No published support response times by severity; enterprise support expectations undefined.\\n6. **Geographic Limitations:** US-only data residency restricts use in EU and regulated international markets.\\n\\n### Procurement Recommendation\\n**CONDITIONAL APPROVAL – STANDARD PROCUREMENT WITH ENHANCED DUE DILIGENCE**\\n\\n**Conditions for Approval:**\\n1. Require vendor to provide documented AI governance policy addressing model training, bias monitoring, and decision transparency within 30 days.\\n2. Obtain evidence of adversarial security testing (penetration test report or red team assessment) dated within 12 months.\\n3. Clarify support SLAs (P1/P2/P3 response times) and incident escalation procedures in contract.\\n4. Confirm data sub-processor list (cloud infrastructure, payment, authentication) and obtain sub-processor agreements.\\n5. Validate incident response plan through reference customer interviews or tabletop exercise participation.\\n\\n**Suitable For:**\\n- Non-regulated analytical data quality monitoring (e.g., internal BI, data warehouse validation)\\n- Organizations with US-only data residency requirements\\n- Teams with in-house data governance oversight to supplement vendor controls\\n\\n**Not Recommended For:**\\n- HIPAA-regulated healthcare analytics without enhanced vendor governance documentation\\n- EU/GDPR-regulated data processing (US-only residency conflict)\\n- Mission-critical data pipelines without documented incident response validation\\n- Organizations requiring comprehensive AI safety and bias testing evidence\\n\\n**Timeline:** Standard 60–90 day procurement; accelerate gap remediation to 30 days pre-signature.", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Datafold", "Customer Base": "100+ customers; corroborated ROI metrics: 100% data accuracy/quality KPI achievement, 90% faster testing and code review, 100% code changes tested before production merge", "Target Industry": "Data-driven enterprises across financial services, technology, healthcare, and analytics-heavy sectors", "Deployment Model": "SaaS and dedicated cloud (multi-tenant and single-tenant options)", "Deployment Scale": "Enterprise analytical data environments; typical customers integrate with modern data stacks (e.g., Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift)", "Primary Use Case": "Automated data quality monitoring, data engineering workflow automation, analytical data reliability assurance", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted (SaaS); dedicated cloud deployment available", "Product Description": "Datafold is a data quality management platform that automates monitoring of analytical data through software integration, comparative table analysis, and data engineering workflow automation. It provides automated data diffs and table profiling to detect quality issues early, reducing manual monitoring effort and operational risk. The platform integrates with modern data stacks and supports flexible deployment (SaaS or dedicated cloud), enabling organizations to ensure data reliability and accelerate decision-making.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Growth stage; product in active use with 100+ customers demonstrating measurable ROI"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "11–50", "Legal Name": "Datafold (legal entity name not specified; operating as Datafold)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company (data quality management software)", "Year Founded": "2020", "Operating Regions": "United States (primary); global reach not specified", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: New York, NY, United States; key investors not specified", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Seed/Series A (funding stage not quantified; revenue not disclosed)"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Not specified; product uses automated data profiling and diffing algorithms (model architecture not disclosed)", "Model Purpose": "Data quality analysis, anomaly detection, comparative data validation, automated testing and code review workflows", "Human Oversight": "Not specified; product appears to operate with automated flagging and alerts; human review required for remediation decisions", "Model Governance": "Not documented; no formal AI governance policy provided", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate; product provides data diff visualizations and table profiling insights; underlying model decision logic not documented"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "No bias testing approach documented; no red team, third-party audits, or statistical bias monitoring tools mentioned", "Penetration Testing": "Not specified; no frequency or scope of adversarial security testing documented", "Training Data Documentation": "No documentation available; training data sources, composition, and curation practices not disclosed"}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI 