, basic logging available; log retention period, export format, and SIEM integration not specified; audit trail completeness not documented", "Timeline to Production-Ready": "10–12 months (subject to vendor remediation)", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available; scope (user counts, query volume, latency, error rates) not detailed; export capability not specified", "Residual Risk (Post-Remediation)": "Moderate (50–60/100) due to startup stage and China-based operations; ongoing monitoring required", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "Limited/Partial; third-party model evaluation results, safety benchmarks, or adversarial robustness testing not available; NDA-protected results not offered", "For Cost-Sensitive, Non-Regulated Use Cases": "DeepSeek Chat is a strong candidate; leverage open-source model for on-premises deployment to mitigate data residency concerns", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "Limited/Partial; formal security policies, compliance documentation, and testing results not provided; evidence files not uploaded", "For Regulated Industries (Healthcare, Finance, Government)": "Recommend established vendors with proven compliance track records (e.g., OpenAI, Anthropic, Azure OpenAI) until DeepSeek achieves SOC 2 Type 2 and HIPAA eligibility"}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "C", "label": "Acceptable Vendor", "summary": "** DeepSeek Chat scores in the Low trust range (42/100) due to significant gaps across governance, compliance, and operational maturity. While the vendor demonstrates transparency through open-source models and cost efficiency, critical deficiencies in security certifications, data retention policies, incident response readiness, and formal AI governance frameworks present material risk for enterprise deployment. The startup stage (founded 2023, 51–200 employees) and absence of independent compliance audits, SLA commitments, and documented bias testing further constrain confidence. Strengths include open-model architecture and basic logging capability; primary gaps are lack of formal certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA), undefined data retention practices, and immature operational controls. ---", "overallScore": 78, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 95.24, "Governance": 74, "Operational": 58}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   78 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         C - Acceptable Vendor\\n  Next step:     Enhanced due diligence; require mitigation roadmap\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       42.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        26.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            4.76  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (42.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (26.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (58.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Raise Governance (74.0/100) - strengthen controls & proof\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Data residency in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 15:25:27.154771+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\