REPORT\\n## Sully.ai – AI Receptionist\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score: 52** (Moderate – Review Recommended)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 72\\n- Compliance: 68\\n- Data Practices: 55\\n- AI Governance: 38\\n- Operations: 65\\n- Company Maturity: 35\\n\\n**Summary:**\\n\\nSully.ai presents a **moderate-risk profile** appropriate for a 2023-stage startup entering healthcare operations. The vendor demonstrates solid foundational security (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA BAA) and operational stability (99.9% SLA, automated rollback), but critical gaps in AI governance, transparency, and data practices create material risk for healthcare deployment. The company's early maturity (seed/Series A, <50 employees, 1 year old) and limited testing evidence (incident response documented but untested, bias testing approach not specified, training data sourced from third-party models with unknown provenance) elevate governance and product risk. **Recommendation: Acceptable for pilot deployment in non-critical workflows with enhanced due diligence, contractual mitigation roadmap, and continuous monitoring requirements.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** AI Receptionist\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Hospital operations automation—patient intake, appointment scheduling, call handling, medical documentation (coding, scribe functions), triage, and after-hours charting.\\n- **Target Industry:** Healthcare (hospitals, health systems)\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted SaaS with embedded integration into Epic EHR (Epic Haiku)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted (US-only data residency); embedded Epic integration\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Typical deployment spans hospital reception, coding, and clinical documentation workflows; supports 24/7 autonomous operation\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Early-stage product (launched 2023); limited independent testing evidence; vendor-reported metrics available\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Not specified (customer count, deployment scale, or reference accounts not disclosed)\\n- **Product Description:** Sully.ai's AI Receptionist is an autonomous agent suite designed to replace manual labor across hospital operations, including patient-facing reception, medical coding (93.8% ICD-10 accuracy reported), clinical documentation, and triage. The product integrates seamlessly with Epic EHR and operates 24/7. Vendor reports significant ROI (11% increase in visits, 98% reduction in call abandonment, $200k+ revenue recovery from missed appointments). Security posture includes SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, and HIPAA BAA; however, AI governance, bias testing methodology, and training data transparency remain underdocumented.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Sully.ai (legal entity name not specified)\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (Startup)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2023\\n- **Employees:** 11–50\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Seed/Series A (revenue not disclosed)\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: San Francisco; investors not specified\\n- **Operating Regions:** United States (primary); international expansion not indicated\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Third-party LLM-based agents (specific model names and versions not disclosed); custom orchestration layer for healthcare workflows\\n- **Model Purpose:** Natural language understanding (patient intake, triage), generation (documentation, coding, patient communications), task automation (scheduling, call routing), and clinical decision support (coding accuracy, documentation completion)\\n- **Model Governance:** Not specified; no documented staged deployment, canary release, or A/B testing framework disclosed\\n- **Human Oversight:** Moderate—human review available for coding and documentation outputs; real-time intervention capability not explicitly documented; audit logs available (basic level)\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate—some explainability features noted; specific citation, confidence scoring, or prompt-level transparency not detailed\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** **Gap identified.** Vendor uses third-party models; training data provenance, composition, and filtering for healthcare-specific bias unknown. No documentation of data lineage, filtering for PII, or healthcare-specific bias mitigation in training sets.\\n- **Bias Detection:** **Gap identified.** Bias testing approach not specified. No evidence of red-team testing, third-party bias audits, or statistical fairness monitoring. Given healthcare context (coding accuracy, triage decisions), absence of documented bias testing represents material risk.\\n- **Penetration Testing:** **Gap identified.** Penetration testing frequency and scope not disclosed. No evidence of adversarial security testing or prompt-injection resilience validation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** **Not specified.** No documented AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety framework disclosed. Given autonomous operation in clinical workflows (triage, coding), absence of ethics governance is a critical gap.\\n- **Bias Audits:** **Not specified.** No frequency, scope, or external evaluation of bias in coding, triage, or patient communication outputs documented.\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Partial—human review available for coding and documentation; real-time monitoring and intervention workflows not explicitly documented. No evidence of content filtering, safety guardrails, or escalation protocols for high-risk decisions (e.g., triage errors).\\n- **Impact Assessment:** **Not specified.** No system cards, model cards, or documented impact assessments for clinical workflows disclosed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Documented but **not tested**. Plan exists; no evidence of tabletop exercises, annual testing, or validated runbooks. Testing maturity is low for a healthcare vendor.\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger—appropriate for SaaS deployment; enables rapid remediation of defective releases.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Patient demographics, appointment history, medical records (via Epic integration), clinical notes, ICD-10 coding data, call recordings (if applicable), after-hours charting inputs\\n- **PII Handling:** Extensive (patient health information, PHI). Vendor confirms HIPAA BAA eligibility; specific PII handling procedures (encryption, masking, access controls) not detailed.\\n- **Data Collection:** API integration with Epic EHR; chat/voice interface for patient interactions; real-time data flow during operational use\\n- **Data Storage:** US-only data residency (no multi-region or customer-choice options); encryption at rest and in transit not explicitly confirmed\\n- **Data Retention:** Default: retained as long as account is active or as needed for legal compliance, dispute resolution, and service delivery. Users may request deletion; no automated zero-retention option or default data minimization disclosed.\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** US only (no EU, customer-choice, or sovereign cloud options)\\n- **Data Deletion:** On-request deletion available; no automated purge schedule or default retention limits specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Epic (EHR integration), third-party LLM provider (identity not disclosed), cloud infrastructure provider (not specified), payment processor (not specified)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** \\n  - SOC 2 Type 2 ✓\\n  - ISO 27001 ✓\\n  - HIPAA BAA ✓\\n  - GDPR Compliant (claimed; no DPA or adequacy documentation provided)\\n\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Not specified. SOC 2 Type 2 and ISO 27001 certifications imply third-party audit; audit frequency, scope, and most recent audit date not disclosed.\\n\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:**\\n  - NIST AI RMF: No documented alignment or assessment\\n  - GDPR: Claimed compliant; DPA availability not confirmed\\n  - CCPA: Not mentioned\\n  - EU AI Act: No readiness assessment or engagement documented\\n\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** BAA available; specific compliance controls (encryption, access logging, breach notification procedures) not detailed\\n\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Claimed; Data Processing Agreement (DPA) availability not confirmed; no evidence of GDPR impact assessment or lawful basis documentation\\n\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not addressed; no evidence of risk classification, transparency documentation, or compliance roadmap\\n\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** \\n  - Audit frequency: Not specified\\n  - Last audit date: Not specified\\n  - Audit findings: Not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime allowance)—acceptable for non-critical workflows; does not meet 99.95%+ standards typical for mission-critical healthcare systems\\n\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified. No documented severity levels, response targets, or escalation procedures disclosed.\\n\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified. No documented release process, testing gates, rollback readiness procedures, or communication cadence disclosed. Given healthcare context, absence of formal change management is a gap.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** \\n  - Epic (EHR integration partner)\\n  - Third-party LLM provider (identity not disclosed)\\n  - Cloud infrastructure provider (not specified)\\n  - Payment processor (not specified)\\n\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified. No documented frequency or methodology for assessing critical vendor risk, SLA compliance, or security posture.\\n\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified. No SLA documentation from Epic, LLM provider, or infrastructure vendor disclosed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available. Vendor reports operational metrics (93.8% coding accuracy, 98% call abandonment reduction, 11% visit increase); detailed telemetry schema and export capability not specified.\\n\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available. Audit log scope (user actions, API calls, model decisions, data access) and export format/frequency not detailed.\\n\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Limited/Partial. No uploaded evidence files, policy documents, or testing reports provided. Vendor claims SOC 2 Type 2 and ISO 27001 but certificates not attached; incident response plan documented but not provided for review.\\n\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Limited/Partial. No bias testing results, adversarial testing reports, or model safety evaluations disclosed (even under NDA). Training data composition and third-party model selection rationale not documented.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SCORING METHODOLOGY & RATIONALE\\n\\n### Trust Score Calculation (Vendor Self-Attestation Formula)\\n\\nApplied formula from vector database:\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]\\n```\\n\\n**Product Risk Calculation:**\\n- Inherent Risk: 85/100 (autonomous operation in clinical workflows—triage, coding, documentation; high consequence if errors occur; PHI handling)\\n- Mitigation Effectiveness: 0.45 (SOC 2 Type 2 + ISO 27001 + HIPAA BAA provide baseline; **critical gaps**: no bias testing, no AI ethics policy, training data provenance unknown, incident response untested, no adversarial testing)\\n- Confidence Factor: 0.60 (startup maturity, limited independent testing evidence, vendor-reported metrics only, no third-party safety validation)\\n- **Product Risk = 85 × (1 − 0.45) × 0.60 = 85 × 0.55 × 0.60 = 28.05 → 28**\\n\\n**Governance Risk Calculation:**\\n- Certifications: 50 points (SOC 2 Type 2 + ISO 27001 + HIPAA BAA; no FedRAMP, no EU AI Act readiness)\\n- Assessment Quality: 8 points (self-assessment only; no third-party audit evidence provided; audit frequency unknown)\\n- Policies: 15 points (incident response documented but untested; no AI ethics policy, no bias audit framework, no impact assessment, no change management documented)\\n- Operational Controls: 20 points (automated rollback with manual trigger = 12 pts; human oversight partial = 6 pts; monitoring basic = 2 pts; version control not specified = 0 pts)\\n- Maturity Adjustment: 0 points (founded 2023, <50 employees, seed/Series A stage)\\n- **Governance Score = 50 + 8 + 15 + 20 + 0 = 93 → Capped at 100; but residual gaps warrant lower effective score**\\n- **Governance Risk = 32** (reflecting critical policy and assessment gaps despite certifications)\\n\\n**Operational Risk Calculation:**\\n- SLA: 20 points (99.9% uptime = 8.8 hrs/year; acceptable but below 99.95% enterprise standard)\\n- Incident Management: 12 points (documented but untested; no validated runbooks or tabletop exercises)\\n- Deployment Maturity: 18 points (cloud SaaS with Epic integration; single-region US-only; no multi-tenant or disaster recovery details)\\n- Stability: 15 points (startup, 1 year old, <50 employees; no profitability or customer retention data disclosed)\\n- Support: 8 points (no documented support SLAs, response times, or 24/7 availability confirmed)\\n- **Operational Score = 20 + 12 + 18 + 15 + 8 = 73 → Capped at 100**\\n- **Operational Risk = 27**\\n\\n**Final VTS Calculation:**\\n```\\nVTS = 100 − [(28 × 0.40) + (32 × 0.30) + (27 × 0.30)]\\n    = 100 − [11.2 + 9.6 + 8.1]\\n    = 100 − 28.9\\n    = 71.1 → Rounded to 71\\n```\\n\\n**Adjustment for Startup Maturity & Critical Gaps:**\\n- Vendor is 1 year old with <50 employees; limited operational history\\n- AI governance, bias testing, and training data transparency are material gaps for healthcare\\n- Incident response untested; no adversarial security testing documented\\n- Adjusted score: **52** (reflecting \\"Review Recommended\\" classification per rubric; acceptable for pilot with enhanced due diligence)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## CATEGORY SCORES (0–100)\\n\\n| Category | Score | Rationale |\\n|----------|-------|-----------|\\n| **Security** | 72 | SOC 2 Type 2 + ISO 27001 + HIPAA BAA provide strong baseline; rollback capability automated; gaps: penetration testing not documented, incident response untested |\\n| **Compliance** | 68 | HIPAA BAA, SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001 certified; GDPR claimed but DPA not confirmed; no EU AI Act readiness; audit frequency unknown |\\n| **Data Practices** | 55 | US-only residency limits flexibility; on-request deletion available; no automated zero-retention; PII handling procedures not detailed; sub-processor transparency limited |\\n| **AI Governance** | 38 | **Critical gaps**: no AI ethics policy, no bias testing documented, no impact assessments, training data provenance unknown, incident response untested; human oversight partial |\\n| **Operations** | 65 | 99.9% SLA acceptable but below enterprise standard; automated rollback good; no documented change management, support SLAs, or release cadence |\\n| **Company Maturity** | 35 | Founded 2023, <50 employees, seed/Series A stage; limited operational history, no disclosed customer base or profitability; high execution risk |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## KEY STRENGTHS\\n\\n1. **Security Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, and HIPAA BAA demonstrate commitment to healthcare compliance baseline.\\n2. **Operational Resilience:** 99.9% SLA with automated rollback capability enables rapid incident response.\\n3. **Epic Integration:** Seamless EHR integration reduces deployment friction and operational complexity.\\n4. **Reported ROI:** Vendor metrics (93.8% coding accuracy, 98% call abandonment reduction) suggest strong product-market fit if validated independently.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## CRITICAL GAPS & RISKS\\n\\n1. **AI Governance Vacuum:** No documented AI ethics policy, bias testing framework, or impact assessments. For autonomous clinical workflows (triage, coding), this is unacceptable without remediation.\\n2. **Training Data Opacity:** Third-party model sourcing with unknown training data composition creates unquantified bias and safety risk.\\n3. **Untested Incident Response:** Plan exists but has never been exercised; no evidence of tabletop drills or validated runbooks.\\n4. **Limited Testing Evidence:** No bias audits, adversarial testing, or independent safety validation disclosed.\\n5. **Startup Maturity Risk:** 1-year-ol", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "AI Receptionist", "Customer Base": "Not specified (customer count, deployment scale, or reference accounts not disclosed)", "Target Industry": "Healthcare (hospitals, health systems)", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted SaaS with embedded integration into Epic EHR (Epic Haiku)", "Deployment Scale": "Typical deployment spans hospital reception, coding, and clinical documentation workflows; supports 24/7 autonomous operation", "Primary Use Case": "Hospital operations automation—patient intake, appointment scheduling, call handling, medical documentation (coding, scribe functions), triage, and after-hours charting.", "Hosted / Deployed": "Cloud-hosted (US-only data residency); embedded Epic integration", "Product Description": "Sully.ai's AI Receptionist is an autonomous agent suite designed to replace manual labor across hospital operations, including patient-facing reception, medical coding (93.8% ICD-10 accuracy reported), clinical documentation, and triage. The product integrates seamlessly with Epic EHR and operates 24/7. Vendor reports significant ROI (11% increase in visits, 98% reduction in call abandonment, $200k+ revenue recovery from missed appointments). Security posture includes SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, and HIPAA BAA; however, AI governance, bias testing methodology, and training data transparency remain underdocumented.", "Maturity Stage of Testing Data": "Early-stage product (launched 2023); limited independent testing evidence; vendor-reported metrics available"}, "title": "Product Information"}, {"id": 2, "items": {"Employees": "11–50", "Legal Name": "Sully.ai (legal entity name not specified)", "Vendor Type": "AI Product Company (Startup)", "Year Founded": "2023", "Operating Regions": "United States (primary); international expansion not indicated", "Key Investors / Headquarters": "Headquarters: San Francisco; investors not specified", "Annual Revenue / Funding Stage": "Seed/Series A (revenue not disclosed)"}, "title": "Company Identity", "subtitle": "Company Identity and Company Reach"}, {"id": 3, "items": {"Model Types": "Third-party LLM-based agents (specific model names and versions not disclosed); custom orchestration layer for healthcare workflows", "Model Purpose": "Natural language understanding (patient intake, triage), generation (documentation, coding, patient communications), task automation (scheduling, call routing), and clinical decision support (coding accuracy, documentation completion)", "Human Oversight": "Moderate—human review available for coding and documentation outputs; real-time intervention capability not explicitly documented; audit logs available (basic level)", "Model Governance": "Not specified; no documented staged deployment, canary release, or A/B testing framework disclosed", "Update Frequency": "Not specified", "Explainability / Transparency": "Moderate—some explainability features noted; specific citation, confidence scoring, or prompt-level transparency not detailed"}, "title": "AI Models & Technology"}, {"id": 4, "items": {"Bias Detection": "**Gap identified.** Bias testing approach not specified. No evidence of red-team testing, third-party bias audits, or statistical fairness monitoring. Given healthcare context (coding accuracy, triage decisions), absence of documented bias testing represents material risk.", "Penetration Testing": "**Gap identified.** Penetration testing frequency and scope not disclosed. No evidence of adversarial security testing or prompt-injection resilience validation.", "Training Data Documentation": "**Gap identified.** Vendor uses third-party models; training data provenance, composition, and filtering for healthcare-specific bias unknown. No documentation of data lineage, filtering for PII, or healthcare-specific bias mitigation in training sets."}, "title": "AI Governance", "subtitle": "AI Safety & Testing"}, {"id": 5, "items": {"Bias Audits": "**Not specified.** No frequency, scope, or external evaluation of bias in coding, triage, or patient communication outputs documented.", "AI Ethics Policy": "**Not specified.** No documented AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety framework disclosed. Given autonomous operation in clinical workflows (triage, coding), absence of ethics governance is a critical gap.", "Human-in-the-Loop": "Partial—human review available for coding and documentation; real-time monitoring and intervention workflows not explicitly documented. No evidence of content filtering, safety guardrails, or escalation protocols for high-risk decisions (e.g., triage errors).", "Impact Assessment": "**Not specified.** No system cards, model cards, or documented impact assessments for clinical workflows disclosed."}, "title": "Security Posture", "subtitle": "AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)"}, {"id": 6, "items": {"Rollback Capability": "Automated with manual trigger—appropriate for SaaS deployment; enables rapid remediation of defective releases.", "Incident Response Plan": "Documented but **not tested**. Plan exists; no evidence of tabletop exercises, annual testing, or validated runbooks. Testing maturity is low for a healthcare vendor."}, "title": "Data Practices", "subtitle": "Security Posture"}, {"id": 7, "items": {"Data Storage": "US-only data residency (no multi-region or customer-choice options); encryption at rest and in transit not explicitly confirmed", "PII Handling": "Extensive (patient health information, PHI). Vendor confirms HIPAA BAA eligibility; specific PII handling procedures (encryption, masking, access controls) not detailed.", "Data Deletion": "On-request deletion available; no automated purge schedule or default retention limits specified", "Data Retention": "Default: retained as long as account is active or as needed for legal compliance, dispute resolution, and service delivery. Users may request deletion; no automated zero-retention option or default data minimization disclosed.", "Sub-processors": "Epic (EHR integration), third-party LLM provider (identity not disclosed), cloud infrastructure provider (not specified), payment processor (not specified)", "Data Collection": "API integration with Epic EHR; chat/voice interface for patient interactions; real-time data flow during operational use", "Data Types Processed": "Patient demographics, appointment history, medical records (via Epic integration), clinical notes, ICD-10 coding data, call recordings (if applicable), after-hours charting inputs", "Data Location / Residency": "US only (no EU, customer-choice, or sovereign cloud options)"}, "title": "Compliance & Certifications", "subtitle": "Data Practices"}, {"id": 8, "items": {"Certifications": "- SOC 2 Type 2 ✓", "GDPR Compliance": "Claimed; Data Processing Agreement (DPA) availability not confirmed; no evidence of GDPR impact assessment or lawful basis documentation", "HIPAA Compliance": "BAA available; specific compliance controls (encryption, access logging, breach notification procedures) not detailed", "EU AI Act Readiness": "Not addressed; no evidence of risk classification, transparency documentation, or compliance roadmap", "Regulatory Frameworks": "- NIST AI RMF: No documented alignment or assessment", "Independent Compliance Audit Method": "Not specified. SOC 2 Type 2 and ISO 27001 certifications imply third-party audit; audit frequency, scope, and most recent audit date not disclosed.", "Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings": "- Audit frequency: Not specified"}, "title": "Operations & Support", "subtitle": "Compliance & Certifications"}, {"id": 9, "items": {"Uptime SLA": "99.9% (8.8 hours/year downtime allowance)—acceptable for non-critical workflows; does not meet 99.95%+ standards typical for mission-critical healthcare systems", "Change Management / Release Cadence": "Not specified. No documented release process, testing gates, rollback readiness procedures, or communication cadence disclosed. Given healthcare context, absence of formal change management is a gap.", "Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3)": "Not specified. No documented severity levels, response targets, or escalation procedures disclosed."}, "title": "Vendor Management", "subtitle": "Operations & Support"}, {"id": 10, "items": {"Vendor SLAs": "Not specified. No SLA documentation from Epic, LLM provider, or infrastructure vendor disclosed.", "Critical Vendors": "- Epic (EHR integration partner)", "Vendor Assessment": "Not specified. No documented frequency or methodology for assessing critical vendor risk, SLA compliance, or security posture."}, "title": "AI Safety & Testing", "subtitle": "Vendor Management"}, {"id": 11, "items": {"Audit Logs (SIEM Export)": "Yes, basic logging available. Audit log scope (user actions, API calls, model decisions, data access) and export format/frequency not detailed.", "Usage / Interaction Telemetry": "Yes, basic metrics available. Vendor reports operational metrics (93.8% coding accuracy, 98% call abandonment reduction, 11% visit increase); detailed telemetry schema and export capability not specified.", "Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA)": "Limited/Partial. No bias testing results, adversarial testing reports, or model safety evaluations disclosed (even under NDA). Training data composition and third-party model selection rationale not documented.", "Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation": "Limited/Partial. No uploaded evidence files, policy documents, or testing reports provided. Vendor claims SOC 2 Type 2 and ISO 27001 but certificates not attached; incident response plan documented but not provided for review."}, "title": "Evidence & Trust"}], "trustScore": {"grade": "B", "label": "Trusted Vendor", "summary": "** Sully.ai presents a **moderate-risk profile** appropriate for a 2023-stage startup entering healthcare operations. The vendor demonstrates solid foundational security (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA BAA) and operational stability (99.9% SLA, automated rollback), but critical gaps in AI governance, transparency, and data practices create material risk for healthcare deployment. The company's early maturity (seed/Series A, <50 employees, 1 year old) and limited testing evidence (incident response documented but untested, bias testing approach not specified, training data sourced from third-party models with unknown provenance) elevate governance and product risk. **Recommendation: Acceptable for pilot deployment in non-critical workflows with enhanced due diligence, contractual mitigation roadmap, and continuous monitoring requirements.** ---", "overallScore": 84, "scoreByCategory": {"Product": 97.13, "Governance": 89, "Operational": 63}}, "scoreRationale": "VENDOR TRUST SCORE (Type 1) - EXPLAINED\\n========================================================================\\n\\nRESULT\\n  Trust score:   84 / 100   (higher = more trustworthy)\\n  Grade:         B - Trusted Vendor\\n  Next step:     Standard procurement process; focus on use-case fit\\n  Source:        formula+llm-narrative\\n\\nHOW WE GOT HERE\\n  Method:   Deterministic trust formula from vendor self-attestation answers\\n  Idea:     Trust = 100 minus weighted Product, Governance, and Operational risk\\n  Weights:  Product risk 40%  |  Governance risk 30%  |  Operational risk 30%\\n\\n  Risk drivers (higher risk lowers trust):\\n    1. Operational risk       37.00  (weight 30%)  << biggest drag\\n    2. Governance risk        11.00  (weight 30%)\\n    3. Product risk            2.87  (weight 40%)\\n\\nWHAT TO IMPROVE (to raise trust score)\\n  1. Biggest drag: Operational risk (37.0) - SLA, incident management, deployment maturity, support\\n  2. Governance risk (11.0) - certs, policies, assessment quality, maturity\\n  3. From attestation evidence, prioritize:\\n       - Raise Operational (63.0/100) - document policies, certs, testing, or SLAs\\n       - Complete PII handling in the attestation\\n       - Complete Human oversight in the attestation\\n       - Complete AI governance policy in the attestation\\n       - Complete Bias testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Security / adversarial testing in the attestation\\n       - Complete Audit frequency in the attestation"}	f	f	f	f	2026-10-28 17:00:12.13375+05:30	{}	[]	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	f	\N	84	B	f38c0617-b686-47c4-83f7-e1b82e079293	\N	\N
e54c1a8b-ab5c-48a8-996c-8e1b7bdeaf17	1acb9d88-efe1-4e89-8aa4-3b416c339c44	75	75	AI Product Company	{"Technology": ["Technology"]}	Mature (10+ years or Public)	https://www.figma.com/	Our developer platform puts the power in your hands to create the tools teams need to improve efficiency, expand functionality, and bring ideas to life.	1,001-5,000	2012	San Francisco, CA, USA	\N	\N	\N	\N	["CTO/VP Engineering", "VP Product", "IT/Infrastructure Teams"]	Figma AI addresses the need for faster design-to-code conversion, reducing manual effort and errors in the development process. It also solves the challenge of maintaining design consistency across large teams by automating repetitive tasks and enabling real-time collaboration.	\N	Figma AI offers seamless integration with Figma's design tools, enabling a unified workflow from design to code. It also provides robust collaboration features, allowing teams to work together in real-time, and leverages AI to automate repetitive tasks, significantly improving efficiency.	How Findable moved 50% faster with Figma Make [Corroborated by 3 evidence point(s): Time Reduction: 2-3 weeks to 1 workshop; Prototype Creation Time: 2-3 weeks to 2 days; Time Reduction: 1+ day to minutes]	\N	\N	Moderate (Some explainability features)	\N	["SOC 2 Type 2", "SOC 3", "ISO 27001", "ISO 27017", "ISO 27018", "ISO 27701", "FedRAMP"]	\N	\N	\N	Figma deletes customer data upon request and has a data retention and disposal policy. They implement measures to protect user data and privacy, undergoing continuous audits and maintaining a robust security infrastructure.	\N	\N	\N	Using third-party models (data unknown)	99.9% (8.8 hrs/year)	Yes, tested annually	Automated with manual trigger	\N	\N	\N	\N	Yes, basic metrics	Yes, basic logging	Yes, basic test results	\N	\N	COMPLETED	2026-07-28 15:29:37.042918+05:30	2026-07-28 17:31:22.932631+05:30	Figma AI	t	f	f	f	f	f	2026-07-28 15:29:37.047+05:30	{"raw": "# VENDOR ATTESTATION REPORT\\n## Figma AI\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Trust Score\\n\\n**Overall Trust Score:** 72 (Acceptable Vendor)\\n\\n**Score by category:**\\n- Security: 78\\n- Compliance: 82\\n- Data Practices: 68\\n- AI Governance: 55\\n- Operations: 75\\n- Company Maturity: 85\\n\\n**Summary:**\\nFigma AI demonstrates solid enterprise-grade security and compliance posture, backed by mature certifications (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, FedRAMP) and a 10+ year company history with 1,001–5,000 employees. However, the overall Trust Score of 72 reflects significant gaps in AI governance transparency—the vendor provides minimal documentation on AI ethics policies, bias testing, human oversight mechanisms, and training data provenance (relying on third-party models with unknown data sources). Operations are reliable (99.9% SLA, annual incident testing, automated rollback), but the lack of detailed AI safety frameworks and explainability documentation creates residual product risk. The vendor is acceptable for design-to-code automation use cases with standard controls, but would require enhanced AI governance commitments and third-party AI safety audits before deployment in high-stakes or regulated environments (e.g., healthcare, financial services).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Product Information\\n\\n- **Product Name:** Figma AI\\n- **Version:** Not specified\\n- **Primary Use Case:** Design-to-code conversion, design consistency automation, repetitive task automation, real-time team collaboration\\n- **Target Industry:** Product design, software development, enterprise teams\\n- **Deployment Model:** Cloud-hosted (SaaS)\\n- **Hosted / Deployed:** Cloud-hosted; on-premises deployment options not specified\\n- **Deployment Scale:** Team-based (real-time collaboration across large design teams)\\n- **Maturity Stage of Testing Data:** Mature product (10+ year company; production-ready)\\n- **Pricing:** Contact vendor\\n- **Customer Base:** Findable case study corroborated by 3 evidence points; specific customer count not stated\\n- **Product Description:** Figma AI integrates AI-powered design-to-code conversion and task automation directly into Figma's collaborative design platform, enabling teams to move from design to prototype in days rather than weeks. The product leverages third-party AI models and provides moderate explainability features, with SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, and FedRAMP certifications supporting enterprise security and compliance. Key differentiators include seamless design-to-code workflow integration, real-time team collaboration, and significant time-to-prototype reduction (corroborated: 2–3 weeks → 2 days for prototypes).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Company Identity and Company Reach\\n\\n- **Legal Name:** Figma, Inc.\\n- **Vendor Type:** AI Product Company (design platform with integrated AI capabilities)\\n- **Year Founded:** 2012\\n- **Employees:** 1,001–5,000\\n- **Annual Revenue / Funding Stage:** Not specified\\n- **Key Investors / Headquarters:** Headquarters: San Francisco, CA, USA; specific investors not stated\\n- **Operating Regions:** Not specified (inferred: global, based on SaaS model and enterprise customer base)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. AI Models & Technology\\n\\n- **Model Types:** Third-party AI models (specific model names, architectures, or LLM providers not disclosed)\\n- **Model Purpose:** Design-to-code conversion, task automation, design consistency enforcement, real-time collaboration enhancement\\n- **Model Governance:** Not documented; no staged deployment framework, safety gates, or rollout policies specified\\n- **Human Oversight:** Not specified; no evidence of advisory boards, human-in-the-loop workflows, or intervention mechanisms documented\\n- **Explainability / Transparency:** Moderate—some explainability features present, but prompt-level control, citation mechanisms, and detailed reasoning transparency not specified\\n- **Update Frequency:** Not specified\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. AI Safety & Testing\\n\\n- **Training Data Documentation:** Minimal—vendor uses third-party models with data provenance unknown; no documentation of training data composition, filtering, or bias mitigation in source datasets\\n- **Bias Detection:** Not specified; no red-team testing, third-party bias audits, statistical monitoring tools, or bias detection framework documented\\n- **Penetration Testing:** Not specified; frequency and scope of adversarial security testing not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. AI Governance (Ethics, Oversight, and Governance)\\n\\n- **AI Ethics Policy:** Not documented; no formal AI ethics policy, usage guidelines, or safety frameworks specified\\n- **Bias Audits:** Not specified; no internal or external bias audit schedule, methodology, or results provided\\n- **Human-in-the-Loop:** Not specified; no evidence of admin controls, content filtering, human review workflows, or intervention capabilities documented\\n- **Impact Assessment:** Not specified; no system cards, model cards, or release impact assessments provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Security Posture\\n\\n- **Incident Response Plan:** Yes, tested annually—demonstrates mature incident management discipline with documented and tested response procedures\\n- **Rollback Capability:** Automated with manual trigger—enables rapid recovery from problematic deployments; manual trigger provides control and verification step\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Data Practices\\n\\n- **Data Types Processed:** Design files, prototypes, code artifacts, team collaboration metadata\\n- **PII Handling:** Not specified; extent of PII processing and whether customer design/code data is used for model training not disclosed\\n- **Data Collection:** API and web interface (Figma platform); specific collection scope not detailed\\n- **Data Storage:** Cloud-hosted infrastructure; specific cloud provider (AWS/Azure/GCP) and encryption methods not specified\\n- **Data Retention:** Figma deletes customer data upon request and maintains a documented data retention and disposal policy; default retention period not specified\\n- **Data Location / Residency:** Not specified; customer choice of data residency not confirmed\\n- **Data Deletion:** On request; automated deletion procedures not specified\\n- **Sub-processors:** Not specified; infrastructure providers, payment processors, and authentication vendors not disclosed\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Compliance & Certifications\\n\\n- **Certifications:** SOC 2 Type 2, SOC 3, ISO 27001, ISO 27017, ISO 27018, ISO 27701, FedRAMP—comprehensive security and data protection certifications indicating enterprise-grade compliance maturity\\n- **Independent Compliance Audit Method:** Third-party audits conducted (implied by SOC 2 Type 2 and FedRAMP certifications); specific audit methodology and frequency not detailed\\n- **Regulatory Frameworks:** \\n  - NIST: Implied by FedRAMP certification (requires NIST SP 800-53 compliance)\\n  - GDPR: Not explicitly confirmed; ISO 27001 and ISO 27701 suggest data protection readiness\\n  - CCPA: Not explicitly confirmed\\n  - EU AI Act Readiness: Not specified\\n- **HIPAA Compliance:** Not specified; BAA eligibility not confirmed (not recommended for healthcare PHI without explicit HIPAA certification)\\n- **GDPR Compliance:** Likely supported (ISO 27001, ISO 27701 certifications suggest DPA availability); formal DPA not confirmed\\n- **EU AI Act Readiness:** Not specified\\n- **Audit Frequency / Last Audit Date / Audit Findings:** Audit frequency not specified; last audit date and findings not provided; continuous audits mentioned in data retention policy\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Operations & Support\\n\\n- **Uptime SLA:** 99.9% (8.8 hours downtime per year)—enterprise-grade availability commitment\\n- **Support Response SLAs (P1 / P2 / P3):** Not specified; severity-based response targets not documented\\n- **Change Management / Release Cadence:** Not specified; release frequency, testing procedures, and rollback readiness practices not documented\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Vendor Management\\n\\n- **Critical Vendors:** Not specified; infrastructure providers, payment processors, and other critical sub-processors not disclosed\\n- **Vendor Assessment:** Not specified; frequency and methodology of vendor risk assessments not documented\\n- **Vendor SLAs:** Not specified; SLAs from critical infrastructure or service vendors not provided\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Evidence & Trust\\n\\n- **Usage / Interaction Telemetry:** Yes, basic metrics available—enables monitoring of product usage and performance; detailed telemetry scope not specified\\n- **Audit Logs (SIEM Export):** Yes, basic logging available—supports audit trail and compliance investigations; export format and retention not specified\\n- **Supporting Testing and Policy Documentation:** Yes, basic test results available—indicates some testing rigor; detailed test plans, coverage, and results not provided\\n- **Model / Safety Testing Results (Under NDA):** Not specified; no indication of additional safety testing documentation available under NDA\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SCORING METHODOLOGY & FORMULA APPLICATION\\n\\n### Trust Score Calculation (VTS Formula)\\n\\nApplied **Vendor Trust Score (VTS)** formula from retrieved guidance:\\n\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(Product_Risk × 0.40) + (Governance_Risk × 0.30) + (Operational_Risk × 0.30)]\\n```\\n\\n**Product Risk Calculation:**\\n- Inherent Risk: 65 (third-party models with unknown training data; moderate explainability; design-to-code use case carries moderate risk of output errors)\\n- Mitigation Effectiveness: 0.60 (SOC 2 Type 2 + ISO 27001 provide security controls; no documented AI safety framework or bias testing reduces mitigation score)\\n- Confidence Factor: 0.75 (basic test results available; no third-party AI safety audit)\\n- **Product Risk = 65 × 0.60 × 0.75 = 29.25 ≈ 29**\\n\\n**Governance Risk Calculation:**\\n- Certifications: 40 points (SOC 2 Type 2, ISO 27001, FedRAMP = strong security governance; no HIPAA or AI-specific certifications)\\n- Assessment Quality: 15 points (third-party audits implied by SOC 2 Type 2; no AI ethics or bias audit documentation)\\n- Policies: 20 points (data retention policy documented; no AI ethics, incident response, or privacy policies detailed)\\n- Operational Controls: 25 points (rollback automated; no human oversight or monitoring policies documented)\\n- Maturity Adjustment: 10 points (10+ year company, 1,001–5,000 employees, mature stage)\\n- **Governance Score = 40 + 15 + 20 + 25 + 10 = 110 → capped at 100**\\n- **Governance Risk = 100 - 100 = 0**\\n\\n**Operational Risk Calculation:**\\n- SLA: 33 points (99.9% uptime = 8.8 hrs/year downtime)\\n- Incident Management: 25 points (tested annually; no automated incident response details)\\n- Deployment Maturity: 25 points (cloud SaaS; enterprise multi-tenant implied; no details on staging/canary deployments)\\n- Stability: 20 points (10+ year company, mature stage, profitable implied)\\n- Support: 15 points (support SLAs not specified; no 24/7 dedicated support confirmed)\\n- **Operational Score = 33 + 25 + 25 + 20 + 15 = 118 → capped at 100**\\n- **Operational Risk = 100 - 100 = 0**\\n\\n**Final VTS:**\\n```\\nVTS = 100 - [(29 × 0.40) + (0 × 0.30) + (0 × 0.30)]\\n    = 100 - [11.6 + 0 + 0]\\n    = 100 - 11.6\\n    = 88.4 ≈ 88\\n```\\n\\n**Adjustment for AI Governance Gaps:**\\nThe formula-derived score of 88 reflects strong operational and governance certifications but does not fully capture the significant gaps in AI-specific governance (no ethics policy, bias testing, human oversight, or training data transparency). Per guidance, residual AI governance concerns warrant a downward adjustment to reflect \\"Managed Product Risk\\" with \\"Mitigation Coverage Gaps\\" in post-deployment monitoring and human oversight.\\n\\n**Adjusted Trust Score: 72** (Acceptable Vendor, Grade C)\\n- Rationale: Strong security/compliance foundation (SOC 2, ISO, FedRAMP) and mature operations (99.9% SLA, annual incident testing) offset by critical AI governance gaps (no ethics policy, bias audits, or training data documentation). Acceptable for design automation use cases with standard controls; requires enhanced AI governance and third-party AI safety audit before high-stakes deployment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## PROCUREMENT RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n**Status:** CONDITIONAL APPROVAL\\n\\n**Recommended Actions:**\\n1. **Require AI Governance Roadmap:** Vendor must commit to documenting AI ethics policy, bias testing framework, and human oversight mechanisms within 90 days of contract execution.\\n2. **Third-Party AI Safety Audit:** Require independent audit of design-to-code model outputs, bias detection, and explainability before production deployment in regulated environments.\\n3. **Data Provenance Disclosure:** Vendor must disclose third-party model providers, training data sources, and any use of customer design/code data for model improvement.\\n4. **Support SLA Definition:** Establish severity-based support response SLAs (P1/P2/P3) in contract.\\n5. **Use Case Fit:** Suitable for design-to-code automation in product development teams; not recommended for healthcare, financial services, or other regulated industries without HIPAA/GDPR-specific certifications and AI safety validation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Report Generated:** [Date]  \\n**Assessment Type:** Vendor Self-Attestation (Assessment Type 1)  \\n**Confidence Level:** Moderate (basic test results and certifications available; AI governance documentation gaps limit confidence in residual risk assessment)", "sections": [{"id": 1, "items": {"Pricing": "Contact vendor", "Version": "Not specified", "Product Name": "Figma AI", "Customer Base": "Findable case study corroborated by 3 evidence points; specific customer count not stated", "Target Industry": "Product design, software development, enterprise teams", "Deployment Model": "Cloud-hosted (SaaS)", "Deployment Scale": "Team-based (real-time collaboration across large design teams)", "Primary Use Case": "Design-to-code conversion, design consi